          1 => '8 kN',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate open',
          1 => '7 kN',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'When to pick a screw gate',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A screw gate is simple and reliable, but it only locks when you lock it. That makes it a good choice for connections you rarely open, and a poor one for connections you open all day. For those, the <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/petzl-amd-carabiner/">Am\'D Triact-Lock</a> locks itself.',
            1 => 'Check your company policy and the applicable standard; many require self-locking connectors in certain positions.',
            2 => 'The Am\'D frame is the same shape climbers already know from the auto-locking version. The asymmetric D keeps the load close to the spine and leaves a wide end for hitches, rope eyes and pulleys, and the keylock nose slides through sewn eyes without snagging.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Habits and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Screw the gate closed and check it with a squeeze every time. Vibration and rope movement can work a screw gate loose, so recheck during the day. Look for grit in the threads, rope wear and cracks. Life-support gear has to meet the applicable standard and the manufacturer\'s instructions, get inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
            1 => 'Clean the threads when they get gritty. Sap and sawdust make a screw gate stiff, which makes it more likely you will leave it partly unlocked. Rinse it in warm water, dry it and retire any carabiner that has taken a hard drop or shows cracks or deep rope grooves.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a screw-lock carabiner OK for tree climbing?',
          'a' => 'It is used for semi-permanent connections, but many climbers and policies prefer auto-locking gates for anything opened often.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What are the ratings?',
          'a' => '28 kN major axis, 8 kN minor axis and 7 kN gate open. Confirm the markings on your carabiner.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'carabiner+chains',
        'creator' => 'brenkee',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/55128416@N05',
        'license' => 'CC0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/55128416@N05/12835828245',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/screw-gate-carabiners/petzl-william-screw-lock-carabiner/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/screw-gate-carabiners/petzl-william-screw-lock-carabiner/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/screw-gate-carabiners/',
      'name' => 'Petzl William Screw-Lock Carabiner',
      'brand' => 'petzl',
      'brand_name' => 'Petzl',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 20,
      'keyword' => 'screw lock carabiner',
      'title' => 'Petzl William Screw Lock Carabiner, Large Pear',
      'h1' => 'Petzl William Screw-Lock Carabiner',
      'meta' => 'The Petzl William is a large pear-shaped screw lock carabiner with room for hitches, rope and several connections. Uses, ratings and screw gate habits.',
      'tagline' => 'A large pear-shaped screw lock carabiner with room for rope and multiple connections.',
      'summary' => 'The Petzl William is a large pear-shaped screw lock carabiner with a wide gate and a roomy basket. It holds several connections, takes a fat rope hitch and gives you space to work with gloves on, which is why it shows up in anchors, rescue kits and rigging setups.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Large pear shape with plenty of room inside',
        1 => 'Wide opening for rope, eyes and slings',
        2 => 'Screw-lock gate for semi-permanent connections',
        3 => 'Keylock nose reduces snagging',
        4 => 'Light aluminum body',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Aluminum',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Shape',
          1 => 'Large pear (HMS)',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate',
          1 => 'Screw-lock',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Major axis',
          1 => '27 kN',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Minor axis',
          1 => '8 kN',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate open',
          1 => '7 kN',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a big pear carabiner',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pear-shaped carabiners give a wide, round end where rope and several connections can sit side by side without binding. The William is one of the larger ones, which makes it handy wherever several things meet: an anchor point, a lowering setup, the master point of a rescue system or a pulley with a sling.',
            1 => 'With gloves on and cold hands, the extra room makes clipping faster and reduces the chance of trapping a sling across the gate.',
            2 => 'Beyond the tree, the William is handy for building a lowering anchor at the base, holding a block and sling together, or clipping a few slings at a central point for a rescue. Its size makes it easy to see in a pile of gear, which helps on a busy job site.',
            3 => 'If you work mostly with gloves, a large carabiner like this is simply easier to handle than a small D.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Screw lock carabiner basics',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A screw lock carabiner locks only when you turn the sleeve closed. It is simple, has few parts and is easy to inspect, but it relies on you. Make a habit of screwing it shut, then squeezing the gate to confirm it is locked.',
            1 => 'Screw gates suit connections you set and leave. For connections you open many times a day, an auto-locking gate is the safer choice. Compare with the <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/screw-gate-carabiners/petzl-amd-screw-lock-carabiner/">Petzl Am\'D Screw-Lock</a> if you want a smaller D shape.',
            2 => 'A simple rule many crews use: screw it closed, give it a quarter turn back so it does not bind, then squeeze to check. Do it the same way every time.',
            3 => 'If your company policy calls for auto-locking connectors in life-support positions, keep the William for anchors, rigging and rescue setups where a screw gate is allowed.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Loading it right',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pear carabiners have a wide basket, so pay attention to how multiple connections sit. The more the load spreads away from the spine, the lower the strength. Keep loads near the spine and avoid three-way loading unless the manufacturer allows it.',
            1 => 'Keep the gate away from limbs and other hardware, and watch that the sleeve does not unscrew as rope runs past it.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check thread condition, gate spring and the inside of the basket for rope grooves. Rinse out sap and grit, dry it, and retire any carabiner that has been dropped hard or shows cracks or deep wear. Life-support gear has to meet the applicable standard and the manufacturer\'s instructions, get inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
            1 => 'Pay special attention to the inside of the basket where rope runs over it. Large carabiners used for lowering or as a master point often see more rope movement than a typical climbing connector, so grooves can develop faster. A groove with a sharp edge can damage rope, so retire it before that point.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a screw lock carabiner?',
          'a' => 'A carabiner with a threaded sleeve on the gate that you turn by hand to lock it closed.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How strong is the Petzl William?',
          'a' => 'It is rated 27 kN major axis, 8 kN minor axis and 7 kN gate open. Always check the markings on your carabiner.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Screw lock or auto-lock for climbing?',
          'a' => 'Auto-locking gates are preferred for connections you open often. Screw locks are fine for semi-permanent connections if you lock and check them every time.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
        2 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => '3D-printed Carabiner Clip',
        'creator' => 'Creative Tools',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/33907867@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/33907867@N02/15004955370',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/screw-gate-carabiners/smc-large-heat-treated-steel-screw-locking-carabiner/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/screw-gate-carabiners/smc-large-heat-treated-steel-screw-locking-carabiner/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/screw-gate-carabiners/',
      'name' => 'SMC Large Heat Treated Steel Screw Locking Carabiner',
      'brand' => 'smc',
      'brand_name' => 'SMC',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 22,
      'keyword' => 'extra large heavy duty carabiner',
      'title' => 'SMC Extra Large Heavy Duty Steel Carabiner',
      'h1' => 'SMC Extra Large Heavy Duty Steel Screw Locking Carabiner',
      'meta' => 'An extra large heavy duty carabiner in heat-treated steel from SMC, with a screw gate and room for several connections. Best rigging uses and inspection tips.',
      'tagline' => 'An extra large, heat-treated steel screw-lock carabiner for heavy-duty connections.',
      'summary' => 'This SMC large steel carabiner is an extra large heavy duty carabiner for connections that see abuse: rigging, anchors and setups where several slings or a thick rope have to fit. Heat-treated steel resists wear from rope and hardware far better than aluminum.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Heat-treated steel for wear resistance',
        1 => 'Large opening and basket',
        2 => 'Screw-lock gate',
        3 => 'Ratings marked on the spine',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Heat-treated steel',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate',
          1 => 'Screw-lock',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Size',
          1 => 'Large',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'Marked on the spine',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why steel',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Steel carabiners weigh more than aluminum, but they shrug off the grooving that rigging rope and steel hardware cause. That makes them a smart choice for rigging connections, anchors and gear that sits in the truck bed between jobs.',
            1 => 'The large size gives room for bigger rope, several slings or a block and a sling together.',
            2 => 'Heat treatment hardens the steel so it resists wear, which is why steel carabiners last longer on rigging lines that carry grit and sap. The trade-off is weight, so most climbers keep steel off the saddle and in the rigging bag.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Read the ratings on the spine and keep loads within them, remembering that rigging forces can far exceed the weight of a piece of wood. Lock the screw gate and check it every time. Retire it if it shows cracks, bending or deep wear. For life-support use, confirm the carabiner is rated for it and follow the applicable standard. See more in <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/screw-gate-carabiners/">screw gate carabiners</a>.',
            1 => 'When stacking several connections in the basket, keep the loads near the spine and avoid three-way loading unless the manufacturer allows it. Screw the gate closed, then squeeze the gate to confirm it is locked, and recheck after rope has been running past it.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is steel stronger than aluminum?',
          'a' => 'Steel carabiners are usually stronger and more wear resistant, but always go by the ratings marked on the carabiner.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is this good for rigging?',
          'a' => 'Steel carabiners are common in rigging connections. Keep within the marked ratings and size hardware for the expected forces.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/smc/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Carabiner',
        'creator' => 'mikemol',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/28208534@N07',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/28208534@N07/8350737864',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/snap-hooks/aluminum-double-locking-snap-hook/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/snap-hooks/aluminum-double-locking-snap-hook/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/snap-hooks/',
      'name' => 'Aluminum Double Locking Snap Hook',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 30,
      'keyword' => 'aluminum snap hook',
      'title' => 'Aluminum Double Locking Snap Hook for Lanyards',
      'h1' => 'Aluminum Double Locking Snap Hook',
      'meta' => 'A light aluminum snap hook with a double locking gate for lanyards and positioning gear. Where it fits, how it compares to steel, and what to inspect each day.',
      'tagline' => 'A lighter double locking snap for lanyards and positioning gear.',
      'summary' => 'This aluminum snap hook has a double locking gate: you press the keeper and then open the gate, so it cannot be opened by a single push. Lighter than steel, it suits lanyard ends and positioning setups where weight adds up over a day.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Double locking gate needs two actions to open',
        1 => 'Lighter than an equivalent steel snap',
        2 => 'Easy to operate with one gloved hand',
        3 => 'Good fit for lanyard ends',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Aluminum',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate',
          1 => 'Double locking',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'Check markings on the snap',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a double locking snap',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A double locking snap closes and locks automatically, and opening it takes two deliberate actions. That makes it hard for a branch, rope or D-ring to open it by accident, which is why snaps like this are common on lanyards and positioning gear.',
            1 => 'Aluminum cuts weight compared with steel, which you notice at the end of a long day on spurs.',
            2 => 'Snap hooks also hold their orientation better than a carabiner on a sewn or spliced lanyard end. They are less likely to twist and cross-load against a D-ring when you lean back on a stem, which matters when you are on spurs and working with both hands.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Wear and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Aluminum wears faster than steel where it rubs against steel hardware or wire core lanyards. Check for grooves, sharp edges, a sticky keeper and a gate that does not snap shut. Make sure the snap is compatible with the D-rings on your saddle. Life-support gear has to meet the applicable standard and the manufacturer\'s instructions, get inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
            1 => 'Compare with the <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/snap-hooks/steel-locking-rope-snap/">steel locking rope snap</a>.',
            2 => 'If you use the snap with a wire core flipline, keep an eye on where the wire rubs. Aluminum grooves faster under steel, and a sharp groove can damage rope or slings that run through it later.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Aluminum or steel snap?',
          'a' => 'Aluminum is lighter; steel resists wear better around wire core and steel hardware. Pick based on how you work and inspect accordingly.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a double locking snap?',
          'a' => 'A snap hook that locks itself when closed and needs two separate actions to open.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Carabiners and Pulley',
        'creator' => 'psd',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/45581782@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/45581782@N00/2411001794',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/snap-hooks/petzl-eashook-open-rope-snap/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/snap-hooks/petzl-eashook-open-rope-snap/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/snap-hooks/',
      'name' => 'Petzl Eashook Open Rope Snap',
      'brand' => 'petzl',
      'brand_name' => 'Petzl',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 45,
      'keyword' => 'petzl eashook',
      'title' => 'Petzl Eashook Open Rope Snap Connector',
      'h1' => 'Petzl Eashook Open Rope Snap',
      'meta' => 'The Petzl Eashook Open is a wide-opening aluminum snap connector for lanyards and anchor points. How it works, where arborists use it, and how to inspect it.',
      'tagline' => 'A wide-opening Petzl snap connector for lanyards and large anchors.',
      'summary' => 'The Petzl Eashook Open is a wide-opening aluminum connector designed for lanyards that clip around large anchors and structures. It locks automatically and opens with one hand, which suits work where you clip and unclip often.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Wide gate opening for larger anchors',
        1 => 'Automatic locking gate',
        2 => 'One-handed operation',
        3 => 'Aluminum body keeps weight down',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Aluminum',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate',
          1 => 'Automatic locking',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Lanyard end connector',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'Marked on the connector',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where it fits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The Eashook family is built for lanyard ends. The Open version has a large gate opening, so it clips around bigger hardware, rebar-type anchors and structures where a standard carabiner will not fit.',
            1 => 'In tree work it often ends up on the end of a positioning lanyard or on a bucket truck setup. Check the instructions to confirm the configuration you plan to use.',
            2 => 'The hook shape keeps the connector oriented on the end of a sewn lanyard, so it stays ready to clip as you move. The wide opening is also easier to clip around an anchor when you are wearing heavy gloves.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Checks',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Make sure the gate closes and locks automatically every time, and that the release works smoothly. Look for wear, cracks and deformation. Life-support gear has to meet the applicable standard and the manufacturer\'s instructions, get inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
            1 => 'Keep the release mechanism clean. Grit and sap make it stiff, which can stop the gate from closing fully. Rinse it in warm water, dry it, and test the action before the next job. Retire it after a hard drop or if it shows cracks or deformation.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use the Eashook Open on my climbing system?',
          'a' => 'Use it only as Petzl describes, typically on lanyard ends. Read the notice for approved uses.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How is it different from a carabiner?',
          'a' => 'It has a larger opening and a lanyard-friendly shape designed to stay oriented on a sewn end.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'bucket',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'carabiner',
        'creator' => 'ani!',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/53018729@N00',
        'license' => 'CC0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/53018729@N00/10235172',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/snap-hooks/steel-locking-rope-snap/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/snap-hooks/steel-locking-rope-snap/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/snap-hooks/',
      'name' => 'Steel Locking Rope Snap',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 25,
      'keyword' => 'locking rope snap',
      'title' => 'Steel Locking Rope Snap for Lanyards and Lines',
      'h1' => 'Steel Locking Rope Snap',
      'meta' => 'A steel locking rope snap for spliced lanyards and climbing lines. Why arborists like steel snaps, how a locking keeper works, and what to inspect before work.',
      'tagline' => 'A tough steel locking snap for spliced lanyards and lines.',
      'summary' => 'A steel locking rope snap is the classic hardware on the end of a spliced lanyard or climbing line. The locking keeper must be pressed before the gate opens, and steel stands up to the wear that wire core and saddle D-rings dish out.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Steel body for wear resistance',
        1 => 'Locking keeper prevents accidental opening',
        2 => 'Classic choice for spliced lanyard ends',
        3 => 'Swivel-free, low-profile design',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Steel',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate',
          1 => 'Locking keeper',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'Check markings on the snap',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why steel snaps last',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Snaps on lanyards rub against D-rings, wire core and bark all day. Steel takes that abuse far better than aluminum, so a steel locking rope snap is a good choice for gear that sees hard daily use.',
            1 => 'The snap is usually spliced or sewn onto the rope by a qualified splicer. Do not tie your own hardware onto life-support rope unless you are trained to do so.',
            2 => 'Steel snaps also handle being dragged across bark, dropped in the truck bed and clipped against D-rings thousands of times. They are heavier than aluminum, but on gear that sees daily abuse that weight buys longer service life.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Daily inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check that the gate and keeper snap closed on their own, the hinge pin is tight, and there is no rust pitting, cracks or wear. Make sure the snap is compatible with your saddle D-rings. Life-support gear has to meet the applicable standard and the manufacturer\'s instructions, get inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
            1 => 'For a lighter option, see the <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/snap-hooks/aluminum-double-locking-snap-hook/">aluminum double locking snap hook</a>.',
            2 => 'Keep the hinge and keeper free of sap and grit. A sticky keeper is the most common problem and it means the snap may not lock fully. Rinse it, dry it and test it before you trust it, and retire any snap that will not close and lock cleanly.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I put this snap on my own lanyard?',
          'a' => 'Hardware on life-support rope should be spliced or sewn by a qualified person and meet the applicable standard.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Steel or aluminum?',
          'a' => 'Steel lasts longer under wear; aluminum is lighter. Both need daily inspection.',
        ),
      ),
