    '/climbing/helmets/kask-super-plasma-climbing-helmet/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/helmets/kask-super-plasma-climbing-helmet/',
      'category' => '/climbing/helmets/',
      'name' => 'Kask Super Plasma Climbing Helmet',
      'brand' => 'kask',
      'brand_name' => 'Kask',
      'kind' => 'helmet',
      'price' => 160,
      'keyword' => 'kask super plasma',
      'title' => 'Kask Super Plasma Helmet for Arborists and Climbers',
      'h1' => 'Kask Super Plasma Climbing Helmet',
      'meta' => 'The Kask Super Plasma is a vented arborist helmet built for climbing and aerial work. Fit, accessories, how it compares and typical street price inside.',
      'tagline' => 'A popular climbing helmet for arborists who want comfort, airflow and accessory options.',
      'summary' => 'The Kask Super Plasma is a climbing helmet that has become a common sight in tree crews thanks to its comfort, airflow and accessory options. The Super Plasma helmet is built for climbing and aerial work, with a secure chin strap and slots for visors and hearing protection.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Ventilated shell for long days aloft',
        1 => 'Secure chin strap for climbing and aerial work',
        2 => 'Wheel adjustment for a quick, precise fit',
        3 => 'Accepts Kask visors and hearing protection',
        4 => 'Washable, replaceable padding',
        5 => 'Available in several colors including high-visibility options',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Climbing and work-at-height helmet',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Kask',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Retention',
          1 => 'Chin strap',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fit adjustment',
          1 => 'Rear wheel',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Accessories',
          1 => 'Visors, earmuffs, lights',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Typical price',
          1 => 'About $150 to $180',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why so many climbers wear it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A climbing helmet spends eight hours a day on your head, so small differences in comfort add up. The Super Plasma is light on the head, well vented and easy to adjust, and the padding can be pulled out and washed after a sweaty week. For many climbers it is the helmet they forget they are wearing, which is exactly what you want.',
            1 => 'The chin strap keeps the helmet on during a slip, swing or fall, which is the main difference between a climbing helmet and a traditional hard hat. A hard hat can come off when you need it most.',
            2 => 'Many climbers also like that the shell shape and color options make the helmet easy to spot from the ground, which helps when the ground crew is watching a climber work high in the canopy.',
            3 => 'The Super Plasma is also a common choice for bucket work, where a chin strap keeps the helmet on if you lean out of the basket or catch a branch. Kask arborist helmet versions with closed shells are the ones to check for electrical work, while vented versions suit climbing in hot weather.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Accessories that turn it into a system',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The Super Plasma takes Kask\'s range of visors and earmuffs, so you can set it up for climbing with a saw. A mesh or clear visor protects your face from chips and twigs, and helmet-mounted muffs drop over your ears when you start the saw and flip up when you are rigging or talking to the ground crew.',
            1 => 'If you run a radio headset, check compatibility before you buy, because mounting systems differ between brands.',
            2 => 'If you run a headlamp for early starts or storm work, the accessory clips make it simple to mount one securely.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Kask arborist helmet or something else',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Climbers often compare the Super Plasma with the <a href="/climbing/helmets/petzl-vertex-vent/">Petzl Vertex Vent</a> and the <a href="/climbing/helmets/pfanner-protos-integral-arborist-helmet-system/">Pfanner Protos Integral</a>. The Vertex Vent has a different suspension feel, while the Protos is a fully integrated system with built-in hearing and face protection. Try them on if you can, since head shape matters more than any spec sheet.',
            1 => 'There are several versions of the Super Plasma, including vented and unvented and high-visibility models. Check the exact version and its certifications against what your employer requires, especially for utility line clearance work where electrical ratings matter.',
            2 => 'Weight and balance matter too. Once you add a visor and muffs, the total load on your neck goes up, so try the full setup on before you commit, not just the bare helmet.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Wash the pads, wipe the shell clean of sap and bar oil, and avoid solvents and stickers that the manufacturer does not approve. Inspect the shell, chin strap and adjustment system regularly.',
            1 => 'Choose a helmet that meets the standard your employer and ANSI Z133 require for the work you do, fit it per the manufacturer\'s instructions, and replace it after a significant impact or when it reaches the maker\'s service life.',
            2 => 'Replace the pads when they flatten or stop wicking, since worn pads change how the helmet sits.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the Kask Super Plasma good for tree climbing?',
          'a' => 'Yes. It is one of the most widely used arborist climbing helmets, with a chin strap, good ventilation and accessory mounts for visors and muffs.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use it for electrical line clearance?',
          'a' => 'Only versions certified for electrical protection are suitable. Vented models are not. Check the label and your employer\'s requirements.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does it come with a visor and earmuffs?',
          'a' => 'The helmet is usually sold on its own, with visors and earmuffs sold separately or in kits. Check the listing.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'bucket',
        2 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/kask/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/helmet-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/helmet-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Arches Canyoneering',
        'creator' => 'ArchesNPS',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/72578886@N08',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/72578886@N08/9737334576',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/helmets/petzl-vertex-vent/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/helmets/petzl-vertex-vent/',
      'category' => '/climbing/helmets/',
      'name' => 'Petzl Vertex Vent Helmet',
      'brand' => 'petzl',
      'brand_name' => 'Petzl',
      'kind' => 'helmet',
      'price' => 145,
      'keyword' => 'petzl helmet',
      'title' => 'Petzl Vertex Vent: Petzl Helmet for Tree Climbers',
      'h1' => 'Petzl Vertex Vent Helmet',
      'meta' => 'The Petzl Vertex Vent is a ventilated Petzl helmet built for climbing and work at height. Fit system, accessories, versions and typical price explained.',
      'tagline' => 'A ventilated work-at-height helmet with a secure fit for long days in the canopy.',
      'summary' => 'The Petzl Vertex Vent is a Petzl helmet built for climbing and work at height, with closable vents for airflow and a chin strap that keeps it on through slips and swings. Its textile suspension and adjustment system give a stable, comfortable fit for long days aloft.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Closable vents for airflow on hot days',
        1 => 'Textile suspension for a comfortable, stable fit',
        2 => 'Chin strap for climbing and work at height',
        3 => 'Accessory slots for visors, earmuffs and headlamps',
        4 => 'Weight of about 490 g',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Work-at-height helmet',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Petzl',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ventilation',
          1 => 'Closable vents',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Retention',
          1 => 'Chin strap',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Weight',
          1 => 'about 490 g (17 oz)',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Typical price',
          1 => 'About $135 to $160',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Built for work at height',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The Petzl Vertex has been a standard in rope access, rescue and tree work for a long time. The Vertex Vent is the ventilated version, with sliding shutters that let you open the vents on hot days and close them to keep out sawdust and rain. For climbers who work through the summer, that airflow makes a real difference.',
            1 => 'Like all climbing helmets, it has a chin strap that keeps the helmet on during a slip, swing or fall. That is something a traditional hard hat does not do.',
            2 => 'Many crews use the Vertex Vent as a standard climbing helmet for exactly that reason: it works in hot weather and still keeps out debris when the vents are closed.',
            3 => 'For new climbers, the Vertex Vent is a sensible first helmet because it is widely used, easy to adjust and well supported with spare parts. Pads, chin straps and accessories are easy to find, which keeps the helmet in service longer.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and comfort',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The Vertex uses a textile suspension that cradles the head instead of perching on it. Combined with the adjustment system, it gives a stable fit that does not shift when you look up into the canopy or lean over a cut. Many climbers with longer heads find the Petzl vertex a better fit than rounder helmets, but head shape varies, so try it on if you can.',
            1 => 'The pads are removable and washable, which helps over a long season.',
            2 => 'Adjustment is quick, so you can snug the fit when you start climbing and loosen it on breaks. That makes it easier to wear the helmet all day instead of taking it off between climbs.',
            3 => 'Petzl also publishes clear inspection guidance, so it is easy to build the helmet into your daily gear check along with your saddle and rope.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Accessories and versions',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Slots on the shell accept Petzl visors, helmet-mounted earmuffs and headlamps, so you can build it into a full saw-ready setup. There are also high-visibility and unvented versions; unvented helmets are the option to look at when electrical insulation matters. Check the label on the exact model against your employer\'s requirements.',
            1 => 'Climbers often compare it with the <a href="/climbing/helmets/kask-super-plasma-climbing-helmet/">Kask Super Plasma</a>. Both are excellent; fit usually decides it. Browse all options in our <a href="/climbing/helmets/">climbing helmets</a> section.',
            2 => 'When you add accessories, check that the combination is approved by Petzl. Mixing parts from different makers can affect fit and protection, and some accessories may not fit every version of the helmet.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Keep the helmet out of strong sun when stored, clean it with mild soap and water, and avoid solvents and unapproved stickers. Check the shell, suspension and chin strap for damage.',
            1 => 'Choose a helmet that meets the standard your employer and ANSI Z133 require for the work you do, fit it per the manufacturer\'s instructions, and replace it after a significant impact or when it reaches the maker\'s service life.',
            2 => 'Keep a note of when you started using it, and check the manufacturer\'s recommended service life for your version.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between the Vertex and the Vertex Vent?',
          'a' => 'The Vertex Vent has closable ventilation openings. The non-vented Vertex is the choice when you need a closed shell, such as for some electrical work.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I add earmuffs and a visor to the Petzl Vertex Vent?',
          'a' => 'Yes. It has slots for Petzl-compatible earmuffs and visors, plus clips for a headlamp.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How much does the Vertex Vent weigh?',
          'a' => 'About 490 g, or roughly 17 ounces, before accessories.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'bucket',
        2 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/helmet-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/helmet-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => '3rd Regiment, Basic Camp, FHCC CST 2021',
        'creator' => 'U.S. Army ROTC',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/136737541@N05',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/136737541@N05/51323491277',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/helmets/pfanner-protos-integral-arborist-helmet-system/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/helmets/pfanner-protos-integral-arborist-helmet-system/',
      'category' => '/climbing/helmets/',
      'name' => 'Pfanner Protos Integral Arborist Helmet System',
      'brand' => 'pfanner',
      'brand_name' => 'Pfanner',
      'kind' => 'helmet',
      'price' => 300,
      'keyword' => 'protos helmet',
      'title' => 'Protos Helmet: Pfanner Protos Integral Arborist System',
      'h1' => 'Pfanner Protos Integral Arborist Helmet System',
      'meta' => 'The Pfanner Protos helmet integrates hearing, face and head protection in one arborist system. How it fits, who it suits, versions and typical price inside.',
      'tagline' => 'A fully integrated arborist helmet with built-in hearing and face protection.',
      'summary' => 'The Pfanner Protos Integral Arborist is the Protos helmet built for climbers, with head, face and hearing protection designed together as one system rather than bolted on. Its chin strap, integrated earmuffs and visor make it a favorite for arborists who run a saw aloft all day.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Integrated earmuffs and visor designed into the helmet',
        1 => 'Chin strap for climbing and work at height',
        2 => 'Adjustable fit system for long days aloft',
        3 => 'Ventilation for hot weather',
        4 => 'Accessory options including neck protection and radio kits',
        5 => 'Replaceable parts to extend service life',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Integrated arborist helmet system',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Pfanner',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Hearing protection',
          1 => 'Integrated earmuffs',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Face protection',
          1 => 'Integrated visor',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Retention',
          1 => 'Chin strap',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Typical price',
          1 => 'About $280 to $330',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'An integrated system, not a helmet with add-ons',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Most helmets start as a shell, then you add a visor and earmuffs. The Protos was designed the other way around. The earmuffs and visor are part of the helmet\'s shape, which keeps everything balanced on your head and stops parts from snagging on branches. It has a distinctive look that has made the Protos helmet one of the most recognized pieces of arborist gear.',
            1 => 'The Integral Arborist version adds a chin strap so the helmet stays on during a slip or swing, which is what separates a climbing helmet from a ground hard hat.',
            2 => 'The integrated design also means fewer parts to lose or forget. When the helmet is on, you have your face and hearing protection with you, which is a big deal for climbers who move between cutting and rigging all day.',
            3 => 'Comfort is a big reason climbers pay more for the Protos. When hearing protection, face protection and fit are designed together, the helmet feels balanced and stable, and many climbers say they forget they are wearing it after a few hours.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'On the job',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Climbers who run a top-handle saw all day tend to love the Protos. The muffs flip up and down quickly, and the visor drops into place without bumping the muffs. Ventilation helps on hot days, and the overall balance means less neck fatigue when you are looking up into the canopy for hours.',
            1 => 'Ground crews use Protos helmets too, especially on felling and bucking jobs, though climbers are the main audience for the Integral Arborist model.',
            2 => 'The fit system is designed for long days, and many climbers find it stays stable when looking up into the canopy for hours.',
            3 => 'If you are switching from a helmet with bolt-on accessories, give yourself a few days to get used to the different feel and controls. The flip-up muffs and visor become second nature quickly.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Versions and accessories',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'There are several Protos models and limited edition colors, and Pfanner has released updated versions over the years, often called Protos 2 or newer generations. Make sure the exact model and its certifications match your employer\'s requirements, and check that replacement parts and accessories match your helmet generation.',
            1 => 'Popular add-ons include neck protection and radio or Bluetooth hearing protection kits. Compare with the <a href="/climbing/helmets/kask-super-plasma-climbing-helmet/">Kask Super Plasma</a> and <a href="/climbing/helmets/petzl-vertex-vent/">Petzl Vertex Vent</a> if you prefer a modular setup.',
            2 => 'Buy spare visor screens and earmuff cushions with your helmet. They wear faster than the shell, and keeping spares means you are not working with a scratched visor while you wait for parts.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care and replacement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Clean the shell and visor with mild soap and water, replace earmuff cushions when they flatten, and inspect the shell, chin strap and fit system regularly.',
            1 => 'Choose a helmet that meets the standard your employer and ANSI Z133 require for the work you do, fit it per the manufacturer\'s instructions, and replace it after a significant impact or when it reaches the maker\'s service life.',
            2 => 'Clean the visor gently; abrasive cleaners scratch the screen and make it harder to see clearly.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the Protos helmet good for climbing?',
          'a' => 'The Integral Arborist version is built for climbing with a chin strap. Other Protos versions may be aimed at ground forestry work, so check the model.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the Protos 2?',
          'a' => 'It is a common name for later generations of the Protos helmet. Check part compatibility between generations before ordering spares.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why is the Protos more expensive than other helmets?',
          'a' => 'It includes integrated hearing and face protection that you would otherwise buy separately, and it is designed as one balanced system.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'felling',
        2 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/pfanner/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/helmet-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/helmet-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'TVCF004',
        'creator' => 'Symic',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00/33549425170',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/buckingham-saddles/buckingham-agility-saddle/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/buckingham-saddles/buckingham-agility-saddle/',
      'category' => '/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/buckingham-saddles/',
      'name' => 'Buckingham Agility Saddle',
      'brand' => 'buckingham',
      'brand_name' => 'Buckingham',
      'kind' => 'saddle',
      'price' => 380,
      'keyword' => 'buckingham tree saddle',
      'title' => 'Buckingham Agility Saddle: Buckingham Tree Saddle',
      'h1' => 'Buckingham Agility Saddle',
      'meta' => 'The Buckingham Agility is a Buckingham tree saddle built for climbers who move a lot in the canopy. Fit, features, who it suits and typical street price.',
      'tagline' => 'A Buckingham climbing saddle aimed at climbers who value freedom of movement.',
      'summary' => 'The Buckingham Agility saddle is a Buckingham tree saddle designed for climbers who move constantly in the canopy. It is built to let you twist, reach and limb walk without the saddle fighting you, while keeping the support you need for long hangs.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Designed for freedom of movement in the canopy',
        1 => 'Bridge attachment for a wide working range',
        2 => 'Padded waist and leg support for long days',
        3 => 'Gear loops and side D-rings for lanyard and tools',
        4 => 'Made by a long-established US saddle maker',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Arborist climbing saddle',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Buckingham',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Attachment',
          1 => 'Bridge and side D-rings',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Tree climbing and work positioning',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Who the Agility suits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Buckingham has been making climbing saddles in the US for a long time, and the Agility is aimed at climbers who spend their day moving: pruning wide crowns, limb walking and repositioning often. A bridge-style attachment lets your friction system travel side to side as you turn, so you are not twisting against a fixed point.',
            1 => 'If you mostly do removals and hang in one spot, the support and seat may matter more to you than mobility. Compare with the <a href="/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/buckingham-saddles/buckingham-buckcraft-saddle/">Buckingham Buckcraft</a>.',
            2 => 'Mobility saddles are especially useful on large-crown pruning, where you spend a lot of time reaching out on limbs and rotating around the stem. Being able to turn without fighting the saddle saves energy over a full day.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Order the size that matches your waist and leg measurements, adjust it with the clothes you climb in, and hang test it before going aloft. Check stitching, webbing, bridge and D-rings before each climb.',
            1 => 'A saddle is life-support equipment: buy one that meets the applicable standard, follow the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133, inspect it before every climb and retire it when wear reaches the maker\'s limits. Get trained before you climb on any new system.',
            2 => 'Look at gear loops and accessory options too. A saddle that carries your handsaw, carabiners and slings where you can reach them will make you more efficient.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the Agility a good first saddle?',
          'a' => 'It can be, if it fits well. Many new climbers are best served by trying on several saddles and getting guidance from a trainer.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the bridge replaceable?',
          'a' => 'Bridges are wear items on most modern saddles. Check Buckingham\'s instructions for replacement parts and procedures.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/buckingham/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/saddle-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/saddle-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Arborist climbing on the tree',
        'creator' => 'Yuliya Krasylenko',
        'creator_url' => 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Yuliya_Krasylenko',
