          1 => 'Sprocket',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Oregon',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'When to replace your bar',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Bars wear out. The rails spread or wear uneven, the groove gets shallow, and the nose sprocket goes rough. A bar that is pinched, bent or worn makes the chain cut crooked and wears out chains faster. When dressing the rails no longer fixes it, a new Oregon bar chainsaw setup brings the saw back to cutting straight.',
            1 => 'Oregon also makes lightweight bar options in some sizes. If balance on a long bar matters to you, compare an Oregon lightweight bar with a standard one.',
            2 => 'Oregon saw bars are made for a long list of saws, which is why they are a common stock item for crews running mixed fleets.',
            3 => 'Order it with a fresh chain so the whole cutting system starts out matched and true.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Match the mount, length, pitch, gauge and drive link count to your saw and chain. Check the listing before ordering.',
            1 => 'Flip the bar each time you sharpen, keep the groove and oil holes clean, grease the nose if it has a grease port, and keep the chain sharp. See more <a href="/jobsite/chainsaw-accessories/chainsaw-bars-and-chain/chainsaw-bars/">chainsaw bars</a>.',
            2 => 'A new bar deserves a new or fresh chain. Running a stretched, worn chain on a new bar wears the bar and sprocket faster.',
            3 => 'Store spare bars flat and oiled.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I know which Oregon bar fits my saw?',
          'a' => 'Check your saw\'s mount, bar length, chain pitch, gauge and drive link count, then match them to the bar listing.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I run .325 chain at .058 gauge on this bar?',
          'a' => 'No. This bar is for .050 gauge chain. The gauge must match the bar groove.',
        ),
      ),
