    '/jobsite/logging-tools/axes-and-mauls/husqvarna-multi-purpose-fiber-handle-axe-a2400/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/logging-tools/axes-and-mauls/husqvarna-multi-purpose-fiber-handle-axe-a2400/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/logging-tools/axes-and-mauls/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna A2400 Multi-Purpose Axe, Fiber Handle',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'wedge-axe',
      'price' => 49,
      'keyword' => 'husqvarna axe',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna Axe A2400: Multi-Purpose Fiber Handle Axe',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna A2400 Multi-Purpose Axe',
      'meta' => 'The Husqvarna axe A2400 is a multi-purpose axe with a tough composite handle for limbing, light splitting and chores. Who it suits and how to care for it.',
      'tagline' => 'Composite-handled multi-purpose axe for limbing, splitting and general chores.',
      'summary' => 'The A2400 is a Husqvarna axe with a fiber composite handle, built as a multi-purpose tool for limbing, light splitting and the general chopping that comes up on tree jobs and at home. The composite handle shrugs off weather and abuse that would ruin a wooden one.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Composite fiber handle that resists weather and rot',
        1 => 'Multi-purpose head for limbing and light splitting',
        2 => 'Low-maintenance choice for the truck',
        3 => 'Balanced for one- or two-handed use',
        4 => 'Comes with an edge guard',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Model',
          1 => 'A2400',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Handle',
          1 => 'Composite fiber',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Head',
          1 => 'Steel, multi-purpose profile',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Multi-purpose axe',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Husqvarna',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why choose a fiber handle',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Wooden handles feel great but need care. Left in a wet truck bed they swell, dry out, crack and work loose. A composite handle does not care if it rides in the rain all week. It is also more forgiving of an overstrike, where the handle rather than the head hits the wood, which is the classic way wooden handles break.',
            1 => 'That makes the A2400 a good truck axe for crews that are hard on tools, and for anyone who wants an axe that is ready when they need it without oiling the handle every season.',
            2 => 'Composite handles also tend to be lighter for their length than hickory, so the axe moves fast. That said, some people prefer the feel and feedback of wood. If you already own a good wooden-handled axe you like, this makes a solid second axe for the truck or the trailer.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What a multi-purpose axe handles',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On a tree job, the multi purpose axe knocks off small limbs, notches a stump for a grinder, trims roots, splits a few rounds, and drives <a href="/jobsite/chainsaw-accessories/wedges/hardhead-felling-wedge/">plastic felling wedges</a> with the poll. At home it splits kindling and handles camp chores.',
            1 => 'It is a compromise tool. For serious firewood splitting, a <a href="/jobsite/logging-tools/axes-and-mauls/husqvarna-31-splitting-axe/">Husqvarna splitting axe</a> does the job faster. For clean limbing on big logs, a saw is usually faster still. But as one axe that does a bit of everything, it earns its spot on the truck.',
            2 => 'It is also a good choice for the ground person who wants one axe on the trailer that nobody needs to baby. When it gets left out overnight in the rain, it is fine the next morning.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Using an axe safely',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Before swinging, check that the head is tight, the edge is sound and there is nothing in your swing path, including overhead branches. Keep other people well back. Stand so that if the axe glances or misses, it goes into the ground or the block, not into your leg or foot. Wear eye protection, gloves and sturdy boots.',
            1 => 'Limb from the opposite side of the log where possible, so the log is between you and the edge. Do not chop near rocks or dirt; they ruin the edge and can make the head bounce. When you are tired, stop. Most axe injuries happen late in the day.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Sharpening and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Keep the edge sharp with a file or stone, holding the original bevel. A sharp axe bites; a dull axe glances. Wipe the head after use and keep a light coat of oil on it to prevent rust, especially if it rides in a damp truck. Keep the edge guard on in transport and storage. Inspect the handle regularly and replace the axe if the handle or head cracks.',
            1 => 'Use a guide or a puck-style stone for sharpening in the field, and save heavy reshaping for the bench with a file and vise. Never use a bench grinder at high speed without cooling the steel, since overheating can ruin the temper of the edge.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a composite handle better than wood?',
          'a' => 'Composite handles resist weather and overstrikes and need little care. Some people prefer the feel of wood. Both work well when the head is sound.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I split firewood with the A2400?',
          'a' => 'Yes, for light splitting and kindling. For heavy firewood work, a dedicated splitting axe or maul is more efficient.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I sharpen my Husqvarna axe?',
          'a' => 'Touch up the edge whenever it starts glancing instead of biting, and after any hit on dirt or stone.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'felling',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/wedge-axe-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/wedge-axe-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Axe in wood',
        'creator' => 'brittgow',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/26489950@N03',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/26489950@N03/4781607809',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/logging-tools/forestry-tools/spencer-logging-tape-75-8ths-no-diameter/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/logging-tools/forestry-tools/spencer-logging-tape-75-8ths-no-diameter/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/logging-tools/forestry-tools/',
      'name' => 'Spencer 75 ft Logging Tape, 8ths, No Diameter',
      'brand' => 'spencer',
      'brand_name' => 'Spencer',
      'kind' => 'log-tool',
      'price' => 65,
      'keyword' => 'loggers tape',
      'title' => 'Spencer Loggers Tape, 75 ft, 8ths, No Diameter Scale',
      'h1' => 'Spencer 75 ft Loggers Tape, 8ths, No Diameter',
      'meta' => 'A Spencer loggers tape, 75 ft long and marked in 8ths with no diameter scale. Hook a log, walk back and buck to length fast. How to use it and keep it working.',
      'tagline' => 'Belt-mounted spring-return logging tape for bucking logs to length.',
      'summary' => 'This Spencer loggers tape is a 75 ft spring-return tape graduated in feet, inches and 8ths, with no diameter scale on the back. It clips to your belt, hooks into the end of a log and pays out as you walk, so a sawyer can measure and mark cuts without a helper.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '75 ft spring-return tape for bucking logs to length',
        1 => 'Graduated in feet, inches and 8ths',
        2 => 'No diameter scale, length markings only',
        3 => 'Hook end bites into bark or the cut end of a log',
        4 => 'Belt-mount case keeps hands free for the saw',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Length',
          1 => '75 ft',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Graduations',
          1 => 'Feet, inches and 8ths',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter scale',
          1 => 'None',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Mount',
          1 => 'Belt clip case',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Return',
          1 => 'Spring retract',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Spencer',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How a logging tape works',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A logging tape is built for one person working alone in the woods. The tape lives in a case on your belt, and the free end has a sharp hook or nail. Drive the hook into the end of the log, walk down the log, and the tape pays out behind you. When you reach the length you want, mark the cut with your saw. A flick of the tape pops the hook loose and the spring pulls it back into the case.',
            1 => 'Spencer is the name most sawyers think of when they hear original loggers tape. Their tapes have been a standard on landings and in firewood yards for a long time, and parts and refills are easy to find.',
            2 => 'The case is the part you will notice most. A good one sits flat on the belt, does not catch on brush and lets the tape pay out smoothly at any angle. Spencer cases are built for that kind of daily use, which is why many sawyers have been wearing the same case for years with just a refill or two.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why 8ths and no diameter',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'This version is graduated in feet and inches down to eighths, which suits anyone cutting to specific lengths for sawlogs, firewood rounds or customer requests. It has no diameter scale on the reverse side. Diameter tapes are useful for timber cruising and scaling, but if you only buck to length, a plain length tape is simpler to read.',
            1 => 'A 75 ft tape covers most bucking work on tree service jobs and in the woods. You can measure a full trunk on the ground and lay out several cuts in one walk, which saves trips back and forth with the saw running.',
            2 => 'Accurate lengths matter more than people expect. A mill may dock or reject logs that come in short, and a firewood customer who asked for 16 inch rounds will notice when half of them are 20. A tape takes the guesswork out of eyeballing cuts against the bar.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Using it on tree jobs',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On removals, a logging tape helps when a customer wants wood cut to set lengths, when you are bucking sawlogs for a mill, or when you are cutting to fit a trailer or a log truck. Mark cuts with a quick nick from the saw or a lumber crayon, then come back and buck.',
            1 => 'Never measure a log that is moving, rolling or under tension, and keep the tape and your hands clear of the chain when someone else is bucking. Hook the tape, step back, then call the cut.',
            2 => 'Clip the case where it does not interfere with your saw hand. Many sawyers wear it on a <a href="/jobsite/chainsaw-accessories/wedges/logging-belt-kit/">logging belt</a> with their wedges and a file.',
            3 => 'On big trunks, lay out every cut before you start the saw. It is faster to walk the log once with the tape and mark each length than to stop between cuts, and it lets you plan around crooks, forks and defects so you get the most usable wood.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care and refills',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Let the tape rewind under control instead of letting it snap back, which wears the hook and the spring. Wipe off mud and sap now and then, and keep the case out of the chipper chute. When the blade finally kinks or snaps, a refill puts it back to work without replacing the whole case. See the <a href="/jobsite/logging-tools/forestry-tools/spencer-logging-tape-measure-50-refill/">Spencer 50 ft refill</a> and confirm the refill length matches your case before you order.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What does 8ths mean on a loggers tape?',
          'a' => 'The tape is marked in feet and inches with eighth-inch divisions, so you can measure lengths precisely.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need a diameter scale?',
          'a' => 'Only if you scale or cruise timber. For bucking logs to length, a plain length tape is all you need.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I replace the tape blade?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Spencer sells refills. Match the refill length and type to your case.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'felling',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/log-tool-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/log-tool-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'peavey handles (hand carved)',
        'creator' => 'Stone Soup Institute, Harpswell, Maine',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/92385698@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/92385698@N02/9819022265',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/logging-tools/forestry-tools/spencer-logging-tape-measure-35/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/logging-tools/forestry-tools/spencer-logging-tape-measure-35/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/logging-tools/forestry-tools/',
      'name' => 'Spencer Logging Tape Measure 35',
      'brand' => 'spencer',
      'brand_name' => 'Spencer',
      'kind' => 'log-tool',
      'price' => 55,
      'keyword' => 'loggers tape',
      'title' => 'Spencer Loggers Tape Measure 35: Belt Logging Tape',
      'h1' => 'Spencer Loggers Tape Measure 35',
      'meta' => 'The Spencer 35 loggers tape measure clips to your belt and hooks the end of a log so one person can measure cuts. Uses, features and care for a Spencer tape.',
      'tagline' => 'Belt-mounted Spencer loggers tape for one-person measuring on the landing.',
      'summary' => 'The Spencer 35 is a loggers tape measure designed for one person: it clips to your belt, the hook end grabs the log, and the tape pays out as you walk. It is a quick way to buck logs, firewood and sawlogs to consistent lengths.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Belt-mounted case keeps both hands free',
        1 => 'Hook end grips the log so you can work alone',
        2 => 'Spring return rewinds the tape after each measure',
        3 => 'Made by Spencer, a long-time name in logging tapes',
        4 => 'Refills available, so the case lasts for years',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Model',
          1 => '35',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Spring-return loggers tape',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Mount',
          1 => 'Belt clip case',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'End',
          1 => 'Hook for log ends and bark',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Spencer',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What makes a loggers measuring tape different',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A regular construction tape measure needs a second person or a nail at the far end. A loggers tape solves that with a hook that bites into wood. You set the hook in the end of the log, walk away and the tape follows. Read the length, mark the cut and give the tape a flick to free the hook. The spring winds it back into the case on your belt.',
            1 => 'That is why Spencer tapes are a fixture with fallers, firewood producers and tree crews who buck wood to length. The tape stays on your hip, so measuring takes seconds and does not slow down the saw.',
            2 => 'It is also faster than any alternative. Pacing off a log or holding the bar against it to estimate length is fine for rough firewood, but it adds up to a lot of error over a day. A tape makes every cut repeatable.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing the right Spencer tape',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Spencer makes tapes in several lengths and with different graduations, including versions with a diameter scale on the back for scaling and cruising. Check the listing for this model\'s length and markings and pick what fits your work. Shorter tapes are lighter on the belt and fine for firewood and short logs. Longer tapes, like the <a href="/jobsite/logging-tools/forestry-tools/spencer-logging-tape-75-8ths-no-diameter/">Spencer 75 ft tape</a>, let you lay out several cuts along a whole trunk in one walk.',
            1 => 'If you only need lengths, a tape without a diameter scale is easier to read. If you buy and sell timber by volume, a diameter scale saves carrying a second tool.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Using it on the job',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Wear the case where you can reach it easily but where it will not bang against the saw. Many sawyers put it on the back or off-hand hip of a logging belt. Set the hook firmly before you walk, keep the tape straight along the log and read the mark at the point you want to cut.',
            1 => 'Never measure a log that is moving, rolling or under tension, and keep the tape and your hands clear of the chain when someone else is bucking. Hook the tape, step back, then call the cut.',
            2 => 'When bucking for a mill, ask for their length specs and trim allowance before you start. A few inches of trim on every log saves a lot of arguing later.',
            3 => 'Firewood cutters often measure once to set the length and then use a mark on the saw or a stick for the rest of the cuts. A tape is still useful for checking every so often that the cuts have not drifted long or short.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Let the tape rewind slowly rather than snapping back. Keep the hook sharp enough to grip. Clean off mud and pitch, and keep the case out of the dirt when you set it down. If the tape blade breaks or wears out, replace it with a refill instead of buying a new case. Confirm the refill fits your model first. Browse the rest of our <a href="/jobsite/logging-tools/forestry-tools/">forestry tools</a> for scaling sticks and other measuring gear.',
            1 => 'Store the tape in a dry spot when you are done for the day. A case left in a puddle in the truck bed can rust inside, and a rusty spring does not rewind well.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do you use a loggers tape?',
          'a' => 'Set the hook in the end of the log, walk along the log as the tape pays out, read the length you want and mark the cut. Flick the tape to free the hook and let it rewind.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a Spencer tape worth it over a cheap tape measure?',
          'a' => 'For bucking logs alone, yes. The hook and belt-mount case let one person measure without help.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I get a refill for my Spencer tape?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Spencer sells refills in several lengths. Match the refill to your case model.',
        ),
      ),
