    '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-395xp-chainsaw/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-395xp-chainsaw/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna 395XP Chainsaw',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'chainsaw',
      'price' => 1399,
      'keyword' => 'husqvarna 395xp',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna 395XP Chainsaw: Specs & Buying Guide',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna 395XP Chainsaw',
      'meta' => 'Husqvarna 395XP chainsaw specs, best bar lengths, and how this 93.6 cc saw performs on big removals, storm work and large hardwood. Read before buying.',
      'tagline' => 'A 90 cc class saw for big removals, large hardwood and storm work.',
      'summary' => 'The Husqvarna 395XP is a 93.6 cc pro chainsaw built for big wood, from large removals to storm cleanup on oversized trunks. It has the torque to pull long bars in dense hardwood while staying simple and durable enough for daily crew use.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '93.6 cc engine with heavy torque for long bars',
        1 => 'Built for large-diameter removals and storm work',
        2 => 'Decompression valve for easier starting',
        3 => 'Durable design with a long service record',
        4 => 'A step up from the 70 cc class without the bulk of the 3120XP',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Displacement',
          1 => '93.6 cc',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Power',
          1 => 'about 6.6 hp (4.9 kW)',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Weight',
          1 => 'about 17.4 lb (power head)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Chain pitch',
          1 => '3/8 in',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Starting',
          1 => 'Decompression valve',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fuel',
          1 => '50:1 gas and two-stroke oil mix',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'When you need a 90 cc saw',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Most crews carry a 50 cc saw for limbing and a 70 cc saw for bucking. The Husqvarna 395 XP comes off the truck when the wood gets big: a 40-inch oak butt, a storm-split sycamore, a stump that has to come down to grade. With 93.6 cc and around 6.6 hp, the 395xp keeps a long bar moving where a 70 cc saw starts to bog.',
            1 => 'It is a big saw, so it is a ground saw. You will not carry it far into the woods all day, and you should not. Park it near the work, bring it in for the big cuts, and let the smaller saw handle the rest.',
            2 => 'Storm crews especially value a saw like this. After a big wind event, the work is often huge trees across roads and houses, and the ability to make a single clean cut through a large trunk saves time and reduces risk. A smaller saw forces you into multiple cuts from both sides, which takes longer and can put you in awkward positions.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Bar and chain setup',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The 395XP is commonly run with bars from 24 to 36 inches. A 28 or 32-inch bar is a good all-round choice for big removal work. Use a 3/8 in pitch chain that matches your bar and sprocket, and keep spare loops sharp and ready. On big hardwood, a sharp chain makes more difference than any other factor.',
            1 => 'Check bar oil output when you install a long bar, and keep the oil tank topped. Long bars use more oil, and a dry bar wears out fast.',
            2 => 'If you cut a lot of dirty wood, such as storm debris with grit in the bark, carry a spare chain or two. A big saw on a long bar dulls quickly in dirty wood, and swapping a chain is faster than filing a badly dulled one in the field. Keep a bar tool, a file kit and spare plugs in the saw box.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Durability and service',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The chainsaw Husqvarna 395xp has a reputation for lasting. It is a manually tuned saw with a straightforward design, which makes it easy for a shop to keep running. Keep the air filter clean, use fresh 50:1 fuel, and replace the spark plug and fuel filter on schedule. Inspect the anti-vibration mounts and the chain brake regularly.',
            1 => 'When the saw starts to feel weak, look at the simple things first: the chain, the air filter, the spark plug and the fuel. A fresh plug and filter fix a surprising number of problems.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safety with big bars',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A long bar on a big saw has a large kickback zone and a lot of chain speed. Wear chaps or chainsaw pants, a helmet with face screen and hearing protection, gloves and cut-resistant boots. Plan cuts on large wood carefully, watch for tension and compression, and keep the ground crew out of the roll zone. Follow ANSI Z133 on tree jobs, and make sure every operator is trained. If you need even more saw, look at the <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-3120xp-chainsaw-power-head-only/">Husqvarna 3120XP</a>.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the displacement of the Husqvarna 395XP?',
          'a' => 'The 395XP has a 93.6 cc engine producing about 6.6 hp.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What bar length should I run on a 395XP?',
          'a' => 'Bars from 24 to 36 in are common. A 28 or 32 in bar suits most big removal work.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the 395XP too big for everyday tree work?',
          'a' => 'For limbing and most bucking, yes. It is best as the big-wood saw on the truck, alongside a 50 cc and 70 cc saw.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How heavy is the 395XP?',
          'a' => 'About 17.4 lb for the power head, before the bar and chain.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'felling',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/chainsaw-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/chainsaw-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'New York National Guard',
        'creator' => 'The National Guard',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/33252741@N08',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/33252741@N08/40253967023',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-450-rancher/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-450-rancher/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna 450 Rancher Chainsaw',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'chainsaw',
      'price' => 429,
      'keyword' => 'husqvarna 450 rancher',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna 450 Rancher Chainsaw: Specs & Buying Guide',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna 450 Rancher Chainsaw',
      'meta' => 'Husqvarna 450 Rancher chainsaw specs, bar sizes, real use on firewood and storm cleanup, and how it compares with pro saws. Read before you buy one.',
      'tagline' => 'A 50 cc all-round saw for firewood, storm cleanup and property work.',
      'summary' => 'The Husqvarna 450 Rancher is a 50.2 cc all-round chainsaw for landowners, farmers and part-time cutters who need more than a homeowner saw. It runs an 18 or 20-inch bar and handles firewood, storm cleanup and small felling without the price of a pro model.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '50.2 cc X-Torq engine for firewood and property work',
        1 => 'Commonly sold with an 18 or 20 in bar',
        2 => 'Smart Start and air purge for easier starting',
        3 => 'Air Injection pre-cleaning to extend air filter life',
        4 => 'Good value step up from homeowner saws',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Displacement',
          1 => '50.2 cc',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Power',
          1 => 'about 3.2 hp (2.4 kW)',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Weight',
          1 => 'about 11.3 lb (power head)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Bar length',
          1 => '18 or 20 in (as sold)',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Chain pitch',
          1 => '0.325 in',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fuel',
          1 => '50:1 gas and two-stroke oil mix',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Who the 450 Rancher is for',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The Husqvarna 450 Rancher chainsaw sits between homeowner saws and the pro XP line. It is built for people who cut regularly but not every day: landowners clearing fence lines, farmers keeping up with blowdowns, and anyone who heats with wood. If that sounds like you, the Husqvarna 450 gives you enough power to cut firewood all afternoon without the cost of a pro saw.',
            1 => 'Tree crews usually run lighter, faster pro saws for daily work, but a Rancher 450 is a common pick for a second saw on the farm truck or for storm cleanup at home.',
            2 => 'It is also a good choice for someone who wants a saw that will grow with them. The 50 cc size handles most property tasks, and the husqvarna rancher 450 is easy to find parts for at almost any dealer.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Performance in real use',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The 50.2 cc X-Torq engine pulls an 18 or 20-inch bar well in softwood and medium hardwood. Bucking 12 to 16-inch logs into firewood rounds is its sweet spot. It will cut bigger wood, but it takes patience, and that is where a 60 or 70 cc saw earns its price.',
            1 => 'Starting is easy for a saw this size thanks to Smart Start and an air purge primer. Keep a sharp chain, clean the air filter often, and use fresh fuel. Most starting and running problems with Rancher saws trace back to old fuel or a clogged filter.',
            2 => 'Match chain to bar. Most 450 Rancher saws run 0.325 in pitch chain. Count the drive links or check the stamp on the bar before ordering a replacement. A spare sharp chain in the truck turns a slow afternoon into a quick one. Keep the chain tension snug but not tight, and check it after the first few cuts on a new chain, since new chains stretch.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => '450 Rancher versus a pro saw',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Compared to the <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-550xp-mark-ii-chainsaw/">Husqvarna 550XP Mark II</a>, the 450 Rancher is heavier for its power and has a more basic build. The 550XP revs higher, cuts faster and is built for daily professional use. If you cut a few weekends a year, the Rancher is the better value. If you cut for a living, buy the pro saw.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Wear chaps, eye and hearing protection, gloves and sturdy boots, and use the chain brake when you walk with the saw. Learn safe felling and bucking before you take on leaning or storm-damaged trees. Trees under tension from storms are some of the most dangerous wood to cut. When in doubt, call a trained tree crew.',
            1 => 'Before each use, check the chain brake, the chain catcher and the throttle lockout. These safety features only help if they work.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What bar comes on the Husqvarna 450 Rancher?',
          'a' => 'It is usually sold with an 18 or 20 in bar. A 20 in bar is a popular choice for firewood.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the 450 Rancher a professional saw?',
          'a' => 'It is a heavy-duty saw for regular use, but it is not part of Husqvarna\'s XP pro line. Daily professional users usually choose an XP model.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the displacement of the Husqvarna 450?',
          'a' => 'The 450 Rancher has a 50.2 cc engine rated at about 3.2 hp.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What fuel does the 450 Rancher use?',
          'a' => 'A 50:1 mix of unleaded gasoline and two-stroke oil. Fresh or pre-mixed fuel helps it start and run well.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'felling',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/chainsaw-5.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/chainsaw-5-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Stumpo getting cut',
        'creator' => 'Sam Beebe',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/28585409@N04',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/28585409@N04/5694764170',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-525pt5s-gas-powered-pole-saw/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-525pt5s-gas-powered-pole-saw/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna 525PT5S Gas Pole Saw',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'pole-saw',
      'price' => 799,
      'keyword' => 'husqvarna pole saw',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna Pole Saw 525PT5S: Gas Telescoping Specs',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna Pole Saw 525PT5S (Gas, Telescoping)',
      'meta' => 'Husqvarna pole saw 525PT5S: telescoping gas pole saw for pruning from the ground. Specs, reach, real crew use and safety tips before you buy.',
      'tagline' => 'Telescoping gas pole saw for pruning and clearance work from the ground.',
      'summary' => 'The Husqvarna pole saw 525PT5S is a professional gas pole saw with a telescoping shaft, built for pruning and clearance work from the ground. A 25.4 cc X-Torq engine drives a 12-inch bar at the end of the shaft, so you can take limbs over driveways and roofs without a ladder.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '25.4 cc X-Torq engine',
        1 => 'Telescoping shaft for extra reach',
        2 => '12 in bar and chain at the cutting head',
        3 => 'Harness-ready for long sessions',
        4 => 'Pro-grade build for daily clearance and pruning',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Displacement',
          1 => '25.4 cc',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Bar length',
          1 => '12 in',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Shaft',
          1 => 'Telescoping',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Engine',
          1 => 'X-Torq two-stroke',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fuel',
          1 => '50:1 gas and two-stroke oil mix',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What a gas pole saw does for a crew',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A lot of tree work is not climbing at all. It is raising canopies over driveways, clearing limbs off roofs, and opening up sight lines along roads and fences. A good gas chainsaw on a pole lets one person do that work from the ground, quickly and without a ladder. The Husqvarna 525PT5S is Husqvarna\'s professional pole saw for that job.',
            1 => 'The telescoping shaft is the key feature. You can collapse it to work low limbs or to load it on the truck, then extend it for high cuts. That means one tool covers a wide range of heights instead of carrying two fixed-length saws.',
            2 => 'For crews, the husqvarna gas pole saw is a quick way to finish up pruning after a climber comes down, or to take small deadwood without setting a line. For property managers and municipal crews, it handles clearance pruning over sidewalks and roads all day.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Engine and cutting head',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The 25.4 cc X-Torq engine sits at the bottom of the shaft, where it balances the weight of the cutting head. It drives a 12-inch bar and chain through the shaft. For pruning cuts up to a few inches thick, that is plenty. On bigger limbs, make a small undercut first so the bark does not tear, and let the saw cut at its own speed.',
            1 => 'Keep the chain sharp, check the bar oil level every time you refuel, and grease the cutting head per the manual. A dry gearbox or a dull chain will make the saw feel weak long before the engine is the problem.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Using it safely',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Never use a pole saw near power lines. Keep at least the minimum approach distance required by OSHA and ANSI Z133, and assume all lines are energized. Only qualified line-clearance workers should work near electrical conductors.',
            1 => 'Stand to the side of the limb, not under it, and wear a helmet, eye protection, hearing protection and gloves. Watch for limbs that can bounce off the shaft or swing back toward you. Use the harness for long sessions to save your back and keep better control.',
            2 => 'Take frequent breaks on long pruning days. Holding a pole saw overhead is tiring work, and fatigue is when mistakes happen. Clear the drop zone before every cut and let helpers know where limbs will fall.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Is it the right pole saw',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'If you do regular pruning or clearance work and want a gas pole saw that holds up to daily use, the 525PT5S is a strong choice. For occasional homeowner use, a lighter or battery pole saw may be easier. For cuts out of reach of any pole, you will need a climber or a bucket. Compare more options in our <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/">chainsaws</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What engine does the Husqvarna 525PT5S use?',
          'a' => 'It uses a 25.4 cc X-Torq two-stroke engine running on 50:1 fuel mix.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What bar length is on the 525PT5S?',
          'a' => 'It has a 12 in bar and chain at the cutting head.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a pole saw near power lines?',
          'a' => 'No. Keep well clear of power lines. Only qualified line-clearance arborists may work near energized conductors, and they follow strict approach distances.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does the shaft telescope?',
          'a' => 'Yes. The telescoping shaft lets you collapse it for low cuts and transport and extend it for higher limbs.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'pruning',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pole-saw-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pole-saw-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Project 365 #211: 300718 A Cut Above',
        'creator' => 'comedy_nose',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/23408922@N07',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/23408922@N07/42842605715',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-550xp-mark-ii-chainsaw/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-550xp-mark-ii-chainsaw/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna 550XP Mark II Chainsaw',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'chainsaw',
      'price' => 779,
      'keyword' => 'husqvarna 550xp',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna 550XP Mark II Chainsaw: Specs & Guide',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna 550XP Mark II Chainsaw',
      'meta' => 'Husqvarna 550XP Mark II specs, bar sizes and how this 50 cc AutoTune pro saw handles limbing, bucking and small removals. Read this before you buy.',
      'tagline' => 'A light, fast 50 cc pro saw for limbing, bucking and small removals.',
      'summary' => 'The Husqvarna 550XP Mark II is a 50.1 cc professional chainsaw that many tree crews use as their everyday ground saw for limbing, bucking and small removals. It is light, revs fast, and uses AutoTune to keep the carburetor set correctly as conditions change.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '50.1 cc engine with a high-revving, fast-cutting feel',
        1 => 'AutoTune adjusts the carburetor automatically',
        2 => 'Light power head for long days of limbing and brush work',
        3 => 'Runs bars from about 13 to 20 in',
        4 => 'Durable Mark II updates over the original 550XP',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Displacement',
          1 => '50.1 cc',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Power',
          1 => 'about 4.0 hp (3.0 kW)',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Weight',
          1 => 'about 11.7 lb (power head)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Recommended bar',
          1 => '13 to 20 in',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Chain pitch',
          1 => '0.325 in',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Carburetor',
          1 => 'AutoTune',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'The everyday ground saw',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On most tree jobs, the saw that runs the most hours is not the biggest one. It is the 50 cc saw that limbs out the brush, cuts small wood for the chipper and bucks the rounds the bigger saw left behind. The Husqvarna 550 XP Mark II is built for exactly that work. It is light enough to carry around the job all day, and it revs fast enough that small cuts take a second or two.',
            1 => 'Many climbers also choose the husqvarna 550xp chainsaw as the saw they send up on a line for bigger cuts aloft, where a top-handle saw runs out of bar. It handles well, balances nicely and does not wear you out.',
            2 => 'On brush-heavy jobs it is often the saw that never gets turned off. One person feeds the chipper while another works through the piles with the 550, cutting long limbs down to lengths that feed cleanly. Light weight matters most in that role.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What the Mark II changed',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The Mark II version updated the original 550XP with improvements aimed at durability and reliability in daily professional use. It keeps the features that made the first one popular: a compact power head, a fast chain speed and AutoTune.',
            1 => 'AutoTune is Husqvarna\'s system for adjusting the carburetor automatically. It compensates for fuel quality, altitude, temperature and the condition of the air filter, so the saw stays close to its best tune without you reaching for a screwdriver. That is useful on crews where several people share saws and nobody wants to spend time tuning.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Bar and chain setup',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The 550XP runs bars from about 13 to 20 inches. A 16-inch bar is the most popular choice for limbing and brush work because it is quick and light. An 18 or 20-inch bar adds reach for bucking and small felling. Use 0.325 in pitch chain that matches the gauge and drive link count stamped on your bar.',
            1 => 'Keep it sharp. A high-revving saw like the 550XP really shows the difference between a fresh chain and a tired one. Touch up the cutters at every fuel stop and the saw will keep cutting fast all day. Clean the air filter regularly and replace the spark plug and fuel filter on schedule.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Who should buy it and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Buy the 550XP Mark II if you need a reliable, light, pro-grade saw for daily limbing, bucking and small removals. For heavier bucking or big wood, pair it with a 70 cc saw such as the <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-572xp-chainsaw/">Husqvarna 572XP</a>. If you want a saw for occasional property work, the <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/chainsaws/husqvarna-450-rancher/">450 Rancher</a> is less expensive.',
            1 => 'Wear chainsaw chaps or pants, a helmet with face and hearing protection, gloves and cut-resistant boots. Use the chain brake when moving between cuts. If you send this saw aloft, use a proper saw lanyard and follow ANSI Z133 and your employer\'s rules for rear-handle saw use in the tree. Only trained operators should run a chainsaw at height.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the displacement of the Husqvarna 550XP?',
          'a' => 'The 550XP Mark II has a 50.1 cc engine producing about 4.0 hp.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What bar length is best on a 550XP?',
          'a' => 'A 16 in bar is the most popular for limbing and brush. An 18 or 20 in bar adds reach for bucking and small felling.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is AutoTune?',
          'a' => 'AutoTune is Husqvarna\'s automatic carburetor adjustment. It compensates for fuel, altitude, temperature and air filter condition so the saw stays well tuned.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the 550XP good for a beginner?',
          'a' => 'It is easy to handle, but it is a fast professional saw. New users should get proper training and start with simple ground cuts.',
        ),
      ),
