      'price' => 7,
      'keyword' => '3/8 eye bolt',
      'title' => '3/8 Eye Bolt for Tree Cabling Hardware',
      'h1' => '3/8 Eye Bolt for Tree Cabling',
      'meta' => '3/8 eye bolt for tree cabling systems. Where a 3/8 in eye bolt fits, how it pairs with cable and thimbles, and installation and inspection tips.',
      'tagline' => 'A 3/8 in eye bolt for anchoring light cabling systems in trees.',
      'summary' => 'This 3/8 eye bolt is an anchor point for light tree cabling systems, installed through a limb with a washer and nut so a cable can be attached to the eye. It suits smaller limbs and lighter systems where the design calls for 3/8 in hardware.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '3/8 in diameter eye bolt',
        1 => 'Through-bolt anchor for light cabling systems',
        2 => 'Pairs with washers, nuts and thimbles',
        3 => 'For smaller limbs and lighter system designs',
        4 => 'Part of a complete cabling and bracing kit',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '3/8 in',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Eye bolt, threaded shank',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Installation',
          1 => 'Through-bolted with washer and nut',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Anchor point for tree cabling',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Length',
          1 => 'See listing',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where a 3/8 eye bolt fits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In a steel cabling system, the cable has to attach to the tree at each end. Eye bolts are one of the most common anchors. The bolt goes through a drilled hole in the limb, with a washer and nut on the far side, and the cable attaches to the eye. That spreads the load through the wood and keeps the cable from girdling the limb.',
            1 => 'A 3/8 eye bolt is on the lighter end of cabling hardware. It suits smaller limbs and systems where the design calls for 3/8 in hardware. Larger stems and heavier loads call for bigger hardware, so let the system design, not the price, decide the size.',
            2 => 'People searching for 3/8 eye bolts are often matching an existing system or replacing worn hardware. When you replace hardware in an existing system, match or exceed the original specification and check the rest of the system while you are in the tree.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Pairing with cable and thimbles',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'When the cable attaches to the eye, a thimble protects the cable from bending too sharply and wearing against the eye. The cable is then terminated with a dead-end grip or other approved method. All parts, including the eye bolt, cable, thimble and termination, need to be rated and sized to work together.',
            1 => 'Close or weld the eye as the system design and manufacturer require, so the cable cannot slip out. Use washers sized for the bolt, and do not countersink nuts so deeply that the wood around them is weakened.',
            2 => 'Keep a supply of matching washers, nuts and thimbles with your eye bolts so you are never tempted to substitute mismatched hardware in the tree. Store hardware dry to limit corrosion before it is installed.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Installation',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Tree support systems should be designed and installed by a qualified arborist following ANSI A300 (Part 3) and ANSI Z133, and inspected regularly once installed.',
            1 => 'Drill straight, correctly sized holes, aligned with the direction of the cable so the bolt is loaded in line. Avoid drilling through decay or cracks. Tighten the nut so the washer seats firmly against the bark without crushing it. Record the installation and plan regular inspections.',
            2 => 'Wear gloves and eye protection when drilling, and use climbing and positioning gear appropriate for work aloft. Drilling and installing hardware while tied in requires the same care as any other work at height.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and related hardware',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Inspect cabling systems regularly. Look for wear on the eye and thimble, corrosion, cracks in the wood around the bolt, wood growing over the hardware and changes in cable tension. Replace worn or damaged hardware promptly.',
            1 => 'Pair this eye bolt with <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cable/ehs-cable/">EHS cable</a> and browse more <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/">cabling hardware</a> to complete your system.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a 3/8 eye bolt used for in tree cabling?',
          'a' => 'It is a through-bolt anchor for lighter cabling systems. The cable attaches to the eye with a thimble and approved termination.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a 3/8 in eye bolt strong enough for my tree?',
          'a' => 'It depends on the limb size and system design. A qualified arborist should size hardware following ANSI A300 Part 3.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need a thimble with an eye bolt?',
          'a' => 'Yes, a thimble protects the cable where it wraps through the eye and reduces wear.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-5.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-5-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Eye bolt snow bokeh',
        'creator' => 'dsgetch',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/66136677@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/66136677@N00/51782009502',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/galvanized-eye-lag-screw/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/galvanized-eye-lag-screw/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/',
      'name' => 'Galvanized Eye Lag Screw',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 12,
      'keyword' => 'eye lag screw',
      'title' => 'Galvanized Eye Lag Screw for Tree Cabling',
      'h1' => 'Galvanized Eye Lag Screw',
      'meta' => 'Galvanized eye lag screw for light tree cabling and guying. How lag eyes anchor a cable, when to use a through-bolt instead, and how to install them cleanly.',
      'tagline' => 'A galvanized lag eye for anchoring light cable systems in small to medium limbs.',
      'summary' => 'This galvanized eye lag screw threads straight into sound wood and gives you a closed eye to terminate a cable on. It is the simple anchor for light cabling jobs in smaller leads where a through-bolt would be overkill. The galvanized finish holds up to weather while the tree closes wood around the shank.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Closed eye for terminating cable with a thimble or dead-end',
        1 => 'Lag (wood screw) thread bites into sound wood',
        2 => 'Galvanized steel resists rust in the canopy',
        3 => 'Installs from one side with a pilot hole and a bar through the eye',
        4 => 'Suited to light cabling in smaller-diameter leads',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Eye lag screw (lag eye)',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Galvanized steel',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Thread',
          1 => 'Lag thread, single-side install',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Eye',
          1 => 'Closed eye',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Typical use',
          1 => 'Light-duty cable anchors in small to medium leads',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Standard',
          1 => 'Install per ANSI A300 Part 3 and the hardware maker\'s instructions',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What an eye lag screw does in a cable system',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A cable system is only as good as its anchors. The galvanized eye lag screw is the lightest anchor in the cabling kit: you drill a pilot hole, thread the lag into sound wood until the eye sits just proud of the bark, and terminate your cable on the eye with a thimble or a preformed dead-end.',
            1 => 'Because it goes in from one side, a lag eye is quick to set from a climbing position or out of a bucket. That makes it a good fit for light cabling in younger trees and smaller codominant leads, where you want support without drilling all the way through the stem.',
            2 => 'Browse the rest of the anchors, cable and dead-ends in our <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/">cabling hardware</a> section.',
            3 => 'Lag eyes are also handy beyond cabling. Crews use them to anchor guying on transplanted trees, to hang a light support for a leaning ornamental, or as a fixed point for a brace on a fence line tree. In each case the logic is the same: sound wood, a straight pilot hole, and a load that pulls in line with the shank.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Lag eye or through-bolt',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Lags rely on thread engagement in the wood, so they are rated lower than a through-bolt with a washer and nut on the far side. On bigger leads, heavier loads, or wood with any question about soundness, step up to a through-bolt or a J-lag sized for the job.',
            1 => 'Read the load ratings published by the hardware maker and match the anchor to the cable you are installing. The current reference for cabling and bracing in the US is ANSI A300 Part 3. If you are new to cabling, learn it from someone who installs systems regularly before you put a lag in a client\'s tree.',
            2 => 'Think about the whole system, not just the anchor. Cable, termination and anchor should all be sized to work together, and the weakest piece sets the real capacity. A heavy cable on a light lag gives you a false sense of security.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Installation tips from the field',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Drill a pilot hole sized to the lag\'s root diameter, in line with the direction the cable will pull. A lag set at an angle to the cable gets side-loaded at the eye. Thread it in with a bar through the eye and stop when the eye is just clear of the bark so the tree can grow without engulfing the termination too soon.',
            1 => 'Inspect the tree before you drill. Do not set anchors in decay, cracks or included bark. Plan to re-inspect the system on a regular schedule, because trees grow, cables loosen, and hardware that was right five years ago may need adjustment.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Working kit and records',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Keep a small kit together for cabling days: a selection of lag eyes, a long auger bit sized for the pilot, a bar or large screwdriver to turn the eye, thimbles, dead-ends and a tape to measure the leads. Having it all in one bag means fewer trips down the rope.',
            1 => 'Write down what you installed, where, and when. A simple record on the work order lets the next inspection check that the anchor, cable and tension are still doing their job, and gives the client a reason to have the system looked at on schedule.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What size eye lag screw should I use for tree cabling?',
          'a' => 'Size the lag to the cable and the lead. Lags are for light systems in smaller leads. Check the maker\'s ratings and ANSI A300 Part 3, and move to a through-bolt when the load or limb size calls for it.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need a pilot hole for a lag eye?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Drill a pilot hole close to the root diameter of the thread so the lag pulls in straight without splitting the wood or stripping out.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How far should the eye stick out from the bark?',
          'a' => 'Leave the eye just clear of the bark. Too far out adds leverage on the shank; flush means the tree swallows the termination quickly.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a hardware-store eye screw instead?',
          'a' => 'Use hardware sold for tree support with published ratings. Light-duty eye screws with bent or open eyes are not made for the loads a cable system sees.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
        1 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Eye Bolt - Basil Watson\'s Sopwith-Type Biplane, Melbourne, Victoria, 1916',
        'creator' => 'Photographer: David Thompson',
        'creator_url' => '',
        'license' => 'CC BY 4.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://collections.museumsvictoria.com.au/items/393624',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/j-lag-screws/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/j-lag-screws/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/',
      'name' => 'J-Lag Screws',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 14,
      'keyword' => 'j-lag screw',
      'title' => 'J-Lag Screws for Tree Cabling: Sizes and Install Tips',
      'h1' => 'J-Lag Screws for Tree Cabling',
      'meta' => 'J-lag screws give a cable system a hook-style anchor set into the lead. How they compare with eye lags, lag studs and through-bolts, and how to set them.',
      'tagline' => 'Hook-style lag anchors for cabling leads with a cable that wraps the J.',
      'summary' => 'J-lag screws are threaded tree cabling anchors with an open J-shaped hook in place of a closed eye. You drill a pilot hole, thread the J-lag into sound wood, and seat the cable termination over the hook. Arborists like them because the cable can be hooked on after the anchor is set.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Open J hook lets you seat the termination after the anchor is in',
        1 => 'Lag thread installs from one side of the lead',
        2 => 'Galvanized finish for long service outdoors',
        3 => 'Pairs with thimbles and preformed dead-ends',
        4 => 'Faster to set than a through-bolt on light systems',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'J-lag (hook) screw',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Galvanized steel',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Thread',
          1 => 'Lag thread',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Head',
          1 => 'Open J-shaped hook',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Install',
          1 => 'Pilot hole, single side',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Standard',
          1 => 'Install per ANSI A300 Part 3 and maker\'s instructions',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How J-lags fit into a cabling job',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On a typical cabling job you set one anchor in each lead, then run cable between them about two-thirds of the way from the weak union to the tops of the leads. A J-lag gives you a hook to drop the thimbled cable end onto, which makes tensioning and final termination easier when you are working alone in the canopy.',
            1 => 'Because it installs from one side, a J-lag saves you drilling through the whole lead. That is a real advantage on light systems in younger trees, where you want to keep wounding to a minimum.',
            2 => 'J-lags also suit retrofit work. When an existing system needs a second cable or a replacement run, a hook anchor lets you set hardware, then bring the new cable to it and hang the termination without threading anything through a closed eye.',
            3 => 'Keep the hardware matched: J-lag, thimble, dead-end and cable from systems that are meant to work together, with published ratings for each part.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'J-lag, eye lag or lag stud',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A closed <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/galvanized-eye-lag-screw/">eye lag screw</a> holds the termination captive. A J-lag lets you hook on after the fact, but the termination must stay seated against the hook. A lag stud, also called a lag-threaded rod, has lag thread on one end and machine thread on the other so you can add an amon-eye nut. All three rely on thread grip in the wood.',
            1 => 'None of them match a through-bolt with a nut and washer on the far side for holding power. Choose the anchor by the published rating, the size of the lead and the load you expect, and follow ANSI A300 Part 3.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Setting a J-lag cleanly',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Drill the pilot hole in line with the cable so the hook pulls straight. Thread the J-lag in until the hook sits just clear of the bark, with the open side facing away from the cable\'s pull so the termination cannot slip off.',
            1 => 'Inspect the wood before drilling and stay out of decay, cracks and included bark. Re-inspect cable systems on a schedule; growth and storms change what the system is doing. If you are learning cabling, get supervised experience first.',
            2 => 'Clean up after yourself: brush shavings out of the hole before threading, and keep the work order notes clear on which lead got which anchor.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection over the life of the system',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Every cable system needs follow-up. Leads grow, cables slacken or tighten, and storms can shift a termination off a hook if it was not seated properly. Put the tree on an inspection schedule and note the hardware type and install date on the work order.',
            1 => 'On a re-inspection, look for the hook opening, any wear where the thimble rides, wood swelling over the J, and cracks or decay near the anchor. If the tree has outgrown a light system, replace it with heavier hardware rather than adding more of the same.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Which way should the hook of a J-lag face?',
          'a' => 'Orient it so the cable pulls the termination into the bottom of the J, with the opening away from the line of pull.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Are J-lags as strong as through-bolts?',
          'a' => 'No. Lag anchors depend on thread grip in wood. Use through-bolts for heavier loads or larger leads.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a lag stud?',
          'a' => 'A rod with lag thread on one end and machine thread on the other. It takes an amon-eye nut, giving you an adjustable eye on a lag anchor.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do J-lags need a pilot hole?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Drill a pilot close to the thread\'s root diameter so the lag goes in straight and grips well.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
        1 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Eye Bolt',
        'creator' => 'Clearly Ambiguous',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/84617037@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/84617037@N00/428612624',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/plant-health-care/lac-balsam-tree-wound-treatment/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/plant-health-care/lac-balsam-tree-wound-treatment/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/',
      'name' => 'Lac Balsam Tree Wound Treatment',
      'brand' => 'lac-balsam',
      'brand_name' => 'Lac Balsam',
      'kind' => 'fluid',
      'price' => 30,
      'keyword' => 'lac balsam',
      'title' => 'Lac Balsam Tree Wound Treatment: When It Makes Sense',
      'h1' => 'Lac Balsam Tree Wound Treatment',
      'meta' => 'Lac Balsam tree wound dressing is a water-based, brush-on coating for bark wounds. Where it is useful, where it is not, and what current pruning standards say.',
      'tagline' => 'A brush-on, water-based tree wound dressing for bark damage and grafts.',
      'summary' => 'Lac Balsam is a water-based tree wound dressing that you brush onto damaged bark, where it dries to a flexible film. Arborists and orchard growers use it on bark tears, mower damage and graft unions rather than on routine pruning cuts.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Water-based, cleans up with water',
        1 => 'Brushes on and dries to a flexible coating',
        2 => 'Used on bark tears, equipment damage and grafts',
        3 => 'Not needed on routine, well-made pruning cuts',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Tree wound dressing',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Base',
          1 => 'Water-based',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Application',
          1 => 'Brush or spatula',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Cleanup',
          1 => 'Water while wet',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where Lac Balsam fits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Most current guidance, including ANSI A300, says wound dressings are not needed on ordinary pruning cuts. A good cut at the branch collar closes on its own. Lac Balsam earns its place on the jobs where you want to protect exposed cambium: a strip of bark torn off by a mower or a lowering mishap, a fresh graft, or a wound on a young tree you are trying to keep moist.',
            1 => 'It is a niche product. If a client asks for wound paint on every cut, explain the standard and save the dressing for the cases above.',
            2 => 'Some growers also like it on grafts, where it helps keep the union from drying out while the scion takes. On young street trees damaged during planting or by string trimmers, a coat can protect the wound while you sort out the cause, usually by mulching a ring so the trimmer stays away.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Applying it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Trim loose, ragged bark back to clean, attached tissue first. Brush a thin layer over the exposed wood and the wound edges, then let it dry. Check the product label for temperature limits and reapplication. Wash brushes in water before it sets.',
            1 => 'A tub goes a long way since you only coat damaged areas. Keep the lid sealed between uses so it does not skin over, and store it where it will not freeze. If a wound keeps getting reopened, fix the cause; no dressing replaces good care.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is Lac Balsam the same as Lak Balsam?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Lak Balsam is a common misspelling of Lac Balsam, the same water-based wound dressing.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Should I put Lac Balsam on every pruning cut?',
          'a' => 'No. Standard practice is to leave proper pruning cuts undressed. Use it on bark damage, grafts and similar wounds.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/fluid-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/fluid-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Gas Can Storage',
        'creator' => 'Hugo-90',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/32109282@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/32109282@N00/49732866582',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/planting/tree-support-web-for-staking-trees-250/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/planting/tree-support-web-for-staking-trees-250/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/planting/',
      'name' => 'Tree Support Web for Staking Trees, 250 ft',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 45,
      'keyword' => 'tree support',
      'title' => 'Tree Support Web for Staking Trees, 250 ft Roll',
      'h1' => 'Tree Support Web for Staking Trees',
      'meta' => 'Soft tree support web for staking newly planted trees. Wide webbing holds a tree to its stakes without girdling bark. Tips for stake support and removal timing.',
      'tagline' => 'Soft, wide staking web on a roll for supporting newly planted trees.',
      'summary' => 'This tree support web is a soft strap on a long roll for tying newly planted trees to their stakes. Wide webbing spreads the load on the bark, so the tree gets support without the cutting and girdling you get from wire or narrow twine.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Wide, soft webbing that does not cut into bark',
        1 => 'Long roll for crews planting many trees',
        2 => 'Cut to length for each tree and stake',
        3 => 'Allows some trunk movement, which builds taper',
        4 => 'Easy to remove when the stakes come out',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Tree staking web',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Length',
          1 => '250 ft roll',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Tie trees to stakes after planting',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Install',
          1 => 'Loose figure-eight or loop around trunk and stake',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Removal',
          1 => 'Typically after one growing season',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why use a web instead of wire',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A newly planted tree with a small root ball can rock in the wind before the roots grab the surrounding soil. Staking gives it a little help. The tie is where most staking goes wrong: wire, even inside a hose section, and thin twine can cut into the bark and girdle the trunk if they are forgotten.',
            1 => 'A wide tree support web spreads the pressure over a larger patch of bark. It holds the trunk steady without clamping it. On a planting crew, a 250 ft roll goes a long way, since each tie only needs a short length.',
            2 => 'The web is also kinder to the crew. It is easy to cut, quick to tie, and soft enough that nobody is fighting stiff wire with pliers on a planting day.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Setting up stake support for trees',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Drive two or three stakes outside the root ball, not through it. Attach the web to each stake and loop it around the trunk loosely, low enough that the top of the tree can still sway. Some movement is good; it is what tells the trunk to add taper and strength.',
            1 => 'Tie a loose figure-eight between trunk and stake so the web does not rub the bark. Keep the attachment at the stake tight and the loop at the trunk relaxed. For larger trees on windy sites, guying with cable and earth anchors may be a better choice; see our <a href="/plant-care/planting/">planting supplies</a>.',
            2 => 'On sites with mowers and trimmers, keep stakes and web where they can be seen. Bright stakes or a mulch ring keep equipment off the trunk while the tree establishes.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Checking and removing ties',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Look at staked trees a few times through the season. Loosen anything that has tightened, and check for rubbing. Most trees can lose their stakes after one growing season once the roots have established. Leaving support on for years does the tree no favors.',
            1 => 'Cut the web off cleanly rather than leaving it wrapped on the trunk. Forgotten ties are one of the most common reasons young street trees fail.',
            2 => 'If bark under the tie has been rubbed raw, move the loop to fresh bark and loosen it. Small marks heal; deep rubbing does not.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Staking as part of good planting',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Staking is a small part of getting a tree established. Planting at the right depth, with the root flare visible, mulched and watered through the first season, does far more for a tree than any tie. Treat the stake and web as a short-term aid, not a fix for a poorly planted tree.',
            1 => 'For trees where you are unsure, try planting without stakes and keep an eye on them after the first few storms. If one starts to lean or rock, add support then. Many trees establish faster when left to move freely from the start.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How long should a tree stay staked?',
          'a' => 'Usually one growing season. Check root establishment and remove the stakes and web as soon as the tree stands on its own.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How tight should a tree support web be?',
          'a' => 'Snug at the stake and loose at the trunk, so the tree can sway a little without rubbing.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does every new tree need staking?',
          'a' => 'No. Many container and balled trees stand fine without it. Stake only when the tree is top-heavy, the site is windy, or people are likely to push on it.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Caution - Tree Surgeon at work',
        'creator' => 'Clint__Budd',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/58827557@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/58827557@N06/47249256141',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/planting/wire-rope-clamps/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/planting/wire-rope-clamps/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/planting/',
      'name' => 'Wire Rope Clamps',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 5,
      'keyword' => 'wire rope clamp',
      'title' => 'Wire Rope Clamps: Sizes, Spacing and Correct Install',
      'h1' => 'Wire Rope Clamps',
      'meta' => 'Galvanized wire rope clamps for tree guying and cable work. How to size a wire rope clip, which side the saddle goes on, and why torque and count matter.',
      'tagline' => 'Galvanized U-bolt wire rope clips for guying trees and making eyes in cable.',
      'summary' => 'Wire rope clamps, also called wire rope clips or cable clamps, let you form an eye or join wire cable without swaging tools. On tree jobs they show up when guying newly planted trees to earth anchors or making temporary cable terminations. They are cheap and simple, but only work when installed the right way around and torqued.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'U-bolt and saddle design, the classic wire rope clip',
        1 => 'Galvanized finish for outdoor service',
        2 => 'Sized to match the cable diameter',
        3 => 'No swaging or special tools, just a wrench',
        4 => 'Used with thimbles to protect the cable eye',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'U-bolt wire rope clip',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Finish',
          1 => 'Galvanized',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sizing',
          1 => 'Match clip size to cable diameter',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Saddle placement',
          1 => 'On the live (load) end',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Count, spacing, torque',
          1 => 'Per maker\'s table for each size',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where wire rope clamps fit on tree jobs',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In the planting world, wire cable clamps are the quick way to terminate guy cable on larger transplanted trees: cable from a collar on the trunk, down to an earth anchor, with a thimbled eye clamped at each end. They also come in handy for temporary cable work around the yard and shop.',
            1 => 'For permanent tree cabling inside the canopy, preformed dead-ends are the usual termination. Pair clamps with <a href="/planting/duck-bill-earth-anchors-small/">duckbill earth anchors</a> when you are guying from the ground.',
            2 => 'Around the shop, clamps also hold a cable on a winch line or make a quick eye on a sling for dragging logs. Keep a few sizes in the truck with matching thimbles.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Never saddle a dead horse',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The U-bolt goes on the dead end, the short tail. The saddle sits on the live end, the side that carries the load. Install a clip backward and the U-bolt crushes the loaded cable and cuts its strength a lot. Every clip on the run goes the same way.',
            1 => 'Use a thimble in the eye so the cable does not kink around a tight bend. Put the first clip one saddle-width from the dead end, the next tight against the thimble, and any others evenly between.',
            2 => 'Seat the cable fully into the thimble groove before tightening the first clip, and keep the thimble from rotating or falling out as you go. A loose thimble lets the eye collapse under load. Tighten the nuts evenly, alternating sides, so the saddle pulls down square on the cable rather than cocking to one side.',
            3 => 'Use the right size. An oversized clip will not grip a small cable, and an undersized one will not close over the larger cable at all.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Count, spacing and torque',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Each size has a minimum number of clips, a turnback length and a torque value published by the maker. Small cables usually need at least two clips and larger ones more. Follow the table that ships with the clamps rather than guessing.',
            1 => 'Torque the nuts, put the cable under load, then retorque. Cable stretches and settles, and loose clips let the cable slip. Check guyed trees through the season and remove guying once the tree has rooted in.',
            2 => 'Keep spare clamps dry and the threads clean. A rusty, galled thread will not reach the right torque, and that means a termination that can slip. Replace any clamp with a bent U-bolt or cracked saddle.',
            3 => 'When you take a guy system down, inspect the cable where the clips sat. Crushed or kinked cable should not go back into service; cut it off and start fresh.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Ratings and overhead work',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A clipped termination does not reach the full strength of the cable. That is fine for guying a tree. Do not use clipped terminations in life-support systems or as a substitute for rated rigging hardware.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between a wire rope clamp and a wire rope clip?',
          'a' => 'Nothing. Wire rope clamp, wire rope clip and cable clamp all describe the same U-bolt and saddle fitting.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Which side does the saddle go on?',
          'a' => 'The saddle goes on the live, load-bearing side of the cable. The U-bolt goes on the dead-end tail.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How many clamps do I need?',
          'a' => 'It depends on cable size. Use the maker\'s table; small cables generally take at least two clips, larger cables more.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need to retighten wire rope clamps?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Retorque after the first load and check them periodically.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Stainless steel eye-bolt',
        'creator' => 'Unknown',
        'creator_url' => '',
        'license' => 'CC0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/6032390/photo-image-public-domain-free-silver',
      ),
    ),
    '/planting/duck-bill-earth-anchors-small/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/planting/duck-bill-earth-anchors-small/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/planting/',
      'name' => 'Duckbill Earth Anchors, Small',
      'brand' => 'duckbill',
      'brand_name' => 'Duckbill',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 39,
      'keyword' => 'duckbill anchors',
      'title' => 'Small Duckbill Anchors for Guying Newly Planted Trees',
      'h1' => 'Small Duckbill Anchors for Tree Guying',
      'meta' => 'Small duckbill anchors for guying newly planted trees. How duckbill earth anchors drive and set, how many to use per tree, and what to check during the season.',
      'tagline' => 'Drive-in earth anchors for guying smaller newly planted trees.',
      'summary' => 'Small duckbill anchors are drive-in earth anchors that let you guy a newly planted tree without wooden stakes. You drive the anchor into the ground with a drive rod, pull back on the cable, and the anchor turns sideways in the soil and locks. They are quick to set and out of the way once installed.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Drive-in installation with a steel drive rod',
        1 => 'Anchor rotates and locks when you pull the cable',
        2 => 'Low profile, nothing for mowers to catch',
        3 => 'Small size for smaller caliper trees',
        4 => 'Use three anchors around the tree for even support',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Drive-in earth anchor',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Size',
          1 => 'Small',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Install',
          1 => 'Drive rod, then load-lock by pulling up',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Typical layout',
          1 => 'Three anchors spaced around the tree',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Holding',
          1 => 'Depends on soil type; see maker\'s chart',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How duckbill earth anchors work',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A duckbill earth anchor looks like a small metal bill with a cable attached. You set it on the end of a drive rod and hammer it into the ground at an angle away from the tree. Pull the rod out, then pull up hard on the cable. The anchor pivots crosswise in the soil, like a toggle bolt in drywall, and resists further pulling.',
            1 => 'That load-lock step matters. An anchor that has not turned holds very little. Pull until you feel it set before attaching the guy to the tree.',
            2 => 'The design is simple and that is the appeal. There are no stakes sticking up for kids or mowers to hit, and the only thing left above ground is the guy cable running to the trunk. On a commercial planting with many trees, a crew can set a set of duckbill earth anchors in minutes per tree.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Guying a newly planted tree',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'For most trees, three duckbill anchors spaced evenly around the root ball give support from every direction. Keep the anchors outside the root ball, and run each guy to a soft collar or strap on the trunk, never bare wire on bark.',
            1 => 'Leave a little slack so the tree can move. Terminate the cable with a thimble and <a href="/plant-care/planting/wire-rope-clamps/">wire rope clamps</a> or the hardware supplied in a duckbill anchor kit.',
            2 => 'Angle the guys so they meet the trunk at roughly one-third to two-thirds of its height, with the anchor far enough out to give a reasonable angle. Flag the guys with tape or covers so people do not walk into them.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Soil, size and limits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Holding power depends on soil. Dense clay holds well; loose sand and wet soil hold less. Small anchors are for small trees. For larger caliper trees, step up to a bigger anchor size and check the maker\'s chart.',
            1 => 'Call before you dig. Driving anchors into buried utility lines is a real hazard, so get locates done first.',
            2 => 'A duckbill anchor kit typically includes the anchors with cable attached and the hardware to terminate them. Check what your kit includes and whether you need a drive rod, which is reusable and worth keeping in the truck.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Removing the guying',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Plan to remove guys after a season or so, once the roots have established. Cut the cable at ground level and leave the anchor in place, or dig it out if the site needs it clear.',
            1 => 'If the tree still moves too much at removal time, check the planting depth and root ball before you add the guys back. Guying cannot fix a tree planted too deep or a root ball that has fallen apart. Note anchor locations on the work order so the next crew knows where the cable runs are, especially near lawns that get aerated.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How deep do small duckbill anchors go?',
          'a' => 'Drive them to the depth the maker specifies for the size, then load-lock. Deeper generally holds better in firm soil.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How many duckbill anchors per tree?',
          'a' => 'Three, spaced evenly around the tree, is the common setup.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I reuse duckbill anchors?',
          'a' => 'They are generally treated as single-use once set. Cut the cable and leave the anchor buried when removing guys.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Eye Bolt - Basil Watson\'s Sopwith-Type Biplane, Melbourne, Victoria, 1916',
        'creator' => 'Photographer: David Thompson',
        'creator_url' => '',
        'license' => 'CC BY 4.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://collections.museumsvictoria.com.au/items/413404',
      ),
    ),
    '/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/distel-replacement-pads-with-rings-and-straps/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/distel-replacement-pads-with-rings-and-straps/',
      'category' => '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/',
      'name' => 'Distel Replacement Pads with Rings and Straps',
      'brand' => 'distel',
      'brand_name' => 'Distel',
      'kind' => 'spur',
      'price' => 79,
      'keyword' => 'distel',
      'title' => 'Distel Replacement Pads with Rings and Straps',
      'h1' => 'Distel Replacement Pads with Rings and Straps',
      'meta' => 'Distel replacement pads with rings and straps refresh a worn set of Distel climbers. When to replace pads and straps, and what to inspect before the next climb.',
      'tagline' => 'Fresh pads, rings and straps to put worn Distel climbers back in service.',
      'summary' => 'Distel replacement pads with rings and straps let you rebuild the upper end of your Distel climbers when the old pads are cracked, compressed or the straps are worn. New pads restore comfort on long removals and keep the spur seated on your leg.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Made to fit Distel tree climbers',
        1 => 'Pads, rings and straps in one set',
        2 => 'Restores comfort and fit on worn spurs',
        3 => 'Cheaper than replacing the whole climber',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Distel tree climbers',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Includes',
          1 => 'Pads, rings and straps',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sold as',
          1 => 'Pair',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'When to replace pads and straps',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pads take a beating on removals. Once the foam is flattened, the leather is cracking, or the straps are stretched, frayed or cut, it is time to rebuild. Worn straps let the spur roll on your leg, and that is how gaffs cut out of the bark.',
            1 => 'Swapping in Distel pads with new rings and straps is a straightforward job at the truck. Confirm the set matches your climber model before you order.',
            2 => 'Replacing pads is also a chance to improve fit. If you have changed boots or the old straps never sat right, take the time to set the new ones up properly, with buckles where they do not dig in and strap tails secured so they do not catch.',
            3 => 'Order the set once you know your model and size so the swap is a one-trip job.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspect the whole climber',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'While the pads are off, look over the shank and the gaff. Check for bends, cracks and loose hardware, and sharpen or replace dull gaffs following Distel\'s guidance. Browse <a href="/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/">replacement gaffs and parts</a> for the rest. Spurs are for removals; climb with a properly rated lanyard and climbing system per ANSI Z133.',
            1 => 'Remember that gaffs only belong in trees that are coming down. Spurs wound live trees, so climb on rope for pruning work.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will these pads fit other brands of climbers?',
          'a' => 'They are made for Distel climbers. Fit on other brands is not assured.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I know my straps need replacing?',
          'a' => 'Replace straps that are cut, frayed, stretched, stiff or have damaged buckles or stitching.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/distel/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/spur-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/spur-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Fall protection field trials',
        'creator' => 'Western Area Power Admin',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05/15137306027',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging-kits/speed-line-kit-with-carabiners/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging-kits/speed-line-kit-with-carabiners/',
      'category' => '/rigging/rigging-kits/',
      'name' => 'Speed Line Kit with Carabiners',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'rope',
      'price' => 260,
      'keyword' => 'speed line kit',
      'title' => 'Arborist Speed Line Kit with Carabiners',
      'h1' => 'Arborist Speed Line Kit with Carabiners',
      'meta' => 'Arborist speed line kit with carabiners for zipping brush and wood out of the tree. How a speedline is rigged, tensioned and anchored, plus safe working limits.',
      'tagline' => 'A speedline setup for zipping brush and wood over obstacles to the drop zone.',
      'summary' => 'This speed line kit gives you the line and connectors to run brush and light wood down a tensioned rope from the canopy to a drop zone away from the tree. It is the setup arborists reach for when the landing area is full of roofs, fences and gardens.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Moves brush away from targets below the tree',
        1 => 'Locking carabiners for clipping loads to the line',
        2 => 'Faster cleanup with brush landing in one pile',
        3 => 'Works with a pulley or carabiners riding the line',
        4 => 'Pairs with a lowering device for tensioning and control',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Speedline kit',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Connectors',
          1 => 'Locking carabiners',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Zip-lining brush and light wood',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Anchoring',
          1 => 'High anchor in tree, low anchor at drop zone',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Tensioning',
          1 => 'Mechanical advantage or lowering device at the low anchor',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What a speedline does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A speedline is a rope run at an angle from a high anchor in the tree to a low anchor on the ground away from the tree. The climber clips cut brush to the line with a carabiner or pulley and it slides down to the landing zone. Ground crew unclip it and drag it to the chipper.',
            1 => 'It turns a tight backyard removal into a steady flow of brush to one spot. Instead of lowering every limb to a crowded drop zone under the tree, you send it over the shed and onto the driveway.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Rigging the line',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Anchor the high end in a sound part of the tree above the work. Anchor the low end to a solid object: another tree, a stump, or the truck if your company allows it. Tension the line with a mechanical advantage system or by taking wraps on a lowering device. Too tight and you overload anchors; too slack and loads drag through the targets.',
            1 => 'Use locking carabiners or a pulley to ride the line. A few loads in, check the anchors and retension. For a ready-to-go system from a known maker, compare this with the <a href="/rigging/rigging-kits/weaver-speed-line-kit/">Weaver speed line kit</a>.',
            2 => 'Pick the angle with care. Too steep and loads arrive fast and hit hard; too shallow and they stall halfway. A controlled line, where a ground worker can hold a friction wrap on the low end, gives you the option to slow or stop a load.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Attaching loads',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Clip the carabiner to the line first, then to the brush, usually through a sling or around the butt of the branch. Keep loads small enough for the line and the anchors. Heavy wood on a speedline puts high forces on the top anchor.',
            1 => 'Have a tag line or control line on anything that could swing. Ground crew stays clear of the line\'s path and the landing zone until the load stops.',
            2 => 'Small, frequent loads are the rule. Several light bundles put far less stress on the anchors than one large limb, and they are easier for the ground crew to handle at the landing zone. Strip side branches from bulky brush so it rides cleanly instead of dragging through shrubs on the way down.',
            3 => 'Keep extra carabiners on the line so the climber can send the next load while the ground crew is still unhooking the last one. A rope bag or bucket at the bottom collects returned connectors.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A speedline is rigging, not life support. Never clip yourself to it. Inspect rope, slings and carabiners before use and retire damaged gear. Work to ANSI Z133 and get trained on speedline setup before you run one over targets.',
            1 => 'Brief the ground crew before the first load. Agree on calls for sending, clear and stop, and keep everyone out from under the line while brush is moving.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What knot should I use to attach a line to a carabiner?',
          'a' => 'For rigging, a figure-eight on a bight or a bowline clipped into a locking carabiner is common. Use a knot you have been trained to tie and inspect, and dress it before loading.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How tight should a speedline be?',
          'a' => 'Tight enough that loads clear the targets, no tighter. Over-tensioning multiplies the forces on both anchors.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a pulley instead of a carabiner on the line?',
          'a' => 'Yes. A pulley runs faster and with less wear on the line. Carabiners slow loads down, which can help on steep lines.',
        ),
      ),
