    '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/double-swing-pulley/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/double-swing-pulley/',
      'category' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/',
      'name' => 'Double Swing Pulley',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'pulley',
      'price' => 65,
      'keyword' => 'double pulley',
      'title' => 'Double Swing Pulley for a Double Pulley System',
      'h1' => 'Double Swing Pulley for a Double Pulley System',
      'meta' => 'Double swing pulley for a compact double pulley system: haul lines, pull lines and tensioning. How to rig a 2 pulley system and what to check before use.',
      'tagline' => 'A two-sheave swing-side pulley for building compact mechanical advantage systems.',
      'summary' => 'A double pulley puts two sheaves side by side in one frame, and this double swing pulley has side plates that open so you can load rope mid-line. It is the building block for a compact double pulley system: pulling over a tree, tensioning a speedline or hauling a heavy piece out of a ditch.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Two sheaves in one frame for compact haul systems',
        1 => 'Swing-open side plates let you load rope mid-line',
        2 => 'Pairs with a second pulley for higher ratios',
        3 => 'Useful for pull lines, speedlines and log handling',
        4 => 'Clips to an anchor with a single locking carabiner',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Double sheave pulley',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Side plates',
          1 => 'Swinging, open to load rope',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sheaves',
          1 => 'Two, side by side',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Connection',
          1 => 'Single attachment point for a carabiner',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'Marked on the pulley',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What a double pulley system does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Mechanical advantage lets one or two people put serious force on a rope. With two double pulleys, one anchored and one on the load, you can build a 4:1 system in a compact package. Add a progress capture or a friction hitch and you can hold what you gain. A single double pulley paired with a single pulley gives you a 3:1.',
            1 => 'On tree jobs that means pulling a back-leaning tree over the notch, tensioning a speedline, or skidding a heavy log onto the trailer without a winch.',
            2 => 'The compact frame matters in the field. Two single pulleys hanging side by side twist around each other and tangle; a double pulley keeps both rope parts in one plane and runs cleaner. Fewer separate pieces also means fewer carabiners to manage, which speeds setup and teardown on a busy day.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why swing side plates matter',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'With fixed side plates you have to thread the rope through from the end. That is slow when the rope is already rigged with a running end tied off a hundred feet away. Swing plates let you open the pulley, drop the rope in, close it and clip it, all at the middle of the line.',
            1 => 'Check that the plates close fully and the carabiner passes through both before loading. A pulley that is not closed can let the rope ride out under load.',
            2 => 'Swing plates also make teardown fast. Once the pull is done, open the plates, lift the rope out and pack the pulley without pulling a hundred feet of line back through.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Building a 2 pulley system',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The simplest 2 pulley system is a single pulley at the anchor and one at the load: rope tied off at one pulley, through the other and back, gives you 2:1 or 3:1 depending on where the end is tied. A double pulley at each end doubles the number of rope parts and the ratio.',
            1 => 'Friction in each sheave eats into the theoretical advantage, so real-world output is less than the math says. Good bearings and correct rope diameter help. Keep ropes from twisting around each other between the pulleys, which adds friction fast.',
            2 => 'More of the haul hardware is in our <a href="/rigging/mechanical-advantage/">mechanical advantage</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safe use',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Mechanical advantage can easily overload anchors, slings and the tree itself. Know the forces you are making, and use hardware rated for them. When pulling trees over, keep everyone clear of the fall zone and the line\'s path if something breaks. Rigging gear is not life support; keep it separate from climbing gear and train crews to ANSI Z133.',
            1 => 'Inspect the pulley before each job: sheaves spin freely, no cracks or bends, side plates tight on the axle.',
            2 => 'Store it clean and dry, and keep it in the rigging bag rather than loose in the truck bed.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What ratio can I get with a double pulley?',
          'a' => 'Two double pulleys rigged together give 4:1 theoretical advantage, or 5:1 if the rope end is tied to the moving pulley. Friction reduces the real output.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between a double pulley and a tandem pulley?',
          'a' => 'A double pulley has two sheaves side by side for mechanical advantage. A tandem pulley has two sheaves in line to ride a tensioned rope.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need progress capture?',
          'a' => 'For hauling and tensioning it is strongly recommended. A friction hitch or progress-capture pulley holds the load when you reset.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Pulley Block Equipment Rigging Edited 2020',
        'creator' => 'chimpwithcan',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188454520@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188454520@N02/49920521488',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/petzl-tandem-pulley/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/petzl-tandem-pulley/',
      'category' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/',
      'name' => 'Petzl Tandem Pulley',
      'brand' => 'petzl',
      'brand_name' => 'Petzl',
      'kind' => 'pulley',
      'price' => 60,
      'keyword' => 'tandem pulley',
      'title' => 'Petzl Tandem Pulley for Speedlines and Tyroleans',
      'h1' => 'Petzl Tandem Pulley',
      'meta' => 'The Petzl Tandem pulley runs two sheaves in line to ride a tensioned rope. How arborists use a tandem pulley on speedlines and how it compares to other pulleys.',
      'tagline' => 'A two-sheave in-line pulley for riding tensioned lines and speedlines.',
      'summary' => 'The Petzl Tandem pulley has two sheaves in line so it rides a tensioned rope smoothly, which is why it shows up on speedlines and tyrolean traverses. Among Petzl pulleys, it is the one built for moving along a line rather than redirecting one.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Two in-line sheaves for riding a tensioned rope',
        1 => 'Smooth travel on speedlines',
        2 => 'Side plates open to install mid-line',
        3 => 'Light, compact Petzl build',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Petzl',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Model',
          1 => 'Tandem',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sheaves',
          1 => 'Two, in line',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'Marked on the pulley',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Using a tandem pulley on a speedline',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On a speedline, brush hung from a single pulley tends to tip and drag. A tandem pulley spreads the load along two sheaves, so it tracks straight and runs fast. Clip the brush to the pulley with a sling and locking carabiner and let it go.',
            1 => 'Line angle and tension decide how fast loads arrive. Have a plan for stopping them at the bottom.',
            2 => 'The side plates open so you can install it on the line without untying, which saves time when each load needs its own pulley. Many crews keep several on the line so the climber always has one ready while the ground crew collects the last load.',
            3 => 'Stop the brush at the bottom with a soft landing, such as a brush pile, or by having the low end tied off on a friction device so it can be eased.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check that both sheaves turn freely, side plates are not bent and the axles are tight. Retire after damage or a severe shock load. Speedlines are rigging, not life support, unless the whole system is designed and rated for that use; follow Petzl\'s instructions and train to ANSI Z133.',
            1 => 'Keep the pulley clean of sap and grit, which slows the sheaves and wears the rope faster.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between a tandem pulley and a double pulley?',
          'a' => 'A tandem has two sheaves in line to ride along a rope. A double has two side by side for building mechanical advantage.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use the Tandem on any rope?',
          'a' => 'Use it within the rope diameter range Petzl lists for it.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Block and Tackle',
        'creator' => 'www.mgaylard.co.uk and thanks for looking',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/16564965@N04',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/16564965@N04/52721499095',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rings-and-other-hardware/crosby-chain-choker-hook/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rings-and-other-hardware/crosby-chain-choker-hook/',
      'category' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rings-and-other-hardware/',
      'name' => 'Crosby Chain Choker Hook',
      'brand' => 'crosby',
      'brand_name' => 'Crosby',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 25,
      'keyword' => 'chain choker hook',
      'title' => 'Crosby Chain Choker Hook for Log Skidding',
      'h1' => 'Crosby Chain Choker Hook',
      'meta' => 'Crosby chain choker hook for making a sliding choker on logging chain. How choker hooks work for skidding logs and what to check before every pull.',
      'tagline' => 'A choker hook that turns a length of chain into a sliding log choker.',
      'summary' => 'A Crosby chain choker hook slides along a logging chain so you can wrap a log, pull the chain tight and drag it. It is a basic piece of wood-handling hardware for skidding logs to the loader or out of the backyard.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Turns a length of chain into a sliding choker',
        1 => 'Grips the log tighter as you pull',
        2 => 'Forged hardware from Crosby',
        3 => 'Useful for skidding with a tractor, mini skid steer or winch',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Crosby',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Chain choker hook',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fit',
          1 => 'Match hook size to chain size',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'Marked on the hook',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How choker hooks work',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Wrap the chain around the log, pass the end back through the choker hook, and hook on to the machine or winch. When you pull, the chain cinches around the log. Release tension and the hook slides loose so you can unwrap.',
            1 => 'Set the choker near the end of the log so it lifts slightly and slides instead of plowing into the ground.',
            2 => 'A sliding choker is quicker than tying a log hitch every time and holds tighter on round wood. Keep a couple of chokers rigged on chain in the truck for pulling logs out of tight backyards to the loader or onto a trailer.',
            3 => 'On a winch line, a short chain with a choker hook saves wear on the winch rope by keeping it off the bark and dirt.',
            4 => 'Hooks also save the chain itself from the kinks a knotted chain picks up.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Sizing and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Use a hook matched to your chain size and grade, and keep the working load within the lowest-rated component. Inspect for spread throats, cracks and worn chain contact points. Stand clear of the chain and log when pulling; a chain that slips or breaks can whip. Do not use skidding hardware for overhead lifting unless it is rated for that.',
            1 => 'Grease and rust-proof the hardware between jobs.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What size choker hook do I need?',
          'a' => 'Match the choker hook to your chain size and grade so it slides correctly and holds the load.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a choker hook for lifting?',
          'a' => 'Only if the hook and chain are rated for overhead lifting. Most choker setups are for skidding.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Project 365 #278: 051011 Going Nuts',
        'creator' => 'comedy_nose',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/23408922@N07',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/23408922@N07/6214688085',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rings-and-other-hardware/crosby-chain-connector/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rings-and-other-hardware/crosby-chain-connector/',
      'category' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rings-and-other-hardware/',
      'name' => 'Crosby Chain Connector',
      'brand' => 'crosby',
      'brand_name' => 'Crosby',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 18,
      'keyword' => 'crosby chain connectors',
      'title' => 'Crosby Chain Connectors for Chain Repair and Rigging',
      'h1' => 'Crosby Chain Connectors',
      'meta' => 'Crosby chain connectors join chain to chain, hooks or rings without welding. Where an arborist uses a chain connecting link and how to size and inspect one.',
      'tagline' => 'A forged connecting link for joining chain, hooks and rings in the field.',
      'summary' => 'Crosby chain connectors join two pieces of chain, or chain to a hook or ring, without welding. On a tree crew that means building a custom-length skidding chain, repairing a damaged one, or fitting a grab hook to the end.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Joins chain to chain, hooks or rings',
        1 => 'No welding, assemble in the field',
        2 => 'Forged hardware from Crosby',
        3 => 'Handy for building and repairing skidding chains',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Crosby',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Chain connecting link',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fit',
          1 => 'Match link to chain size and grade',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'Marked on the link',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Using a chain connecting link',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A Crosby chain link is the quick fix when a chain is too short, a hook is damaged, or you want a ring on the end for a winch line. Join the pieces, assemble the link per Crosby\'s instructions, and the chain is back in service.',
            1 => 'Match the connector to the chain size and grade so the weakest point is not the link itself.',
            2 => 'They are also useful for linking a winch cable eye to a chain, or adding a shortening hook so one chain covers different log sizes. Keep a few links in the truck bin; a broken chain at the start of a big cleanup is a long trip back to the shop.',
            3 => 'Clean and lightly oil connectors before storing them so the pins and bodies do not rust together.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and limits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check connectors for cracks, wear and spread before each use, and replace any that look stretched. The working load of the assembly is that of its lowest-rated part. Use rated alloy components if the chain will ever be used for lifting, and keep chain work clear of people; a failing chain can whip.',
            1 => 'Do not grind, heat or weld connecting links; it changes the steel.',
            2 => 'Retire any link that will not assemble cleanly.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I reuse a Crosby chain connector?',
          'a' => 'Follow Crosby\'s instructions for the specific link. Inspect it before reuse and replace if it is damaged or deformed.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does the connector reduce chain strength?',
          'a' => 'The assembly is limited by its lowest-rated component, so match the link to the chain grade.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-5.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-5-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Eye bolt snow bokeh',
        'creator' => 'dsgetch',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/66136677@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/66136677@N00/51782009502',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rings-and-other-hardware/galvanized-clevis/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rings-and-other-hardware/galvanized-clevis/',
      'category' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rings-and-other-hardware/',
      'name' => 'Galvanized Clevis',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 9,
      'keyword' => 'clevis',
      'title' => 'Galvanized Clevis: Uses, Sizing and Load Ratings',
      'h1' => 'Galvanized Clevis',
      'meta' => 'A galvanized clevis connects chain, cable and hardware with a removable pin. How a clevis is used on tree jobs, rated versus unrated hardware, and safe pinning.',
      'tagline' => 'A galvanized pinned connector for chain, cable and hitch work around the yard.',
      'summary' => 'A galvanized clevis is a U-shaped connector closed with a removable pin, used to join chain, cable, rings and hitch points. Around a tree service it shows up on equipment hitches, skidding chains, cable work and anywhere you need a solid, pinned connection.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'U-shaped body with removable pin',
        1 => 'Galvanized finish for outdoor use',
        2 => 'Connects chain, cable, rings and hitches',
        3 => 'Simple, strong and easy to inspect',
        4 => 'Choose rated hardware for any critical load',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Clevis with pin',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Finish',
          1 => 'Galvanized',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Pin',
          1 => 'Removable, secured to stop backing out',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Connections for chain, cable and hitches',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'Use only marked, rated hardware for rigging loads',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What a clevis does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A clevis is one of the oldest pieces of connecting hardware. The U-shaped body goes around whatever you are connecting, and a pin closes the open end. Unlike a hook, it cannot come unhooked when the load goes slack, and unlike a carabiner, it is built for steel-on-steel work like chain and hitches.',
            1 => 'On a tree crew you will see a clevis on the hitch of a log trailer, at the end of a skidding chain, on cable runs for guying, or holding a snatch block to an anchor strap on a winch setup.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Clevis, shackle and the difference that matters',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'People use clevis and shackle loosely, and many shapes overlap. What matters for safety is whether the piece is rated. A rated shackle or clevis is marked with its working load and made to a known standard. Generic farm-store clevises are often unmarked, and their strength is unknown.',
            1 => 'For hitching and general yard use, a galvanized clevis is fine. For anything that will hold a load over people, anchor a rigging system, or be part of a winch pull near the crew, use hardware with a marked working load limit and stay within it.',
            2 => 'Browse rated rings, shackles and connectors in <a href="/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rings-and-other-hardware/">rings and other hardware</a>.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Pinning and loading',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Make sure the pin goes all the way through both ears and is secured with its cotter, clip or nut so it cannot work loose under vibration. A pin that is only half seated, or a threaded pin that has backed out, is a common way connections fail.',
            1 => 'Load the clevis in line through the bow and pin. Side-loading the ears bends the body and the pin. Do not substitute a bolt for the proper pin; the grade and fit may be wrong.',
            2 => 'Size the clevis so the chain or hardware sits fully in the bow without being forced. A tight fit pries the ears apart under load.',
            3 => 'If the pin is hard to start, check for a bent ear or burred threads before forcing it.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspecting hardware',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Look for bent pins, spread ears, cracks, heavy rust and wear at the bow. Galvanizing slows corrosion but does not stop wear. Replace any clevis that has been overloaded or deformed; steel does not go back into shape safely. Keep the working load of the whole assembly within the rating of its weakest piece, and train the crew on rigging hardware per ANSI Z133.',
            1 => 'Store spare clevises and pins together in a bin so pins do not get lost and replaced with bolts in a pinch. A labeled bin of rated hardware next to a separate bin of yard hardware keeps the two from getting mixed up on the truck.',
            2 => 'When you buy, note the marking on rated hardware and keep the paperwork. Knowing what a clevis is rated for is what lets you use it with confidence.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between a clevis and a shackle?',
          'a' => 'The terms overlap. Both are pinned U-shaped connectors. What matters is whether the hardware is marked with a rated working load.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a galvanized clevis for tree rigging?',
          'a' => 'Only if it is marked with a working load limit suitable for the forces involved. Unmarked clevises belong on hitches and general yard work.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I keep a clevis pin from backing out?',
          'a' => 'Use the correct pin and secure it with its cotter pin, clip or locking nut. Check it during the job.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Eye Bolt - Basil Watson\'s Sopwith-Type Biplane, Melbourne, Victoria, 1916',
        'creator' => 'Photographer: David Thompson',
        'creator_url' => '',
        'license' => 'CC BY 4.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://collections.museumsvictoria.com.au/items/393624',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/lowering-devices/buckingham-medium-portawrap/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/lowering-devices/buckingham-medium-portawrap/',
      'category' => '/rigging/lowering-devices/',
      'name' => 'Buckingham Medium Portawrap',
      'brand' => 'buckingham',
      'brand_name' => 'Buckingham',
      'kind' => 'rigging-device',
      'price' => 230,
      'keyword' => 'portawrap',
      'title' => 'Buckingham Medium Portawrap Lowering Device',
      'h1' => 'Buckingham Medium Portawrap',
      'meta' => 'The Buckingham medium Portawrap is a trunk-mounted friction device for lowering wood. How the port a wrap works, how many wraps to take, and how to inspect it.',
      'tagline' => 'A medium friction lowering device for controlled lowering on everyday removals.',
      'summary' => 'The Buckingham medium Portawrap is the classic friction lowering device: a tube with horns that you mount to the trunk and wrap the rigging line around. The ground worker controls how fast each piece comes down by adding wraps and holding tension on the tail. The medium size covers the rope sizes most tree crews rig with day to day.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Friction lowering device mounted at the base of the tree',
        1 => 'Adjust friction by the number of wraps',
        2 => 'Horns keep the wraps from riding off',
        3 => 'Medium size for common rigging line diameters',
        4 => 'Simple design with no moving parts to service',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Buckingham',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Size',
          1 => 'Medium',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Friction lowering device',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Mounting',
          1 => 'Sling, chain or strap around the trunk',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Friction control',
          1 => 'Number of wraps plus tail tension',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See Buckingham\'s instructions and markings',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How the port a wrap works',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A port a wrap turns the rigging line\'s energy into heat through friction on the tube. You anchor the device to the base of the tree or a nearby tree, run the line from the block above down to the tube, and wrap it around between the horns. Each wrap adds friction. The ground worker holds the tail and feeds or stops the line.',
            1 => 'Without a lowering device, the friction comes from wraps around the trunk itself, which works but wears the bark and gives you less control. A Portawrap puts the friction in one consistent, inspectable spot and lets you set it to suit the load.',
            2 => 'It is the right tool for crews that lower wood regularly but do not need a winch or capstan. See all <a href="/rigging/lowering-devices/">lowering devices</a> to compare options.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing the number of wraps',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Light pieces need few wraps; heavy pieces and long falls need more. Too few and the ground worker cannot hold the load. Too many and the piece shock-loads the rigging when it stops instead of running a little to soften the catch. The art of rigging is letting pieces run just enough.',
            1 => 'Start with more wraps than you think you need while you learn the device, and adjust as you get a feel for each tree. Keep the tail controlled at all times, with gloves on, and never wrap the tail around a hand.',
            2 => 'Communication matters as much as wraps. The climber calls the cut, the ground worker confirms ready, and nobody stands between the device and the load. Agree on calls before the first piece.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Mounting it properly',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Mount the Portawrap on the side of the trunk facing the block so the line runs in a straight path from above. Use a sling, chain or strap rated for the forces involved and set it so the device cannot slide up the trunk under load.',
            1 => 'Some crews mount it on a neighboring tree to get the ground worker out of the drop zone. That works if the anchor tree is sound and the line runs clear. Keep the area around the device free of brush so the operator has good footing.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the tube and horns for grooves worn by rope, cracks and bends. Deep grooves can damage rope and reduce friction control. Inspect the mounting sling or chain every time you set up.',
            1 => 'Rigging forces add up fast. Size every component, from block to sling to Portawrap, for the loads you plan to drop. Lowering devices are rigging equipment, not life support. Train ground workers on wraps and line handling before they run the device, and work to ANSI Z133.',
            2 => 'Rope wear concentrates on the tube, so rotate which part of the line takes the wraps, and retire rope that shows glazing or heavy fuzzing.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What rope size fits the medium Portawrap?',
          'a' => 'The medium is sized for the mid-range rigging lines most crews use. Check Buckingham\'s listed range for your rope before you buy.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a Portawrap the same as a port-a-wrap?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Portawrap, port-a-wrap and port a wrap all refer to the same style of friction lowering device.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How many wraps should I use?',
          'a' => 'It depends on load weight, fall distance and rope. Start with more friction while learning and reduce as you gain experience.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Medium or large Portawrap?',
          'a' => 'Medium suits typical rigging. Choose the large for heavier wood and bigger rope.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/buckingham/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Pruning in RVA (Carytown, Virginia)',
        'creator' => 'Gamma Man',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06/48982894227',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/lowering-devices/good-rigging-control-system/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/lowering-devices/good-rigging-control-system/',
      'category' => '/rigging/lowering-devices/',
      'name' => 'Good Rigging Control System (GRCS)',
      'brand' => 'good-rigging',
      'brand_name' => 'Good Rigging',
      'kind' => 'rigging-device',
      'price' => 2100,
      'keyword' => 'grcs rigging',
      'title' => 'GRCS Rigging: Good Rigging Control System',
      'h1' => 'GRCS Rigging: Good Rigging Control System',
      'meta' => 'The Good Rigging Control System combines a lowering device and winch for GRCS rigging. How the GRCS works on removals, pulls and lifts, and who needs one.',
      'tagline' => 'A trunk-mounted lowering device and winch in one, for crews that rig big wood.',
      'summary' => 'GRCS rigging means running your lowering line through the Good Rigging Control System, a trunk-mounted unit that combines a friction drum for lowering with a winch for lifting and tensioning. It lets one ground worker lower heavy wood, pull a top over or lift a piece off a roof with the same tool.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Lowering device and winch in one unit',
        1 => 'Mounts to the trunk with straps',
        2 => 'Lower, lift, tension and pull with one setup',
        3 => 'Removable winch handle',
        4 => 'Built for crews rigging heavy wood regularly',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Good Rigging',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Model',
          1 => 'Good Rigging Control System (GRCS)',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Lowering device with winch',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Mounting',
          1 => 'Straps around the trunk',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Functions',
          1 => 'Lowering, lifting, tensioning, pulling',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See Good Rigging\'s manual',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What the GRCS does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A standard lowering device only adds friction. The Good Rigging Control System adds a winch drum, so you can also take in rope under load. That opens up jobs that used to need a crane or a lot of people: lifting a cut piece clear of a roof before lowering it, pre-tensioning the rigging line so a top does not drop before it catches, or pulling a leaning tree back over the notch.',
            1 => 'When lowering, the rope wraps on the drum like on any friction device and the operator controls descent with the tail. Switch to winching and the same rope can be pulled in with the handle. Nothing gets re-rigged.',
            2 => 'The design keeps the operator on the ground with full control. One person can manage what used to take a couple of people heaving on a pull line, and the drum gives them a mechanical advantage that hand-tensioning never could.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where it pays off',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Crews doing removals over houses, pools and gardens get the most from a GRCS. Being able to lift a piece rather than letting it swing into a target is a big deal. On take-downs where you need to pull a stem against its lean, a tensioned rigging line through the GRCS can replace a separate pull line and come-along setup.',
            1 => 'It is a serious investment and a serious piece of gear. Many crews start with a <a href="/rigging/lowering-devices/buckingham-medium-portawrap/">Portawrap</a> and add a GRCS once their work routinely needs lifting and pre-tensioning.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Setup and operation',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Strap the unit to the trunk or a nearby anchor tree so the line runs straight from the block to the drum. Follow Good Rigging\'s manual for wraps, rope size and strapping. Keep the handle off the drum when you are not winching so it cannot spin.',
            1 => 'Practice with light loads first. The winch makes it easy to put large forces into the rigging, the block, the sling and the tree itself, and those forces add up faster than most people expect.',
            2 => 'Rope choice matters. Use rigging line in the diameter range the manual lists, in good condition, and keep it clean; grit on a winch drum wears rope fast. Watch for heat on long, heavy lowers and give the drum and rope time to cool between big pieces.',
            3 => 'Store the unit in a case or secure spot on the truck so the drum and mounting hardware are not knocked around in transport.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Everything in the system must be rated for the loads: rope, block, slings and anchors. Inspect the GRCS, straps and rope before each job. It is rigging equipment, not life support. Operators should be trained on its use, and crews should work to ANSI Z133 with clear communication between climber and ground.',
            1 => 'When pre-tensioning a top before the cut, agree on how much tension to apply. Too much can pull the piece off the stub early or split the stem. The climber should be out of the line of any piece that could swing before the operator starts winching.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is GRCS rigging?',
          'a' => 'Rigging run through the Good Rigging Control System, a combined lowering device and winch that mounts to the trunk.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can the GRCS lift loads?',
          'a' => 'Yes. The winch can take in rope under load to lift or tension, within the limits in the manual.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the GRCS worth it for a small crew?',
          'a' => 'If you routinely rig heavy wood over targets or need to pre-tension and pull, it can replace extra gear and labor. For occasional rigging, a friction device may be enough.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/good-rigging/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Pruning in RVA (Carytown, Virginia)',
        'creator' => 'Gamma Man',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06/48982704071',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/lowering-devices/hobbs-h2-lowering-device/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/lowering-devices/hobbs-h2-lowering-device/',
      'category' => '/rigging/lowering-devices/',
      'name' => 'Hobbs H2 Lowering Device',
      'brand' => 'hobbs',
      'brand_name' => 'Hobbs',
      'kind' => 'rigging-device',
      'price' => 1150,
      'keyword' => 'hobbs lowering device',
      'title' => 'Hobbs Lowering Device H2 for Heavy Tree Rigging',
      'h1' => 'Hobbs Lowering Device H2',
      'meta' => 'The Hobbs H2 lowering device mounts to the trunk to control heavy rigging. How a Hobbs lowering device works, who it suits, and how to set it up and inspect it.',
      'tagline' => 'A trunk-mounted friction lowering device built for heavy rigging control.',
      'summary' => 'The Hobbs lowering device H2 is a trunk-mounted friction device built for controlled lowering of heavy wood. It gives the ground worker a big, solid friction surface and positive control of the rigging line, so pieces come down smoothly and stop where they should.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Mounts to the trunk with straps',
        1 => 'Controls heavy rigging loads at the base',
        2 => 'Ground worker sets friction by wraps',
        3 => 'Rugged build for daily removal work',
        4 => 'Keeps friction off the bark of the tree',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Model',
          1 => 'Hobbs H2',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Friction lowering device',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Mounting',
          1 => 'Strapped to trunk or anchor tree',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Friction control',
          1 => 'Wraps on the drum plus tail tension',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See the maker\'s manual',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How a Hobbs lowering device works',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Like other friction devices, the Hobbs takes wraps of the rigging line, and the ground worker controls descent by holding the tail. The difference is in the size and build: a larger drum and solid mounting suited to heavier wood than a small tube device handles comfortably.',
            1 => 'Mounted at the base, it puts friction in one controlled place instead of on the bark. The operator can let pieces run, then bring them to a smooth stop instead of a hard catch.',
            2 => 'Because the device is strapped rather than chained, it sets up fast on trunks of different sizes and comes off cleanly when the removal is done. That matters on days with several removals where every minute spent rigging is a minute not cutting.',
            3 => 'It also reduces bark wear on trees you are only pruning, where wrapping the trunk itself would leave scars.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Who it suits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Crews who rig big wood every week get the most from a Hobbs lowering device. If your work is mostly small pruning drops, a <a href="/rigging/lowering-devices/large-portawrap-lowering-device/">large Portawrap</a> may do the job for less money. If you need to lift and pre-tension as well as lower, compare with the Good Rigging Control System, which adds a winch.',
            1 => 'Whatever you pick, match the device to the rope diameter and load range in the maker\'s manual.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Setting up',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Strap the device to the trunk on the side facing the rigging point so the line feeds straight onto the drum. Use straps in good condition and tighten them so the device cannot creep up the stem under load. Some crews prefer to mount on a neighboring tree to keep the operator out of the drop zone; the anchor tree has to be sound.',
            1 => 'Clear the ground around the device for good footing. The operator needs room to manage the tail and step back from the line\'s path.',
            2 => 'Keep the rope bag at the operator\'s feet and the tail running cleanly off the top, so it feeds without snagging when a big piece runs. A snag at the wrong moment turns a smooth lower into a shock load.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the drum and horns for rope grooves, cracks and loose hardware, and inspect the straps for cuts and UV damage. Rope wear at the drum is normal, but deep grooves need attention.',
            1 => 'All components in a rigging system must be rated for the loads and inspected before use. Lowering devices are not life support. Train operators before they run heavy pieces and work to ANSI Z133.',
            2 => 'Agree on calls between climber and ground before the first piece. Keep the device and its straps out of the sun and weather between jobs; UV and moisture shorten strap life. Record inspections so the whole crew knows the gear\'s history.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the Hobbs lowering device used for?',
          'a' => 'Controlled lowering of heavy wood during removals, with friction set by the number of wraps on the drum.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How is the Hobbs different from a Portawrap?',
          'a' => 'It is a larger, heavier-duty friction device with strap mounting, aimed at crews lowering big wood regularly.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can it lift loads?',
          'a' => 'It is a lowering device. For lifting and pre-tensioning, look at units with a built-in winch such as the GRCS.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/hobbs/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Pruning in RVA (Carytown, Virginia)',
        'creator' => 'Gamma Man',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06/48982705461',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/lowering-devices/large-portawrap-lowering-device/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/lowering-devices/large-portawrap-lowering-device/',
      'category' => '/rigging/lowering-devices/',
      'name' => 'Large Portawrap Lowering Device',
      'brand' => 'buckingham',
      'brand_name' => 'Buckingham',
      'kind' => 'rigging-device',
      'price' => 290,
      'keyword' => 'porta wrap',
      'title' => 'Large Porta Wrap Lowering Device for Heavy Rigging',
      'h1' => 'Large Porta Wrap Lowering Device',
      'meta' => 'The large porta wrap lowering device handles bigger rope and heavier wood. When to step up from a medium port-a-wrap, how to mount it, and how to set friction.',
      'tagline' => 'The large friction lowering device for bigger rigging lines and heavy wood.',
      'summary' => 'The large porta wrap is the bigger version of the classic friction lowering device, sized for heavier rigging lines and the bigger pieces that go with them. Mount it at the base, take wraps and the ground worker has full control of how fast each piece comes down.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Large tube for bigger rigging lines',
        1 => 'Friction set by number of wraps',
        2 => 'Horns keep wraps in place',
        3 => 'Simple, no moving parts',
        4 => 'Suits crews lowering heavy wood',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Buckingham',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Size',
          1 => 'Large',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Friction lowering device',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Mounting',
          1 => 'Sling, chain or strap to trunk',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See Buckingham\'s markings and instructions',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'When to step up to the large size',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'If you are rigging big wood on thicker rope, a medium device starts to run out of room. The large port-a-wrap has the surface and diameter for heavier lines and gives the ground worker more friction to work with. It also suits crews who run long lowers, where the heat built up on a smaller tube becomes an issue.',
            1 => 'Smaller crews that mostly do pruning and light removals are usually better served by the <a href="/rigging/lowering-devices/buckingham-medium-portawrap/">medium Portawrap</a>.',
            2 => 'A larger tube also spreads heat over more surface, which helps on long lowers. If your rigging line has grown from medium to heavy over the years, the device should grow with it.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Mounting and wraps',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Anchor the device to the trunk on the side facing the rigging point, with a sling, chain or strap rated for the load. Set it so it cannot ride up the stem. Wrap the line around the tube between the horns: more wraps for heavier pieces and longer drops, fewer for light ones.',
            1 => 'Let pieces run a little before stopping them. A hard stop multiplies the force on the block, the sling and the top of the tree. The ground worker should hold the tail with gloved hands and never wrap it around a hand or arm.',
            2 => 'Mount height matters too. Keep the device low enough that the operator can work it standing comfortably, but high enough that the tail does not drag in debris. Set the anchor before the climber goes up so rigging starts without delay.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Rope care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Friction devices create heat. On long lowers with heavy wood, the rope and tube can get hot enough to glaze rope fibers. Give the system a breather on big jobs, and inspect the rope for glazing and stiffness.',
            1 => 'Keep the tube clean of sap and grit, which wears rope faster. A rope bag at the base keeps the tail off the dirt and ready to run.',
            2 => 'Wash rope that has been through mud or sawdust, and let it dry before storage. Clean rope grips the tube more predictably, so the ground worker can feel exactly how much friction they have.',
            3 => 'Retire rigging rope that shows core damage, flattening or heavy glazing; a lowering device cannot make up for a tired line.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the tube and horns for grooves, cracks and bends before each job, along with the mounting hardware. Retire a device with deep grooves that could cut rope. Lowering devices are rigging gear, not life support. Size every component for the load, train the crew, and work to ANSI Z133.',
            1 => 'Brief ground workers who are new to the large device. It holds more and stops harder, so they should practice on modest pieces first and learn how quickly heavy wood builds speed on a long drop.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a porta wrap used for?',
          'a' => 'It adds controlled friction to a rigging line so the ground worker can lower cut pieces smoothly from the tree.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Large or medium porta wrap?',
          'a' => 'Choose the large for thicker rope and heavier wood; the medium covers most everyday rigging.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I put the porta wrap on another tree?',
          'a' => 'Yes, if that tree is sound and the line runs clear. It can keep the operator out of the drop zone.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/buckingham/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Pruning in RVA (Carytown, Virginia)',
        'creator' => 'Gamma Man',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06/48982144423',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/maasdam-continuous-rope-puller-replacement-handle/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/maasdam-continuous-rope-puller-replacement-handle/',
      'category' => '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/',
      'name' => 'Maasdam Continuous Rope Puller Replacement Handle',
      'brand' => 'maasdam',
      'brand_name' => 'Maasdam',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 30,
      'keyword' => 'rope puller handle',
      'title' => 'Maasdam Rope Puller Handle Replacement',
      'h1' => 'Maasdam Rope Puller Handle (Replacement)',
      'meta' => 'Replacement rope puller handle for the Maasdam continuous rope puller. Get a bent or lost handle replaced and the puller back to work pulling trees and logs.',
      'tagline' => 'A replacement handle to get a Maasdam continuous rope puller back to work.',
      'summary' => 'This replacement rope puller handle fits the Maasdam continuous rope puller and gets the tool working again when the original handle is bent, cracked or left behind on a job. The handle is the lever that drives the puller, so a damaged one is worth replacing rather than nursing along.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Replacement handle for the Maasdam rope puller',
        1 => 'Restores full leverage on the puller',
        2 => 'Faster than replacing the whole tool',
        3 => 'Worth keeping a spare in the truck',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Maasdam',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Maasdam continuous rope puller',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Replacement operating handle',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Drives the rope puller mechanism',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Note',
          1 => 'Match to your puller model',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why replace the handle',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On a rope puller, the handle is what turns your effort into pulling force. A bent handle can bind, slip off, or fail to give full stroke, and that makes a pull slower and harder to control. A cracked handle can let go under load, which is the last thing you want with a tensioned rope in front of you.',
            1 => 'Handles also go missing. They come off for storage, and one left in the grass at a job site is a common story. A spare in the truck bin keeps the puller working.',
            2 => 'If you are buying the whole tool, see the <a href="/rigging/mechanical-advantage/masdaam-continuous-rope-puller-kit/">Maasdam rope puller kit</a>.',
            3 => 'A worn pivot hole is another sign. If the handle wobbles where it attaches, the puller loses stroke and the jaws may not grip consistently. Replacing a twenty-dollar part is much cheaper than fighting a tool all day on a felling job.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fitting the new handle',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check that the handle matches your Maasdam model before you order. Fit it per Maasdam\'s instructions, cycle the puller a few strokes without load to confirm smooth action, then make a light test pull before you put it to work on a tree.',
            1 => 'Never extend the handle with a pipe or cheater bar. The handle length is matched to the tool\'s rating; extra leverage overloads the puller and the rope.',
            2 => 'If the puller came secondhand, compare the handle to Maasdam\'s parts listing to confirm you are replacing like with like. Some older tools differ in detail, and a correct fit matters for safe operation. Keep the old handle out of service so nobody grabs it by mistake.',
            3 => 'While the handle is off, it is a good moment to look over the jaws and frame.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Rope puller care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Keep the puller clean and lightly lubricated as the manual describes. Inspect the jaws or grips that hold the rope, since worn grips can slip. Check the hook, frame and rope for damage before every use.',
            1 => 'Store it dry, with the handle stowed so it does not get bent under other gear in the truck. A little care keeps the tool working for years.',
            2 => 'Keep a record of when parts were replaced. It helps track how hard the tool is being worked and when it may be time for a full overhaul.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safe use',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Rope pullers are for pulling and tensioning, not for lifting people. When pulling trees over, keep everyone outside the fall zone and clear of the rope\'s path if something gives. Size the rope, slings and anchors for the load, and train the crew per ANSI Z133.',
            1 => 'Stand to the side of the rope when working the handle, not in line with it, so a slipped grip or broken rope does not come straight back at you.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will this handle fit other rope pullers?',
          'a' => 'It is made for the Maasdam continuous rope puller. Fit on other brands is not assured.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a longer handle for more pull?',
          'a' => 'No. A longer lever overloads the puller and rope. Use the handle supplied for the tool.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I know my handle needs replacing?',
          'a' => 'Replace it if it is bent, cracked, worn at the pivot, or slips off during use.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'chainsaw',
        'creator' => 'sf-dvs',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/46207792@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/46207792@N00/64064283',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/masdaam-continuous-rope-puller-kit/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/masdaam-continuous-rope-puller-kit/',
      'category' => '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/',
      'name' => 'Maasdam Continuous Rope Puller Kit',
      'brand' => 'maasdam',
      'brand_name' => 'Maasdam',
      'kind' => 'rigging-device',
      'price' => 150,
      'keyword' => 'maasdam rope puller',
      'title' => 'Maasdam Rope Puller Kit: Rope Come Along for Tree Work',
      'h1' => 'Maasdam Rope Puller Kit',
      'meta' => 'Maasdam rope puller kit, a rope come along for pulling trees, tensioning lines and moving logs. How a continuous rope puller works and how to use it safely.',
      'tagline' => 'A rope come along that pulls any length of rope for tree and yard work.',
      'summary' => 'The Maasdam rope puller is a rope come along: a hand-operated puller that grips rope instead of winding cable on a drum. Because it works on a continuous length of rope, you can pull as far as your rope reaches, which makes it a favorite for pulling trees over, tensioning lines and dragging logs.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Rope come along with unlimited pull length',
        1 => 'Grips the rope with jaws, no drum to fill',
        2 => 'Light enough to carry on most jobs',
        3 => 'Uses rope instead of steel cable',
        4 => 'Good for pulling trees, tensioning and skidding',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Maasdam',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Continuous rope puller (rope come along)',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Operation',
          1 => 'Hand lever',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Pull length',
          1 => 'Limited only by rope length',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See Maasdam\'s markings and manual',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How a continuous rope puller works',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A cable come along winds steel cable onto a drum, and when the drum is full you have to reset. A rope puller instead grips the rope with jaws and pulls it through as you work the handle. The rope passes out the back, so you can keep pulling as long as there is rope left.',
            1 => 'Rope is lighter than cable, easier to handle, and kinder to bark when wrapped around a trunk. That is why a rope come along fits so naturally into a tree crew\'s kit.',
            2 => 'The kit format helps too. Rather than buying puller, rope and hooks separately and hoping they match, the kit gives you a working setup on day one. Keep it in a bag in the truck so it is ready when a tree needs a pull.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where it earns its keep',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The common job is helping a tree fall the right way: rope high in the tree, rope puller anchored to another tree or stump out past the fall zone, and steady tension as the sawyer works the back cut. It is also handy for tensioning a speedline, pulling a hung-up top free from a safe distance, or skidding a log into position for bucking.',
            1 => 'Pair it with pulleys from our <a href="/rigging/mechanical-advantage/">mechanical advantage</a> section to redirect the pull or gain more force.',
            2 => 'A rope puller also works as a backup on a stuck saw. If a bar gets pinched in a leaning stem, a little tension from the side can open the kerf enough to free it.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Rope and anchors',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Use rope of the diameter and type the puller is designed for, in good condition. Glazed, cut or crushed rope can slip in the jaws. Anchor the puller to something solid with a rated sling, and keep the line of pull straight.',
            1 => 'The force you put on the rope goes into the tree, the anchor and the hardware. Pulling hard on a tree with a weak union or a small anchor tree can cause failures you did not plan for. Think the whole system through before you start cranking.',
            2 => 'Use a block or pulley to redirect the pull when the anchor tree is not in line with the direction of fall. A redirect at the base of the anchor tree keeps the puller operator out of the fall zone and lets them work from a comfortable stance.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Rope pullers are not for lifting people or for overhead lifting unless the manual says so. Keep the crew out of the fall zone and the rope\'s path. Do not extend the handle. Inspect the puller, hook and rope before each job, and train everyone who runs it per ANSI Z133.',
            1 => 'Take steady, even strokes rather than hard jerks, and watch the tree as well as the puller. If something looks wrong, such as a crack opening in the wrong place, stop and reassess. Keep a spare handle; a lost or bent one ends the pull.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a rope come along?',
          'a' => 'A hand-operated puller that grips and pulls rope, rather than winding steel cable on a drum like a cable come along.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What rope works with the Maasdam rope puller?',
          'a' => 'Use the rope diameter and type Maasdam specifies. The kit may include rope sized for the puller.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use it to pull a tree over?',
          'a' => 'Yes, it is a common use. Anchor out past the fall zone, keep tension steady and keep everyone clear.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'felling',
        2 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Pulley Block Equipment Rigging Edited 2020',
        'creator' => 'chimpwithcan',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188454520@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188454520@N02/49920521488',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/petzl-micro-traxion-pulley/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/petzl-micro-traxion-pulley/',
      'category' => '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/',
      'name' => 'Petzl Micro Traxion Pulley',
      'brand' => 'petzl',
      'brand_name' => 'Petzl',
      'kind' => 'pulley',
      'price' => 130,
      'keyword' => 'petzl micro traxion',
      'title' => 'Petzl Micro Traxion Progress Capture Pulley',
      'h1' => 'Petzl Micro Traxion Pulley',
      'meta' => 'The Petzl Micro Traxion is a compact progress capture pulley with a ball-bearing sheave. How arborists use the micro traxion in haul systems and SRT setups.',
      'tagline' => 'A small, efficient progress capture pulley for haul systems and SRT tending.',
      'summary' => 'The Petzl Micro Traxion is a compact pulley with a built-in cam that lets rope run one way and locks it the other. That progress capture makes it the go-to piece for mechanical advantage haul systems, and arborists also use it to tend slack in single-rope climbing setups.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Built-in cam for progress capture',
        1 => 'Sealed ball-bearing sheave runs efficiently',
        2 => 'Cam can be locked open to use as a simple pulley',
        3 => 'Very compact and light',
        4 => 'Popular in haul systems and SRT setups',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Petzl',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Model',
          1 => 'Micro Traxion',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Weight',
          1 => 'about 3 oz (85 g)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope',
          1 => '8 to 11 mm',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sheave',
          1 => 'Sealed ball bearing',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'Marked on the device; see Petzl\'s instructions',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What progress capture does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In a haul system, every time you reset the pulleys the load wants to slide back. Progress capture holds what you gained. The micro traxion does it in one small device: rope runs freely through the sheave as you pull, and the toothed cam grabs it the instant you let go.',
            1 => 'That turns a simple 3:1 setup into something one person can run efficiently, pulling a tree over, tensioning a line or lifting a piece back onto a trailer.',
            2 => 'Its efficiency is part of the appeal. A ball-bearing sheave wastes less of your effort than a carabiner or bushing pulley, so more of what you put in reaches the load. Over a long haul, that difference is noticeable in your arms.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How arborists use the Micro Traxion',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Beyond haul systems, climbers use it in SRT. Paired with a rope wrench or mechanical device, it can tend slack so the climber does not have to hand-feed rope through the friction device on the way up. Some climbers also use it as part of a foot or knee ascender setup.',
            1 => 'Any use in a life-support system must follow Petzl\'s instructions and the configuration the maker describes, and climbers should be trained before using it that way. Check that your rope\'s diameter falls within the range Petzl lists.',
            2 => 'Because it is small and light, many climbers just leave one on the saddle. When a pull line needs tensioning or a stuck rope needs a haul, it is already there.',
            3 => 'It packs small enough to live on the saddle.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Rope and cam care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The cam\'s teeth grip the rope sheath. Shock loading or a fall onto a toothed cam can damage the sheath, so keep it in systems where it is used as designed. Keep teeth clean of sap and grit with a brush; clogged teeth grip poorly.',
            1 => 'Lock the cam open with its catch when you want a plain pulley. It is one of the reasons this piece is so versatile: one device, two jobs.',
            2 => 'More pulleys and haul gear are in the <a href="/rigging/mechanical-advantage/">mechanical advantage</a> section.',
            3 => 'Wash it in fresh water after muddy or sappy jobs and let it dry before storage. Do not oil the cam teeth, since lubricant attracts grit and reduces grip. A light drop on the cam pivot, per Petzl\'s guidance, keeps the action smooth.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Before each use, check the sheave turns freely, the cam springs back and grips, the teeth are not worn or clogged, and the side plates and attachment hole show no cracks or wear. Retire the device if anything looks wrong or after a severe fall load. Follow ANSI Z133 and Petzl\'s instructions for life-support use.',
            1 => 'Write the purchase date on your gear log; Petzl\'s instructions cover lifespan and inspection frequency, and a record makes those checks easy to keep up with.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the Petzl Micro Traxion used for?',
          'a' => 'Progress capture in haul systems, and as a slack-tending pulley in SRT climbing setups.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What rope diameter fits the Micro Traxion?',
          'a' => 'Petzl lists it for ropes about 8 to 11 mm. Check the current instructions for your rope.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use it as a regular pulley?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Lock the cam open and it runs as a simple pulley.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can the Micro Traxion catch a fall?',
          'a' => 'It is not designed as fall arrest. Use it only in the configurations Petzl describes.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Block and tackle',
        'creator' => 'quinet',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/91994044@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/91994044@N00/14200854012',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/rigging-kits/port-a-wrap-with-soft-anchor-rigging-sling/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/rigging-kits/port-a-wrap-with-soft-anchor-rigging-sling/',
      'category' => '/rigging/rigging-kits/',
      'name' => 'Port-a-Wrap with Soft Anchor Rigging Sling',
      'brand' => 'buckingham',
      'brand_name' => 'Buckingham',
      'kind' => 'rigging-device',
      'price' => 300,
      'keyword' => 'port-a-wrap',
      'title' => 'Port-a-Wrap with Soft Anchor Rigging Sling Kit',
      'h1' => 'Port-a-Wrap with Soft Anchor Rigging Sling',
      'meta' => 'A port-a-wrap friction lowering device paired with a soft anchor rigging sling for mounting it to the trunk. A ready rigging kit for crews lowering wood.',
      'tagline' => 'A friction lowering device and soft anchor sling, ready to mount and rig.',
      'summary' => 'This kit pairs a port-a-wrap friction lowering device with a soft anchor rigging sling, so you have the device and the means to mount it in one purchase. Wrap the sling around the trunk, clip the port-a-wrap on, and the ground worker is ready to lower.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Friction lowering device plus mounting sling',
        1 => 'Soft sling is kind to bark',
        2 => 'Quick to set up at the base of the tree',
        3 => 'Good starter rigging kit for growing crews',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Includes',
          1 => 'Port-a-wrap and soft anchor rigging sling',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Friction lowering device kit',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Mounting',
          1 => 'Sling around trunk',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See markings on each component',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why buy it as a kit',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A lowering device is only as good as its anchor. A soft sling spreads load over the bark, is easy to adjust for different trunk sizes, and packs small. Buying the two together means the sling is sized for the job rather than something grabbed from the truck.',
            1 => 'It is a sensible first rigging setup for a crew moving from roping down limbs on a natural crotch to controlled lowering.',
            2 => 'The soft sling also comes off easily when the job is done and packs into the same bag as the device, so the whole setup lives together on the truck. Nothing gets left behind at the base of a tree.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Setup and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Wrap the sling around the trunk on the side facing the rigging point and connect the port-a-wrap so the line runs straight onto the tube. Take wraps to suit the load and keep the tail under control.',
            1 => 'Inspect the sling for cuts, abrasion and UV fading, and the device for grooves and cracks, before every job. Lowering gear is rigging, not life support. Train ground workers on wraps and line handling, and follow ANSI Z133.',
            2 => 'Size the setup to the work: this kit suits everyday rigging, while very heavy wood calls for larger devices and slings.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a port-a-wrap?',
          'a' => 'A friction lowering device mounted at the base of the tree. Rope wraps around it so the ground worker can control descent.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why use a soft sling to mount it?',
          'a' => 'It adapts to trunk size, protects bark and is easy to carry and inspect.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/buckingham/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-5.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-5-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Nautical #5',
        'creator' => 'Graham B Finney',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188487900@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188487900@N02/51798984894',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/rigging-kits/weaver-speed-line-kit/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/rigging-kits/weaver-speed-line-kit/',
      'category' => '/rigging/rigging-kits/',
      'name' => 'Weaver Speed Line Kit',
      'brand' => 'weaver',
      'brand_name' => 'Weaver',
      'kind' => 'rope',
      'price' => 280,
      'keyword' => 'speed line',
      'title' => 'Weaver Speed Line Kit for Arborist Brush Removal',
      'h1' => 'Weaver Speed Line Kit',
      'meta' => 'The Weaver speed line kit puts the rope and hardware for a speedline in one bag. How a speed line moves brush to the drop zone, how to rig it, and safe limits.',
      'tagline' => 'A packaged speedline setup from Weaver for zipping brush to the drop zone.',
      'summary' => 'A speed line lets you send brush down a tensioned rope from the canopy to a landing spot well away from the tree, and the Weaver speedline kit packages what you need to rig one. It is the answer for removals where the drop zone under the tree is crowded with roofs, decks and plantings.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Packaged speedline setup from Weaver',
        1 => 'Moves brush over targets to a clear landing zone',
        2 => 'Speeds cleanup by landing brush in one spot',
        3 => 'Keeps lowering line free for wood',
        4 => 'Comes together in one bag for the truck',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Weaver',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Speedline kit',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Zip-lining brush and light wood',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Anchoring',
          1 => 'High in tree, low at landing zone',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See markings on each component',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why run a speed line',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On a tight lot, getting brush to the ground is easy. Getting it to the ground without hitting the house, the fence and the flower beds is the hard part. A speed line runs from a high anchor in the tree to a low anchor at the landing zone, and each bundle of brush slides down it, clearing everything underneath.',
            1 => 'It changes the pace of a job. The climber keeps cutting and sending, the ground crew keeps clearing the landing zone, and the brush ends up next to the chipper instead of spread across the yard. Meanwhile the main lowering line stays free for wood.',
            2 => 'It is also a cleaner job for the client. Fewer crushed shrubs and dented gutters means fewer callbacks and a better reputation for the crew. Many arborists who try a speed line on one tight job end up keeping a kit in the truck permanently.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Rigging it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pick the high anchor above the work in sound wood, and the low anchor at the landing zone: a stump, another tree or a ground anchor your company approves. The line angle sets the speed. A steep line delivers fast and hard; a shallow one may stall loads halfway.',
            1 => 'Tension the line with a mechanical advantage system or with wraps on a lowering device at the low end. A controllable low anchor lets you slacken the line to bring loads down gently into the pile. Over-tensioning multiplies forces on both anchors, so tension only enough to clear the targets.',
            2 => 'For a store-assembled alternative, compare the <a href="/rigging-kits/speed-line-kit-with-carabiners/">speed line kit with carabiners</a>.',
            3 => 'Protect the bark at the high anchor with a sling or friction saver rather than running the line around a bare limb, and use a sling sized for the forces a loaded speed line can put on it.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Sending loads',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Clip a pulley or carabiner to the line first, then to the brush with a sling. Keep loads small and trimmed so they ride clean. Several light bundles are easier on the anchors and the crew than one heavy limb. Have extra connectors on the line so the climber is not waiting for gear to come back.',
            1 => 'Everyone stays out from under the line and the landing zone while loads are moving. Agree on calls for sending, clear and stop. If a load hangs up halfway, do not stand under it to free it; use the low anchor to slack and lower it.',
            2 => 'Strip awkward side branches so bundles ride without catching. A tangle of twigs dragging along the line slows everything and wears the rope.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safety and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A speed line is rigging, not a life-support system. Never clip a person to it. Check the rope, slings, pulleys and carabiners before every job, and retire anything cut, glazed or deformed. Re-check the anchors and tension after the first few loads, since rope stretches and slings settle.',
            1 => 'Train the crew to set up and run a speed line before using one over property, and work to ANSI Z133. The line cuts a job\'s risk only when everyone knows how it works and where to stand.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a speed line in tree work?',
          'a' => 'A tensioned rope from a high anchor in the tree to a low anchor at the landing zone, used to slide brush away from targets.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How steep should a speed line be?',
          'a' => 'Steep enough that loads slide without stalling, shallow enough that they arrive under control. A controllable low anchor lets you adjust.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I send wood down a speed line?',
          'a' => 'Light wood, yes. Heavy pieces put large forces on the anchors; use the lowering line for those.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/weaver/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Coiling the Rope',
        'creator' => 'blachswan',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40883175@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40883175@N06/34254142686',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/rigging-slings/adjustable-slings/3-4-whoopie-sling/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/adjustable-slings/3-4-whoopie-sling/',
      'category' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/adjustable-slings/',
      'name' => '3/4" Whoopie Sling',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 85,
      'keyword' => 'whoopie sling',
      'title' => '3/4" Whoopie Sling for Heavy Rigging Anchors',
      'h1' => '3/4" Whoopie Sling',
      'meta' => 'The 3/4 in whoopie sling is an adjustable rope sling for heavy rigging anchors. How a whoopie sling adjusts, why sailors and arborists use them, and inspection.',
      'tagline' => 'An adjustable 3/4 in rope sling for anchoring heavy rigging blocks.',
      'summary' => 'A 3/4 in whoopie sling is an adjustable rope sling made from hollow-braid rope, with a fixed eye at one end and an adjustable eye at the other. Arborists use them to hang heavy rigging blocks on trunks of any size, and the same design is popular in sailing for adjustable rigging.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Adjustable length fits a wide range of trunk sizes',
        1 => '3/4 in diameter for heavy rigging',
        2 => 'Hollow-braid construction adjusts by hand',
        3 => 'Fixed eye and adjustable eye',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '3/4 in',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Construction',
          1 => 'Hollow-braid rope, adjustable',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ends',
          1 => 'Fixed eye and adjustable eye',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See tag and maker\'s information',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How a whoopie sling works',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A whoopie sling uses a hollow-braid rope buried inside itself. Slide the bury to make the adjustable eye larger or smaller; under load the outer braid squeezes down on the inner section and holds. The result is one sling that fits a small stem or a big trunk without wasting length or wraps.',
            1 => 'Sailors use the same trick for adjustable shrouds and lashings, which is why you see the term whoopie sling with sailing. In tree work the 3/4 in size is for heavy rigging blocks and big wood.',
            2 => 'Because it adjusts, one whoopie can do the work of several fixed-length slings, which saves space and weight in the rigging bag. It is also kind to bark, since there is no long tail wrapped around the trunk.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Wrap the trunk, pass the fixed eye through the adjustable eye or connect with a shackle or carabiner, and snug it down. Make sure the adjustable section has the minimum bury the maker specifies before loading.',
            1 => 'Check for cuts, abrasion, glazing and stiff or crushed spots before each use. Retire a sling that has been overloaded. Rigging slings are not life support; size every part of the system for the load and work to ANSI Z133.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why is it called a whoopie sling?',
          'a' => 'It is a traditional name for an adjustable buried-braid sling, used for years in sailing and now common in tree rigging.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => '3/4 in or 5/8 in whoopie sling?',
          'a' => 'The 3/4 in suits heavy rigging and big blocks. The 5/8 in is lighter for everyday rigging.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Untitled',
        'creator' => 'Unknown',
        'creator_url' => '',
        'license' => 'CC0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/5948349/free-public-domain-cc0-photo',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/rigging-slings/adjustable-slings/5-8-whoopie-sling/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/adjustable-slings/5-8-whoopie-sling/',
      'category' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/adjustable-slings/',
      'name' => '5/8" Whoopie Sling',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 75,
      'keyword' => 'whoopie sling',
      'title' => '5/8" Whoopie Sling: Adjustable Arborist Rigging Sling',
      'h1' => '5/8" Whoopie Sling',
      'meta' => 'The 5/8 in whoopie sling is an adjustable arborist rigging sling for blocks and lowering devices. How it adjusts, how to set it on a trunk, and when to retire.',
      'tagline' => 'An adjustable 5/8 in rope sling for hanging blocks on any trunk size.',
      'summary' => 'The 5/8 in whoopie sling is an adjustable arborist rigging sling: a hollow-braid rope with a fixed eye and an adjustable eye, sized for the everyday rigging most crews do. One sling fits stems from small to large, so you carry fewer slings and set blocks faster.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Adjusts by hand to fit different trunk sizes',
        1 => '5/8 in diameter for everyday rigging',
        2 => 'Fixed eye and adjustable eye',
        3 => 'Lighter than heavy-duty 3/4 in slings',
        4 => 'Packs small in the rigging bag',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '5/8 in',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Construction',
          1 => 'Hollow-braid rope, adjustable',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ends',
          1 => 'Fixed eye and adjustable eye',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Typical use',
          1 => 'Hanging rigging blocks and lowering devices',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See tag and maker\'s information',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why crews carry whoopie slings',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A fixed-length sling either fits the trunk or it does not. Too long and you are taking wraps to use up the extra; too short and it will not go round. A whoopie sling adjusts. Pull the adjustable eye open, wrap the stem, choke or connect it, then slide it tight. Under load the braid grips itself and holds.',
            1 => 'That flexibility is why the whoopie sling is one of the most common arborist slings. It handles block anchors high in the tree, base anchors for lowering devices, and redirects, all with one piece.',
            2 => 'Compared with steel chain or webbing, rope slings are lighter, easier on bark and simpler to inspect by hand. A couple of 5/8 in whoopies in the bag handle most of what a typical residential crew rigs in a day.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Setting it on the trunk',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Wrap the sling around the stem above a branch collar or crotch that will stop it sliding down, then pass the eye through or connect the eyes with a carabiner or shackle rated for the load. Slide the adjustable eye down until it is snug. Choked around the trunk, it bites tighter as load comes on.',
            1 => 'Hang the block so the rigging line runs cleanly without rubbing the sling. When removing, slack the sling fully before trying to slide the eye open.',
            2 => 'See other adjustable slings in the <a href="/rigging/rigging-slings/adjustable-slings/">adjustable slings</a> section, including the heavier 3/4 in version.',
            3 => 'On a tree you are pruning rather than removing, set the sling over a protective layer or choose a spot where the bark is thick, and avoid sawing the sling around under load, which scuffs bark and wears the sling. On removals, set it where the stem is sound and large enough for the forces the block will see.',
            4 => 'Some climbers like to pre-set the whoopie on the ground at roughly the right size, then fine-tune it once it is around the stem, saving time aloft.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Keeping the adjustment working',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A whoopie needs its minimum bury to hold. Never adjust the sling so short that the buried section is less than the maker\'s minimum. Some slings mark the limit; respect it.',
            1 => 'Dirt and sap inside the braid make adjustment stiff and can wear fibers from the inside. Wash slings in clean water when they get filthy and let them dry out of the sun.',
            2 => 'Store slings coiled loosely in a bag away from fuel, bar oil and battery acid. Chemicals can weaken synthetic fibers without much visible sign, and the truck bed is where that kind of damage happens.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and retirement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Before each job, run the sling through your hands and look for cuts, pulled strands, glazing from friction, stiff or flattened spots and discoloration from chemicals. Check the eyes and the adjustable section closely, since that is where wear concentrates.',
            1 => 'Retire a sling that has been shock loaded beyond its rating or shows damage. Rigging slings are not life support. Size every component for the load, and train crews to ANSI Z133.',
            2 => 'Keep a simple log of each sling\'s purchase date and any heavy loads it has caught. When in doubt, retire it; a new sling costs far less than a failure.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a whoopie sling used for in tree work?',
          'a' => 'Anchoring rigging blocks, lowering devices and redirects to trunks of different sizes with one adjustable sling.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How does a whoopie sling hold its adjustment?',
          'a' => 'The outer braid squeezes the buried inner section under load, gripping it so the eye does not slip.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How short can I adjust a whoopie sling?',
          'a' => 'Only down to the minimum bury the maker specifies. Shorter than that, it may not hold.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Climbing paraphernalia',
        'creator' => 'Ruth and Dave',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/95142644@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/95142644@N00/3584852771',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/5-8-x-20-premium-crane-sling/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/5-8-x-20-premium-crane-sling/',
      'category' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/',
      'name' => '5/8" x 20\' Premium Crane Sling',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 130,
      'keyword' => 'crane sling',
      'title' => '5/8" x 20\' Premium Crane Sling for Tree Picks',
      'h1' => '5/8" x 20\' Premium Crane Sling',
      'meta' => 'The 5/8 in x 20 ft premium crane sling is a rope sling for crane-assisted removals. How crews choke and set a crane sling on picks, and what to inspect daily.',
      'tagline' => 'A 5/8 in x 20 ft rope sling for setting chokers on crane-assisted removals.',
      'summary' => 'This 5/8 in x 20 ft premium crane sling is a rope sling for choking pieces on crane-assisted tree removal. The 20 ft length wraps big wood with room to spare, and rope is easier on the climber\'s hands and the tree than steel.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '5/8 in rope sling for crane picks',
        1 => '20 ft length wraps large stems',
        2 => 'Eyes at each end for choking and hooking',
        3 => 'Lighter to carry aloft than chain or steel',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '5/8 in',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Length',
          1 => '20 ft',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ends',
          1 => 'Eyes at both ends',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Choker for crane-assisted removals',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See tag and maker\'s information',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How crews use a crane sling',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On a crane removal, the climber sets one or two slings on each pick, choked around the piece above its balance point, and hooks the eye onto the crane hook. The crane takes tension before the cut so the piece is supported the moment it comes free.',
            1 => 'A 20 ft sling gives you enough length to choke a large stem or wrap a wide top. Rope is lighter to carry up the tree than steel and does not bite into the climber\'s gloves.',
            2 => 'Many crews carry a matched pair so the climber can set two chokers on long or unbalanced pieces, controlling how the pick hangs once it is free.',
            3 => 'Mark your crane slings so they are not mixed in with everyday rigging gear.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Ratings and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Crane work involves high, sudden loads. Know the weight of each pick, and use slings with a rated capacity for the hitch you are using; a choker hitch carries less than a straight vertical pull. Follow the crane operator\'s and qualified rigger\'s direction on every pick.',
            1 => 'Inspect crane slings daily for cuts, abrasion, heat damage and missing or unreadable tags, and remove damaged slings from service. Follow ANSI Z133 and the crane safety rules that apply to your work.',
            2 => 'Store crane slings clean and dry, coiled loosely, away from fuel and bar oil. Keep the tags legible, and replace a sling whose tag is missing even if it looks fine.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a crane sling used for in tree work?',
          'a' => 'It chokes cut pieces so a crane can hold and lift them free during a removal.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does a choker hitch reduce sling capacity?',
          'a' => 'Yes. A choker hitch has a lower rated capacity than a vertical hitch. Check the sling\'s tag.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'D75_8819-1',
        'creator' => 'Mark F. Levisay',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/31395633@N08',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/31395633@N08/52706266887',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/endless-loop-round-slings/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/endless-loop-round-slings/',
      'category' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/',
      'name' => 'Endless Loop Round Slings',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 35,
      'keyword' => 'round slings',
      'title' => 'Endless Loop Round Slings for Tree Rigging and Cranes',
      'h1' => 'Endless Loop Round Slings',
      'meta' => 'Endless round slings are soft polyester slings for crane picks and heavy rigging. How a round sling is rated by color, how to pick a hitch, and how to inspect.',
      'tagline' => 'Soft endless polyester slings for crane picks, rigging anchors and lifts.',
      'summary' => 'Round slings are endless loops of load-bearing polyester fiber inside a protective cover. They are soft, flexible and grip wood well, which makes them a favorite for crane-assisted removals, heavy rigging anchors and lifting logs with a loader or excavator.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Endless loop, use any part of the sling',
        1 => 'Soft cover protects bark and grips wood',
        2 => 'Color-coded by rated capacity',
        3 => 'Light and flexible for carrying aloft',
        4 => 'Works in vertical, choker and basket hitches',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Endless round sling',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Core',
          1 => 'Polyester load yarns',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Cover',
          1 => 'Protective polyester jacket',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Capacity coding',
          1 => 'Color and tag indicate rating',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Hitches',
          1 => 'Vertical, choker, basket',
        ),
        5 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See tag on each sling',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why tree crews use round slings',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'An endless sling has no eyes to wear out and no fixed contact point. You can rotate it so a different section takes the load each time, which spreads wear. The soft cover conforms to the wood and grips, and it is gentle on bark when you anchor a block to a tree you are keeping.',
            1 => 'On crane removals, round slings are fast to set: choke the piece, drop the loop over the hook and go. With a loader or excavator, they wrap logs without the steel-on-wood slipping you get with chain.',
            2 => 'They also pack small. A few round slings take up less room in the truck than the equivalent steel, and they do not rust or kink.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Reading capacity and choosing a hitch',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Round slings are usually color-coded by rated capacity, and each sling carries a tag with its ratings for vertical, choker and basket hitches. The color helps you pick the right sling quickly, but the tag is what counts. A sling with a missing or unreadable tag should come out of service.',
            1 => 'Hitch choice changes capacity. A choker hitch is rated well below vertical; a basket hitch can be rated higher if the legs are near vertical. Wide sling angles reduce capacity fast. Pick a sling with margin for the hitch you will use, and remember that tree work brings dynamic loads that static lifting tables do not show.',
            2 => 'For rope-based crane slings, see the <a href="/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/5-8-x-20-premium-crane-sling/">5/8 in x 20 ft crane sling</a>.',
            3 => 'When a pick needs two slings, set them so the piece hangs as planned once cut. A choker set too close to the end lets the piece tip; one set near the balance point keeps it level. Talk through each pick with the crane operator and the ground crew before the cut.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Protecting the sling',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The cover protects the core, but a chainsaw cuts through both instantly. Keep the saw well clear of slings, and never cut a piece with the sling across your cut line. Sharp edges, such as metal or a freshly cut stub, can cut a loaded round sling; use wear pads where needed.',
            1 => 'Keep round slings away from heat and chemicals. Bar oil, fuel and battery acid can weaken polyester. Store them dry, out of the sun, in a bag or bin.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Before each use, check the cover for cuts, holes and burns, and feel for knots, lumps or soft spots in the core. Exposed core yarns mean the sling is done. Look for melted or discolored areas from friction.',
            1 => 'Retire any sling that is damaged or has been overloaded. Follow the maker\'s instructions, applicable sling standards and ANSI Z133 for tree work.',
            2 => 'Keep a log of the slings on the truck and when each was put into service. Round slings tend to get used hard on crane days, and a record helps you spot the ones due for closer inspection. Clean mud off with fresh water and let them dry before packing.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between a round sling and an endless sling?',
          'a' => 'Round slings are a type of endless sling: a loop of polyester yarns inside a cover. Endless slings can also be webbing.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What do round sling colors mean?',
          'a' => 'Colors are commonly used to indicate rated capacity, but always confirm with the tag on the sling.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a round sling to anchor a rigging block?',
          'a' => 'Yes, if its rating suits the forces involved. Use wear protection on sharp edges.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Pruning in RVA (Carytown, Virginia)',
        'creator' => 'Gamma Man',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06/48982146938',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/rigging-slings/heavy-duty-slings/1-2-bull-rope-rigging-sling/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/heavy-duty-slings/1-2-bull-rope-rigging-sling/',
      'category' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/heavy-duty-slings/',
      'name' => '1/2" Bull Rope Rigging Sling',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 45,
      'keyword' => 'bull rope rigging sling',
      'title' => '1/2" Bull Rope Rigging Sling for Block Anchors',
      'h1' => '1/2" Bull Rope Rigging Sling',
      'meta' => 'A 1/2 in bull rope rigging sling, the bull tail for tying off blocks and hanging pieces. How to choose a bull tail for rigging and inspect it before each job.',
      'tagline' => 'A 1/2 in rope sling, or bull tail, for anchoring blocks and tying off pieces.',
      'summary' => 'This 1/2 in bull rope rigging sling is a length of rigging rope with an eye spliced in one end, often called a bull tail. Arborists use it to anchor rigging blocks with a timber hitch or wraps, to tie off pieces, and as an all-purpose rigging connection.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Spliced eye at one end, tail at the other',
        1 => 'Ties to the tree with wraps and hitches',
        2 => '1/2 in rope for light to moderate rigging',
        3 => 'Versatile, adjusts to any trunk size',
        4 => 'Easy to inspect by hand',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '1/2 in',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Spliced-eye rope sling (bull tail)',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ends',
          1 => 'Spliced eye and open tail',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Block anchors, tie-offs, redirects',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See tag and maker\'s information',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What a bull tail does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Bull tail rigging is one of the oldest setups in tree work. A short length of strong rope with a spliced eye is tied around the stem, usually with a series of wraps and a timber hitch, cow hitch or similar knot, and the block is clipped to the eye. Because the tail is tied rather than fixed length, it fits any trunk size.',
            1 => 'Bull rope, the strong double-braid line used for rigging, is what these slings are made from. In 1/2 in it suits light to moderate rigging: pruning drops, small removals and redirects.',
            2 => 'It is also the sling to use when you are short on gear. A spare bull tail can stand in for a fixed sling, a tag line anchor or a pull-line tie-off, which is why many climbers keep one clipped to the saddle.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Tying it off',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Wrap the stem enough times to spread the load and stop the sling sliding, then finish with a hitch you were trained on. Leave a reasonable tail. The eye should hang where the block can run without the rigging line rubbing the trunk.',
            1 => 'A bull tail is also handy for tying off a piece before the cut, setting a natural crotch redirect, or anchoring a lowering device on a small tree. See other options in <a href="/rigging/rigging-slings/heavy-duty-slings/">heavy-duty slings</a>.',
            2 => 'Dress the wraps neatly so they lie flat and share the load. Crossed or bunched wraps can slip or pinch. When the job is done, the tied wraps come off quickly, which is part of why bull tails are still popular after decades of new gear.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing size and type',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'For heavier wood, step up to a larger diameter or to a dedicated whoopie or loopie sling. A 1/2 in bull tail is the light end of the range, chosen because it is quick to tie and easy to carry.',
            1 => 'Match the sling\'s rating to the forces in your rigging, not just the weight of the piece. A dropped piece stopped suddenly can put several times its weight into the block and sling.',
            2 => 'Some crews keep a few lengths: a short bull tail for limbs and a longer one for big stems. Mark each one so it is easy to grab the right length from the bag. Spliced eyes should be made by someone trained to splice that rope type.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the splice for pulled or loose tucks, and the rope for cuts, glazing, flattened sections and chemical stains. Retire a sling that has been overloaded or shows core damage. Rigging slings are not life support. Work to ANSI Z133.',
            1 => 'Store it clean and dry with the rest of the rigging gear, and keep it out of the sun when not in use.',
            2 => 'Never use a rigging bull tail as part of a climbing system.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a bull tail in tree rigging?',
          'a' => 'A spliced-eye rope sling that is tied around the stem to anchor a block or tie off a piece.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is 1/2 in rope enough for a rigging sling?',
          'a' => 'For light to moderate rigging, yes, within its rating. Use larger slings for heavier wood.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What knot do I use to tie off a bull tail?',
          'a' => 'Several wraps finished with a timber hitch or similar hitch is common. Use a hitch you have been trained on and dress it well.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Caution - Tree Surgeon at work',
        'creator' => 'Clint__Budd',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/58827557@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/58827557@N06/47249256141',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/rigging-slings/heavy-duty-webbing-slings/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/heavy-duty-webbing-slings/',
      'category' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/',
      'name' => 'Heavy Duty Webbing Slings',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 30,
      'keyword' => 'webbing sling',
      'title' => 'Heavy Duty Webbing Sling for Tree Rigging',
      'h1' => 'Heavy Duty Webbing Slings',
      'meta' => 'A heavy duty webbing sling is a flat sewn arborist sling for block anchors, log lifts and rigging redirects. How to choose one and inspect it before each job.',
      'tagline' => 'Flat sewn webbing slings for block anchors, log lifts and redirects.',
      'summary' => 'A heavy duty webbing sling is a flat strap of sewn synthetic webbing that wraps the trunk or a log and grips well. Arborists use webbing slings to anchor blocks, set redirects, and lift or drag logs, and they lie flat against bark for less wear on trees you are keeping.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Flat webbing spreads load across bark',
        1 => 'Sewn eyes for connecting hardware',
        2 => 'Light and easy to carry',
        3 => 'Works as anchor, choker or basket sling',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Sewn webbing sling',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ends',
          1 => 'Sewn eyes',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Hitches',
          1 => 'Vertical, choker, basket',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See tag on each sling',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where a webbing sling fits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Flat webbing is a good arborist sling where you want grip and bark protection. It wraps a stem or a log like a belt, so it spreads pressure and is less likely to slide. Use one as a block anchor on trees you are pruning, as a choker on logs for the loader, or as a basket around a short round.',
            1 => 'Webbing packs flat and dries quickly, and a few in the truck cover a lot of jobs.',
            2 => 'Webbing also works well for lifting logs with a loader or excavator, where its wide contact grips bark and reduces slipping. Pick lengths that suit the diameters you usually handle.',
            3 => 'Keep them separate from climbing gear.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Ratings and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Each sling carries a tag with its rated capacity by hitch. Choose a sling with margin for the hitch and the dynamic loads of tree work. Keep chainsaws and sharp edges well away; webbing cuts easily under load.',
            1 => 'Before each use, check for cuts, burns, broken stitching, holes and a readable tag. Retire damaged slings. Rigging slings are not life support; follow ANSI Z133.',
            2 => 'Store webbing out of sunlight, since UV weakens synthetic fibers over time.',
            3 => 'Wash muddy slings in clean water and let them dry before packing.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Webbing sling or rope sling?',
          'a' => 'Webbing grips and spreads load across bark; rope slings handle abrasion and are easier to adjust and inspect.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What does the tag on a webbing sling mean?',
          'a' => 'It lists rated capacities for each hitch type. A sling without a readable tag should be removed from service.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Untitled',
        'creator' => 'Unknown',
        'creator_url' => '',
        'license' => 'CC0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/5948349/free-public-domain-cc0-photo',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/rigging-slings/light-duty-slings/cmi-heavy-duty-loop-runners/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/light-duty-slings/cmi-heavy-duty-loop-runners/',
      'category' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/light-duty-slings/',
      'name' => 'CMI Heavy Duty Loop Runners',
      'brand' => 'cmi',
      'brand_name' => 'CMI',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 18,
      'keyword' => 'loop runner',
      'title' => 'CMI Heavy Duty Loop Runner Slings',
      'h1' => 'CMI Heavy Duty Loop Runners',
      'meta' => 'CMI heavy duty loop runners are sewn webbing loops for anchors, redirects and light rigging. How arborists use a loop runner and what to check before each job.',
      'tagline' => 'Sewn webbing loops for quick anchors, redirects and light rigging.',
      'summary' => 'A CMI heavy duty loop runner is a sewn loop of webbing that you girth hitch, basket or choke around a limb to make a quick anchor. Arborists use loop runners for redirects, light rigging, anchoring pulleys and gear on the ground.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Sewn webbing loop, no knots to tie',
        1 => 'Girth hitch, basket or choke around limbs',
        2 => 'Light and quick to carry on the saddle',
        3 => 'CMI build quality',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'CMI',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Sewn webbing loop runner',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Hitches',
          1 => 'Girth, choker, basket',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'Marked on the runner',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How arborists use a loop runner',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A loop runner is the quickest anchor you can carry. Girth hitch it around a limb and clip a pulley for a redirect, or basket it around a stub to anchor a light rigging point. On the ground, use it to anchor a pulley at the base of a tree for a pull line.',
            1 => 'Because they are sewn, there is no knot to untie after a load, and they weigh almost nothing on the saddle.',
            2 => 'Carry a few in different lengths so you can wrap both small limbs and larger stems. Color-coding by length makes it easy to grab the right one from the saddle.',
            3 => 'They also make tidy gear loops on the ground.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and limits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Girth hitches reduce a runner\'s strength compared with a straight pull, and sharp edges reduce it more. Check the marking for its rating and use runners within it. Inspect the stitching and webbing for cuts, burns and fraying before use.',
            1 => 'If a runner is part of a climbing system, it must be rated for life support and used as CMI instructs. Keep rigging and climbing gear separate, and train to ANSI Z133.',
            2 => 'Wash dirty runners in clean water and air dry them.',
            3 => 'Retire any runner with a faded or missing marking.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a loop runner?',
          'a' => 'A sewn loop of webbing used as a quick sling for anchors and redirects.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a loop runner for climbing?',
          'a' => 'Only if it is rated for life support and used per CMI\'s instructions. Keep rigging runners separate.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/cmi/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Climbing paraphernalia',
        'creator' => 'Ruth and Dave',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/95142644@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/95142644@N00/3584852771',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/rigging-slings/ring-slings/double-head-rigging-sling-3-4-x-25-long/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/ring-slings/double-head-rigging-sling-3-4-x-25-long/',
      'category' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/heavy-duty-slings/',
      'name' => 'Double-Head Rigging Sling, 3/4 in x 25 ft',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 85,
      'keyword' => 'rigging sling',
      'title' => 'Double-Head Rigging Sling 3/4 in x 25 ft',
      'h1' => 'Double-Head Rigging Sling, 3/4 in x 25 ft',
      'meta' => 'A 3/4 in x 25 ft double-head rigging sling for tying off big wood, choking stems and setting rigging points on removals. Typical price and buying notes.',
      'tagline' => 'A long, heavy rigging sling with an eye at each end for big removals and wide stems.',
      'summary' => 'This double-head rigging sling gives you 25 feet of 3/4 in rope with a spliced eye at each end, so you can wrap a wide trunk, choke a heavy leader or set a high rigging point without tying knots in your main line. It is built for crews that regularly bring down big wood.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Spliced eye on both ends',
        1 => '3/4 in diameter for heavy rigging',
        2 => '25 ft length wraps wide stems',
        3 => 'Works as a choker, basket or girth hitch',
        4 => 'Saves your rigging line from knots and wear',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '3/4 in',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Length',
          1 => '25 ft',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ends',
          1 => 'Spliced eye on each end',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How crews use a long double-eye sling',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On a removal, the sling usually goes around the stem above a crotch or a stout limb to set a block, or it gets choked around a piece you are about to cut so the rigging line has something clean to clip to. Twenty-five feet is long enough to go around trunks that a short sling simply will not reach, and you can still double it up on smaller wood.',
            1 => 'Because both ends are spliced eyes, you can choke the sling through itself, pass one eye through the other, or clip both eyes into a block or carabiner for a basket. Pair it with a rigging block or ring and a dedicated <a href="/rope/rigging-rope/samson-1-2-rigging-rope/">rigging rope</a> and you have the core of a lowering setup.',
            2 => 'On the ground, a long sling also makes a solid anchor for a lowering device or a pull line when the base of the tree is too wide for a short strap.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Picking the right sling and keeping it in service',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Size the sling to the load, not the other way around. Heavy pieces, shock loading and sharp bend angles all eat into working strength, so leave a generous margin and follow the manufacturer\'s rated loads and the ANSI Z133 standard. Only trained crew members should design rigging systems.',
            1 => 'Inspect the sling before each job for cuts, glazing, melted fibers, pulled strands and wear at the splices. Retire it if you find damage you cannot explain. Keep it away from saw chain and bar oil, and let it dry before storing it in a rope bag.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What does double-head mean on a rigging sling?',
          'a' => 'It means the sling has an eye spliced into both ends, so you can choke it, basket it or clip both eyes into one connector without tying a knot.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a rigging sling for climbing?',
          'a' => 'No. Rigging slings are for moving wood, not for life support. Climbing anchors need gear rated and sold for that purpose, used by a trained climber.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'felling',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'D75_8819-1',
        'creator' => 'Mark F. Levisay',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/31395633@N08',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/31395633@N08/52706266887',
      ),
    ),
    '/rope-bags/courant-cargo-bag-50l-blue/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rope-bags/courant-cargo-bag-50l-blue/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/rope-bags/',
      'name' => 'Courant Cargo Bag 50L, Blue',
      'brand' => 'courant',
      'brand_name' => 'Courant',
      'kind' => 'case-bag',
      'price' => 110,
      'keyword' => 'courant bag',
      'title' => 'Courant Bag: Cargo 50L Rope and Gear Bag, Blue',
      'h1' => 'Courant Cargo Bag 50L (Blue)',
      'meta' => 'This Courant bag, the Cargo 50L in blue, is a tough, open-top gear and rope bag for climbers who want saddle, lines and hardware in one place on the job.',
      'tagline' => 'A 50-liter carry bag that swallows a climbing line, saddle and hardware.',
      'summary' => 'The Courant bag known as the Cargo is a 50-liter carryall built for tree work, with room for a climbing line, a saddle and a pile of hardware in one load. The blue color makes it easy to spot in a truck bed or at the base of the tree.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '50 liter capacity',
        1 => 'Wide opening for fast packing',
        2 => 'Carry handles for truck-to-tree hauls',
        3 => 'Bright blue color is easy to find on site',
        4 => 'Holds rope, saddle and hardware together',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Capacity',
          1 => '50 L',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Color',
          1 => 'Blue',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Courant',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a dedicated gear bag helps',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Life-support gear lasts longer and stays easier to inspect when it is not rattling around a truck box with saws, wedges and fuel. A bag like the Cargo keeps your climbing line, saddle and connectors together, off the ground and away from bar oil and two-stroke mix.',
            1 => 'At 50 liters it handles a full climbing kit for a day of pruning or a removal. Many climbers keep one bag for the main line and saddle and a second smaller bag for throw line and spare hardware.',
            2 => 'A bag with a wide mouth also makes the end of the day faster. Flake the rope straight in, drop the saddle on top and zip or cinch it, rather than coiling a line that will kink on the next job.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Living with it on the job',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pack the rope loosely so it feeds out without kinks, and set the bag where it will not get run over by the chipper or buried under brush. Let wet gear dry before it goes back in for the night.',
            1 => 'A good bag does not replace inspection. Pull your gear out regularly, check it against the manufacturer\'s instructions, and retire anything that fails.',
            2 => 'If you share a truck with other climbers, a distinct color like this blue makes it obvious whose kit is whose, which helps keep personal life-support gear with the person who inspects it.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What fits in a 50L Courant Cargo bag?',
          'a' => 'A typical climbing line of around 150 ft, a saddle, a few carabiners, a friction device and lanyard will usually fit, depending on rope diameter and how you pack it.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the Cargo bag waterproof?',
          'a' => 'Treat it as weather resistant rather than waterproof. Dry wet rope and saddles before storing them in any bag.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/courant/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Climbing Gear for Mt. Fuji',
        'creator' => 'KE-TA',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/41894154003@N01',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/41894154003@N01/1262155760',
      ),
    ),
    '/rope-lanyards/replacement-rope-lanyard-without-snaps/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rope-lanyards/replacement-rope-lanyard-without-snaps/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/fliplines-and-lanyards/rope-lanyards/',
      'name' => 'Replacement Rope Lanyard (No Snaps)',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'lanyard',
      'price' => 55,
      'keyword' => 'replacement rope lanyard',
      'title' => 'Replacement Rope Lanyard Without Snaps',
      'h1' => 'Replacement Rope Lanyard Without Snaps',
      'meta' => 'A replacement rope lanyard without snaps lets you rebuild a worn flipline with your own connectors and adjuster. What to look for, sizing and safety.',
      'tagline' => 'Rope-only lanyard for climbers rebuilding a worn flipline with their own hardware.',
      'summary' => 'A replacement rope lanyard without snaps is the rope section of a flipline sold on its own, so you can swap out a worn or damaged lanyard and keep using connectors and an adjuster you already trust. If you have cut, glazed or simply lost the rope part of your lanyard, this is the part you need.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Rope only, no snaps or carabiners included',
        1 => 'Lets you reuse inspected hardware',
        2 => 'Pairs with a mechanical adjuster or friction hitch',
        3 => 'Cheaper than a complete new lanyard kit',
        4 => 'Common lengths suit most spar and limb work',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Rope lanyard, no hardware',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Flipline / work positioning',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Ends',
          1 => 'Varies by build; check listing',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Hardware',
          1 => 'Not included',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Standard',
          1 => 'Must meet ANSI Z133 as assembled',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What this lanyard is for',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The flipline is the piece of gear that keeps you on the stem while you climb spurs on a removal or position yourself for a cut. It takes a beating: bark grit, saw near-misses, pitch and constant cycling through an adjuster. Most climbers wear out the rope long before the snaps and adjuster are done.',
            1 => 'A replacement rope lanyard without snaps covers that situation. You keep your locking carabiners or snap and your adjuster, and replace only the rope. It is also a practical fix if the rope has gone missing from your kit or you want a second length for big trunks.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing length and setup',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pick a length that wraps the biggest wood you regularly climb with some tail left to manage. A shorter lanyard is easier to handle on small spars; a longer one gives you options on wide trunks and lets you double it up. Many climbers carry two: a standard flipline and a long one.',
            1 => 'Check that the rope diameter matches your adjuster or hitch. A rope that is too thin or too thick for a mechanical adjuster can slip or jam. If you run a friction hitch instead, follow the hitch cord and rope combination the manufacturers recommend.',
            2 => 'Some climbers like a lanyard with a spliced eye on one end and a knotted or stitched termination on the other, while others want eyes on both ends so they can choose how to clip it. Look at how your old lanyard was built and match it, or have a qualified person advise you before changing the design.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Assembly and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Because this is life-support equipment, the finished lanyard has to be assembled correctly with compatible, rated hardware. If you are not sure how to terminate the rope or which connectors are approved, have a qualified person do it or buy a complete lanyard. Life-support gear has to meet the applicable standard (ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions), be inspected before every use, and be used by trained climbers.',
            1 => 'Never climb on a lanyard you cannot verify. Steel-core flipline is the usual choice when you are cutting close to the lanyard on removals; a rope lanyard without a core must be kept well clear of the saw. Pair the flipline with a second attachment when you cut, as Z133 requires two points of attachment for chainsaw work aloft.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and retirement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Look at the full length before each climb. Retire it if you see cuts, melted or glazed sections, core showing through the cover, stiff spots or heavy contamination from fuel and bar oil. Keep the lanyard out of the sun when not in use and store it dry in a <a href="/rope-bags/courant-cargo-bag-50l-blue/">gear bag</a>.',
            1 => 'Write down when each new lanyard goes into service. Fliplines often get retired on condition alone, but knowing the age helps you spot one that has been in the kit too long.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does this lanyard come with snaps or carabiners?',
          'a' => 'No. It is the rope only. You supply compatible, rated connectors and an adjuster, or have a qualified person assemble it.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'I lost the strap of my lanyard. Can I just replace the rope?',
          'a' => 'Yes, if your hardware is still in good condition and inspected. Replace the rope section with one that matches your adjuster and connectors, and assemble it correctly before climbing.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should a rope lanyard be replaced?',
          'a' => 'There is no fixed date. Replace it whenever inspection turns up damage, contamination or heavy wear, and follow the manufacturer\'s service life guidance.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I cut near a rope lanyard?',
          'a' => 'Keep the saw well away from any rope lanyard. For close cutting on removals, many climbers prefer a steel-core flipline, plus a second point of attachment.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'TVCF004',
        'creator' => 'Symic',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00/33549425170',
      ),
    ),
    '/rope/accessory-cord/sterling-shock-cord-by-the-foot/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rope/accessory-cord/sterling-shock-cord-by-the-foot/',
      'category' => '/rope/tech-cordage/',
      'name' => 'Sterling Shock Cord (By the Foot)',
      'brand' => 'sterling-rope',
      'brand_name' => 'Sterling Rope',
      'kind' => 'rope',
      'price' => 1,
      'keyword' => 'bungee cord by the foot',
      'title' => 'Sterling Shock Cord: Bungee Cord by the Foot',
      'h1' => 'Sterling Shock Cord, Bungee Cord by the Foot',
      'meta' => 'Sterling shock cord is bungee cord by the foot for gear loops, tool tethers, rope bag closures and truck tie-downs. Cut exactly the length you need.',
      'tagline' => 'Stretchy accessory cord cut to length for gear tethers, bag closures and tie-downs.',
      'summary' => 'Sterling shock cord is bungee cord by the foot, so you can cut exactly what you need for gear loops, tool keepers, bag closures and truck organization. It is a utility cord for the shop and the truck, not a life-support line.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Sold by the foot, cut to length',
        1 => 'Elastic core with a braided cover',
        2 => 'Handy for tool tethers and bag closures',
        3 => 'Keeps the truck and trailer organized',
        4 => 'Not for climbing or rigging loads',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Elastic shock cord',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sold',
          1 => 'By the foot',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Sterling Rope',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where shock cord earns its keep',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Most crews find a dozen uses for a few feet of bungee cord: a keeper on a handsaw scabbard, a closure on a throw-line cube, a stretch tether for a radio, or a quick tie-down to keep wedges and files from sliding around the truck box.',
            1 => 'Buying it by the foot means you are not stuck with a pack of pre-made bungees with hooks that never fit. Tie your own ends, add cord locks or hog rings, and make exactly what the job needs.',
            2 => 'On the climbing side, a short loop of shock cord on a saddle ring can keep a spare carabiner or a hand-saw lanyard tidy, as long as nothing load-bearing ever depends on it. In the shop, a few lengths keep coiled rigging lines and rolled-up tarps from coming undone between jobs.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What it is not for',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Shock cord stretches by design and has a low strength compared with climbing or rigging rope. Never use it for life support, rigging loads or anything where a failure could drop a person or a piece of wood. Keep it to organization and light-duty tasks.',
            1 => 'UV and heat break down the elastic over time. When the cord stops springing back or the cover starts to fray, cut a fresh piece.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use shock cord as a tool lanyard aloft?',
          'a' => 'Only as a light tether for small items if it is rated for that use. For saws and heavier tools, use a lanyard made and rated for tools aloft.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I finish the ends of shock cord?',
          'a' => 'Most people tie a knot, use a hog ring or crimp, or add a cord lock. Seal the cut ends with heat to stop the cover from fraying.',
        ),
      ),
