    '/safety/eye-protection/erb-safety-o-n-e-live-free-camo-safety-glasses/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/safety/eye-protection/erb-safety-o-n-e-live-free-camo-safety-glasses/',
      'category' => '/safety/eye-protection/',
      'name' => 'ERB Safety O.N.E. Live Free Camo Safety Glasses',
      'brand' => 'erb-safety',
      'brand_name' => 'ERB Safety',
      'kind' => 'eye-ear',
      'price' => 9,
      'keyword' => 'erb safety',
      'title' => 'ERB Safety O.N.E. Live Free Camo Safety Glasses',
      'h1' => 'ERB Safety O.N.E. Live Free Camo Safety Glasses',
      'meta' => 'ERB Safety O.N.E. Live Free camo safety glasses are light, low-cost eye protection for climbing, chipping and brush work. Fit, lens choice and care.',
      'tagline' => 'Light, low-cost camo safety glasses for everyday tree work.',
      'summary' => 'The ERB Safety O.N.E. Live Free glasses are camo safety glasses that give you light, comfortable eye protection for climbing, chipping and dragging brush. They are cheap enough to keep a few pairs in the truck so nobody works without them.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Camo frame with a wraparound style',
        1 => 'Lightweight for all-day wear',
        2 => 'Low cost, easy to stock for a crew',
        3 => 'Works with most helmets and earmuffs',
        4 => 'Good for chipping, brush and ground work',
        5 => 'Check lens markings for the rating',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'ERB Safety',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Style',
          1 => 'Wraparound safety glasses',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Frame',
          1 => 'Camo',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rating',
          1 => 'Check frame and lens markings',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Lens',
          1 => 'Check listing for tint',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why eye protection matters on every job',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Sawdust, bark flakes, twigs and chipper spit are constant on a tree job. One bad hit can take you out of work for weeks. Safety glasses are the simplest piece of protective gear there is, and they only work if they are on your face.',
            1 => 'That is where inexpensive, comfortable glasses earn their place. If they are light and do not fog or pinch, people keep wearing them all day.',
            2 => 'Chipper work deserves special mention. Chips and twigs kick back out of the infeed with real force, and a stray piece can hit you from an angle you do not expect. Wraparound glasses give better side coverage than flat lenses for that reason.',
            3 => 'Grinding stumps and running a blower kick up the same kind of debris, and glasses are the minimum for both.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What the O.N.E. Live Free brings',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'ERB safety glasses are a common choice for crews that buy in bulk. The O.N.E. Live Free has a wraparound shape for side coverage and a camo frame that a lot of people simply like. It is light enough to wear under a helmet visor and slim enough to sit under earmuff cushions without breaking the seal badly.',
            1 => 'Check the frame and lens markings for the impact rating before you rely on them for saw work, and choose a lens tint suited to the light. A dark lens is fine in full sun but a bad idea under a heavy canopy or at dusk.',
            2 => 'Keep a box of spares in the truck. Glasses get lost, sat on and scratched, and a spare on hand means nobody has an excuse to work without them. A cord or retainer helps keep them on your neck when you push them up between cuts.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Glasses and visors together',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Mesh helmet visors like the <a href="/safety/eye-protection/kask-zenith-visor/">Kask Zenith visor</a> stop larger chips but let fine dust through. Many climbers wear safety glasses under a mesh visor for that reason. On the ground, around the chipper and when grinding stumps, glasses plus a face shield is the safer combination.',
            1 => 'Follow your employer\'s PPE policy and the ANSI Z133 requirements for eye and face protection for the task.',
            2 => 'If you wear prescription glasses, look at over-the-glasses safety frames or prescription safety lenses instead of trying to stack two pairs.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care and replacement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Rinse grit off before wiping the lens, or you will scratch it. Store them in a pouch or case rather than loose in the truck. Replace any pair with deep scratches, cracks or a loose frame. At this price there is no reason to work through a scuffed lens.',
            1 => 'If fogging is a problem, look for an anti-fog lens or an anti-fog treatment. Glasses that fog get pushed up and left there, which defeats the purpose.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Are ERB safety glasses ANSI rated?',
          'a' => 'Many ERB glasses are marked for ANSI Z87.1. Check the markings on the specific frame and lens you receive.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I wear camo safety glasses under a helmet visor?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Many climbers wear safety glasses under a mesh visor to block fine dust that gets through the mesh.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Are tinted safety glasses OK for tree work?',
          'a' => 'In bright sun, yes. Under a dense canopy or in low light, use a clear or light lens so you can see hazards.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I replace safety glasses?',
          'a' => 'Whenever the lens is scratched enough to affect your vision, or the frame is cracked or loose.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'safety',
        1 => 'ground',
        2 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/erb/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/eye-ear-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/eye-ear-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Sharpening Chainsaw Blades',
        'creator' => 'NCDOTcommunications',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03/36914543386',
      ),
    ),
    '/safety/eye-protection/kask-zenith-visor/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/safety/eye-protection/kask-zenith-visor/',
      'category' => '/safety/eye-protection/',
      'name' => 'Kask Zenith Visor',
      'brand' => 'kask',
      'brand_name' => 'Kask',
      'kind' => 'eye-ear',
      'price' => 45,
      'keyword' => 'kask visor',
      'title' => 'Kask Visor: Zenith Clear Eye Visor for Kask Helmets',
      'h1' => 'Kask Visor for the Zenith Helmet',
      'meta' => 'This Kask visor for the Zenith helmet is a clear eye-protection shield that flips up when you do not need it. Fit, uses, care and typical price for climbers.',
      'tagline' => 'A clear flip-up eye visor for climbers wearing Kask helmets.',
      'summary' => 'This Kask visor is a clear eye-protection shield made for the Kask Zenith helmet and compatible Kask models, and it flips up out of the way when you do not need it. Climbers like it because it keeps eye protection on the helmet instead of in a pocket.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Clear visor for eye protection',
        1 => 'Mounts to compatible Kask helmets',
        2 => 'Flips up when not needed',
        3 => 'Always on the helmet, never lost in a pocket',
        4 => 'Works with many earmuffs',
        5 => 'Replaceable when scratched',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Kask',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Clear eye visor',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fit',
          1 => 'Compatible Kask helmets, check listing',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Mount',
          1 => 'Flip-up',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Lens',
          1 => 'Clear',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a helmet-mounted visor',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Safety glasses get pushed up, fogged or left on the dashboard. A visor that lives on your helmet is there every time you need it, and you can flip it down in one move before starting the saw or pushing through a dense crown.',
            1 => 'Many climbers who search for Kask visors want exactly that: eye protection that does not depend on remembering a separate pair of glasses.',
            2 => 'A clear visor also gives some protection to the face from flying bark and twigs, which glasses alone do not. When you push through a dense crown, a flipped-down visor keeps small branches out of your eyes.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and compatibility',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Kask makes several visors and helmets, and not every visor fits every shell. Check the listing and Kask\'s compatibility chart for your helmet model before ordering. If you are looking for the Kask Zenith X2 visor specifically, confirm the version matches your helmet\'s mount.',
            1 => 'Make sure the visor sits clear of your nose and glasses and does not interfere with earmuffs. If you wear prescription glasses, test the fit before your first climb.',
            2 => 'Climbers who wear glasses should check the visor does not press on the frames or fog up from breath. Mounting adjustments and the right visor version usually solve both problems.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Visor, glasses or both',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A clear visor stops chips and twigs from reaching your eyes and gives some face coverage. Fine dust can still get around the edges, so on dusty tasks some climbers wear safety glasses underneath, such as the <a href="/safety/eye-protection/erb-safety-o-n-e-live-free-camo-safety-glasses/">ERB O.N.E. Live Free</a>.',
            1 => 'Follow your employer\'s PPE policy and the ANSI Z133 requirements for eye and face protection for the task, and check the visor\'s markings for the rating it carries.',
            2 => 'Mesh visors and clear visors each have a place. Mesh lets air through and does not fog, but fine dust gets through. A clear visor blocks dust better but can fog on humid days. Some climbers own both and switch with the season or the task.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Rinse dust off with water before wiping, and use a soft cloth so you do not scratch the lens. Avoid solvents and harsh cleaners that can cloud or weaken polycarbonate. Replace the visor when scratches start to affect your view or if it cracks.',
            1 => 'Check the hinge and mounting points from time to time. A loose visor that drops unexpectedly or will not stay up is a distraction aloft. Keep a spare visor in the truck if you climb every day, since scratches build up fast in sawdust.',
            2 => 'When the helmet goes in the truck, set it so the visor is not pressed against tools or rough surfaces.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does the Kask Zenith visor fit all Kask helmets?',
          'a' => 'No. Check Kask\'s compatibility list for your helmet model and visor version before ordering.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Should I wear safety glasses under a Kask visor?',
          'a' => 'On dusty tasks, many climbers do. A visor blocks chips and debris, while glasses help keep fine dust out.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I clean a Kask visor without scratching it?',
          'a' => 'Rinse with water first, then wipe gently with a soft cloth. Avoid solvents.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the Kask visor rated eye protection?',
          'a' => 'Check the markings on the visor you receive for the standard it meets.',
