          1 => 'Oval',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate',
          1 => 'Triple-action auto-locking',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where an oval earns its spot',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Clip a micro pulley onto a D-shaped carabiner and it slides toward the narrow end, twisting the pulley. On an oval it sits square, and the load stays even. That simple trait makes ovals the go-to connector for hitch tending pulleys, rope wrench tethers and swivel setups.',
            1 => 'The triple-action gate requires two moves before it opens, which keeps it closed when it rubs against rope, bark or other gear. ISC\'s aluminum version keeps weight down on a full saddle.',
            2 => 'A few spare ovals in the truck make it easy to rebuild a hitch system, add a redirect or set up a pulley for a light lowering job. Keep them clean, out of the saw bucket and away from fuel and bar oil, and retire any that show grooves, cracks or a gate that does not close and lock on its own every time.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Ovals usually carry a lower rating than D shapes, so check the markings and make sure it suits the job. Avoid cross-loading and loading over edges.',
            1 => 'Carabiners in a climbing system are life-support gear. Use only connectors rated for the purpose, follow ANSI Z133 and the maker\'s instructions, load them along the major axis, and inspect the gate, spring and body before each use. Compare other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/">auto-locking carabiners</a>.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why are oval carabiners used with pulleys?',
          'a' => 'Their symmetrical shape keeps a pulley centered so it does not twist under load.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does a triple-locking gate open with one hand?',
          'a' => 'Yes, with practice, but it takes deliberate twist-and-push movements, which is the point.',
        ),
      ),
