          1 => 'Tool storage',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Parking a tool aloft',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Every climber needs somewhere to put the saw down when both hands are busy. A tool hook on the saddle gives you that spot, so you can park a top-handle saw on its lanyard, hang a handsaw, or hold a bag of rigging gear while you set a lanyard or tie in.',
            1 => 'Mount it where you can reach it without twisting, and keep the saw on a proper chainsaw lanyard as well, so a slip off the hook does not mean a dropped saw. Consistency helps: always parking the saw on the same side becomes muscle memory.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Not for life support',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Racking clips and tool hooks are made to carry gear, not people. Keep them visibly different from the connectors in your climbing system so nobody grabs one by mistake in a hurry. A different color or shape on the saddle helps, and so does keeping them on the gear loops only.',
            1 => 'Check them now and then like any other hardware: a gate that no longer closes, a bent hook, or a cracked body means it goes in the bin. A dropped saw or handsaw can injure the ground crew, so make sure whatever hangs from the clip is secured the way the maker intends. Browse more <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/saddle-storage/">saddle storage</a>.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Should the saw still be on a lanyard?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Use a chainsaw lanyard as well so the saw cannot fall if it comes off the hook.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the Honos hook for life support?',
          'a' => 'No. It is for gear storage only.',
        ),
