          'h' => 'Why replace just the rope',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The rope is the part of a positioning lanyard that takes the most abuse. It wraps rough bark, picks up sap and grit, and is the part closest to the saw. Snaps and adjusters often outlast two or three ropes. Replacing only the rope keeps a familiar setup working for less money.',
            1 => 'Before ordering, confirm the rope diameter is compatible with your adjuster or hitch cord.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Inspect the new rope and any reused hardware carefully. Retire connectors with grooves, cracks, or sticky gates. Test the adjuster at ground level after setup. Compare other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/fliplines-and-lanyards/rope-lanyards/">rope lanyards</a> in this section.',
            1 => 'Keeping the same rope on a replacement lanyard means your adjuster and hitch feel the same as before. That consistency matters when you are leaning on the lanyard to make a cut.',
            2 => 'Because rope details vary, confirm the rope diameter and construction from the product tag or listing before ordering. Write the in-service date on a tag when you put it into use, and keep the old lanyard out of service so it does not end up back on a saddle by mistake.',
            3 => 'Cut up and dispose of retired lanyards so they cannot be reused.',
            4 => 'Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard (ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions), be inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I know my hardware is still good?',
          'a' => 'Check gates for full closure and smooth return, look for grooves, cracks, and corrosion, and follow the manufacturer\'s inspection guidance. Replace anything in doubt.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will this rope work with my rope grab?',
          'a' => 'Only if its diameter and construction are on the rope grab maker\'s compatibility list.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'TVCF004',
        'creator' => 'Symic',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00/33549425170',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/fliplines-and-lanyards/rope-lanyards/sterling-tritech-lanyard-with-triple-locking-snap/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/fliplines-and-lanyards/rope-lanyards/sterling-tritech-lanyard-with-triple-locking-snap/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/fliplines-and-lanyards/rope-lanyards/',
      'name' => 'Sterling TriTech Lanyard with Triple Locking Snap',
      'brand' => 'sterling-rope',
      'brand_name' => 'Sterling',
      'kind' => 'lanyard',
      'price' => 99,
      'keyword' => 'sterling tritech lanyard with snap',
      'title' => 'Sterling TriTech Lanyard with Triple Locking Snap',
      'h1' => 'Sterling TriTech Lanyard with Triple Locking Snap',
      'meta' => 'The Sterling TriTech lanyard with snap uses a triple locking snap for a secure saddle connection. See how it fits your lanyard setup and its typical price.',
      'tagline' => 'A Sterling rope lanyard terminated with a triple-locking snap.',
      'summary' => 'The Sterling TriTech lanyard with snap is a rope positioning lanyard terminated with a triple-locking snap hook. Use it as a replacement lanyard rope or build your own setup with the adjuster of your choice.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Sterling TriTech lanyard rope',
        1 => 'Triple-locking snap termination',
        2 => 'Pair with your choice of adjuster',
        3 => 'All-rope body for lighter weight',
        4 => 'Good replacement for a worn lanyard',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope',
          1 => 'Sterling TriTech',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Connector',
          1 => 'Triple-locking snap',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Rope positioning lanyard',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Build or replace',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'This is the lanyard rope with a snap on it, without a full kit around it. That makes it a good choice when you already have an adjuster you like, whether a prusik or a mechanical positioner, and only need fresh rope.',
            1 => 'The triple-locking snap needs three actions to open, which helps keep it shut when it rubs against bark or other gear.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Setup and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Confirm your adjuster is compatible with the rope, set it up, and test it at ground level. Inspect the snap gate and rope before every climb. Retire the lanyard after saw contact. See more <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/fliplines-and-lanyards/rope-lanyards/">rope lanyards</a>.',
            1 => 'Many climbers keep two lanyards on a big removal: one wrapped on the stem and one as a backup when cutting near the first. A plain lanyard with a snap is an economical way to add that second one.',
            2 => 'When you set it up with your adjuster, make sure the snap faces the right way on the D-ring so the gate is clear of the trunk and other hardware. Test it with your full weight at ground level before you climb.',
            3 => 'Mark the in-service date on a tag and record it in your gear log.',
            4 => 'Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard (ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions), be inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does this include an adjuster?',
          'a' => 'This listing is for the lanyard with a snap. Add a prusik or mechanical adjuster that is compatible with the rope.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why a triple-locking snap instead of a carabiner?',
          'a' => 'Snaps sit naturally on saddle D-rings and resist cross-loading. The triple lock reduces the chance of the gate opening by accident.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/sterling-rope/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Wood pole rescue',
        'creator' => 'Western Area Power Admin',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05/16974622426',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/fliplines-and-lanyards/rope-lanyards/yale-spearmint-lanyard-with-steel-double-locking-snap/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/fliplines-and-lanyards/rope-lanyards/yale-spearmint-lanyard-with-steel-double-locking-snap/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/fliplines-and-lanyards/rope-lanyards/',
      'name' => 'Yale Spearmint Lanyard with Steel Double Locking Snap',
      'brand' => 'yale-cordage',
      'brand_name' => 'Yale Cordage',
      'kind' => 'lanyard',
      'price' => 89,
      'keyword' => 'yale spearmint lanyard',
      'title' => 'Yale Spearmint Lanyard with Steel Snap',
      'h1' => 'Yale Spearmint Lanyard with Steel Double Locking Snap',
      'meta' => 'The Yale Spearmint lanyard with a steel double locking snap is a rope positioning lanyard for tree work. See what it suits, how to set it up, and typical price.',
      'tagline' => 'A Yale Spearmint rope lanyard with a durable steel double-locking snap.',
      'summary' => 'The Yale Spearmint lanyard is a rope positioning lanyard finished with a steel double-locking snap. Steel wears slowly, so the snap holds up well to the rubbing a lanyard end takes on a saddle D-ring.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Yale Spearmint lanyard rope',
        1 => 'Steel double-locking snap',
        2 => 'Durable termination for daily use',
        3 => 'Pair with a prusik or mechanical adjuster',
        4 => 'Good replacement lanyard',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope',
          1 => 'Yale Spearmint',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Connector',
          1 => 'Steel double-locking snap',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Rope positioning lanyard',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Steel snap, rope body',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A steel snap is heavier than aluminum but far more resistant to wear, which matters on a connector that rides on a D-ring all day. The double-locking gate needs two actions to open, a long-standing design for arborist lanyards.',
            1 => 'The rope body is light and flexible, easy to flip around a stem, and simple to inspect.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Setup and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Add a compatible adjuster, test it at ground level, and check the snap closes and locks every time. Inspect the rope for cuts, glazing, and hard spots. Retire it after saw contact. Compare other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/fliplines-and-lanyards/rope-lanyards/">rope lanyards</a> here.',
            1 => 'Pairing a rope body with a steel snap gives you a lanyard that is light where it wraps the stem and tough where it connects to the saddle. It is a setup many climbers run for years, replacing the rope as it wears and checking the snap each time.',
            2 => 'When you set up your adjuster, make sure the snap faces so the gate is clear of the trunk and other hardware. Test the setup at ground level with your full weight before you climb.',
            3 => 'Keep the snap clean and dry; steel can corrode if stored wet, so let it dry out after a rainy day before bagging it.',
            4 => 'Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard (ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions), be inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Double-locking or triple-locking snap?',
          'a' => 'Both are used on arborist lanyards. A triple lock adds one more step to open; a double lock is quicker. Follow your company\'s policy and the manufacturer\'s instructions.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is steel better than aluminum for a snap?',
          'a' => 'Steel wears more slowly and handles abuse better; aluminum is lighter. For a lanyard end that rubs all day, many climbers prefer steel.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/yale-cordage/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Fall Protection Training, 2013',
        'creator' => 'Western Area Power Admin',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05/8813827776',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/ansi-arborist-crane-tie-in-point-with-carabiners/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/ansi-arborist-crane-tie-in-point-with-carabiners/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/',
      'name' => 'ANSI Arborist Crane Tie-In Point with Carabiners',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 179,
      'keyword' => 'arborist crane tie in point',
      'title' => 'Arborist Crane Tie-In Point with Carabiners',
      'h1' => 'ANSI Arborist Crane Tie-In Point with Carabiners',
      'meta' => 'An arborist crane tie in point gives a climber a dedicated connection to the crane hook. See what the kit includes, how it is used, and what it typically costs.',
      'tagline' => 'A crane tie-in assembly with connectors for climbers working off a crane.',
      'summary' => 'An arborist crane tie in point is a dedicated connection that lets a climber attach to a crane hook or load line when being flown into position. This version comes with carabiners, so the assembly arrives ready to inspect and use.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Dedicated climber connection for crane work',
        1 => 'Supplied with carabiners',
        2 => 'Keeps the climber\'s attachment separate from the load',
        3 => 'For crews doing crane-assisted removals',
        4 => 'Inspect before every lift',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Crane tie-in point assembly',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Includes',
          1 => 'Carabiners',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Crane-assisted tree work',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How it is used',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On crane-assisted removals, the climber is sometimes lifted into the tree or rides the hook to a pick point. ANSI Z133 sets specific requirements for crane-assisted climbing, and the climber\'s connection should stay independent of the load rigging. A dedicated tie-in point gives a clear, rated attachment on the crane\'s hook block.',
            1 => 'This version includes carabiners, so you have matched connectors for the assembly.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safety and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Crane work requires a qualified crane operator, a qualified signal person, and a plan agreed before the lift. Inspect the tie-in point and connectors before every use and retire anything with wear, cuts, or deformation. Compare other anchors in <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/">friction savers</a>.',
            1 => 'Plan where the tie-in point sits on the hook block before the lift, and make sure everyone on the ground knows how the climber is connected. Use clear hand signals or radios agreed in advance.',
            2 => 'Keep the climber\'s tie-in separate from slings and chokers on the load so a load release never affects the climber\'s connection.',
            3 => 'Store the tie-in point clean and dry, away from chemicals and sharp hardware in the truck. Keep the manufacturer\'s paperwork and inspection records with it so they are available on site.',
            4 => 'Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard (ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions), be inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can a climber tie in to the crane hook with a regular lanyard?',
          'a' => 'Follow ANSI Z133, the crane manufacturer, and your company\'s crane procedures. A dedicated tie-in point keeps the climber\'s connection clear and inspectable.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Who can do crane-assisted tree work?',
          'a' => 'Only trained, qualified climbers working with a qualified crane operator and signal person, under an agreed lift plan.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
        2 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Climbing paraphernalia',
        'creator' => 'Ruth and Dave',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/95142644@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/95142644@N00/3584852771',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/ansi-arborist-crane-tie-in-point/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/ansi-arborist-crane-tie-in-point/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/',
      'name' => 'ANSI Arborist Crane Tie-In Point',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 129,
      'keyword' => 'crane tie in point',
      'title' => 'ANSI Arborist Crane Tie-In Point',
      'h1' => 'ANSI Arborist Crane Tie-In Point',
      'meta' => 'A crane tie in point gives an arborist a dedicated connection to the crane hook during crane-assisted removals. See how it is used, what to inspect, and price.',
      'tagline' => 'A dedicated climber connection for crane-assisted tree work.',
      'summary' => 'A crane tie in point is a dedicated attachment that lets a climber connect to a crane hook block separately from the load rigging. This version is the assembly without carabiners, for crews that supply their own rated connectors.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Dedicated climber attachment for crane work',
        1 => 'Keeps climber connection separate from the load',
        2 => 'Use with your own rated connectors',
        3 => 'For crane-assisted removals',
        4 => 'Inspect before every lift',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Crane tie-in point',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Includes',
          1 => 'Tie-in only (connectors not included)',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Crane-assisted tree work',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where it fits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On crane jobs, the climber may ride the hook into the tree or need a secure attachment to the hook block while a pick is rigged. A dedicated tie-in point gives a clear, inspectable connection and helps keep the climber\'s attachment independent from slings and chokers on the load.',
            1 => 'This listing is the tie-in point alone, for crews that already carry the connectors their procedures specify.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Crane-assisted tree work needs a qualified crane operator, a signal person, and an agreed lift plan. Use only rated, compatible locking connectors, and inspect everything before each lift. See also <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/">friction savers</a> for tree anchors.',
            1 => 'Plan the attachment and climber position with the crane operator before each lift, and agree on signals in advance. Keep the climber\'s connection separate from the load rigging at all times.',
            2 => 'If the tie-in point shows any cuts, abrasion, heat damage, or contamination, retire it. Keep a written inspection record with the gear, as many crane procedures require.',
            3 => 'Store it clean and dry, away from chemicals and sharp hardware, and keep it in a dedicated bag so it is not mixed up with rigging slings or used for loads by mistake.',
            4 => 'Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard (ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions), be inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What connectors should I use with it?',
          'a' => 'Use rated, locking connectors that are compatible with the tie-in point and the crane hook, as specified by the manufacturer and your crane procedures.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is this a rigging sling?',
          'a' => 'No. It is for the climber\'s attachment only. Never use it to rig loads.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
        2 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'D75_8819-1',
        'creator' => 'Mark F. Levisay',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/31395633@N08',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/31395633@N08/52706266887',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/art-ropeguide/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/art-ropeguide/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/',
      'name' => 'ART RopeGuide',
      'brand' => 'art',
      'brand_name' => 'ART',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 139,
      'keyword' => 'art ropeguide',
      'title' => 'ART RopeGuide Friction Saver',
      'h1' => 'ART RopeGuide',
      'meta' => 'The ART RopeGuide is a canopy rope management device that reduces friction and bark wear at your tie-in. See how it works, who it suits, and price.',
      'tagline' => 'A canopy rope guide from ART for smoother running and less bark wear.',
      'summary' => 'The ART RopeGuide is a canopy device that guides your climbing line over a limb, reducing friction and protecting the bark at your anchor or redirect. It is aimed at climbers who want smoother rope travel than a bare branch union gives.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Guides rope over a limb with less friction',
        1 => 'Protects bark and cambium',
        2 => 'Cuts down on rope wear',
        3 => 'Useful at anchors and redirects',
        4 => 'From ART, makers of the Positioner and Spiderjack',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Rope guide / friction saver',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Anchor and redirect rope management',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What it does in the canopy',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Climbing over a bare union wears your rope, damages bark, and adds friction you fight on every advance. A rope guide sits on the limb and gives the rope a smoother path. On a moving rope system that means easier hitch tending, and at a redirect it keeps the line running where you want it.',
            1 => 'Read ART\'s instructions for the setups it is rated for, including whether it is approved as a primary anchor or only as a guide or redirect.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check all metal parts for grooves, cracks, and sharp edges, and inspect any textile parts for cuts and abrasion. Make sure it is set on a sound limb. Compare cambium savers and other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/">friction savers</a> in this section.',
            1 => 'Reducing friction at the anchor is good for more than the rope. Less friction means less effort when you advance your hitch, and less heat and wear on hitch cord. Over a season, a rope guide can noticeably extend the life of your climbing line.',
            2 => 'Set it on a sound limb of suitable size, and check it from below before committing your weight. If you are unsure how ART intends it to be installed, practice low or ask a trainer.',
            3 => 'Keep it in a dry bag between jobs.',
            4 => 'Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard (ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions), be inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can the ART RopeGuide be used as a primary anchor?',
          'a' => 'Follow ART\'s instructions. Use it only in the configurations the manufacturer approves for your climbing system.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is it for MRS or SRS?',
          'a' => 'Check the manufacturer\'s instructions for the systems it is designed for before you set it in the canopy.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/art/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Pruning in RVA (Carytown, Virginia)',
        'creator' => 'Gamma Man',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06/48982146938',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/art-snakeanchor/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/art-snakeanchor/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/',
      'name' => 'ART SnakeAnchor',
      'brand' => 'art',
      'brand_name' => 'ART',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 199,
      'keyword' => 'art snakeanchor',
      'title' => 'ART SnakeAnchor Adjustable Tree Anchor',
      'h1' => 'ART SnakeAnchor',
      'meta' => 'The ART SnakeAnchor is an adjustable canopy anchor that wraps limbs of different sizes. See how it works, who it suits, and what it typically costs.',
      'tagline' => 'An adjustable ART canopy anchor that wraps limbs of different sizes.',
      'summary' => 'The ART SnakeAnchor is an adjustable canopy anchor that wraps a limb or stem and gives your climbing system a defined attachment point. It is built to handle different limb sizes without carrying several fixed-length slings.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Adjustable to different limb diameters',
        1 => 'Gives a defined anchor point in the canopy',
        2 => 'Protects bark compared with a bare tie-in',
        3 => 'Reduces the need for multiple fixed slings',
        4 => 'Designed for professional climbers',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Adjustable canopy anchor',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Climbing anchor',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why an adjustable anchor',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A fixed-length cambium saver or sling only fits a range of limb sizes. An adjustable anchor like the SnakeAnchor can be snugged around whatever union or stem you pick, which helps when the best tie-in is not the size your gear is cut for.',
            1 => 'Follow ART\'s instructions for installation, retrieval, and the climbing systems it is rated for. Choose a sound, appropriately sized limb as you would with any anchor.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Inspect all components before every climb. Look for cuts, abrasion, and stiff spots on textiles, and wear or deformation on metal parts. Retire it after any shock load or damage. Compare other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/">friction savers</a> and anchors in this section.',
            1 => 'Adjustable anchors also help when you are working through a canopy and want to move your tie-in without carrying several slings. One device that fits many limb sizes simplifies your rack.',
            2 => 'Practice installing and retrieving the SnakeAnchor at ground level or on a low limb before you rely on it high in the tree. Make sure everyone on your crew who uses it has been trained on how it goes in and comes out.',
            3 => 'Store it clean and dry, and keep any manufacturer paperwork with it for inspections.',
            4 => 'Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard (ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions), be inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the SnakeAnchor retrievable from the ground?',
          'a' => 'Check ART\'s instructions for the retrieval methods and configurations they approve.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Who is the SnakeAnchor for?',
          'a' => 'Trained climbers who want one anchor that adapts to different limb sizes. Newer climbers often start with a simple ring-and-ring friction saver.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/art/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Caution - Tree Surgeon at work',
        'creator' => 'Clint__Budd',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/58827557@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/58827557@N06/47249256141',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/buckingham-buck-supersaver/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/buckingham-buck-supersaver/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/',
      'name' => 'Buckingham Buck SuperSaver',
      'brand' => 'buckingham',
      'brand_name' => 'Buckingham',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 89,
      'keyword' => 'buckingham supersaver',
      'title' => 'Buckingham Buck SuperSaver Friction Saver',
      'h1' => 'Buckingham Buck SuperSaver',
      'meta' => 'The Buckingham SuperSaver is a friction saver that protects bark and your climbing rope at the tie-in. See how it installs, who it suits, and typical price.',
      'tagline' => 'A Buckingham friction saver that protects the tree and your climbing line.',
      'summary' => 'The Buckingham SuperSaver is a friction saver that goes over your tie-in limb to protect the bark and keep your climbing rope from wearing on the union. It gives your rope a smoother, more consistent path on a doubled-rope system.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Protects bark and cambium at the tie-in',
        1 => 'Reduces rope wear and friction',
        2 => 'Smoother hitch tending on MRS',
        3 => 'Simple to install with a throw line',
        4 => 'Durable Buckingham construction',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Friction saver',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Tie-in point protection',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why use a friction saver',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On a moving rope system the rope runs back and forth over the tie-in limb every time you advance or descend. Over a bare union, that grinds the bark and wears your rope. A friction saver gives the rope a ring or eye to run through, which protects the tree, extends rope life, and makes the system feel smoother.',
            1 => 'Install it with your throw line so the rings or eyes sit correctly over a sound limb, and follow Buckingham\'s instructions for retrieval.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the strap or rope body for cuts and abrasion, and look at the rings or hardware for grooves and wear. Retire it if it shows damage or has been shock loaded. Compare other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/">friction savers</a> in this section.',
            1 => 'Friction savers are also a good habit for working in the same trees year after year. Repeated rope wear at a single union can damage the bark enough to invite decay. A friction saver keeps that from becoming a problem on clients\' trees.',
            2 => 'Match the friction saver to the limb size you usually tie in on, and make sure your hitch and micro pulley will not jam against the rings during a descent.',
            3 => 'Practice retrieval low before you rely on it in a big tree, and keep it in your rope bag so it is always on the truck.',
            4 => 'Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard (ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions), be inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does a friction saver help on SRS?',
          'a' => 'It can serve as an anchor in some SRS setups. Follow the manufacturer\'s instructions for the configurations it is approved for.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I get a friction saver out of the tree?',
          'a' => 'Most are retrieved from the ground by pulling the rope through in the order shown in the instructions. Practice low before you rely on it in a big tree.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/buckingham/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Untitled',
        'creator' => 'Unknown',
        'creator_url' => '',
        'license' => 'CC0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/5948349/free-public-domain-cc0-photo',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/buckingham-webbing-friction-saver/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/buckingham-webbing-friction-saver/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/',
      'name' => 'Buckingham Webbing Friction Saver',
      'brand' => 'buckingham',
      'brand_name' => 'Buckingham',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 45,
      'keyword' => 'buckingham webbing friction saver',
      'title' => 'Buckingham Webbing Friction Saver for Tree Climbing',
      'h1' => 'Buckingham Webbing Friction Saver',
      'meta' => 'The Buckingham webbing friction saver uses tough webbing and two rings to protect the bark and your climbing line at the tie-in. Simple and proven.',
      'tagline' => 'Classic two-ring webbing cambium saver for climbers who want simple and reliable.',
      'summary' => 'The Buckingham webbing friction saver is a length of heavy webbing with a large ring on one end and a small ring on the other, set over a union so your rope runs through the rings instead of over the bark. It cuts wear on the tree and on your line, and it makes the climbing system run smoother on every advance.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Webbing strap with a large ring and a small ring',
        1 => 'Keeps the climbing line off the bark at the tie-in point',
        2 => 'Reduces rope wear and heat at the union',
        3 => 'Retrievable from the ground with the right setup',
        4 => 'Light and packs flat in a gear bag',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Ring-and-ring friction saver',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Construction',
          1 => 'Webbing with two metal rings',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'System',
          1 => 'Moving rope (MRS/DdRT)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Buckingham',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How it works on the job',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Install the saver over a sound union with the throw line, pull it up, and run your climbing line through both rings. The small ring follows the large ring when you retrieve it, so the saver comes down without leaving a tangle in the crown. Size the webbing so the rings sit close together without binding on a thick stem.',
            1 => 'Compared with tying in directly over bark, a webbing saver keeps sap, grit and heat off your rope. That adds up over a season of moving-rope climbing, and it also keeps the cambium from getting burned by a line running back and forth all day.',
            2 => 'Webbing has a flat profile that spreads the load over more bark than a rope saver, which is kind to thin-barked species. It also lies flat in a gear bag and does not hold a set the way some rope bodies do. Many climbers keep one in the kit even if their main saver is adjustable, simply because it is light and quick to install.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and fit',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the webbing for cuts, glazing, chainsaw nicks and UV fade, and look at the stitching and rings for wear grooves before each climb. This is life-support gear. Use it only within the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133 practice, inspect it before every climb, and retire it when in doubt.',
            1 => 'Webbing savers suit moderate stems and climbers who want the lightest option. If you work big leads often, compare adjustable models in our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/">friction savers</a> range.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use the Buckingham friction saver for SRS?',
          'a' => 'It is built as a moving-rope tie-in point. For single-rope work, follow Buckingham\'s instructions and use a setup rated for that use.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I get it out of the tree?',
          'a' => 'With the right ring orientation and a retrieval setup, you pull the small-ring side and the saver comes down through the union. Practice on the ground first.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/buckingham/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Climbing paraphernalia',
        'creator' => 'Ruth and Dave',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/95142644@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/95142644@N00/3584852771',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/clogger-ropeguard-chainsaw-protective-rope-sleeve/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/clogger-ropeguard-chainsaw-protective-rope-sleeve/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/',
      'name' => 'Clogger RopeGuard Chainsaw Protective Rope Sleeve',
      'brand' => 'clogger',
      'brand_name' => 'Clogger',
      'kind' => 'rope',
      'price' => 40,
      'keyword' => 'clogger ropeguard',
      'title' => 'Clogger RopeGuard Chainsaw Protective Rope Sleeve',
      'h1' => 'Clogger RopeGuard Chainsaw Protective Rope Sleeve',
      'meta' => 'The Clogger RopeGuard is a cut-resistant sleeve that rides on your climbing line near the tie-in, adding protection where a saw is most likely to touch it.',
      'tagline' => 'Cut-resistant sleeve that adds a layer of protection to the line near your saw.',
      'summary' => 'The Clogger RopeGuard is a sleeve made from Clogger\'s cut-resistant fabric that slides over the climbing line close to your bridge, the part of the rope that sits nearest a running saw. It is a backup layer, not a license to cut near your line, but it buys margin on an awkward cut.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Cut-resistant fabric sleeve for the climbing line',
        1 => 'Sits near the tie-in where saw contact risk is highest',
        2 => 'Light enough to leave on the rope all day',
        3 => 'Made by Clogger, known for chainsaw protective clothing',
        4 => 'Adds margin on awkward one-handed positioning cuts',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Protective rope sleeve',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Clogger cut-resistant fabric',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Clogger',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where it helps',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Most rope cuts happen close to the climber: reaching across the line for a limb, cutting near a lanyard, or a saw kicking back while the rope is pinned against the stem. The RopeGuard covers that short section, so a brush from the chain is more likely to grab fabric than to go straight through rope fibers.',
            1 => 'Fit it so it can slide along the line but stays where you need it. Check that it does not interfere with your hitch, mechanical device or rope runner, and that it does not hide the rope from your inspection.',
            2 => 'The sleeve is most useful for climbers who do a lot of work close to the tie-in: crown reductions, deadwooding and removals where you are cutting near your own line. On those jobs a small extra layer of protection costs little in weight and handling. It is a good addition to a kit for newer climbers who are still building saw-and-rope awareness.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What it does not do',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'No sleeve makes a rope chainsaw-proof. Keep your saw well clear of all life-support lines, use two points of attachment when cutting, and follow ANSI Z133 practice and your training. Inspect both the sleeve and the rope under it before every climb.',
            1 => 'If the sleeve takes a cut, retire the sleeve and inspect the rope closely. Browse the rest of our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/">friction savers and rope protection</a> for other ways to protect the line.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does the RopeGuard make my rope cut-proof?',
          'a' => 'No. It adds a protective layer that can slow or stop light chain contact. Saw discipline and two points of attachment are still the primary protection.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will it work with my climbing device?',
          'a' => 'It sits on the rope below the device, but check fit with your hitch or mechanical device before climbing on it.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/clogger/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Walt coiling the rope at the summit',
        'creator' => 'Tristan Higbee',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62264264@N03',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62264264@N03/8305035257',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/dmm-retrieval-cone/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/dmm-retrieval-cone/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/',
      'name' => 'DMM Retrieval Cone',
      'brand' => 'dmm',
      'brand_name' => 'DMM',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 15,
      'keyword' => 'dmm retrieval cone',
      'title' => 'DMM Retrieval Cone for Friction Saver Retrieval',
      'h1' => 'DMM Retrieval Cone',
      'meta' => 'The DMM retrieval cone helps friction savers and anchors slide out of the tree by guiding hardware through the union. A small part that saves stuck-gear grief.',
      'tagline' => 'A small cone that helps friction savers and anchors slide out of the tree cleanly.',
      'summary' => 'The DMM retrieval cone is a small tapered piece that sits on the line ahead of your hardware so it can slide through a narrow union instead of catching on bark or forks. It is a cheap add-on that makes ring savers and retrievable anchors come down more reliably.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Tapered shape guides hardware through tight unions',
        1 => 'Cuts down on stuck friction savers and anchors',
        2 => 'Light and simple, no moving parts',
        3 => 'Lives on the line, so it is always there at retrieval',
        4 => 'Made by DMM',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Retrieval cone',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Friction saver and anchor retrieval',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'DMM',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a cone helps',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Rings and connectors have flat faces that like to snag on bark ridges and tight crotches as they are pulled back over a union. A cone presents a smooth taper instead, so the hardware rolls up and over instead of hooking. The difference shows most in rough-barked species and in narrow V-shaped unions.',
            1 => 'Set the cone so the taper leads the hardware in the direction it will travel on retrieval. Practice the retrieval on a low limb before you rely on it in a tall tree, and keep a throw line handy as a backup plan.',
            2 => 'Climbers who use retrievable anchors for single-rope work tend to get the most from a cone, because a stuck anchor means a second climb or a long fight with the throw line. It is also handy for crews that climb conifers with rough bark and lots of small branch stubs around the union.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The cone is not part of the load path, but it shares space with life-support gear, so look it over during your daily inspection. Replace it if it cracks or the bore wears enough to slide out of position.',
            1 => 'Pair it with a ring-and-ring saver or retrievable anchor from our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/">friction savers</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the retrieval cone load-bearing?',
          'a' => 'No. It is a retrieval aid. Your climbing system\'s load-rated parts carry the load.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will it fit my friction saver?',
          'a' => 'It suits common ring-and-ring and retrievable setups, but check that it fits your rope diameter and hardware before use.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/dmm/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Route Clearance',
        'creator' => 'Georgia National Guard',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40994485@N04',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40994485@N04/33581236171',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/fitch-saver-mid-line-attachable-redirect/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/fitch-saver-mid-line-attachable-redirect/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/',
      'name' => 'Fitch Saver Mid-Line Attachable Redirect',
      'brand' => 'fitch-saver',
      'brand_name' => 'Fitch Saver',
      'kind' => 'rigging-device',
      'price' => 95,
      'keyword' => 'fitch saver',
      'title' => 'Fitch Saver Mid-Line Attachable Redirect for Climbers',
      'h1' => 'Fitch Saver Mid-Line Attachable Redirect',
      'meta' => 'The Fitch Saver is a mid-line attachable redirect that clips onto your rope without an end, so you can add a redirect after you are already working in the tree.',
      'tagline' => 'Add a redirect mid-climb without untying or reaching for a rope end.',
      'summary' => 'The Fitch Saver is a mid-line attachable redirect, meaning you can put it on your climbing line anywhere along its length without feeding a rope end through it. That makes it handy for setting a redirect or protecting a union while you are already working in the crown.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Attaches mid-line, no rope end needed',
        1 => 'Sets a redirect to improve your working angle',
        2 => 'Keeps the line off the bark at the redirect',
        3 => 'Useful for limb walks and wide crowns',
        4 => 'Install and remove from your work position',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Mid-line attachable redirect',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Attachment',
          1 => 'Mid-line, no rope end required',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Redirects and tie-in protection',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Using a mid-line redirect',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A redirect changes the angle of your climbing line so you can reach the end of a long limb or work the far side of a crown without swinging. Normally that means a sling and carabiner, or feeding a rope end through hardware. A mid-line attachable unit skips that step, so you can set it from where you are and keep working.',
            1 => 'Choose a sound limb that can take the force of a redirect, which can be higher than your body weight on a steep angle. Keep the redirect close enough to the main anchor that a slip does not produce a big swing.',
            2 => 'Mid-line attachment also helps when your rope is already tied off at the base or rigged through other hardware. Instead of untying and re-threading, you add the redirect where you are, use it, and take it off on the way back. That saves time on big spreading crowns where you might set two or three redirects in one climb.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Before you climb on it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Read the manufacturer\'s instructions for rope diameter, loading direction and the systems it is rated for. This is life-support gear. Use it only within the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133 practice, inspect it before every climb, and retire it when in doubt. Practice clipping and unclipping on the ground before you use it aloft.',
            1 => 'For other ways to protect the tie-in point, see our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/">friction savers</a>.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need the rope end to install the Fitch Saver?',
          'a' => 'No. It is designed to attach mid-line, which is the main reason climbers choose it.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can it be used as a main anchor?',
          'a' => 'Follow the maker\'s instructions on that. Most climbers use it as a redirect; use a rated anchor for your main tie-in.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
