          1 => 'Throw line storage and deployment',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Setting a line faster',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Most of the time spent setting a line is not throwing, it is untangling. A good storage bag gives the line a clean stack to pay out from, so a throw goes up and over without snagging on grass and sticks. Missed throws go back in the bag quickly, ready for another try.',
            1 => 'Place the bag on firm ground in front of you, clear of your feet, and secure the tail end so the line cannot leave with the throw bag.',
            2 => 'Good throw line storage also speeds up setting a second line, whether that is a rigging line or a separate access line for single-rope work. Keep each line in its own bag so they cannot knot together on the ground.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Looking after throw line',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Throw line wears fast where it runs over bark. Check it as you repack, and retire line that is fuzzed, glazed or flattened before it fails at the wrong moment.',
            1 => 'Throw line kits get used on nearly every climb, so small time savings add up. A clean, contained line means fewer re-throws, less frustration and less time standing at the base of the tree before the real work starts.',
            2 => 'Find more options in our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/">gear storage</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What throw lines fit the FTC Matriosac 3?',
          'a' => 'It is made for arborist throw line. Check capacity if you run a long or thick line.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I keep throw bags in it too?',
          'a' => 'Yes, the throw bag can sit with the line, which keeps the kit together.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
