          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Nine millimeter cord is a good starting point when you are not sure whether your hitch needs more or less bite. It holds firmly on common climbing lines and still releases cleanly when you break the hitch to descend.',
            1 => 'Tie your chosen friction hitch to suit your rope diameter, dress and set it before you weight it, and test it close to the ground. Small changes in cord, rope and hitch change how much grip you get, so check every new combination before you trust it aloft.',
            2 => 'Carry a spare in your gear bag. Hitch cords wear out on their own schedule, and a glazed cord found at the base of the tree should not end the climbing day. Note when each cord goes into service so you learn how long cords last with your rope and your style of climbing.',
          ),
