        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How climbers use it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Thinner hitch cord grips harder, and heat resistance helps it survive the friction of fast descents. Sewn eyes are compact, so the hitch stays short and leaves room on the bridge for a micro pulley.',
            1 => 'Tie your chosen friction hitch to suit your rope diameter, dress and set it before you weight it, and test it close to the ground. Small changes in cord, rope and hitch change how much grip you get, so check every new combination before you trust it aloft.',
            2 => 'Carry a spare in your gear bag. Hitch cords wear out on their own schedule, and a glazed cord found at the base of the tree should not end the climbing day. Note when each cord goes into service so you learn how long cords last with your rope and your style of climbing.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and replacement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Hitch cord is a wear item. Heat and abrasion glaze and fuzz the cover, and the sewn or spliced terminations need a close look every day. Retire the cord at the first sign of glazing, stiffness, cuts, core showing or damaged stitching. This is life-support gear. Use it only within the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133 practice, inspect it before every climb, and retire it when in doubt.',
            1 => 'Match the cord to your climbing line and compare options in our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/hitch-cord-and-split-tails/eye-and-eye-prusiks/">eye-and-eye prusiks</a> range.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Sewn or spliced Beeline: which should I buy?',
          'a' => 'Sewn eyes are compact and consistent; spliced eyes are more flexible. Both are common, so choose the one that suits your hitch.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What rope suits 8mm Beeline?',
          'a' => 'Smaller and slicker climbing lines are the usual match. Test it on your own rope near the ground first.',
