          1 => 'SMC',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Hitch tending, redirects, light hauling',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Uses in tree work',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A small pulley is one of the most useful pieces on the saddle. Under a hitch, it makes slack tending a one-hand job. On a redirect, it cuts friction and rope wear. In a simple haul, it helps tension a line or lift a small load.',
            1 => 'The Crevasse is light enough that you will not mind carrying it. Many climbers keep one clipped as a spare for redirects even if they run another pulley for hitch tending.',
            2 => 'It also makes sense in a small rescue or haul kit for the truck, where you want a few light pulleys that do not take up much space. When a rope gets hung up or a piece needs a quick lift, a light pulley and a few slings can save a lot of pulling by hand. Keep one with each rope bag and it will get used more often than you might expect.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Checks before use',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Confirm the pulley fits your rope diameter and the carabiner you connect it with. Check that the side plates close fully and that the sheave turns freely without wobble.',
            1 => 'Retire it if you see cracks, deep grooves or bent plates. Use it within SMC\'s ratings and instructions, and use life-support systems under ANSI Z133 practice after proper training. Browse more in <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/micro-pulleys/">micro pulleys</a>.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is it strong enough for rigging?',
          'a' => 'It is for light use. Heavy rigging needs a block rated for those forces.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use it for hitch tending?',
          'a' => 'Yes, many climbers do. Check the fit with your hitch and carabiner.',
        ),
