          1 => 'Replaceable tree gaffs',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Steel spurs for removal work',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Steel climbers weigh more than aluminum, but they shrug off the knocks of daily removal work: being thrown in a truck box, kicked against a trunk, or used on hardwoods with thick bark. Buckingham\'s steel climbers use adjustable shanks and replaceable tree gaffs so you can keep them in service for years.',
            1 => 'This set comes with simple L-shaped pads. They are light, easy to strap on, and inexpensive to replace, which makes the package a practical first set of spurs or a backup set for the crew.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit, gaffs, and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Adjust the shank so the top sits just below the knee joint, and wear a stiff, supportive boot that fits the stirrup well. Keep gaffs sharpened to the correct profile and replace them when worn short or bent. Browse other <a href="/climbing/spurs/buckingham-steel-spurs/">Buckingham steel spurs</a> to compare pad options.',
            1 => 'When you order, have your calf length and boot style in mind: the shank should end just below the knee, and the stirrup should sit under the arch of a boot with a defined heel. Store the climbers with gaff guards on, wipe off sap and moisture after use, and sharpen with a hand file to the original profile. A grinder can overheat the gaff and soften the steel.',
            2 => 'Spurs belong on removals and on trees that are coming down, not on trees you are pruning. Inspect gaffs, straps, and pads before each use, follow the manufacturer\'s instructions, and get proper training before you climb on spikes.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Steel or aluminum spurs?',
          'a' => 'Steel is heavier and very durable. Aluminum saves weight on long days. Many climbers start on steel and move to aluminum later.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Are these good first spurs?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Steel climbers with L-pads are a common, affordable first set for climbers learning removal work.',
        ),
      ),
