      'keyword' => 'distel eye bolt',
      'title' => 'Distel Eye Bolt M8x11 Replacement Spur Part',
      'h1' => 'Distel Eye Bolt M8x11',
      'meta' => 'Distel eye bolt M8x11, a genuine replacement part for Distel climbers. Swap a worn or lost bolt and keep your spurs complete. Fit and inspection notes.',
      'tagline' => 'Genuine Distel M8 eye bolt for keeping your Distel spurs complete and serviceable.',
      'summary' => 'The Distel eye bolt M8x11 is a small replacement part for Distel climbers, used where the spur\'s strap or fitting attaches. Replace one that is worn, bent or lost instead of improvising with hardware-store bolts.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Genuine Distel replacement part',
        1 => 'M8 thread',
        2 => 'For Distel climbers',
        3 => 'Replace worn, bent or missing bolts',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Thread',
          1 => 'M8',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Size',
          1 => 'M8x11',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Distel climbers',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use the right part',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Spur hardware sees a lot of vibration and load. A generic eye bolt may look the same but differ in thread length, material or strength. Using the genuine Distel part keeps the spur working as designed.',
            1 => 'Check your Distel model and the part location against Distel\'s documentation before ordering. Other spur parts are in <a href="/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/">replacement gaffs and parts</a>.',
            2 => 'Distel spurs are built to tight tolerances, and small parts like this are what keep them that way. When a fitting is lost on the job, it is tempting to grab whatever bolt is in the truck. Don\'t. Order the right part and keep a spare.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fitting and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Thread the bolt in fully and check that it is tight and sitting square. Look over the surrounding strap and fittings while you are at it.',
            1 => 'Inspect all spur hardware before each climb. If you are unsure whether a repair is sound, have a qualified person check it. Follow Distel\'s instructions and ANSI Z133.',
            2 => 'Spur parts are cheap next to a fall or a lost day. Keep a small kit in the climbing bag with a file, a gauge, spare screws and a strap, so a worn part gets swapped at the truck instead of nursed through the rest of the job.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a hardware-store M8 eye bolt instead?',
          'a' => 'Use the genuine Distel part. Generic bolts may differ in length, material or strength.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Which Distel climbers use this bolt?',
          'a' => 'Check Distel\'s parts information for your model before ordering.',
        ),
      ),
