      'kind' => 'hand-saw',
      'price' => 32,
      'keyword' => 'jameson 11 straight blade hand saw',
      'title' => 'Jameson 11" Straight Blade Hand Saw for Pruning',
      'h1' => 'Jameson 11" Straight Blade Hand Saw',
      'meta' => 'The Jameson 11 inch straight blade hand saw is a compact pruning saw for small cuts aloft and on the ground. Who it suits, how it cuts and how to care for it.',
      'tagline' => 'A compact 11-inch straight pruning saw for small cuts and tight spaces.',
      'summary' => 'This Jameson 11" straight blade hand saw is a compact saw for small pruning cuts, crown cleaning and work in tight spots. It is light on the saddle and easy to control with one hand.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '11-inch straight blade',
        1 => 'Compact and light on the saddle',
        2 => 'Good for small pruning cuts',
        3 => 'Straight blade tracks well in tight spots',
        4 => 'Pull-stroke cutting',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Blade length',
          1 => '11 in',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Blade shape',
          1 => 'Straight',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Cutting stroke',
          1 => 'Pull',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Jameson',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'A compact saw for detail work',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Not every cut needs a 13-inch blade. Crown cleaning, small deadwood and fine pruning cuts often happen in tight spots where a shorter saw is easier to control. The Jameson 11-inch saw is sized for that work and weighs less on your saddle.',
            1 => 'A straight blade tracks predictably and works well for cuts at or below shoulder height. For bigger limbs or cuts overhead, a longer curved saw is often faster. Browse our <a href="/cutting-and-pruning/hand-saws-and-scabbards/">hand saws and scabbards</a> to compare.',
            2 => 'It is also a good first saw for new climbers learning pruning cuts, because a shorter blade is easier to control and less likely to wander into a branch collar. On the ground it handles small limbing, cutting roots at planting time and trimming shrubs.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Carrying and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'When you carry a hand saw aloft, keep it in a scabbard on your saddle and make two-handed cuts away from your rope and lanyard. Follow ANSI Z133 and get trained before you climb.',
            1 => 'Clean off sap after use and keep the blade dry. Replace the blade or saw when teeth are dull or the blade has a kink. Keep the scabbard clear of sawdust so the saw seats fully.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is an 11-inch saw big enough for climbing?',
          'a' => 'It handles small pruning and crown cleaning well. Many climbers carry it alongside a longer saw for bigger cuts.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Straight or curved blade?',
          'a' => 'Straight blades track predictably and suit cuts at or below shoulder level. Curved blades bite more aggressively, especially overhead.',
        ),
      ),
