      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Comfort for a day of cuts',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A pruning job can mean hundreds of saw strokes. A shaped grip spreads the load across your hand and helps you keep control when your forearm starts to tire. The Jameson ergonomic handle is built for that, and the 13-inch blade handles most limbs you would cut by hand rather than with a top-handle saw.',
            1 => 'If you prefer the feel of wood, Jameson also makes this saw with a wooden handle. Compare both in our <a href="/cutting-and-pruning/hand-saws-and-scabbards/">hand saws and scabbards</a>.',
            2 => 'A good grip matters most when you are cutting one-handed while positioned on a lanyard, or reaching out to the end of a limb. A secure hold means fewer slips and cleaner cuts at the branch collar, which is better for the tree.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Carrying and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'When you carry a hand saw aloft, keep it in a scabbard on your saddle and make two-handed cuts away from your rope and lanyard. Follow ANSI Z133 and get trained before you climb.',
            1 => 'Wipe off sap and keep the blade dry. Retire or re-blade the saw when it starts to drift in the cut or the teeth are damaged. Wear gloves that still let you feel the grip.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What size limbs can a 13-inch hand saw cut?',
          'a' => 'It handles most limbs a climber would cut by hand. For larger wood, use an appropriate saw and technique.',
