        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why use a leg strap',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A long curved hand saw rides on the saddle or belt, and the scabbard hangs well down your leg. Without anything holding it, the tip catches on branches as you move through the canopy and bangs your knee on every step on the ground. A leg strap anchors it so the saw stays where you expect it when you reach down to draw it.',
            1 => 'The elastic gives as your leg bends, so it holds the scabbard without cutting off circulation or limiting movement on a climb.',
            2 => 'It also helps saw safety. A scabbard that stays put means the saw goes back into it cleanly every time, rather than you fumbling for a moving target with a sharp blade in your hand. And a scabbard tip that isn\'t flapping around is less likely to hook a branch and pull the saw partway out while you move. Ground crews walking through brush get the same benefit.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Attach the strap to the scabbard\'s lower slot or loop, then wrap and fasten it around your thigh snug enough to stop the swing. Check the elastic for fraying or loss of stretch and replace it when it no longer holds.',
            1 => 'Keep the strap clear of your climbing system and lanyard. For pole saws, poles and kits, see our <a href="/cutting-and-pruning/poles-and-kits/">poles and pole kits</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do the straps fit all Silky scabbards?',
          'a' => 'They are made for Silky scabbards with a leg strap attachment point. Check that your scabbard has one.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will a leg strap get in the way while climbing?',
          'a' => 'Adjusted snug and routed clear of your hitch and lanyard, most climbers find it reduces snags rather than adding them.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'pruning',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/silky/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'REL-NL-WK7-2-duffel-bag-10454',
        'creator' => 'Trendhim',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/148219711@N08',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/148219711@N08/50326073447',
      ),
    ),
    '/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/phoenix-saw-head/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/phoenix-saw-head/',
      'category' => '/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/',
      'name' => 'Phoenix Saw Head',
      'brand' => 'phoenix',
      'brand_name' => 'Phoenix',
      'kind' => 'pole-saw',
      'price' => 45,
      'keyword' => 'phoenix saw head',
      'title' => 'Phoenix Saw Head for Pole Saws',
      'h1' => 'Phoenix Saw Head',
      'meta' => 'The Phoenix saw head mounts a pruning blade to a sectional pole for cutting limbs overhead from the ground. Check blade and pole fit before you order.',
      'tagline' => 'A pole saw head that turns a sectional pole into an overhead pruning saw.',
      'summary' => 'The Phoenix saw head is a pole saw head that clamps a pruning saw blade to the top of a pole, so you can cut limbs overhead from the ground. It is the business end of a pole saw setup and the part that takes the most abuse.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Mounts a pruning blade to a pole',
        1 => 'For overhead cuts from the ground or a bucket',
        2 => 'Replaces a cracked or worn saw head',
        3 => 'Check blade bolt pattern and pole fit before buying',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Pole saw head',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Mounting a pole saw blade to a pole',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Blade',
          1 => 'Sold separately unless listed',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where it fits in a pole kit',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A pole saw is three things: the pole, the head and the blade. The head holds the blade at the right angle and carries every push and pull into the pole. When a head cracks, the bolt holes wear or the pole fitting loosens, the blade wobbles and the saw stops tracking straight.',
            1 => 'A fresh saw head brings back a stiff, predictable tool for lifting canopies, clearing deadwood and cutting back from structures where you can\'t climb or don\'t need to.',
            2 => 'Pole saw heads usually fail at stress points: around the blade bolt holes, at the base where the head meets the pole fitting, and on the hook or guide if the head has one. Most of that damage comes from prying the pole to free a pinched blade. Instead, relieve the pinch with an undercut or a cut from another angle. Inspect the head whenever you change blades and replace it at the first crack, since a head that breaks overhead drops a sharp blade toward the operator.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Before you order',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Saw heads are not universal. Confirm the head fits your pole\'s diameter and connection style, and that your blade\'s bolt pattern matches the head. Tighten blade bolts evenly and recheck them after the first few cuts.',
            1 => 'Never use a pole saw near energized lines unless you are qualified and the tool is rated for that work. Compare other heads and blades in our <a href="/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/">pole saws and scabbards</a> range.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will the Phoenix saw head fit my pole?',
          'a' => 'Check the pole diameter and connection type against the head\'s listing. Fit varies between pole systems.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does it come with a blade?',
          'a' => 'Saw heads are often sold without a blade. Check the listing and match the blade bolt pattern.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'pruning',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/phoenix/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pole-saw-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pole-saw-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Project 365 #211: 300718 A Cut Above',
        'creator' => 'comedy_nose',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/23408922@N07',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/23408922@N07/42842605715',
      ),
    ),
    '/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/silky-saw-head-for-standard-poles/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/silky-saw-head-for-standard-poles/',
      'category' => '/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/',
      'name' => 'Silky Saw Head for Standard Poles',
      'brand' => 'silky',
      'brand_name' => 'Silky',
      'kind' => 'pole-saw',
      'price' => 40,
      'keyword' => 'silky pole saw head',
      'title' => 'Silky Pole Saw Head for Standard Poles',
      'h1' => 'Silky Pole Saw Head for Standard Poles',
      'meta' => 'Silky pole saw head that fits standard sectional poles, so you can run a Silky blade on the pole system you already own. Clean, fast pull-stroke cuts overhead.',
      'tagline' => 'Run a Silky pole saw blade on a standard sectional pole.',
      'summary' => 'This Silky pole saw head lets you mount a Silky pole saw blade on a standard pruning pole instead of a Silky telescoping pole. If your crew already runs sectional fiberglass poles, it is a way to get Silky\'s pull-stroke cutting on the same kit.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Mounts a Silky blade on standard poles',
        1 => 'Pull-stroke cutting for fast overhead work',
        2 => 'Use with the poles you already carry',
        3 => 'Confirm blade model and pole fit',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Pole saw head adapter',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Standard sectional poles (confirm diameter)',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Blade',
          1 => 'Silky pole saw blades (sold separately unless listed)',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why crews use it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Silky pole saw blades have a reputation for cutting fast on the pull stroke with impulse-hardened teeth. Many crews like that blade but prefer the reach, stiffness or dielectric features of the sectional poles they already own. This head bridges the two.',
            1 => 'It is also a tidy way to standardize: one pole system on the truck, with saw and pruner heads that swap as the job changes from lifting canopy to clearing deadwood.',
            2 => 'There is a practical side too. Telescoping poles are quick to adjust but can be heavy and harder to keep tight at full extension. Sectional poles let you add exactly the reach you need, and they are simple to repair because each section can be replaced on its own. Putting a Silky blade on a sectional pole gives you the cutting speed of one system with the reach and repairability of the other. Inspect the head and blade bolts each time you change blades.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Setup and use',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Fit the head to the top pole section and bolt the blade on evenly. Check that nothing twists before you start cutting. Let the pull stroke do the work, and make an undercut on heavier limbs so the bark doesn\'t tear back toward the trunk.',
            1 => 'Keep the blade in a scabbard when not cutting. See more heads, blades and covers in our <a href="/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/">pole saws and scabbards</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Which Silky blades fit this head?',
          'a' => 'Check the listing for compatible Silky pole saw blades and match the bolt pattern.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use it on a telescoping pole?',
          'a' => 'It is made for standard sectional pole fittings. Telescoping poles usually take their own heads.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'pruning',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/silky/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pole-saw-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pole-saw-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => '2009. Lorraine MacLauchlan (B.C. Ministry of Forests, Southern Interior Region) with pole-pruner to sample Douglas-fir tussock moth larvae, pre-spray. Testing the effectiveness of low-dose application rates of TM-Biocontrol-1. Kamloops, B.C.',
        'creator' => 'USDA Forest Service',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/151887236@N05',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/151887236@N05/39161789941',
      ),
    ),
    '/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/weaver-pole-saw-scabbard-fits-13-hook-tip-blades/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/weaver-pole-saw-scabbard-fits-13-hook-tip-blades/',
      'category' => '/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/',
      'name' => 'Weaver Pole Saw Scabbard for 13" Hook Tip Blades',
      'brand' => 'weaver',
      'brand_name' => 'Weaver',
      'kind' => 'case-bag',
      'price' => 30,
      'keyword' => 'weaver pole saw scabbard',
      'title' => 'Weaver Pole Saw Scabbard for 13" Hook Tip Blades',
      'h1' => 'Weaver Pole Saw Scabbard for 13" Hook Tip Blades',
      'meta' => 'Weaver pole saw scabbard sized for 13 inch hook tip blades. Covers the teeth for transport and storage, protecting the blade, the truck and the crew.',
      'tagline' => 'A blade cover for 13 inch hook tip pole saw blades.',
      'summary' => 'The Weaver pole saw scabbard for 13 inch hook tip blades covers the teeth and hook of a pole saw blade when it is not cutting. It protects the blade\'s edge, the truck and anyone handling the poles.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Sized for 13 inch hook tip pole saw blades',
        1 => 'Covers teeth and hook for transport',
        2 => 'Protects crew hands and other gear',
        3 => 'Weaver build for daily truck use',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Pole saw scabbard',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => '13 in hook tip blades',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Weaver',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why cover the blade',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A pole saw blade rides in the truck or on the rack between jobs, and an uncovered blade is a hazard. It catches on rope bags, chews up tarps and slices hands when someone grabs the poles in a hurry. Teeth also dull fast when they rattle against steel and gravel.',
            1 => 'A scabbard sized for the blade stays on during travel and keeps the hook tip from snagging anything on the way.',
            2 => 'It also saves money. Pole saw blades are not cheap, and teeth that bang against tools and the truck bed lose their edge long before they wear out from cutting. A blade that stays covered between jobs cuts faster for longer and needs replacing less often. On crews that share poles, a scabbard on every blade is a simple rule that protects everyone who handles the gear.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Getting the right fit',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'This version is cut for 13 inch blades with a hook tip. A scabbard that is too short leaves teeth exposed, and one made for a straight tip won\'t cover the hook properly. Measure your blade and check its tip shape before ordering.',
            1 => 'Slide the scabbard on whenever the pole goes down for more than a moment, and keep it clean so grit doesn\'t wear the teeth. Weaver also makes a 16 inch version. Browse our <a href="/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/">pole saws and scabbards</a> for more.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will this fit a 13 inch blade without a hook tip?',
          'a' => 'It may, but it is shaped for hook tip blades. A straight tip blade may sit loose.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I leave the scabbard on while carrying the pole on site?',
          'a' => 'Yes, and you should. Remove it only when you are ready to cut.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'pruning',
        1 => 'ground',
        2 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/weaver/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Climbing Gear for Mt. Fuji',
        'creator' => 'KE-TA',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/41894154003@N01',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/41894154003@N01/1262155760',
      ),
    ),
    '/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/weaver-pole-saw-scabbard-fits-16-blades/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/weaver-pole-saw-scabbard-fits-16-blades/',
      'category' => '/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/',
      'name' => 'Weaver Pole Saw Scabbard for 16" Blades',
      'brand' => 'weaver',
      'brand_name' => 'Weaver',
      'kind' => 'case-bag',
      'price' => 32,
      'keyword' => 'weaver 16 inch pole saw scabbard',
      'title' => 'Weaver 16 Inch Pole Saw Scabbard: Blade Cover',
      'h1' => 'Weaver 16 Inch Pole Saw Scabbard',
      'meta' => 'Weaver 16 inch pole saw scabbard sized for 16 inch blades. Keeps teeth covered in the truck and on site so the blade stays sharp and nobody gets cut.',
      'tagline' => 'A blade cover sized for 16 inch pole saw blades.',
      'summary' => 'The Weaver 16 inch pole saw scabbard covers a 16 inch pole saw blade during transport and storage. Longer blades are more exposed and harder to manage, so a properly sized cover matters even more.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Sized for 16 inch pole saw blades',
        1 => 'Protects teeth from dulling in transport',
        2 => 'Keeps hands and gear clear of the edge',
        3 => 'Weaver construction for daily use',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Pole saw scabbard',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => '16 in pole saw blades',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Weaver',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Covering a long blade',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A 16 inch pole saw blade gives you a big bite for heavier limbs, and a lot of exposed teeth when the pole is lying in the truck. Without a cover it snags rope, cuts into bags and puts the crew at risk when they grab poles off the rack.',
            1 => 'The scabbard slides over the blade and stays on until you are ready to cut, protecting the teeth from rocks, steel and other tools.',
            2 => 'Covered blades also stay sharper. Teeth that rattle against steel, gravel and other tools lose their edge long before they wear out from cutting, and a dull pole saw is slow, tiring and harder to control overhead. Keeping a scabbard on between cuts is one of the cheapest ways to get more life from a blade. On crews that share poles, a scabbard on every blade is a simple rule that protects everyone who handles the gear.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Measure the blade before ordering. A scabbard that is too short leaves the tip teeth bare. If you run a 13 inch hook tip blade, Weaver makes a separate scabbard for that size.',
            1 => 'Shake out sawdust and grit regularly so it doesn\'t grind the teeth in storage. Replace the scabbard if it splits along the edge. More covers and blades are in our <a href="/cutting-and-pruning/pole-saws-and-scabbards/">pole saws and scabbards</a> range.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will a 16 inch scabbard work on a 13 inch blade?',
          'a' => 'It will cover it, but it will sit loose and can slide off. Use a scabbard sized to your blade.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does it fit hook tip blades?',
          'a' => 'Check the tip shape of your blade against the scabbard. Weaver offers a version shaped for 13 inch hook tip blades.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'pruning',
        1 => 'ground',
        2 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/weaver/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'army duffel bag',
        'creator' => 'Prince Roy',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/17352537@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/17352537@N00/20210201772',
      ),
