        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Gear bag',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What fits in a 45 L bag',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A 45-liter bag holds a saddle, a rack of carabiners and devices, hitch cords, a lanyard, and throw line, with room left over for a helmet in many setups. Your main climbing line usually goes in its own rope bag.',
            1 => 'Packing the same way every time makes it obvious when something is missing before you leave the shop.',
            2 => 'Keep a small checklist in the bag so you can confirm before leaving the shop that your saddle, hitch cords, lanyard, throw line, and carabiners are all there. A forgotten piece of life-support gear can end a climbing day before it starts.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Protecting life-support gear',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Bar oil, fuel, and battery acid can damage soft goods without visible signs. A dedicated bag keeps the kit clean and separate from saws and fluids. Empty and dry it after wet days so gear does not sit damp.',
            1 => 'Store the bag somewhere dry and away from fuel and bar oil. If something leaks onto it, check every piece of soft gear inside before it goes back into service, and retire anything contaminated.',
            2 => 'See more <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/gear-bags/">gear bags</a>. Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard (ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions), be inspected before every use, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will a 45 L bag hold my climbing rope?',
          'a' => 'Some shorter lines may fit, but most climbers keep the main rope in a separate rope bag.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why not just keep gear in a truck box?',
          'a' => 'Shared boxes expose soft goods to fuel, oil, and sharp tools. A dedicated bag protects life-support gear.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
