      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why arborists use ship augers',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Installing eye bolts, threaded rod and other through hardware means drilling straight, clean holes through living wood, often more than a foot deep. A ship auger has a single open flute and a lead screw that pulls the bit into the cut. It clears chips well in wet green wood where twist bits clog and bind.',
            1 => 'This 13/16 in. bit suits larger through hardware. ANSI A300 Part 3 sets out how holes should be sized relative to the hardware, and in general the hole should be only slightly larger than through hardware so it fits snugly. Check the standard and the hardware maker\'s instructions for the exact size.',
            2 => 'Larger bits demand more from the drill and the person running it. Use a drill that can handle the torque, keep both hands on it, and be ready for the bit to bind if it hits a knot or decay pocket. Back it out and clear chips rather than forcing it.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Drilling from a climbing position',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Drilling aloft puts a lot of torque on you and the drill. Use a drill with a side handle and clutch if you have one, secure your position with two points of attachment where required, and keep your lanyard and climbing line clear of the bit. Back the bit out often to clear chips, and keep the bit sharp so it does not stall. Cabling and bracing is structural work on a living tree. It should be specified and installed by a trained arborist following ANSI A300 Part 3, ANSI Z133 and the hardware maker\'s instructions, and inspected on a regular schedule after install. Shop hardware and tools in our <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/">cabling and bracing</a> section.',
          ),
