          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Installing eye bolts, threaded rod and other through hardware means drilling straight, clean holes through living wood, often more than a foot deep. A ship auger has a single open flute and a lead screw that pulls the bit into the cut. It clears chips well in wet green wood where twist bits clog and bind.',
            1 => 'This 5/8 in. bit is a common mid-size choice in cabling kits. ANSI A300 Part 3 sets out how holes should be sized relative to the hardware, and in general the hole should be only slightly larger than through hardware so it fits snugly. Check the standard and the hardware maker\'s instructions for the exact size.',
            2 => 'A sharp, straight bit keeps the hole clean and on line with the cable, which matters for hardware that stays in the tree for years. Store bits in a roll or tube, touch up the cutting edges with a fine file when they dull, and do not use them on painted or treated lumber.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Drilling from a climbing position',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Drilling aloft puts a lot of torque on you and the drill. Use a drill with a side handle and clutch if you have one, secure your position with two points of attachment where required, and keep your lanyard and climbing line clear of the bit. Back the bit out often to clear chips, and keep the bit sharp so it does not stall. Cabling and bracing is structural work on a living tree. It should be specified and installed by a trained arborist following ANSI A300 Part 3, ANSI Z133 and the hardware maker\'s instructions, and inspected on a regular schedule after install. Shop hardware and tools in our <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/">cabling and bracing</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What size hardware is a 5/8 in. bit for?',
          'a' => 'It depends on the hardware and the standard you follow. Check ANSI A300 Part 3 and the hardware maker\'s chart to match bit to hardware.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I run a ship auger in a cordless drill?',
          'a' => 'Yes, a strong cordless drill with a suitable chuck and side handle works. Larger bits need a high-torque drill and a firm stance.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
        1 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/power-tool-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/power-tool-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'NCCC - Fuels Reduction',
        'creator' => 'Joshua Tree National Park',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/115357548@N08',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/115357548@N08/51765717788',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/havens-grips/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/havens-grips/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/',
      'name' => 'Havens Grip Cable Puller',
      'brand' => 'klein',
      'brand_name' => 'Klein Tools',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 95,
      'keyword' => 'havens grip',
      'title' => 'Havens Grip for Tensioning Tree Support Cable',
      'h1' => 'Havens Grips',
      'meta' => 'Havens grip for tensioning steel tree support cable. Clamps onto the strand so a come-along can pull it snug before you terminate it on cabling hardware.',
      'tagline' => 'Cable grip that clamps steel strand so you can pull it tight before terminating.',
      'summary' => 'A Havens grip clamps onto steel cable so you can pull the line snug with a come-along before you terminate it. In tree cabling it is the tool that takes up slack between anchors so the finished system sits at the right tension.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Clamps onto steel strand without a fixed end',
        1 => 'Pairs with a come-along or hoist to pull cable',
        2 => 'Grip tightens as load increases',
        3 => 'Size the grip to the cable diameter you use',
        4 => 'Inspect jaws for wear before every job',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Cable grip (Havens / come-along grip)',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Tensioning steel tree support cable',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sizes',
          1 => 'Select by cable diameter range',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How it is used in cabling',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'When installing a static steel cable between two leaders, you anchor one end, run the cable to the second anchor and then need to take up slack before terminating. The Havens grip clamps onto the cable, a come-along hooks to the grip and the anchor, and you pull the leaders together just enough to remove slack, not to drag them into a new position.',
            1 => 'Grip jaws are designed to bite harder as load increases, but they only work on the cable diameter range they are rated for. A grip that is too big will slip, and one that is too small will not close properly. Check the stamped size range and the working load against the job.',
            2 => 'A Havens grip is also handy beyond cabling. Crews use come-along grips to snug guy lines and to hold cable while a second fitting is installed. Treat it as a tensioning tool with a rated capacity, and do not use it for lifting or for life support.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Keep the jaws clean of sap and grit and check the teeth for wear. Worn jaws can slip under load. Stay out of the line of pull when tensioning, since a slipping grip or failed cable can snap back. Cabling and bracing is structural work on a living tree. It should be specified and installed by a trained arborist following ANSI A300 Part 3, ANSI Z133 and the hardware maker\'s instructions, and inspected on a regular schedule after install. See more tools in our <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/">cabling and bracing</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What size Havens grip do I need?',
          'a' => 'Match the grip\'s rated cable diameter range to the cable you are installing. The range is usually stamped on the grip.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can a Havens grip be left on the cable?',
          'a' => 'No. It is a tensioning tool. Terminate the cable with the correct hardware and remove the grip.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/klein/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-5.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-5-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Eye bolt snow bokeh',
        'creator' => 'dsgetch',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/66136677@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/66136677@N00/51782009502',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/hit-cable-cutter/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/hit-cable-cutter/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/',
      'name' => 'HIT Cable Cutter',
      'brand' => 'hit',
      'brand_name' => 'HIT',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 70,
      'keyword' => 'hit cable cutter',
      'title' => 'HIT Cable Cutter for Steel Tree Cabling Strand',
      'h1' => 'HIT Cable Cutter',
      'meta' => 'HIT cable cutter for trimming steel cable and strand cleanly during tree cabling installs. A compact hand tool for the cabling kit that saves time and fraying.',
      'tagline' => 'Hand cable cutter for clean cuts on steel strand during tree cabling jobs.',
      'summary' => 'The HIT cable cutter is a hand tool for cutting steel cable and strand cleanly, which is a regular job when installing tree support systems. A clean cut keeps the strand from unraveling and makes terminations neater.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Made for cutting steel cable and strand',
        1 => 'Clean cut that limits fraying',
        2 => 'Hand-powered, no blade to change',
        3 => 'Check the rated cable size and type before cutting',
        4 => 'A useful tool for every cabling kit',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'HIT',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Hand cable cutter',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Cutting steel cable and strand within its rated capacity',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a dedicated cable cutter',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Cutting galvanized strand with bolt cutters or a grinder tends to crush and fray the end, and a grinder throws sparks and heat. A dedicated cable cutter has shaped jaws that shear the wires together, leaving an end that is easier to feed into grips and dead-ends.',
            1 => 'Every cutter has a rated capacity, and some are not made for hardened steel such as high-strength strand. Check the tool\'s rated cable size and type before using it, and do not force it on cable beyond that rating, which can damage the jaws.',
            2 => 'Keep the cutter in the cabling bag with the grips, dead-ends and thimbles, so everything needed to finish a run of cable is in one place. A cutter that lives in the truck tool box is a cutter that is not there when you are in the tree with a cable end to trim.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Using it on the job',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Tape the cable on both sides of the cut to keep it tidy, set the cable deep in the jaws and cut with a steady squeeze. Wear eye protection and gloves since cut strand ends are sharp. Keep the pivot clean and lightly oiled. Cabling and bracing is structural work on a living tree. It should be specified and installed by a trained arborist following ANSI A300 Part 3, ANSI Z133 and the hardware maker\'s instructions, and inspected on a regular schedule after install. Find more tools in our <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/">cabling and bracing</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will it cut EHS strand?',
          'a' => 'Only if the cutter is rated for it. Check the capacity and cable types listed for the model you have.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I stop the cut end from fraying?',
          'a' => 'Wrap tape on both sides of the cut point before cutting, and cut with the cable seated deep in the jaws.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Eye Bolt - Basil Watson\'s Sopwith-Type Biplane, Melbourne, Victoria, 1916',
        'creator' => 'Photographer: David Thompson',
        'creator_url' => '',
        'license' => 'CC BY 4.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://collections.museumsvictoria.com.au/items/393624',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/preformed-line-products-tree-cabling-lag-spinner/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/preformed-line-products-tree-cabling-lag-spinner/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/',
      'name' => 'Preformed Line Products Tree Cabling Lag Spinner',
      'brand' => 'preformed-line-products',
      'brand_name' => 'Preformed Line Products',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 45,
      'keyword' => 'lag spinner',
      'title' => 'Preformed Line Products Lag Spinner for Tree Cabling',
      'h1' => 'Preformed Line Products Tree Cabling Lag Spinner',
      'meta' => 'Preformed Line Products lag spinner for tree cabling. A drill attachment that drives lag eye hardware into pilot holes faster and straighter than by hand.',
      'tagline' => 'Drill attachment for driving lag-type cabling hardware into the tree.',
      'summary' => 'The Preformed Line Products lag spinner lets you drive lag-type cabling hardware with a drill instead of cranking it in with a bar. It speeds up installs and helps set hardware to depth evenly.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Drives lag-type eye hardware with a drill',
        1 => 'Faster than turning hardware in by hand',
        2 => 'Helps set hardware straight and to depth',
        3 => 'Pairs with a properly sized pilot hole',
        4 => 'A handy addition to a cabling kit',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Preformed Line Products',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Lag spinner / driver',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Installing lag-type tree cabling hardware',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What it does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Lag eyes and similar hardware are threaded into a pilot hole drilled into the leader. Turned by hand with a bar through the eye, it is slow and easy to wobble off line. A lag spinner fits the eye and is driven by a drill, so the hardware goes in steadily and straight.',
            1 => 'Use a drill with a clutch or controlled speed so you can stop when the hardware reaches depth. Over-driving can strip the threads in the wood and weaken the hold.',
            2 => 'A lag spinner is most useful on jobs with several anchors, such as multi-leader systems. Setting each lag at the same speed and depth helps keep the system consistent. Keep the spinner clean and check it for wear where it bears on the eye, since a worn spinner can slip and mark the hardware.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Installation basics',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Drill the pilot hole to the size the hardware maker specifies for lags, keep the hardware in line with the cable direction, and stop when the eye is set. Drilling and driving aloft takes a solid position, so keep your climbing system clear of the drill and work with two points of attachment where required. Cabling and bracing is structural work on a living tree. It should be specified and installed by a trained arborist following ANSI A300 Part 3, ANSI Z133 and the hardware maker\'s instructions, and inspected on a regular schedule after install. See more in our <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/">cabling and bracing</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use it with any drill?',
          'a' => 'Use a drill with enough torque and a clutch or variable speed so you can control the final turns. Check the spinner\'s fit against the hardware you use.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is lag hardware right for every tree?',
          'a' => 'No. Hardware type depends on the stem size, wood condition and system design. Follow ANSI A300 Part 3 and get a qualified arborist to specify the system.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/preformed-line-products/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Eye Bolt',
        'creator' => 'Clearly Ambiguous',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/84617037@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/84617037@N00/428612624',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/weaver-nylon-basket-for-cable/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/weaver-nylon-basket-for-cable/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/',
      'name' => 'Weaver Nylon Basket for Cable',
      'brand' => 'weaver',
      'brand_name' => 'Weaver',
      'kind' => 'case-bag',
      'price' => 45,
      'keyword' => 'weaver nylon basket for cable',
      'title' => 'Weaver Nylon Basket for Cable and Cabling Hardware',
      'h1' => 'Weaver Nylon Basket for Cable',
      'meta' => 'Weaver nylon basket for cable that keeps a coil of tree support cable and hardware together for the climb. Clips to the saddle or hangs on a line in the canopy.',
      'tagline' => 'Nylon basket to carry tree cable and hardware up the tree.',
      'summary' => 'The Weaver nylon basket for cable gives you a place to carry a coil of support cable and hardware into the tree. Having everything in one basket at the work position saves trips and keeps parts from dropping to the ground.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Nylon basket for cable and cabling hardware',
        1 => 'Keeps parts together at the work position',
        2 => 'Can be sent up on a line or hung near the work',
        3 => 'Durable nylon from a long-time arborist gear maker',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Weaver',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Nylon basket / bucket',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Carrying cable and cabling hardware aloft',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a basket helps on cabling jobs',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A cabling install means a coil of cable, grips, dead-ends, hardware, a drill and bits, all needed at the same spot high in the crown. Stuffing hardware in pockets is how parts end up in the lawn. A basket hung near the work position holds the coil and small parts so you can reach them without juggling.',
            1 => 'Weaver has been making arborist gear for a long time, and this basket is made in tough nylon that stands up to cable ends and hardware better than a light bag.',
            2 => 'Cable coils are springy and awkward, and cut strand ends are sharp. A basket with a stiff body holds the coil open so it does not unwind across your lap. Keep hand tools, such as the cable cutter and grips, in the basket too so you are not reaching for pockets while on rope.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Using it safely aloft',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Hang the basket from a dedicated attachment point, not from your life-support system, and keep it below the weight the basket and attachment are rated to hold. Keep the work zone below clear in case anything drops, and send heavy items up on a separate line. Inspect the stitching and handles before each job. Find more tools and hardware in our <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/">cabling and bracing</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I clip it to my saddle?',
          'a' => 'Use a gear loop or a dedicated tool attachment, never your life-support connection points, and keep the load light.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will it hold a full coil of cable?',
          'a' => 'It depends on the coil size. Check the basket\'s dimensions against the cable you carry.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
        1 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/weaver/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Climbing Gear for Mt. Fuji',
        'creator' => 'KE-TA',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/41894154003@N01',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/41894154003@N01/1262155760',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/1-2-turnbuckle/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/1-2-turnbuckle/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/',
      'name' => '1/2 in. Turnbuckle',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 18,
      'keyword' => '1/2 turnbuckle',
      'title' => '1/2 Turnbuckle for Tree Cabling and Guying',
      'h1' => '1/2 Turnbuckle (1/2 in. Thread)',
      'meta' => '1/2 turnbuckle in galvanized steel for adjusting tension in tree support and guying systems. Fine take-up adjustment once cable is set between two anchors.',
      'tagline' => 'A 1/2 in. turnbuckle for fine tension adjustment in guying and support systems.',
      'summary' => 'A 1/2 turnbuckle, meaning one with 1/2 in. threads, gives you fine tension adjustment in a steel cable or guying system. Turning the body draws the two ends together or lets them out, so slack can be taken up after the system is installed.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '1/2 in. thread size',
        1 => 'Fine tension adjustment after installation',
        2 => 'Used in guying and some support systems',
        3 => 'Choose galvanized hardware for outdoor use',
        4 => 'Check the rated working load for your system',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Turnbuckle',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Thread size',
          1 => '1/2 in',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where turnbuckles fit',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Turnbuckles are used where a system needs to be adjusted after it is in place, such as guying a newly planted or leaning tree to a ground anchor. They are less common in modern static cabling between leaders, where cable is usually tensioned with a grip and come-along before termination.',
            1 => 'Pick the end fittings (eye, hook or jaw) to suit the hardware they connect to. Hooks can open under shock load, so many installers prefer eye or jaw ends for anything structural.',
            2 => 'Use galvanized hardware so the turnbuckle matches the rest of the system and resists rust outdoors. Check the turnbuckle\'s rated working load against the system design, and never use a turnbuckle as part of a life-support system or rigging setup.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Install and inspect',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Thread both ends in evenly so the turnbuckle has room to take up or let out. Lock the body with wire or a lock nut so it cannot back off with movement. Tension only enough to take out slack. Check the system on a regular schedule and adjust or remove guying when it is no longer needed. Cabling and bracing is structural work on a living tree. It should be specified and installed by a trained arborist following ANSI A300 Part 3, ANSI Z133 and the hardware maker\'s instructions, and inspected on a regular schedule after install. See more hardware in our <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/">cabling hardware</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need a turnbuckle for tree cabling?',
          'a' => 'Not usually for leader-to-leader cabling. They are more common in guying, where tension may need adjusting over time.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I stop a turnbuckle loosening?',
          'a' => 'Lock the body with safety wire or lock nuts after setting tension.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Stainless steel eye-bolt',
        'creator' => 'Unknown',
        'creator_url' => '',
        'license' => 'CC0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/6032390/photo-image-public-domain-free-silver',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/5-8-eye-bolt/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/5-8-eye-bolt/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/',
      'name' => '5/8 in. Eye Bolt',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 22,
      'keyword' => '5/8 eye bolt',
      'title' => '5/8 Eye Bolt for Tree Cabling Through Hardware',
      'h1' => '5/8 Eye Bolt (5/8 in. Diameter)',
      'meta' => '5/8 eye bolt, drop-forged, for through-bolt tree cabling and bracing anchors. Install with a washer and nut through the leader to the ANSI A300 standard.',
      'tagline' => 'A 5/8 in. eye bolt anchor for through-bolted tree cabling systems.',
      'summary' => 'A 5/8 eye bolt is a 5/8 in. diameter through-bolt anchor for tree cabling. It passes through the leader and is held with a washer and nut on the far side, giving the cable a strong attachment point.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '5/8 in. diameter through-bolt anchor',
        1 => 'Drop-forged eye for cable attachment',
        2 => 'Installed with washer and nut on the far side',
        3 => 'Choose galvanized for outdoor durability',
        4 => 'Select length to suit the stem diameter',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Eye bolt (through hardware)',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '5/8 in',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Lengths',
          1 => 'Select by stem diameter',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How eye bolts are used',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Through hardware is the strong option in a cabling system. The eye bolt passes completely through the leader, so the load is shared by wood on both sides. A flat or beveled washer and nut on the far side seat the hardware against the bark, and the cable attaches to the eye.',
            1 => 'Choose a length that passes through the stem with enough thread for the washer and nut. Drill the hole in line with the direction of the cable so the eye does not bend under load, and size the hole per ANSI A300 Part 3.',
            2 => 'Through hardware such as a 5/8 eye bolt is common in cabling and in some bracing setups. Using the same diameter hardware throughout a system keeps the drill bits, washers and nuts consistent in the kit and makes it easier to inspect.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Install and inspect',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Use drop-forged, galvanized hardware rated for the job, and use a thimble where the cable passes through the eye. Do not set the eye deep into the wood or cut away bark to seat hardware. Inspect for corrosion, bending and wood decay around the hardware on a regular schedule. Cabling and bracing is structural work on a living tree. It should be specified and installed by a trained arborist following ANSI A300 Part 3, ANSI Z133 and the hardware maker\'s instructions, and inspected on a regular schedule after install. Find the rest of the system in our <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/">cabling hardware</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What size hole do I drill for a 5/8 eye bolt?',
          'a' => 'Follow ANSI A300 Part 3 and the hardware maker\'s chart. Through-hardware holes are drilled only slightly larger than the bolt.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is through hardware better than lags?',
          'a' => 'Through hardware is generally stronger and is often preferred where the stem size allows. The system designer should choose based on the tree.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Eye Bolt - Basil Watson\'s Sopwith-Type Biplane, Melbourne, Victoria, 1916',
        'creator' => 'Photographer: David Thompson',
        'creator_url' => '',
        'license' => 'CC BY 4.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://collections.museumsvictoria.com.au/items/413404',
      ),
