        array (
          0 => 'Ratings',
          1 => 'See tag on sling and ring markings',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Big stems, heavy wood',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'When the rigging point is a large trunk and the wood is heavy, you need length and strength. At 15 ft, this sling can wrap wide stems and still leave tail for a good choke, and 3/4 inch rope carries the loads that come with big removals.',
            1 => 'The ring gives the line a smooth, friction-adding path, or a place to clip a block. More rigging slings are in our <a href="/rigging/rigging-slings/heavy-duty-slings/">heavy-duty slings</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and limits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Before each job, run the sling through your hands. Look for cut or pulled strands, glazed or melted fibers, flat or stiff spots, and heavy UV fading, and check that the splices and buries are intact. Retire it if you find damage you can\'t explain or after a shock load bigger than you planned for.',
            1 => 'Heavy rigging puts serious load on the stem as well as the gear, so pick a sound rigging point. Rigging gear is for moving wood, not for life support. Match every component to the load with a working margin, follow the manufacturer\'s ratings and ANSI Z133, and make sure the crew is trained in rigging before relying on it. Note when each piece went into service, so worn gear gets retired on time instead of living on the truck for years.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why such a long sling?',
          'a' => 'Length lets you wrap large stems with enough tail for a proper choke and to position the ring where you want it.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Should the rope run through the ring or a block?',
          'a' => 'A ring adds friction and saves weight; a block runs freer. Choose based on wood size and how much friction you want at the top.',
        ),
      ),
