          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pick a sling and connector rated for the loads you plan to put on the system, and confirm rope diameter compatibility with the manufacturer before you buy. Compare it with other <a href="/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rings-and-other-hardware/">rigging rings and hardware</a> to see which style suits your work.',
            1 => 'Check the rope-bearing surface for burrs, grooves, or rough spots that could damage rope, and retire the device if it is cracked or deformed. Rig only within the rated strength of every component in the system, inspect gear before each job, and make sure the crew is trained in rigging practice under ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a low-friction ring the same as a pulley?',
          'a' => 'No. It has no rotating sheave, so it adds some friction. That can be useful for controlling a lowering line, but a pulley is more efficient for hauling.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What rope sizes does it take?',
          'a' => 'Check Tylaska\'s published specifications for this model. Rope fit and rated strength vary by size, and the manufacturer\'s figures are the ones to follow.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/tylaska/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Pruning in RVA (Carytown, Virginia)',
        'creator' => 'Gamma Man',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06/48982704071',
      ),
    ),
    '/rings/dmm-matt-gray-anchor-rings/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rings/dmm-matt-gray-anchor-rings/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/rings/',
      'name' => 'DMM Anchor Rings, Matt Grey',
      'brand' => 'dmm',
      'brand_name' => 'DMM',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 24,
      'keyword' => 'dmm anchor ring',
      'title' => 'DMM Anchor Ring, Matt Grey, for Tree Climbing',
      'h1' => 'DMM Anchor Rings, Matt Grey',
      'meta' => 'DMM anchor rings in matt grey for friction savers, adjustable anchors, and spliced eyes. See how climbers use them, what to inspect, and the typical price.',
      'tagline' => 'Machined DMM rings for friction savers, anchors, and custom climbing setups.',
      'summary' => 'DMM anchor rings are machined rings that arborists use to build friction savers, adjustable tie-in anchors, and other custom climbing setups. The matt grey finish is a low-glare color option that is easy to tell apart from colored rings on a busy harness.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Machined one-piece ring from DMM',
        1 => 'Matt grey finish',
        2 => 'Smooth bore for rope and hitch cord',
        3 => 'Used for friction savers and adjustable anchors',
        4 => 'Easy to sort from colored rings',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'DMM',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Finish',
          1 => 'Matt grey',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Anchor ring',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How climbers use anchor rings',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Rings show up all over a modern climbing kit. A pair of rings on a length of rope makes a ring-and-ring friction saver that you can set and retrieve from the ground. A single ring on a sling makes a canopy anchor, and rings are often used on adjustable SRS base anchors and lanyard setups.',
            1 => 'Because a ring has no gate, it can take loading from several directions. The smooth bore lets rope and hitch cord run without catching, which matters when you retrieve a friction saver or advance a hitch around it. Having a few spare rings in the gear bag also makes it easy to build a quick redirect or replace a worn ring on a saver.',
            2 => 'A neutral matt grey ring is a good way to keep track of which rings belong to which system. Some climbers use one color for friction savers and another for lanyards so they can grab the right piece at a glance.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing, inspecting, and retiring',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the ring\'s rated strength and inner diameter before you build a system around it, and make sure it suits the rope and cord you will run through it. See the full range of <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/rings/">climbing rings</a> for other sizes and colors.',
            1 => 'Retire rings with deep grooves from rope wear, sharp edges, cracks, or any deformation, and after a serious fall or impact. Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard (ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions), be inspected before every use, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use these rings for a ring-and-ring friction saver?',
          'a' => 'Yes, that is a common use. Pair them with a rope or sling and build the saver following the maker\'s instructions and your training.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does the color affect strength?',
          'a' => 'No. The finish is cosmetic. Strength comes from the ring\'s design and material, so check the marked rating.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/dmm/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Carabiners and Pulley',
        'creator' => 'psd',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/45581782@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/45581782@N00/2411001794',
      ),
    ),
    '/rope-bags/all-gear-heavy-duty-rope-bag/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rope-bags/all-gear-heavy-duty-rope-bag/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/rope-bags/',
      'name' => 'All Gear Heavy Duty Rope Bag',
      'brand' => 'all-gear',
      'brand_name' => 'All Gear',
      'kind' => 'case-bag',
      'price' => 59,
      'keyword' => 'heavy duty rope bag',
      'title' => 'All Gear Heavy Duty Rope Bag for Climbing Lines',
      'h1' => 'All Gear Heavy Duty Rope Bag',
      'meta' => 'The All Gear heavy duty rope bag keeps climbing and rigging lines clean, dry, and ready to deploy. Learn sizing, packing tips, and what it typically costs.',
      'tagline' => 'A tough rope bag for keeping climbing and rigging lines clean between jobs.',
      'summary' => 'The All Gear heavy duty rope bag gives a climbing or rigging line a clean place to live between jobs, away from chips, fuel, and sun. Flake the rope in, close it up, and it pays out cleanly at the next tree.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Heavy-duty construction for daily truck use',
        1 => 'Keeps rope out of dirt, sap, and fuel',
        2 => 'Rope flakes in and pays out without tangles',
        3 => 'Carry handles for moving between trees',
        4 => 'Helps rope last longer',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'All Gear',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Rope bag',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Climbing or rigging line storage',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a rope bag matters',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Rope wears out from more than climbing. Grit works into the braid, chainsaw bar oil and fuel attack fibers, and UV fades and weakens the cover. A heavy duty rope bag keeps the line off the truck bed and out of the chip pile, so it lasts longer and is easier to inspect.',
            1 => 'Flaking the rope straight into the bag, rather than coiling it, means it feeds back out without kinks. Leave the end with your termination on top so you can grab it and go at the next tree.',
            2 => 'A sturdy bag also makes the truck safer and tidier. Rope that lives in a bag is not underfoot on the deck, is not wrapped around saw bars, and is not sitting in a puddle of bar oil. On a busy day, being able to toss one bag over a shoulder and walk to the next tree saves real time.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Sizing and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Choose a bag sized for your longest line with room to spare; a stuffed bag is slow to pack and hard to close. Compare capacities among other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/rope-bags/">rope bags</a> if you carry several ropes.',
            1 => 'Empty the bag now and then to shake out sawdust, and let it dry if it gets wet before storing rope in it. Keep life-support rope separate from fuel cans and sharp tools, and inspect the rope itself before every climb.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Should I flake or coil rope into the bag?',
          'a' => 'Flake it. Feeding rope in loosely, hand over hand, lets it pay out cleanly later without twists.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I store two ropes in one bag?',
          'a' => 'It is possible but they tend to tangle. One rope per bag, or a bag with separate compartments, is faster on the job.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/all-gear/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'army duffel bag',
        'creator' => 'Prince Roy',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/17352537@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/17352537@N00/20210201772',
      ),
    ),
    '/rope-bags/dmm-transit-rope-bag-30-l/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rope-bags/dmm-transit-rope-bag-30-l/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/rope-bags/',
      'name' => 'DMM Transit Rope Bag 30 L',
      'brand' => 'dmm',
      'brand_name' => 'DMM',
      'kind' => 'case-bag',
      'price' => 49,
      'keyword' => 'dmm transit rope bag',
      'title' => 'DMM Transit Rope Bag 30 L for Arborists',
      'h1' => 'DMM Transit Rope Bag, 30 L',
      'meta' => 'The DMM Transit rope bag in 30 L keeps climbing rope clean and organized between trees. See what fits, how it packs, and typical price in the US.',
      'tagline' => 'A 30 liter DMM rope bag for carrying and deploying climbing line.',
      'summary' => 'The DMM Transit rope bag in the 30 L size is a carry bag for climbing rope that keeps the line clean and ready to deploy. The 30 L size suits a single climbing line of moderate length and is compact enough to clip to a saddle or tuck behind the seat.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '30 liter capacity',
        1 => 'Holds rope clean and off the ground',
        2 => 'Easy to carry between trees',
        3 => 'Rope pays out without tangles',
        4 => 'Part of DMM\'s Transit bag range',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'DMM',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Capacity',
          1 => '30 L',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Rope bag',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Packing and using the Transit bag',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Flake your rope in loosely, hand over hand, and leave the working end on top. At the next tree you open the bag and the line feeds out without the knots and twists you get from a coiled rope tossed in the truck.',
            1 => 'The 30 L size suits a single climbing line of moderate length and is compact enough to clip to a saddle or tuck behind the seat. If you climb on one rope and rig with another, a second bag keeps them apart and makes it obvious which line is which.',
            2 => 'A bag also protects rope from UV while it sits in the sun on the drop zone, and it keeps the line from being stepped on, driven over, or dragged through mud between trees. Those small savings add up to a longer service life for an expensive climbing line.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Sizing and rope care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A bag that is too small is slow to pack and hard to close, so size up if you are unsure. See the other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/rope-bags/">rope bags</a> for more capacities.',
            1 => 'Shake out sawdust regularly and let the bag dry before storing a rope in it. Keep climbing rope away from fuel, bar oil, and sharp tools, and inspect it before every climb; life-support rope must be retired if it shows cuts, glazing, or contamination.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How much rope fits in the 30 L bag?',
          'a' => 'It depends on rope diameter and length. Check DMM\'s capacity guidance, and allow some room so the rope is not crammed in.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is it only for climbing rope?',
          'a' => 'No. It works for throw line, slings, or a short rigging line, but most climbers dedicate it to one climbing rope.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/dmm/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'STE-NL-WK3-2-duffel-bag-10454',
        'creator' => 'Trendhim',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/148219711@N08',
