      'price' => 239,
      'keyword' => 'samson true blue',
      'title' => 'Samson True Blue 12-Strand Climbing Rope',
      'h1' => 'Samson True Blue Climbing Rope',
      'meta' => 'Samson True Blue is a classic 1/2 in 12-strand climbing rope that arborists have tied in on for years. See how it handles, hitch tips, and the typical price.',
      'tagline' => 'Samson\'s classic 12-strand arborist climbing line in 1/2 in.',
      'summary' => 'Samson True Blue is a classic 12-strand climbing rope that many arborists learned to climb on. It has a firm hand, a predictable feel with friction hitches, and the easy-to-splice construction of a 12-strand braid.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '12-strand braid construction',
        1 => '1/2 in (about 12.7 mm) diameter',
        2 => 'Works with friction hitches and many devices',
        3 => 'Bright color stands out in the canopy',
        4 => 'Sold in common arborist lengths',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Samson Rope',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Construction',
          1 => '12-strand braid',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '1/2 in (about 12.7 mm)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Primary use',
          1 => 'Tree climbing, MRS and SRS',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How True Blue climbs',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'True Blue has been a standard on climbing crews for a long time, and its appeal is consistency. A 12-strand braid has no separate cover, so there is no cover slippage, and it is simple to splice an eye for a hardware termination.',
            1 => 'On a doubled-rope (MRS) system it works well with traditional friction hitches and a micro pulley to tend slack. Because it is a 12-strand, it can wear differently than a 24-strand line under a mechanical device, so check the device maker\'s approved rope list before using it on SRS.',
            2 => 'Because it has been around so long, True Blue is a useful reference rope. Plenty of trainers and crews know how common hitches behave on it, which makes it a sensible choice for climbers still settling on a system.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing length and caring for it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pick a length that covers twice the height of your typical tie-in for MRS, or the full height plus tail for SRS. Compare similar lines in <a href="/rope/climbing-rope/">climbing rope</a>.',
            1 => 'Keep the rope in a bag, away from fuel, bar oil, and sunlight, and run it through your hands before each climb looking for cuts, glazing, and flat or stiff spots. Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard (ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions), be inspected before every use, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is Samson True Blue good for beginners?',
          'a' => 'Many climbers learn on it because it holds friction hitches predictably and is easy to inspect. Training from a qualified climber is still essential.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I splice True Blue?',
          'a' => 'Yes. 12-strand rope is spliceable, but life-support splices should be done by a trained splicer following Samson\'s instructions.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
