          1 => '16-strand braid',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '1/2 in (about 12.7 mm)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Primary use',
          1 => 'MRS / doubled-rope climbing',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How XTC Plus climbs',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Sixteen-strand rope has a cover-and-core build that stays round under load, so Blake\'s, Distel, and VT hitches grab and release consistently. XTC Plus has the slightly stiffer hand many climbers associate with a durable work rope, and it softens with use.',
            1 => 'It runs cleanly through a friction saver and a micro pulley on your hitch. On a long limb walk it has a little more stretch than a low-stretch 24-strand, which some climbers prefer for comfort. Check device approvals before using it on SRS.',
            2 => 'Whatever line you choose, set it up the same way every time: a clean termination, a hitch or device you have tested on that rope, and a stopper knot in the tail. Consistency is what makes a rope feel predictable at height, and it makes problems easier to spot.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing length and caring for it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pick a length that covers twice the height of your typical tie-in for MRS, or the full height plus tail for SRS. Compare similar lines in <a href="/rope/climbing-rope/16-strand-climbing-rope/">16-strand climbing rope</a>.',
            1 => 'Keep the rope in a bag, away from fuel, bar oil, and sunlight, and run it through your hands before each climb looking for cuts, glazing, and flat or stiff spots. Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard (ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions), be inspected before every use, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is XTC Plus the same as XTC Fire or other XTC colors?',
          'a' => 'The XTC family shares a 16-strand design, but colors and versions can differ. Check Yale\'s spec sheet for the exact rope you buy.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does XTC Plus need breaking in?',
          'a' => 'Most 16-strand ropes feel stiff at first and soften after several climbs. Test your hitch low each time while it breaks in.',
        ),
      ),
