      'meta' => 'Teufelberger drenaLINE Climbing Line, Stars and Stripes for tree climbing on a hitch or device. How it handles on the job, what to check, and the typical price.',
      'tagline' => 'Teufelberger climbing line for tree climbing on a hitch or device.',
      'summary' => 'The Teufelberger drenaLINE Climbing Line is a climbing line for tree climbing on a hitch or device. Here is how it handles on the job, what to check before you buy, and how to keep it in service.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '24-strand braided climbing line',
        1 => 'Holds its round shape under a hitch',
        2 => 'Runs smoothly through pulleys and devices',
        3 => 'Bright colors easy to track in the canopy',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Teufelberger',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Construction',
          1 => '24-strand braided climbing rope',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Color',
          1 => 'Red, white and blue (Stars and Stripes pattern)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sold',
          1 => 'Hanks; length varies by listing',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Primary use',
          1 => 'Tree climbing (MRS/DdRT, SRS where approved)',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How it climbs',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'drenaLINE is one of Teufelberger\'s best-known arborist lines, and the Stars and Stripes version is the same rope in a red, white and blue pattern. Climbers pick it for a firm, round braid that keeps its shape under load and a cover that bites cleanly in a friction hitch.',
            1 => 'A 24-strand cover is tightly braided and round, so the rope holds its shape under a hitch, feeds smoothly through pulleys and mechanical devices, and resists flattening over branch unions. Lines like this are used for moving rope (MRS/DdRT) climbing and, where the manufacturer lists the rope as compatible, for stationary rope (SRS) work. Most climbers buy them in hanks with a spliced eye on one end.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Compatibility, inspection and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Before you buy, check the diameter range of your hitch cord, ascenders and descent device, since compatibility is set by those makers. You can compare it with the other <a href="/rope/climbing-rope/24-strand-climbing-rope/">24-strand climbing ropes</a> we carry.',
            1 => 'Climbing rope is life-support equipment. It must meet the applicable standard, ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions. Inspect it before every climb for cuts, glazing, sheath damage and soft or stiff spots, keep it away from saws, fuel and bar oil, and retire it when in doubt. Get trained before you climb.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use Teufelberger drenaLINE Climbing Line for SRS as well as MRS?',
          'a' => 'Many 24-strand lines work for both, but device compatibility is set by the device maker. Check that your SRS device is approved for this rope and its diameter before you climb on it.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What hitch cord works with Teufelberger drenaLINE Climbing Line?',
          'a' => 'Hitch cord should be smaller in diameter than the climbing line and tested on it before use. Tie the hitch, load it on the ground, and make sure it grabs and releases the way you expect.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/teufelberger/',
