      'meta' => 'Sterling 9mm hitch cord sold by the foot for friction hitches and eye-to-eye prusiks. Which climbing lines it pairs with and when to retire it.',
      'tagline' => 'A 9mm Sterling cord by the foot for building your own friction hitch.',
      'summary' => 'Sterling 9mm hitch cord sits between the common 8mm and 10mm sizes, giving climbers a middle option for friction hitches on popular arborist climbing lines. It is sold by the foot so you cut the length your hitch needs.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '9mm diameter',
        1 => 'Sold by the foot',
        2 => 'Middle ground between 8mm and 10mm cords',
        3 => 'For friction hitches and eye-to-eye prusiks',
        4 => 'From Sterling Rope',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '9 mm',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sold by',
          1 => 'The foot',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Sterling Rope',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a 9mm cord',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A hitch that grabs too hard is tiring to tend; one that grabs too little is dangerous. Going from 8mm to 10mm changes the bite a lot, and a 9mm cord gives you a step in between. Climbers often reach for it when 8mm feels grabby on their line and 10mm feels slow.',
            1 => 'Because you buy it by the foot, you can cut a length for a Valdotain, Distel, or other hitch, and finish the ends with knots or have eyes made. Look through other <a href="/rope/tech-cordage/">hitch cord and tech cordage</a> to compare sizes.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Testing, inspection, and replacement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Every hitch cord and climbing line combination behaves differently, and a new rope or a wet day can change it. Set up and test close to the ground before climbing, and adjust wraps and braids until the hitch holds and releases the way you want.',
            1 => 'A by-the-foot cord also makes it easy to experiment. Cut one length for a Valdotain tresse and another for a Distel, climb on each for a week, and keep the one that suits your rope and style. Write down the length that works so you can cut replacements without guessing.',
            2 => 'Check the cord daily for glazing, stiffness, and cover wear, and swap it out at the first sign of heat damage. Hitch cord is life-support gear: use it only in a system that meets ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions, inspect it before every climb, and get trained before you tie into anything new.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is 9mm hitch cord a good match for 11.7mm rope?',
          'a' => 'Many climbers find the middle size works on common 11 to 12mm lines, but test your exact combination low before using it aloft.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I tie my own eyes?',
          'a' => 'You can finish the ends with appropriate stopper knots per training, or have eyes sewn or spliced by a qualified shop.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/sterling-rope/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'coiling',
        'creator' => 'michael pollak',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06/6796140986',
      ),
    ),
    '/rope/tech-cordage/teufelberger-sirius-reep-schnur-per-foot/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rope/tech-cordage/teufelberger-sirius-reep-schnur-per-foot/',
      'category' => '/rope/tech-cordage/',
      'name' => 'Teufelberger Sirius Reepschnur Accessory Cord (Per Foot)',
      'brand' => 'teufelberger',
      'brand_name' => 'Teufelberger',
      'kind' => 'rope',
      'price' => 2,
      'keyword' => 'teufelberger reepschnur',
      'title' => 'Teufelberger Sirius Reepschnur Cord, Per Foot',
      'h1' => 'Teufelberger Sirius Reepschnur Accessory Cord',
      'meta' => 'Teufelberger Sirius Reepschnur accessory cord sold by the foot for prusiks, tag lines, and gear tethers. What it is for and how to check it.',
      'tagline' => 'Teufelberger accessory cord by the foot for prusiks, tethers, and utility jobs.',
      'summary' => 'Teufelberger Reepschnur is accessory cord from the Sirius line, sold by the foot so you can cut prusik loops, tethers, and utility lengths. Reepschnur is simply the German word for accessory cord.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Sold by the foot',
        1 => 'Teufelberger Sirius accessory cord',
        2 => 'Cut to length for prusiks and tethers',
        3 => 'Handy for tag lines and gear lanyards',
        4 => 'Check diameter at order time',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Accessory cord (Reepschnur)',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sold by',
          1 => 'The foot',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Teufelberger',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What accessory cord does on a tree crew',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Every climber ends up needing short lengths of good cord: a prusik to back up a rigging line, a tether for a hand saw, a loop to hang a water bottle, or a short tag line. Buying Reepschnur by the foot lets you cut what you need instead of buying pre-made pieces for each job.',
            1 => 'Confirm the diameter you are ordering, since accessory cord is offered in several sizes and the right one depends on what you are tying it to. Browse other <a href="/rope/tech-cordage/">tech cordage</a> for dedicated hitch cords.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use it within its limits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Not every cord is meant for life support. Before you use any cord in a climbing system, confirm with the seller and the manufacturer\'s information that it is rated and intended for that use. For anything that holds a person, use a dedicated arborist hitch cord or a certified prusik.',
            1 => 'Keeping a short hank of accessory cord in the gear bag saves a lot of improvising on the job. It ties off a loose limb before a cut, hangs a water jug in the shade, or rigs a quick tether for a pole saw head. Because you cut your own lengths, there is no waste and no hunting for the right pre-made loop.',
            2 => 'Seal cut ends cleanly, check cord for abrasion and stiffness, and replace it once it is worn or has been heated. Train on any hitch you plan to use before you rely on it.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What does Reepschnur mean?',
          'a' => 'It is German for accessory cord, the small-diameter cord used for prusiks, tethers, and utility jobs.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use it as my climbing hitch cord?',
          'a' => 'Only if the manufacturer rates that diameter for life-support friction hitches. Otherwise use a dedicated arborist hitch cord.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/teufelberger/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Walt coiling the rope at the summit',
        'creator' => 'Tristan Higbee',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62264264@N03',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62264264@N03/8305035257',
      ),
