          array (
            0 => 'Confirm the glasses are marked to the impact standard your work requires. Replace them once lenses are scratched, pitted, or cracked, since damaged lenses reduce both vision and protection.',
            1 => 'Keep both clear and tinted pairs on hand so crew members can match the lens to the light as it changes through the day. Remember to swap to clear when you move into deep shade or start work in a canopy. Store them in a case or a clean bag rather than loose on the dash, where they scratch and pick up sap. Replace any pair the moment it is scratched.',
            2 => 'Wear safety glasses for saw work, chipping, and climbing, and add a face screen for heavy chip and sawdust exposure. Follow ANSI Z133 for personal protective equipment.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I wear these under a mesh face screen?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Safety glasses under a mesh screen is a common setup for saw and chipper work.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'When should I use smoke lenses?',
          'a' => 'Smoke lenses cut glare on bright days, for open ground work, brush hauling, and roadside jobs.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
