          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A mesh visor is the standard face screen for chainsaw work. It does not fog, you can see through it in shade and sun, and it stops the chips and twigs that come off the bar. On the Protos it rides in the integrated mount and tucks up into the helmet when you do not need it.',
            1 => 'Mesh does let fine dust through, so many users wear safety glasses underneath when cutting dry wood. Our <a href="/safety/eye-protection/">eye protection</a> section has glasses and clear screens if you want a backup.',
            2 => 'Mesh visors also handle rain better than clear lenses, since water runs through rather than beading across your view. That makes mesh the default for most climbers in mixed weather, with a clear screen saved for chipping and grinding.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'When to replace the mesh',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Replace the screen when the mesh is torn, dented into your field of view, or clogged with pitch you cannot clean out. A damaged mesh can also rub or catch when you raise it.',
            1 => 'Check your Protos version against the listing before ordering, and follow Pfanner\'s instructions for removal and fitting.',
            2 => 'Some climbers keep a spare mesh screen in their gear bag. If a branch bends the mesh mid-job, a fresh screen gets you back to work in a few minutes rather than finishing the day without face protection. Clean pitch off the old screen promptly, because it hardens and becomes much harder to remove once it sets.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What does G16 mean?',
          'a' => 'It identifies this mesh visor version in Pfanner\'s range. Match it to your helmet using the listing or Pfanner\'s fit guide.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I clean pitch off the mesh?',
          'a' => 'A little citrus or resin cleaner and a soft brush usually works. Avoid harsh solvents that could damage the frame, and rinse with water afterward.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'safety',
        1 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/pfanner/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/helmet-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/helmet-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => '3rd Regiment, Basic Camp, FHCC CST 2021',
        'creator' => 'U.S. Army ROTC',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/136737541@N05',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/136737541@N05/51323491277',
      ),
    ),
    '/safety/fall-protection/6-reinforced-cross-arm-strap/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/safety/fall-protection/6-reinforced-cross-arm-strap/',
      'category' => '/safety/fall-protection/',
      'name' => '6 ft Reinforced Cross Arm Strap',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 59,
      'keyword' => 'cross arm strap',
      'title' => '6 ft Reinforced Cross Arm Strap Anchor',
      'h1' => '6 ft Reinforced Cross Arm Strap',
      'meta' => 'A 6 ft reinforced cross arm strap wraps a beam, pole or limb to make a quick anchor point. How cross arm straps are used, inspected and matched to your system.',
      'tagline' => 'A 6 ft wrap-around anchor strap for beams, poles and solid limbs.',
      'summary' => 'A cross arm strap is a reinforced webbing strap with a D-ring or loop at each end that wraps around a beam, pole or limb to create an anchor point. This 6 ft version gives enough wrap for most structural members and many tree stems.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '6 ft length for wrapping beams, poles and stems',
        1 => 'Reinforced webbing with wear protection',
        2 => 'Quick anchor without tying knots',
        3 => 'Packs small in a gear bag',
        4 => 'Useful for aerial lift and structure work',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Length',
          1 => '6 ft',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Reinforced cross arm anchor strap',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Webbing',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How a cross arm strap works',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'You wrap the strap around the anchor, pass one end through the other, and clip into the end ring. It cinches down on the member and gives you a clean connection point with no knots to tie. Utility crews use them on cross arms and poles; tree crews use them as quick anchors on structures and as a wrap on a stem when a rope sling is not handy.',
            1 => 'The reinforced section protects the webbing where it bears on edges and bark. Even so, keep it off sharp corners and keep it away from the saw. See our <a href="/safety/fall-protection/">fall protection</a> range for lanyards and other anchors.',
            2 => 'A 6 ft strap gives enough wrap for most beams and poles, plus many stems. If the anchor is too small for the strap to cinch properly, choke it with more wraps only if the manufacturer allows, or choose a shorter strap.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and limits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the webbing for cuts, burns, abrasion and chemical damage, and the hardware for cracks or distortion, before each use. Retire it after a fall or if you find damage.',
            1 => 'Use it only as the manufacturer\'s instructions allow and within its rated strength. Life-support and fall-arrest gear must meet the applicable standard, including ANSI Z133 for tree work, and should be used only by trained workers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use this as a tree climbing anchor?',
          'a' => 'Only if the manufacturer\'s instructions allow it for that use and the anchor itself is sound. Many climbers prefer a purpose-built friction saver or anchor sling for the tree.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why reinforced?',
          'a' => 'The reinforcement protects the webbing where it bears against the anchor, which is where straps usually wear first.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'safety',
        1 => 'bucket',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Climbing paraphernalia',
        'creator' => 'Ruth and Dave',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/95142644@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/95142644@N00/3584852771',
      ),
    ),
    '/safety/fall-protection/fred-the-rescue-dummy-rubber-filler/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/safety/fall-protection/fred-the-rescue-dummy-rubber-filler/',
      'category' => '/safety/fall-protection/',
      'name' => 'Fred the Rescue Dummy Rubber Filler',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'first-aid',
      'price' => 59,
      'keyword' => 'rescue dummy filler',
      'title' => 'Fred the Rescue Dummy Rubber Filler',
      'h1' => 'Fred the Rescue Dummy Rubber Filler',
      'meta' => 'Rubber filler for Fred the rescue dummy adds weight back to a training manikin for aerial rescue practice. How crews use rescue dummies and keep them realistic.',
      'tagline' => 'Rubber filler to bring a Fred rescue training dummy back up to weight.',
      'summary' => 'This rescue dummy filler is the rubber fill used to give Fred the rescue dummy its weight. Aerial rescue drills only teach the right lessons when the dummy is heavy enough to feel like a real person, and the filler is how you keep it that way.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Rubber fill for the Fred rescue dummy',
        1 => 'Restores realistic weight for drills',
        2 => 'Lets you adjust weight for different scenarios',
        3 => 'Keeps a training manikin in service',
        4 => 'Supports regular aerial rescue practice',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Rubber filler for rescue training dummy',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'For',
          1 => 'Fred rescue dummy',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Aerial rescue training',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why the dummy\'s weight matters',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Aerial rescue drills are about moving a limp, heavy person out of a tree or bucket. If the dummy is light, the lowering setup, the friction and the effort all feel easier than they will on a real day. Rubber filler brings the dummy up to a weight that makes practice honest.',
            1 => 'Crews often add or remove fill to simulate different climbers, which is a good way to test a rescue system with more than one load. Check our <a href="/safety/fall-protection/">fall protection</a> section for the rescue and fall-arrest gear you train with.',
            2 => 'Store the dummy dry between sessions. Moisture adds weight unevenly and can break down the fill and the dummy\'s covering over time. Check seams and closures before each drill so filler does not leak out mid-rescue, which is a mess to clean up and changes the weight halfway through the exercise.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Training notes',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Drill aerial rescue on a regular schedule and with the gear you actually carry. Rig for the dummy as if it were a person, inspect all life-support equipment before use, and have a trained, qualified person run the session.',
            1 => 'Follow ANSI Z133 and your employer\'s rescue plan. Confirm the filler amount and fit with the dummy\'s maker before filling.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How much filler do I need?',
          'a' => 'It depends on the dummy and the target weight. Check with the dummy\'s manufacturer for its fill capacity and intended weight.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why not just use sandbags?',
          'a' => 'Rubber fill spreads through the dummy and moves more like a body, while sandbags shift and concentrate weight in odd places.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/first-aid-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/first-aid-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'First Aid Kit',
        'creator' => 'dlg_images',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/131260238@N08',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/131260238@N08/16800384582',
      ),
    ),
    '/safety/fall-protection/petzl-asap-lock-fall-arrestor/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/safety/fall-protection/petzl-asap-lock-fall-arrestor/',
      'category' => '/safety/fall-protection/',
      'name' => 'Petzl ASAP Lock Fall Arrester',
      'brand' => 'petzl',
      'brand_name' => 'Petzl',
      'kind' => 'ascender',
      'price' => 189,
      'keyword' => 'petzl asap lock',
      'title' => 'Petzl ASAP Lock Fall Arrester: Specs and Uses',
      'h1' => 'Petzl ASAP Lock Fall Arrester',
      'meta' => 'The Petzl ASAP Lock is a mobile fall arrester for a backup rope that can be locked in place. How it works, rope fit, and where it fits in an arborist kit.',
      'tagline' => 'Mobile rope fall arrester with a locking function for backup on a second line.',
      'summary' => 'The Petzl ASAP Lock is a mobile fall arrester that rides a separate backup rope and locks if you fall. Its locking function lets you fix it in place on the rope, which is useful when you are working near the edge or want to limit how far it can travel.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Follows you up and down the backup rope',
        1 => 'Locking function fixes it at a point on the rope',
        2 => 'Opens to install anywhere on the rope',
        3 => 'Used with a Petzl energy absorber in fall arrest',
        4 => 'Compact and quick to clip in',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Mobile fall arrester for rope',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope diameter',
          1 => '10 to 13 mm',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Weight',
          1 => 'about 15 oz (425 g)',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Petzl',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How the ASAP Lock works',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The ASAP Lock runs on a separate backup line and slides freely as you move. If you fall or lose control, it grabs the rope and stops you. The lock function lets you hold it at a fixed point on the rope so it will not slide down past you, which is handy when you are setting up near an edge or working from a structure.',
            1 => 'In fall-arrest use it is paired with a Petzl energy absorber, such as the ASAP\'SORBER, between the device and your harness. Read the instructions for the exact connector and absorber combinations it is approved with. More options are in our <a href="/safety/fall-protection/">fall protection</a> section.',
            2 => 'In tree work the ASAP Lock tends to show up in situations that borrow from rope access, such as working from structures, towers or cranes, or where a site rule calls for a separate backup line. For ordinary canopy climbing, most arborists rely on their primary climbing system and training rather than a mobile fall arrester.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Rope, inspection and training',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Use it only on the rope types and diameters Petzl lists for it, and check the device locks correctly on your rope before every use. Inspect the cam, body and connector for wear, cracks and debris.',
            1 => 'This is life-support equipment. It must be used per the manufacturer\'s instructions and the applicable standard, including ANSI Z133 for tree work, and only by people trained in rope access and rescue.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use the ASAP Lock as my climbing device?',
          'a' => 'No. It is a backup fall arrester on a separate line, not a primary ascent or descent device.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What rope does it work on?',
          'a' => 'Petzl lists semi-static rope in the 10 to 13 mm range. Check the current instructions for the exact compatible ropes.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'safety',
        1 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/ascender-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/ascender-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Georgia Guardsman in the Best Ranger Competition',
        'creator' => 'Georgia National Guard',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40994485@N04',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40994485@N04/5666140072',
      ),
    ),
    '/safety/first-aid/buckingham-blood-stopper-kit-green/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/safety/first-aid/buckingham-blood-stopper-kit-green/',
      'category' => '/safety/first-aid/',
      'name' => 'Buckingham Blood Stopper Kit (Green)',
      'brand' => 'buckingham',
      'brand_name' => 'Buckingham',
      'kind' => 'first-aid',
      'price' => 45,
      'keyword' => 'buckingham blood stopper kit',
      'title' => 'Buckingham Blood Stopper Kit for Tree Crews',
      'h1' => 'Buckingham Blood Stopper Kit (Green)',
      'meta' => 'The Buckingham Blood Stopper Kit puts bleeding control supplies in a pouch you carry on a saddle or belt. What it is for and how crews should carry and train.',
      'tagline' => 'Wearable bleeding control kit for chainsaw and climbing crews.',
      'summary' => 'The Buckingham Blood Stopper Kit is a compact bleeding control pouch built to ride on a saddle or belt where you can reach it fast. Chainsaw cuts bleed hard and quickly, and having the supplies on your body instead of in the truck can save critical minutes.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Bleeding control supplies in one pouch',
        1 => 'Green pouch that is easy to spot',
        2 => 'Mounts on a saddle or belt',
        3 => 'Keeps first aid within reach aloft',
        4 => 'Made by Buckingham for tree crews',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Bleeding control kit with pouch',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Color',
          1 => 'Green',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Buckingham',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why carry it on your body',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A chainsaw cut to the leg or arm can bleed fast. If the only kit is in the truck cab, a climber in the tree or a groundie at the back of a lot loses minutes getting to it. A blood stopper kit on the saddle or belt keeps the supplies where they are needed.',
            1 => 'Check the listing for the exact contents of the kit you buy and add items your crew trains with, such as a tourniquet, if they are not included. Browse our <a href="/safety/first-aid/">first aid</a> section for refills and larger kits.',
            2 => 'The green color makes it easy to tell apart from other saddle pouches, which matters when someone else has to grab it from you in a hurry. Talk through where everyone on the crew keeps their kit so nobody has to search.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Training and upkeep',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Supplies only help if people know how to use them. Get the crew trained in first aid and bleeding control, and practice getting the kit open with gloves on.',
            1 => 'Check the pouch regularly, replace anything used or expired, and keep it dry. Follow your employer\'s emergency plan and call emergency services for any serious injury.',
            2 => 'Keep a matching kit in the truck and chipper so the ground crew has one close by too.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is inside the kit?',
          'a' => 'Contents can change, so check the listing. It is built around bleeding control supplies in a pouch you can wear.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Where should I mount it?',
          'a' => 'Somewhere both you and a coworker can reach quickly, such as the side of your saddle or belt, and not under other gear.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/buckingham/',
      'photo' => 
