        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Tree cabling and bracing',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where an eye bolt fits in a cabling system',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In a steel cable system, each end of the cable needs a solid anchor in the tree. A through-bolted eye bolt is one of the most common: you drill straight through the limb, run the bolt through, and secure it with a washer and nut on the far side. The cable then attaches to the eye, usually with a thimble and a preformed dead-end grip or wire rope clips.',
            1 => 'The 1/2 in size suits a range of medium leaders. Larger stems and heavier loads call for bigger hardware. Find matching cable and fittings in our <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/">cabling hardware</a> section.',
            2 => 'Eye bolts are typically galvanized or otherwise corrosion-resistant so they hold up in the tree for years. Check the listing for the bolt length and whether a nut and washer are included.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Sizing and installation',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Choose the bolt length to pass fully through the limb with enough thread for the washer and nut. Drill a hole matched to the bolt, set the eye in line with the cable, and avoid drilling into decay.',
            1 => 'Cabling should be designed and installed by a qualified arborist following ANSI A300 and ANSI Z133. Inspect systems regularly and keep records.',
            2 => 'Plan the cable height and anchor spacing before drilling; once a hole is in the tree it cannot be undone.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What length do I need?',
          'a' => 'Long enough to pass through the limb at the install point with room for the washer and nut. Measure the stem diameter where you will drill.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use it with synthetic cable?',
          'a' => 'Some synthetic systems use their own anchors. Follow the cable maker\'s instructions for compatible hardware.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Eye Bolt - Basil Watson\'s Sopwith-Type Biplane, Melbourne, Victoria, 1916',
        'creator' => 'Photographer: David Thompson',
        'creator_url' => '',
        'license' => 'CC BY 4.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://collections.museumsvictoria.com.au/items/393624',
      ),
    ),
    '/shop-all/1-8mm-throw-line/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/shop-all/1-8mm-throw-line/',
      'category' => '/climbing/throw-weight-and-line/throw-line/',
      'name' => '1.8 mm Throw Line',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'throw-line',
      'price' => 32,
      'keyword' => '1.8mm throw line',
      'title' => '1.8mm Throw Line for Setting Climbing Lines',
      'h1' => '1.8 mm Throw Line',
      'meta' => 'A 1.8mm throw line is the thin, slick cord arborists use with a throw weight to set climbing and rigging lines. Thin vs thick line, storage, and care tips.',
      'tagline' => 'Thin, slick throw line for setting climbing and rigging lines high in the canopy.',
      'summary' => 'A 1.8mm throw line is the thin, low-friction cord you tie to a throw weight to set a climbing or rigging line in the canopy. At 1.8 mm it is on the thin side for throw line, which helps it fly farther and slip over unions easily.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '1.8 mm diameter for long, clean throws',
        1 => 'Slick surface slides over bark and unions',
        2 => 'Ties to a standard throw weight',
        3 => 'Works with a throw cube or bag',
        4 => 'For pulling up climbing and rigging lines',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '1.8 mm',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Arborist throw line',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Setting climbing and rigging lines',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Thin line, longer throws',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Throw line comes in a few diameters, and thinner line weighs less and drags less through the air, so the weight carries farther. It also slides over branch unions with less friction, which makes isolating a tie-in point easier. The trade-off is that very thin line tangles more and is harder to handle with gloves on.',
            1 => 'At 1.8 mm, this line is on the thinner side. Use a cube or bag to keep it organized, and pair it with a throw weight sized to your canopy. See more in our <a href="/climbing/throw-weight-and-line/throw-line/">throw line</a> section.',
            2 => 'Thin line is also less likely to damage bark or pull small branches down as it runs, and it takes up less space in a throw cube. Practice a clean throw-and-retrieve routine on the ground first so that the line does not snarl the first time you need to set a high tie-in.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Throw line is for setting lines, not for life support or lifting loads. Once the throw line is set, use it to pull up your climbing line, then inspect the tie-in point from the ground before you commit to it.',
            1 => 'Keep the line clean and dry, check for nicks and glazed spots, and cut out damaged sections. Store it in a cube so it pays out cleanly on the next throw.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is 1.8 mm too thin?',
          'a' => 'It is thinner than many throw lines and some climbers love it for distance. If you struggle with tangles or gloves, a slightly thicker line is easier to handle.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How long should my throw line be?',
          'a' => 'Long enough to go up and over your highest tie-in and back to the ground. Many arborists carry around 180 ft or more.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/throw-line-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/throw-line-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Surgeon',
        'creator' => 'Tim Sheerman-Chase',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/68932647@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/68932647@N00/7738607258',
      ),
    ),
    '/shop-all/10-5mm-replacement-rope-bridge/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/shop-all/10-5mm-replacement-rope-bridge/',
      'category' => '/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/saddle-parts-and-shoulder-harnesses/',
      'name' => '10.5 mm Replacement Rope Bridge',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'saddle',
      'price' => 35,
      'keyword' => 'replacement rope bridge',
      'title' => '10.5mm Replacement Rope Bridge for Saddles',
      'h1' => '10.5 mm Replacement Rope Bridge',
      'meta' => 'A 10.5mm replacement rope bridge restores the main attachment point on a climbing saddle. When to replace a bridge, how to check fit, and inspection basics.',
      'tagline' => 'Replacement 10.5 mm rope bridge for arborist climbing saddles.',
      'summary' => 'A replacement rope bridge restores the main attachment point on a climbing saddle that uses a rope bridge. This one is 10.5 mm cord, and it is a wear item that should be swapped well before it looks worn out.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '10.5 mm rope bridge',
        1 => 'Replaces a worn saddle bridge',
        2 => 'Restores the saddle\'s main connection point',
        3 => 'Keeps your saddle in service',
        4 => 'Check fit to your saddle model',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '10.5 mm',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Saddle rope bridge',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Saddle main attachment point',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why bridges wear out',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The bridge is the part of the saddle your climbing system connects to, and on many modern saddles it is a length of rope. Every movement slides your carabiner or ring along it, so it wears faster than the rest of the saddle. Fuzzing, glazing, flat spots or a stiff section are all reasons to replace it.',
            1 => 'Check that the length, diameter and end terminations match your saddle before ordering. Bridges are often model-specific. Our <a href="/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/saddle-parts-and-shoulder-harnesses/">saddle parts</a> section has more options.',
            2 => 'Replacing the bridge on schedule is one of the cheapest ways to keep a saddle safe. Many climbers keep a spare in the gear bag so a worn bridge never becomes a reason to climb on it one more day. Mark the date you installed it on a tag or in your gear log.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fitting and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Install the new bridge exactly as the saddle maker\'s instructions describe, including any stopper knots or terminations, and check it before every climb.',
            1 => 'The bridge is life-support equipment. Use only parts approved for your saddle, follow the manufacturer\'s instructions and ANSI Z133, and retire the bridge at the first sign of damage.',
            2 => 'When you fit the new bridge, check the attachment points on the saddle for wear too. A fresh bridge will not help if the loops or rings it passes through are worn.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I replace my bridge?',
          'a' => 'It depends on how much you climb. Inspect it before every climb and replace it when you see wear, glazing or damage, or as the saddle maker directs.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will a 10.5 mm bridge fit my saddle?',
          'a' => 'Only if your saddle is designed for that diameter and length. Check the saddle maker\'s spec before ordering.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/saddle-6.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/saddle-6-sm.webp',
        'title' => '160320-F-QP401-181',
        'creator' => 'DoD News Photos',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/127934495@N07',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/127934495@N07/25354925433',
