          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In a moving-rope system the friction hitch is what holds you and lets you descend. A split tail is a separate length of cord that ties the hitch onto your climb line, so you can replace the wear-heavy hitch without cutting the end off your rope. Most climbers tie a Distel, VT, or similar hitch and tend it with a micro pulley.',
            1 => 'The hitch cord has to suit your climbing line\'s diameter and cover. Too loose and the hitch slips; too grabby and it locks up. See our other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/hitch-cord-and-split-tails/">hitch cords and split tails</a> to compare.',
            2 => 'Hi-Vee is a popular pairing with Teufelberger climbing lines, but always confirm the combination on the ground. A hitch that is too grabby makes descending jerky, while one that is too slick may creep under load.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and replacement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Hitch cord wears faster than anything else in your system because it takes the heat of every descent. Check it before every climb for glazing, flat or stiff spots, cover damage, and a hitch that no longer grabs evenly, and replace it early.',
            1 => 'Tie and dress your hitch carefully and test it on the ground before leaving it. Treat it as life-support equipment: it must meet ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions, be inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Which climbing rope works with the Hi-Vee?',
          'a' => 'Check Teufelberger\'s guidance for compatible rope diameters and test the hitch on your own line before climbing.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How long does a split tail last?',
          'a' => 'It depends on how much you descend on it. Replace it when you see glazing, stiffness, or cover wear.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/teufelberger/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'IMG_0397',
        'creator' => 'hojaleaf',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/55231619@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/55231619@N00/499171630',
      ),
    ),
    '/srs-climbing-devices/replacement-friction-pins-for-chicane/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/srs-climbing-devices/replacement-friction-pins-for-chicane/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/ascent-and-descent/mechanical-friction-devices/devices-for-srs/',
      'name' => 'Replacement Friction Pins for Chicane',
      'brand' => 'petzl',
      'brand_name' => 'Petzl',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 29,
      'keyword' => 'replacement friction pins for chicane',
      'title' => 'Replacement Friction Pins for Chicane SRS Device',
      'h1' => 'Replacement Friction Pins for Chicane',
      'meta' => 'Replacement friction pins for Chicane SRS braking devices. When to swap worn pins, how to check them for wear, and why to follow the service instructions.',
      'tagline' => 'Fresh friction pins to keep a Chicane braking smoothly on a stationary rope.',
      'summary' => 'These replacement friction pins for Chicane devices let you renew the wear surfaces that the rope runs across. Worn pins change how the device brakes, so swapping them restores smooth, predictable friction on a stationary rope.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Wear parts for the Chicane',
        1 => 'Restore smooth braking',
        2 => 'Cheaper than replacing the device',
        3 => 'Install per the maker\'s instructions',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Chicane',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Part type',
          1 => 'Replacement friction pins',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Install',
          1 => 'Per manufacturer instructions',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why the pins wear',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Every foot of rope you descend drags across the friction surfaces of an SRS device. Grit in the rope speeds that up, and over time the pins develop grooves or flat spots. Worn pins change the friction you get, which can make the descent grabby in one spot and slippery in another.',
            1 => 'Replacing the pins is cheaper than replacing the whole device, as long as the rest of it passes inspection. Browse other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/ascent-and-descent/mechanical-friction-devices/devices-for-srs/">SRS climbing devices</a> if your device is due for retirement.',
            2 => 'Climbers who descend long distances on dirty rope will see pin wear sooner, so keeping your rope clean helps the pins last.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Swapping and checking',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Install the pins exactly as the manufacturer\'s instructions show, and only if the maker permits user replacement. Check every fastener afterward and test the device on the ground, with your full weight and a backup, before you climb on it.',
            1 => 'Inspect pins and frame before each climb for grooves, sharp edges, and cracks. Treat it as life-support equipment: it must meet ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions, be inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
            2 => 'If you are unsure whether the pins are within limits, retire the device or send it for service.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I know the pins are worn?',
          'a' => 'Look for grooves, flat spots, or sharp edges where the rope runs, and notice any change in how the device brakes.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I fit these myself?',
          'a' => 'Only if the manufacturer\'s instructions allow user replacement. If they do not, have the device serviced or retired.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Sharpening Chainsaw Blades',
        'creator' => 'NCDOTcommunications',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03/36914543386',
      ),
    ),
    '/static-climbing-rope/teufelberger-kmtree-purple-green/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/static-climbing-rope/teufelberger-kmtree-purple-green/',
      'category' => '/rope/climbing-rope/static-climbing-rope/',
      'name' => 'Teufelberger KMtree Static Climbing Rope (Purple/Green)',
      'brand' => 'teufelberger',
      'brand_name' => 'Teufelberger',
      'kind' => 'rope',
      'price' => 260,
      'keyword' => 'teufelberger kmtree',
      'title' => 'Teufelberger KMtree Static Climbing Rope, Purple/Green',
      'h1' => 'Teufelberger KMtree Static Climbing Rope (Purple/Green)',
      'meta' => 'Teufelberger KMtree in purple and green, a static kernmantle climbing rope for SRS and MRS tree work. How it handles, what to pair it with, and inspection.',
      'tagline' => 'A static kernmantle climbing rope for arborists who climb SRS and MRS.',
      'summary' => 'Teufelberger KMtree is a static kernmantle climbing rope for tree work, here in a purple and green colorway. Low stretch makes it efficient for stationary-rope ascents and gives mechanical devices a consistent rope to work on.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Static kernmantle construction',
        1 => 'Low stretch for efficient SRS ascents',
        2 => 'Works with mechanical devices and hitches',
        3 => 'Purple/green color is easy to spot',
        4 => 'From Teufelberger',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Static kernmantle',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Color',
          1 => 'Purple / green',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Teufelberger',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'SRS and MRS tree climbing',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where a static kernmantle rope shines',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In a stationary rope system, stretch is wasted effort: every bounce at the start of an ascent is energy you do not get back. A static kernmantle line keeps that to a minimum, so footlocking, rope walking, and ascending with a chest ascender feel direct. It also gives SRS devices a consistent diameter and firm cover to grip.',
            1 => 'The same rope works for moving-rope climbing with a compatible hitch. See our other <a href="/rope/climbing-rope/static-climbing-rope/">static climbing ropes</a> if you want to compare handling and diameter.',
            2 => 'Bright colors also make it easier for the ground crew to tell the climbing line apart from rigging lines, which matters when the drop zone gets busy.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Pairing and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check that your devices, hitch cord, and ascenders are rated for this rope\'s diameter before you climb on it. Static rope can feel stiff at first; it usually softens with use.',
            1 => 'Before every climb run the whole rope through your hands, feeling for cuts, glazing, soft or flat spots, and cover damage. Keep it away from saws, chemicals, and fuel, and store it in a rope bag out of sunlight. Treat it as life-support equipment: it must meet ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions, be inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use KMtree for moving-rope climbing?',
          'a' => 'Yes, with a compatible hitch and hitch cord. Test the hitch on the ground before leaving it.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What length should I buy?',
          'a' => 'Most climbers choose 150 ft for general work and 200 ft for big trees. Check available lengths in the listing.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/teufelberger/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'coiling',
        'creator' => 'michael pollak',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06/6796140986',
      ),
