        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Setting climbing and rigging lines',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How it handles',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A good throwline has to come off the pile cleanly, fly far behind the bag, and then slide over bark without hanging up while you isolate the limb. A slick, thin line like the Sushi does all three, so you get more height with less arm.',
            1 => '1.8mm is a common middle ground: thin enough to throw high, thick enough to handle and pull a climbing line without cutting your hands. Compare other <a href="/climbing/throw-weight-and-line/throw-line/">throw lines</a> if you prefer a thinner or stiffer line.',
            2 => 'The bright finish is easy to follow through the crown, so you can see which side of a limb the line went over before you start pulling.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care and use',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Store it in a cube or bag so it pays out without tangles, and keep it clean; sap and grit slow it down. Check the line for nicks and thin spots, and cut out damage rather than throwing on it.',
            1 => 'Throw lines are not life-support gear. Use them only to set your climbing or rigging line, and make sure the crew is clear of the drop zone when you throw or pull a bag down.',
            2 => 'A small cube clipped to the saddle or the throw bag keeps it ready for the next tree.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a 1.8mm throwline with any throw weight?',
          'a' => 'Yes, it works with standard throw bags. Lighter bags fly further on thin line; heavier bags pull it through dense crowns.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I store it?',
          'a' => 'Flake it into a throw cube or bag so it comes out without knots.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/courant/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/throw-line-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/throw-line-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'TVCF002',
        'creator' => 'Symic',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00/33804504181',
      ),
    ),
    '/throw-line/ftc-1-6mm-stiffline-yellow-80m/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/throw-line/ftc-1-6mm-stiffline-yellow-80m/',
      'category' => '/climbing/throw-weight-and-line/throw-line/',
      'name' => 'FTC 1.6mm Stiffline Yellow 80m',
      'brand' => 'ftc',
      'brand_name' => 'FTC',
      'kind' => 'throw-line',
      'price' => 55,
      'keyword' => 'ftc 1.6mm stiffline',
      'title' => 'FTC 1.6mm Stiffline Throw Line, Yellow, 80m',
      'h1' => 'FTC 1.6mm Stiffline Yellow 80m',
      'meta' => 'FTC 1.6mm Stiffline in yellow, an 80m throw line with a stiff, tangle-resistant body for setting climbing lines. How it handles and when to choose it.',
      'tagline' => 'An 80m, 1.6mm stiff throw line in bright yellow for long, high throws.',
      'summary' => 'FTC 1.6mm Stiffline is a throw line in yellow, supplied in an 80m length. Its stiffer body resists tangling and pays out cleanly, while the thin diameter helps a throw bag reach high crotches.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '1.6 mm diameter',
        1 => '80 m length',
        2 => 'Stiff body resists tangles',
        3 => 'Bright yellow is easy to see in the canopy',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '1.6 mm',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Length',
          1 => '80 m',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Color',
          1 => 'Yellow',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'FTC',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a stiffer throw line',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Soft throw line flies well but can knot on itself in the cube or wrap around twigs in the canopy. A stiffer line holds its shape, comes off the pile with fewer snarls, and is easier to work back through branches when you are isolating a union.',
            1 => 'At 1.6mm it is thin enough for long throws, and 80m is plenty for tall trees with line to spare. Compare other <a href="/climbing/throw-weight-and-line/throw-line/">throw lines</a> if you prefer a softer or thicker line.',
            2 => 'Stiffer line also makes it easier to flip the line off a twig or push it over a union with a pole, which saves resets on cluttered trees.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Handling and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Flake it into a throw cube or bag and let it pay out freely. Thin line can be hard on bare hands when pulling a heavy climbing line up, so wear gloves or use a thicker line for big hauls.',
            1 => 'Check for nicks and thin spots and cut out damage. Throw line is not life-support gear; use it only to set your climbing or rigging line, and keep the crew out of the drop zone when throwing.',
            2 => 'Keeping a spare throw line in the truck saves a job when a line gets cut or stuck in the crown.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is 80m enough?',
          'a' => 'For most trees, yes. It gives you enough line to go up and over a high limb and back to the ground with extra.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Stiff or soft throw line?',
          'a' => 'Stiff line tangles less and pushes through branches better; soft line often flies a bit further. Many climbers carry both.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/ftc/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/throw-line-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/throw-line-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'coiling',
        'creator' => 'michael pollak',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06/6796140986',
      ),
