          1 => 'Arborwear',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'A belt for working days',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A belt has a hard job on a tree crew: you bend, climb, crawl through brush, and haul limbs to the chipper all day. A solid work belt keeps your pants in place without digging in when you squat or kneel.',
            1 => 'The chain design gives the After Hours belt a look that nods to the trade, so it works on the job and after it. Pair it with <a href="/clothing/tree-climbing-pants/">tree climbing pants</a> for a complete work outfit.',
            2 => 'Arborwear started with clothing made for climbing and tree work, so its gear tends to be built to take the bending, reaching, and brush that come with the job. The After Hours belt carries that into an accessory you can wear every day, on site or off.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and wear with a saddle',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'If you wear a climbing saddle, check that the buckle does not sit under the saddle\'s waist belt where it can press into you on a long climb. Many climbers wear a low-profile belt or none at all in the tree.',
            1 => 'Order by your pant waist size and check the listing for sizing guidance. A belt is clothing, not safety gear; never use it as part of a climbing or fall-protection system.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How should I size the belt?',
          'a' => 'Most belts are sized by pant waist size. Check the listing\'s size chart before ordering.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I wear it under my climbing saddle?',
          'a' => 'Some climbers do, but make sure the buckle does not create a pressure point under the saddle on long climbs.',
        ),
      ),
