          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Buying by the foot lets you cut the exact length you need for your hitch. A longer cord gives you more wraps and more grip, while a shorter one is quicker to tend. Climbers often keep a spare length to retie after wear.',
            1 => 'Polyester holds up better to heat than nylon, which matters when a hitch is running during a descent.',
            2 => 'Buying cord by the foot is also handy when you want to try a different hitch length or tie a second hitch for a backup.',
            3 => 'Many climbers like 10 mm polyester for eye-to-eye hitches because it grabs well, runs smoothly, and holds its shape after many descents. It also works for prusiks and backup hitches on rigging and rescue setups.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Matching and inspecting',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Match the hitch cord to your climbing rope. A 10 mm cord works on many 11 to 13 mm climbing lines, but test the hitch low. More cords are in <a href="/rope/tech-cordage/">tech cordage</a>.',
            1 => 'Check hitches before each climb for glazing, stiffness, and cover wear, and retire them early. Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard, follow ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions, and be inspected before every climb. Get trained before you rely on it.',
            2 => 'Store cord out of the sun and away from oil and fuel. Hitch cord wears much faster than climbing rope, so keep spares on hand.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How long should I cut my hitch cord?',
          'a' => 'It depends on your hitch and rope. Many climbers start around 30 in and adjust.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I tie my own eyes?',
          'a' => 'Only if you are trained in the correct knots. Many climbers buy spliced eye-to-eye cords.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/teufelberger/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Walt coiling the rope at the summit',
        'creator' => 'Tristan Higbee',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62264264@N03',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62264264@N03/8305035257',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/husqvarna-air-filter-assembly-for-550xp-mark-ii-chainsaw/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/husqvarna-air-filter-assembly-for-550xp-mark-ii-chainsaw/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna Air Filter Assembly for 550XP Mark II',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 27,
      'keyword' => 'husqvarna 550xp mark ii air filter',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna 550XP Mark II Air Filter Assembly',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna 550XP Mark II Air Filter Assembly',
      'meta' => 'Husqvarna 550XP Mark II air filter assembly to keep dust and chips out of the engine. Cleaning tips, when to replace the filter, and how to check the fit.',
      'tagline' => 'A replacement air filter assembly for the 550XP Mark II.',
      'summary' => 'This Husqvarna 550XP Mark II air filter assembly replaces the filter that keeps dust and chips out of the engine on Husqvarna\'s 50 cc pro saw. A clean filter keeps the saw running strong and protects the piston and cylinder.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Replacement air filter assembly',
        1 => 'For the Husqvarna 550XP Mark II',
        2 => 'Protects the engine from dust',
        3 => 'Easy tool-free swap',
        4 => 'Keep a spare in the truck',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Husqvarna 550XP Mark II',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Air filter assembly',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Husqvarna',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why the air filter matters',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The 550XP Mark II is a favorite for limbing and bucking, and it spends its life in sawdust, chips, and dry, dusty brush piles. The air filter is what stands between that debris and the engine. A clogged filter starves the engine of air and cuts power, and a torn or poorly seated filter lets fine dust into the cylinder, where it wears the piston and rings.',
            1 => 'The Mark II also uses AutoTune, which adjusts the fuel mixture as conditions change. A dirty filter makes it work harder to compensate and can make the saw feel sluggish or run hot.',
            2 => 'Check the filter daily during heavy use. On a dusty job, that may mean checking it at every fuel stop.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Cleaning, replacing, and fitting',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Tap loose debris off or blow it clean with low-pressure air from the inside out so you do not push dirt into the media. Follow Husqvarna\'s guidance on whether and how to wash the filter. Replace it when it is torn, warped, oil-soaked, or will not come clean. Other service parts are in <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/">power equipment parts</a>.',
            1 => 'Confirm the part number for your saw and serial before ordering, since the original 550XP and the Mark II use some different parts. When you install it, make sure it seats fully and the cover locks down so no air slips past the edge.',
            2 => 'Keep a spare filter in the truck so you can swap a dirty one on the job and clean it back at the shop. Work on a cool saw with the chain brake on.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I wash the 550XP Mark II air filter?',
          'a' => 'Follow Husqvarna\'s cleaning guidance for this filter. When it is torn, warped, or will not come clean, replace it.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does it fit the original 550XP?',
          'a' => 'Some parts differ between the original 550XP and the Mark II. Check the part number against your model and serial.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Sharpening Chainsaw Blades',
        'creator' => 'NCDOTcommunications',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03/36914543386',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/weaver-saddles/weaver-stratus-saddle/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/weaver-saddles/weaver-stratus-saddle/',
      'category' => '/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/',
      'name' => 'Weaver Stratus Saddle',
      'brand' => 'weaver',
      'brand_name' => 'Weaver',
      'kind' => 'saddle',
      'price' => 399,
      'keyword' => 'weaver stratus saddle',
      'title' => 'Weaver Stratus Saddle for Tree Climbing',
      'h1' => 'Weaver Stratus Saddle',
      'meta' => 'Weaver Stratus saddle for tree climbers. How to fit a Weaver saddle, what to check before every climb, and how to set it up for the work you do most.',
      'tagline' => 'A Weaver tree climbing saddle for daily work positioning.',
      'summary' => 'The Weaver Stratus saddle is a tree climbing saddle from Weaver, a long-time name in arborist saddles. Like any saddle, fit and comfort matter most when you hang in it all day.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Tree climbing saddle from Weaver',
        1 => 'For work positioning in the canopy',
        2 => 'Adjustable fit',
        3 => 'Check Weaver\'s sizing chart before ordering',
        4 => 'Inspect before every climb',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Weaver',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Tree climbing saddle',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Work positioning',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing and fitting a saddle',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A climbing saddle is where you spend most of a work day, so fit and comfort matter as much as anything on the spec sheet. A good saddle spreads your weight across the waist and legs, keeps the bridge or attachment points where you can reach them, and carries the tools you need without getting in the way.',
            1 => 'Weaver has made tree saddles for a long time, and many climbers like their straightforward, durable designs. Before ordering, check the sizing chart against your waist and leg measurements, and be honest about the clothing you wear in winter. If you can, hang in a saddle before you commit to it and check how the leg loops and waist belt feel when you lean back into your lanyard.',
            2 => 'Think about how you climb. An SRT climber, a spur-heavy removal climber, and a pruning climber may want different attachment points and gear storage.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Setup, inspection, and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Adjust the saddle per Weaver\'s instructions and set up your gear loops so the tools you use most are easiest to reach. Compare other models in <a href="/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/weaver-saddles/">Weaver saddles</a>.',
            1 => 'Inspect webbing, stitching, buckles, D-rings, and the bridge or attachment points before every climb. Look for cuts, burns, chemical stains, worn stitching, and cracked or bent hardware. Keep the saddle clean, dry it away from direct heat, and store it out of the sun.',
            2 => 'Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard, follow ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions, and be inspected before every climb. Get trained before you rely on it.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I size a Weaver saddle?',
          'a' => 'Use Weaver\'s sizing chart for this model and measure over the clothing you usually climb in.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can parts of the saddle be replaced?',
          'a' => 'Check Weaver\'s instructions for which parts, if any, are replaceable, and use only Weaver parts.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/weaver/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/saddle-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/saddle-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Surgeon',
        'creator' => 'Tim Sheerman-Chase',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/68932647@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/68932647@N00/7738607258',
      ),
    ),
    '/wrenches-and-files/pferd-chain-saw-sharpening-set/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/wrenches-and-files/pferd-chain-saw-sharpening-set/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/chainsaw-accessories/wrenches-and-files/',
      'name' => 'PFERD Chain Saw Sharpening Set',
      'brand' => 'pferd',
      'brand_name' => 'PFERD',
      'kind' => 'wrench-file',
      'price' => 39,
      'keyword' => 'pferd chainsaw sharpening set',
      'title' => 'PFERD Chainsaw Sharpening Set for Field Filing',
      'h1' => 'PFERD Chainsaw Sharpening Set',
      'meta' => 'PFERD chainsaw sharpening set with files and guides for keeping chain sharp in the field. How to pick a file size, what to check, and good filing habits.',
      'tagline' => 'A field sharpening set for keeping chainsaw chain cutting clean.',
      'summary' => 'The PFERD chainsaw sharpening set puts the files and guides you need to touch up a chain in one kit, so you can keep the saw cutting clean on the job. PFERD is a German maker known for quality files.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Chainsaw sharpening set from PFERD',
        1 => 'Files and guides in one kit',
        2 => 'For field touch-ups',
        3 => 'Choose the file size for your chain',
        4 => 'Quality German files',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'PFERD',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Chainsaw sharpening set',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Field sharpening',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Sharp chain is safe chain',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A dull chain makes fine dust instead of thick chips, pulls the saw off line, and makes you lean on the saw to get through the cut. That wears you out, wears the bar and sprocket, and raises the chance of a kickback or a bad cut. Touching up the chain with a file every tank or two in clean wood keeps it cutting the way it should.',
            1 => 'A good field sharpening set puts the pieces you need in one place, so you are not hunting for the right file in the truck. Look for a round file sized to your chain, a holder or guide that keeps your angle and depth consistent, and a flat file and depth gauge tool for setting the rakers.',
            2 => 'PFERD is a German maker with a long history in files, and its chainsaw files hold their cut well.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing a size and filing well',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the contents of the set and make sure the round file matches your chain\'s pitch and the file size its maker calls for. Using the wrong file size changes the shape of the cutter. Other tools are in <a href="/jobsite/chainsaw-accessories/wrenches-and-files/">wrenches and files</a>.',
            1 => 'Set the chain brake or lock the bar in a vise, wear gloves, and give each cutter the same number of strokes so they stay the same length. File the rakers lightly after several sharpenings, not every time. If you hit a rock or a nail, take the chain to the shop for a grind instead of trying to file it all back in the field.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What file size do I need?',
          'a' => 'Check your chain\'s packaging or the chain maker\'s chart. File size depends on the chain pitch and cutter type.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I file my chain?',
          'a' => 'Touch it up every tank or two in clean wood, and any time it starts making dust instead of chips.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/pferd/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/wrench-file-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/wrench-file-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => '20160823-FS-LSC-0474',
        'creator' => 'USDAgov',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/41284017@N08',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/41284017@N08/28725898983',
      ),
    ),
    '/rope/rigging-rope/samson-pro-master-1-2-rigging-rope/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rope/rigging-rope/samson-pro-master-1-2-rigging-rope/',
      'category' => '/rope/rigging-rope/',
      'name' => 'Samson Pro-Master 1/2" Rigging Rope',
      'brand' => 'samson-rope',
      'brand_name' => 'Samson Rope',
      'kind' => 'rope',
      'price' => 219,
      'keyword' => 'samson pro-master',
      'title' => 'Samson Pro-Master 1/2 in Rigging Rope',
      'h1' => 'Samson Pro-Master 1/2" Rigging Rope',
      'meta' => 'Samson Pro-Master 1/2 in rigging rope for lowering limbs and moderate wood. How to size a rigging line, match it to blocks and devices, and inspect it often.',
      'tagline' => 'A 1/2 in rigging line from Samson for everyday lowering.',
      'summary' => 'Samson Pro-Master rigging rope in 1/2 inch is a rigging line for lowering limbs and moderate wood on removals. Half-inch is a common size for pruning and light to moderate rigging.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Samson rigging rope',
        1 => '1/2 in diameter',
        2 => 'For light to moderate rigging',
        3 => 'Pair with suitable blocks and devices',
        4 => 'Inspect before every job',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '1/2 in',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Samson Rope',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Rigging',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where a 1/2 inch rigging line fits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A 1/2 inch rigging line is the everyday size for a lot of crews. It is light enough to throw over a limb, run through a small block, and handle all day, while still being up to lowering limbs and smaller wood on pruning jobs and removals. Bigger wood calls for bigger rope, but a 1/2 inch line covers a lot of the work.',
            1 => 'Samson is a long-time name in arborist rope, and Pro-Master is one of its rigging lines. Before you buy, check the published strength, construction, and length options for the rope you are ordering, and make sure they suit the gear and loads in your system.',
            2 => 'Keep rigging rope for rigging only. Rigging lines take shock loads, abrasion, and pitch that a climbing line should never see.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Sizing, care, and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Work well under the rated strength of the rope and every other part of the system, and remember that a piece that drops before the rope comes tight puts far more load on everything. Match the rope to your blocks, lowering device, and slings. Compare other options in <a href="/rope/rigging-rope/">rigging rope</a>.',
            1 => 'Inspect the line before every job for cuts, pulled strands, glazing, flat spots, and hard or stiff sections. Keep it clean, out of the sun, and away from fuel and bar oil, and retire it when damage is found. Make sure everyone on the crew is trained in rigging under ANSI Z133.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is 1/2 in rope enough for big wood?',
          'a' => 'No. Heavy pieces call for larger rope and gear rated for the loads. Use a 1/2 in line for limbs and smaller wood.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I splice Pro-Master?',
          'a' => 'Check Samson\'s splicing instructions for this rope\'s construction, or buy it with a factory splice.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/samson-rope/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Coiling the Rope',
        'creator' => 'blachswan',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40883175@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40883175@N06/34254142686',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/screw-gate-carabiners/dmm-revolver-rig-screw-gate/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/screw-gate-carabiners/dmm-revolver-rig-screw-gate/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/screw-gate-carabiners/',
      'name' => 'DMM Revolver Rig Screw Gate Carabiner',
      'brand' => 'dmm',
      'brand_name' => 'DMM',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 32,
      'keyword' => 'dmm revolver rig',
      'title' => 'DMM Revolver Rig Screw Gate Carabiner',
      'h1' => 'DMM Revolver Rig Screw Gate',
      'meta' => 'DMM Revolver Rig screw gate carabiner with a built-in pulley wheel for redirects, haul systems, and rigging. How the roller helps and where screw gates fit in.',
      'tagline' => 'A screw gate carabiner with a built-in pulley wheel to cut friction.',
      'summary' => 'The DMM Revolver Rig is a screw gate carabiner with a built-in pulley wheel in the base, cutting rope friction in redirects and haul systems. It is a compact way to add a small pulley without carrying one.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Screw gate carabiner from DMM',
        1 => 'Built-in pulley wheel',
        2 => 'Cuts friction in redirects',
        3 => 'Compact and light',
        4 => 'Handy for haul systems',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'DMM',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate',
          1 => 'Screw gate',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Feature',
          1 => 'Integrated pulley wheel',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How a pulley carabiner helps',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The DMM Revolver line puts a small pulley wheel in the base of a carabiner. Rope running over that wheel sees less friction than it would over a plain carabiner, which makes redirects smoother, saves rope wear, and helps when you are building a small mechanical advantage system to tension a line or haul gear.',
            1 => 'The Revolver Rig version uses a screw gate, which is simple, durable, and easy to inspect. Because the wheel is built in, you get some of the benefit of a pulley without carrying a separate one and a second carabiner.',
            2 => 'Climbers often use one as a redirect on a long SRT line or for a quick haul system to bring up a saw or a pole tool. In rigging, it can help on light lines where you want rope to run cleanly.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use, limits, and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A pulley carabiner is not a substitute for a full rigging block or a proper pulley when the loads are big. Check DMM\'s ratings for the wheel and the carabiner and stay well inside them. Compare other options in <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/screw-gate-carabiners/">screw gate carabiners</a>.',
            1 => 'Screw gates must be screwed fully closed before loading, and many tree care employers require auto-locking connectors for life support. Follow your employer\'s policy and the applicable standard. Check that the wheel spins freely and that the gate closes and locks fully. Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard, follow ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions, and be inspected before every climb. Get trained before you rely on it.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a screw gate for life support?',
          'a' => 'Many tree care rules call for auto-locking connectors in life support. Follow your employer\'s policy and the applicable standard.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does the wheel need care?',
          'a' => 'Rinse grit out with fresh water, let it dry, and check that it spins freely before use.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/dmm/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => '3D-printed Carabiner Clip',
        'creator' => 'Creative Tools',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/33907867@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/33907867@N02/15004955370',
      ),
    ),
    '/cutting-and-pruning/hand-saws-and-scabbards/ibuki-16-390mm-silky-curved-saw-w-scabbard/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/cutting-and-pruning/hand-saws-and-scabbards/ibuki-16-390mm-silky-curved-saw-w-scabbard/',
      'category' => '/cutting-and-pruning/hand-saws-and-scabbards/',
      'name' => 'Silky Ibuki 16" (390mm) Curved Saw with Scabbard',
      'brand' => 'silky',
      'brand_name' => 'Silky',
      'kind' => 'hand-saw',
      'price' => 109,
      'keyword' => 'silky ibuki',
      'title' => 'Silky Ibuki 390mm Curved Hand Saw with Scabbard',
      'h1' => 'Silky Ibuki 16" (390mm) Curved Saw with Scabbard',
      'meta' => 'Silky Ibuki 390mm curved hand saw with scabbard for climbers cutting big limbs. How the long curved blade cuts, carrying it aloft, and blade care tips.',
      'tagline' => 'A long curved Silky hand saw with scabbard for cutting big limbs.',
      'summary' => 'The Silky Ibuki is a 390mm curved hand saw with a scabbard, made for cutting big limbs fast without starting a chainsaw. The long curved blade pulls into the cut and takes big bites.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '390mm curved blade',
        1 => 'Large teeth for fast cuts',
        2 => 'Comes with scabbard',
        3 => 'Good for climbers and ground work',
        4 => 'Replaceable blade',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Blade length',
          1 => '390 mm',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Blade shape',
          1 => 'Curved',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Silky',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Includes',
          1 => 'Scabbard',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a long curved saw',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The Silky Ibuki is built for cutting big wood by hand. A 390 mm curved blade pulls into the cut on the pull stroke, so you spend less effort pushing the saw and more time cutting. That length handles limbs that would take forever with a short folding saw, and it can save you from starting a saw for a few cuts in a crown.',
            1 => 'A hand saw is also quiet and has no fuel, chain, or kickback, which makes it a good choice near homes, in tight crowns, and in delicate pruning where you want precise cuts at the branch collar.',
            2 => 'Climbers often carry a big Silky on the saddle as the main pruning tool and leave the top-handle saw for removals.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Carrying it and caring for it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Keep the blade in its scabbard any time it is not in your hand, and clip the scabbard securely to your saddle so it does not swing. A long blade is sharp along its full length, so watch your rope, lanyard, and legs when you are cutting close. More options are in <a href="/cutting-and-pruning/hand-saws-and-scabbards/">hand saws and scabbards</a>.',
            1 => 'Wipe sap off the blade after use and keep it dry to prevent rust. Silky blades are hard to sharpen well, so most climbers swap in a replacement blade when it gets dull or bent. Confirm the replacement blade part fits your handle before ordering.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I replace the Ibuki blade?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Silky sells replacement blades. Confirm the part matches your saw before ordering.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the Ibuki good for climbing?',
          'a' => 'Many climbers carry it, but it is a long blade. Keep it in a secure scabbard and be careful around your rope and lanyard.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'pruning',
        1 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/silky/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/hand-saw-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/hand-saw-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Dave has a go with the pruning saw',
        'creator' => 'jennifrog',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/38061770@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/38061770@N00/6158415896',
      ),
    ),
    '/auto-locking-carabiners/isc-offset-d-supersafe-carabiner-with-locking-pin/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/auto-locking-carabiners/isc-offset-d-supersafe-carabiner-with-locking-pin/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/',
      'name' => 'ISC Offset D Supersafe Carabiner with Locking Pin',
      'brand' => 'isc',
      'brand_name' => 'ISC',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 27,
      'keyword' => 'isc offset d supersafe carabiner',
      'title' => 'ISC Offset D Supersafe Carabiner, Locking Pin',
      'h1' => 'ISC Offset D Supersafe Carabiner with Locking Pin',
      'meta' => 'ISC Offset D supersafe carabiner with a locking pin for climbers who want an auto-locking connector that resists rollout. Shape, gate action, and inspection.',
      'tagline' => 'An offset D auto-locking carabiner with a locking pin for tree climbing.',
      'summary' => 'The ISC Offset D supersafe carabiner is an auto-locking connector with an extra locking pin that guards against accidental opening. The offset D shape opens wide and loads well.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Offset D shape',
        1 => 'Auto-locking gate',
        2 => 'Locking pin for extra security',
        3 => 'ISC build quality',
        4 => 'Good for saddle attachment',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'ISC',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Shape',
          1 => 'Offset D',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate',
          1 => 'Auto-locking with locking pin',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why an extra locking step',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In tree work, carabiners rub against bark, ropes, and other hardware all day. A standard auto-locking gate can still be opened by a rope or branch twisting across it in the right direction. The ISC supersafe design adds a locking pin, so the gate needs an extra deliberate action before it opens.',
            1 => 'The offset D shape puts the load close to the spine, where the carabiner is strongest, and gives a wide gate opening for clipping a bridge, a rope, or a thick eye. That makes it a good fit for saddle attachment points, lanyards, and other places where you want maximum security.',
            2 => 'The trade-off is that it takes a bit more practice to open, especially with gloves or cold hands.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Load the carabiner along its long axis, and do not let it cross-load against the gate. Check the markings and ISC\'s instructions for ratings and approved uses. Compare other options in <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/">auto-locking carabiners</a>.',
            1 => 'Before each climb, check that the gate opens smoothly, closes fully, and that the locking pin engages every time. Look for grooves, sharp edges, cracks, and corrosion, and retire the carabiner after a big fall or drop. Rinse grit out with fresh water and dry it well. Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard, follow ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions, and be inspected before every climb. Get trained before you rely on it.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is it hard to open with gloves?',
          'a' => 'It takes a little practice. Work the gate on the ground until opening it is second nature.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is it rated for life support?',
          'a' => 'Check the markings and ISC\'s instructions for ratings and approved uses, and follow your employer\'s policy.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/isc/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Carabiner',
        'creator' => 'mikemol',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/28208534@N07',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/28208534@N07/8350737864',
      ),
    ),
    '/16-strand-climbing-rope/tygra-16-strand-climbing-rope/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/16-strand-climbing-rope/tygra-16-strand-climbing-rope/',
      'category' => '/rope/climbing-rope/16-strand-climbing-rope/',
      'name' => 'Teufelberger tygra 16-Strand Climbing Rope',
      'brand' => 'teufelberger',
      'brand_name' => 'Teufelberger',
      'kind' => 'rope',
      'price' => 280,
      'keyword' => 'teufelberger tygra',
      'title' => 'Teufelberger tygra 16-Strand Climbing Rope',
      'h1' => 'Teufelberger tygra 16-Strand Climbing Rope',
      'meta' => 'Teufelberger tygra is a firm 16-strand climbing rope for MRS and SRS work. How it handles, which hitches and devices suit it, and how to care for it.',
      'tagline' => 'A firm, round 16-strand climbing line for climbers who run both MRS and SRS.',
      'summary' => 'Teufelberger tygra is a 16-strand climbing rope built to stay round and firm, so it runs cleanly through friction hitches and mechanical devices alike. It suits climbers who switch between moving-rope and stationary-rope setups through the week.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '16-strand construction with a firm, round profile',
        1 => 'Works with friction hitches and many mechanical devices',
        2 => 'Low stretch for efficient ascent on SRS',
        3 => 'Common in eye-spliced lengths for climbing systems',
        4 => 'Made by an established European rope maker',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Construction',
          1 => '16-strand',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Tree climbing, MRS and SRS',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Teufelberger',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How tygra works on the job',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Tygra is built for climbers who want one line that behaves on a hitch and in a device. The cover is tight and the rope holds its shape under load, so a Distel or VT grabs and releases predictably and a mechanical device does not flatten the line into an oval after a season of use.',
            1 => 'On a stationary rope the low stretch means less bounce when you start a pitch, and on a moving rope it feeds through a friction saver without a lot of drag. Compare it with other <a href="/rope/climbing-rope/16-strand-climbing-rope/">16-strand climbing ropes</a> if you want a softer or lighter hand.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care, compatibility and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the manufacturer\'s compatibility list before pairing tygra with a specific device, and match your hitch cord to the rope diameter. Keep it in a rope bag, out of bar oil and fuel, and away from sharp edges and running saws.',
            1 => 'Treat it as life-support gear: follow the manufacturer\'s instructions, check it before every climb, and retire it after a hard fall, a drop onto a hard surface, or any damage you can\'t explain. Use it within an ANSI Z133 program and only after proper training. Retire the rope if you find glazing, cuts through the cover, core damage or stiff spots.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use tygra for SRS?',
          'a' => 'Yes. It is designed for both moving-rope and stationary-rope climbing. Confirm your specific device is approved for it in the device maker\'s instructions.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is tygra good for rigging?',
          'a' => 'No. Keep climbing rope for life support only and use a dedicated rigging line for lowering wood.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/teufelberger/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'IMG_0397',
        'creator' => 'hojaleaf',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/55231619@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/55231619@N00/499171630',
      ),
    ),
    '/2-in-1-lanyards/yale-phantom-pink-and-black-lanyard-without-hardware/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/2-in-1-lanyards/yale-phantom-pink-and-black-lanyard-without-hardware/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/fliplines-and-lanyards/',
      'name' => 'Yale Phantom Lanyard (Pink and Black, No Hardware)',
      'brand' => 'yale-cordage',
      'brand_name' => 'Yale Cordage',
      'kind' => 'lanyard',
      'price' => 60,
      'keyword' => 'yale phantom lanyard',
      'title' => 'Yale Phantom Lanyard, Pink and Black (No Hardware)',
      'h1' => 'Yale Phantom Lanyard, Pink and Black, Without Hardware',
      'meta' => 'The Yale Phantom lanyard in pink and black, sold without hardware so you can set it up with your own adjuster and carabiners. Setup tips and inspection notes.',
      'tagline' => 'A pink and black rope lanyard sold bare so you can build it your way.',
      'summary' => 'The Yale Phantom lanyard comes without hardware, so you pick the adjuster, snap or carabiners that fit your climbing style. The pink and black color makes it easy to tell apart from your climbing line and the rest of the crew\'s gear.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Sold without hardware for a custom setup',
        1 => 'High-visibility pink and black colorway',
        2 => 'Pairs with a mechanical adjuster or a friction hitch',
        3 => 'Rope lanyard for positioning on the stem',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Rope lanyard',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Color',
          1 => 'Pink and black',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Hardware',
          1 => 'Not included',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Building your own lanyard',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Buying a lanyard without hardware makes sense if you already own a favorite adjuster or want to match the carabiners on the rest of your saddle. Most climbers run it with a mechanical adjuster or a hitch on one end and a locking connector on both ends, set up as a 2-in-1 or a standard flipline.',
            1 => 'Bright, two-tone rope also helps the ground crew. They can see at a glance which line is your lanyard when they are watching you work, and you are less likely to grab the wrong tail when you are tired. Mark the date you put it in service on a tag or in your gear log so you know how old it is when inspection time comes.',
            2 => 'Match any adjuster to the rope diameter listed by its maker, and check that the eye fits your carabiner without side-loading the gate. See our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/fliplines-and-lanyards/">fliplines and lanyards</a> for complete kits if you would rather buy it ready to climb.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Using it safely',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A lanyard is your second point of attachment when you cut, so keep it snug and above the saw\'s path. Never cut toward your lanyard, and choose a steel-core flipline when you are removing wood close to it with a saw.',
            1 => 'Treat it as life-support gear: follow the manufacturer\'s instructions, check it before every climb, and retire it after a hard fall, a drop onto a hard surface, or any damage you can\'t explain. Use it within an ANSI Z133 program and only after proper training.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What hardware does it need?',
          'a' => 'At minimum a locking carabiner or snap at each end and an adjuster or friction hitch. Use hardware rated for life support and compatible with the rope diameter.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is this lanyard cut resistant?',
          'a' => 'It is a rope lanyard, not a wire-core flipline. Keep the saw well away from it.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/yale-cordage/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/lanyard-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Wood pole rescue',
        'creator' => 'Western Area Power Admin',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05/16974622426',
      ),
    ),
    '/arborist-boots/haix-protector-ultra-chainsaw-boots/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/arborist-boots/haix-protector-ultra-chainsaw-boots/',
      'category' => '/clothing/arborist-boots/',
      'name' => 'HAIX Protector Ultra Chainsaw Boots',
      'brand' => 'haix',
      'brand_name' => 'HAIX',
      'kind' => 'boot',
      'price' => 430,
      'keyword' => 'haix protector ultra',
      'title' => 'HAIX Protector Ultra Chainsaw Boots',
      'h1' => 'HAIX Protector Ultra Chainsaw Boots',
      'meta' => 'HAIX Protector Ultra chainsaw boots are leather saw boots for ground crews and fellers. What the protection covers, fit tips and how to keep them in shape.',
      'tagline' => 'Leather chainsaw-protective boots for fellers and ground crew on the saw all day.',
      'summary' => 'The HAIX Protector Ultra is a leather chainsaw boot made for crews who run a saw on the ground every day. It combines cut-protective inserts with a supportive, laced fit for felling, bucking and brush work on rough ground.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Chainsaw-protective boot for ground saw work',
        1 => 'Leather upper with a supportive laced fit',
        2 => 'Aggressive sole for slopes, logs and wet ground',
        3 => 'Built for long days on the saw',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Chainsaw-protective boot',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Upper',
          1 => 'Leather',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Closure',
          1 => 'Laced',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why saw boots matter',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Chaps and saw pants stop most cuts above the ankle, but a kickback or a slip while bucking can put the chain on your foot. A chainsaw-protective boot adds fibers in the upper that clog the sprocket, plus a protective toe, so your feet get the same kind of protection your legs do.',
            1 => 'Saw boots also need to work with the rest of your kit. Check that your chaps or saw pants overlap the top of the boot with no gap when you kneel, and that the sole has the grip you need on wet bark and steep ground. A boot that fits well is one you will actually wear on the hot afternoons, which is when most people cut corners.',
            2 => 'The Protector Ultra is built for ground crews who stand on logs, brush piles and side hills. Browse our <a href="/clothing/arborist-boots/">arborist boots</a> if you also need a lighter climbing boot.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Try boots on in the afternoon with the socks you wear at work, and give them a week of short days to break in. Clean off mud and sap, dry them away from direct heat, and treat the leather so it stays supple.',
            1 => 'Check the protection rating on the boot label against what your employer or the job requires. No boot is cut-proof, so keep good saw technique, a sharp chain and a working chain brake.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Are these good for climbing?',
          'a' => 'They are a heavier ground and felling boot. Many climbers prefer a lighter boot for spurs or SRS and keep saw boots for ground days.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I still need chaps or saw pants?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Boots protect the feet; leg protection is still required for chainsaw work on the ground.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'felling',
        2 => 'apparel',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/boot-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/boot-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Old Boot, New Boot',
        'creator' => 'General Wesc',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/35518575@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/35518575@N00/5198346327',
      ),
    ),
    '/auto-locking-carabiners/dmm-ceros-locksafe-carabiner-with-captive-bar/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/auto-locking-carabiners/dmm-ceros-locksafe-carabiner-with-captive-bar/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/',
      'name' => 'DMM Ceros Locksafe Carabiner with Captive Bar',
      'brand' => 'dmm',
      'brand_name' => 'DMM',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 32,
      'keyword' => 'dmm ceros',
      'title' => 'DMM Ceros Locksafe Carabiner with Captive Bar',
      'h1' => 'DMM Ceros Locksafe Carabiner with Captive Bar',
      'meta' => 'The DMM Ceros Locksafe carabiner has a captive bar that holds a load in place and stops cross-loading. Where arborists use it and how to check it before use.',
      'tagline' => 'An auto-locking carabiner with a captive bar that keeps the load where it belongs.',
      'summary' => 'The DMM Ceros carabiner pairs a Locksafe auto-locking gate with a captive bar that holds a device or rope in one position. That keeps the load off the minor axis, which is useful anywhere a connector could rotate on a bridge, ring or device.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Captive bar holds the load in position',
        1 => 'Locksafe auto-locking gate',
        2 => 'Helps prevent cross-loading',
        3 => 'Useful for devices, bridges and fixed attachments',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate',
          1 => 'Locksafe auto-locking',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Feature',
          1 => 'Captive bar',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'DMM',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What the captive bar does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A carabiner that can rotate can end up loaded across the gate, where it is much weaker. The Ceros bar splits the basket so a device, eye or ring stays at the spine end. Climbers use it to attach a mechanical device to a bridge, to hold a rope wrench tether in line, or anywhere they want the connector to stay oriented.',
            1 => 'Keep the Ceros dedicated to the job you set it up for. Moving it between roles makes it easy to forget which side of the bar carries the load. Many climbers mark it with colored tape on the spine, away from the gate and the rope-bearing surfaces, so it stays on the device it belongs to.',
            2 => 'The Locksafe gate needs a deliberate sequence to open, so it will not roll open against bark or a branch. See our other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/">auto-locking carabiners</a> for different shapes and sizes.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Checks before every climb',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Open and close the gate and make sure it snaps fully shut and locks on its own. Look for grooves worn by rope, burrs, cracks or a sticky gate, and clean out grit with warm water if the action slows down.',
            1 => 'Treat it as life-support gear: follow the manufacturer\'s instructions, check it before every climb, and retire it after a hard fall, a drop onto a hard surface, or any damage you can\'t explain. Use it within an ANSI Z133 program and only after proper training.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does the bar reduce strength?',
          'a' => 'The rated strengths are marked on the spine. Load it along the major axis as shown in DMM\'s instructions.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I clip a rope through the bar side?',
          'a' => 'Follow DMM\'s diagrams for which side to load. The bar is meant to keep the load in the spine-side section.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/dmm/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Carabiners and Pulley',
        'creator' => 'psd',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/45581782@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/45581782@N00/2411001794',
      ),
    ),
    '/auto-locking-carabiners/dmm-shadow-hms-carabiner-matt-gray/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/auto-locking-carabiners/dmm-shadow-hms-carabiner-matt-gray/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/',
      'name' => 'DMM Shadow HMS Locksafe Carabiner (Matt Gray)',
      'brand' => 'dmm',
      'brand_name' => 'DMM',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 24,
      'keyword' => 'dmm shadow hms carabiner',
      'title' => 'DMM Shadow HMS Carabiner, Matt Gray',
      'h1' => 'DMM Shadow HMS Carabiner (Matt Gray)',
      'meta' => 'The DMM Shadow HMS carabiner in matt gray: a pear-shaped auto-locker with a wide basket for hitches and rope. Arborist uses, sizing notes and inspection tips.',
      'tagline' => 'A pear-shaped HMS auto-locker with room for a hitch, a rope and a bit of hardware.',
      'summary' => 'The DMM Shadow HMS carabiner is a pear-shaped locking connector with a wide, rounded basket. The matt gray finish keeps glare down and the HMS shape gives a hitch or rope room to sit flat.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Pear (HMS) shape with a wide basket',
        1 => 'Auto-locking gate',
        2 => 'Matt gray finish',
        3 => 'Good for hitches, bridges and anchors',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Shape',
          1 => 'HMS (pear)',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate',
          1 => 'Auto-locking',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Finish',
          1 => 'Matt gray',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where an HMS fits on a saddle',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The wide end of an HMS gives a friction hitch, a Munter or two ropes space to lie flat instead of pinching. Arborists use the Shadow to tie into a bridge, to connect a hitch and pulley, or as a general anchor carabiner when building a canopy anchor or a basal setup.',
            1 => 'The wide nose also makes it easier to clip and unclip with gloves on, which helps on cold mornings. If you carry several lockers, a different finish makes it quick to pick out the one you use for your hitch or anchor without looking twice.',
            2 => 'Because the basket is generous, it also handles thicker rope and stacked hardware better than a small D. Compare sizes in our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/">auto-locking carabiners</a>.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check that the gate closes and locks by itself every time, and watch for rope wear grooves at the basket, which form faster when rope runs over the carabiner. Rinse grit out with clean water and let it dry before storage.',
            1 => 'Treat it as life-support gear: follow the manufacturer\'s instructions, check it before every climb, and retire it after a hard fall, a drop onto a hard surface, or any damage you can\'t explain. Use it within an ANSI Z133 program and only after proper training.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What does HMS mean?',
          'a' => 'It refers to the pear shape designed for a Munter hitch. The wide end gives rope and hitches more room.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the matt finish just cosmetic?',
          'a' => 'Mostly. It reduces shine and helps you tell this carabiner apart from others on your saddle.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/dmm/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'carabiner',
        'creator' => 'ani!',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/53018729@N00',
        'license' => 'CC0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/53018729@N00/10235172',
      ),
    ),
    '/auto-locking-carabiners/dmm-zodiac-carabiner-matt-gray/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/auto-locking-carabiners/dmm-zodiac-carabiner-matt-gray/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/',
      'name' => 'DMM Zodiac Locking Carabiner (Matt Gray)',
      'brand' => 'dmm',
      'brand_name' => 'DMM',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 22,
      'keyword' => 'dmm zodiac carabiner',
      'title' => 'DMM Zodiac Carabiner, Matt Gray',
      'h1' => 'DMM Zodiac Carabiner (Matt Gray)',
      'meta' => 'The DMM Zodiac carabiner in matt gray is a locking connector for climbing systems. Where it fits on a tree climbing setup, what to check and how to care for it.',
      'tagline' => 'A locking DMM carabiner in matt gray for everyday climbing connections.',
      'summary' => 'The DMM Zodiac carabiner is a locking aluminum connector in a low-glare matt gray finish. It is a practical choice for routine connections on a climbing system, such as hitch-to-bridge or lanyard ends.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Locking gate for life-support connections',
        1 => 'Matt gray finish cuts glare',
        2 => 'Light aluminum body',
        3 => 'Fits everyday saddle and hitch connections',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Locking carabiner',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Aluminum',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Finish',
          1 => 'Matt gray',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Using the Zodiac in a climbing system',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A tree climber\'s saddle can carry half a dozen locking connectors, each doing a slightly different job. The Zodiac is a general-purpose locker: clip it to a lanyard eye, use it between a hitch and your bridge, or keep it as a spare for building anchors.',
            1 => 'Before you buy, think about what the Zodiac will carry. A rope eye, a micro pulley and a hitch cord all take space in the basket, and a connector that is too small will crowd the gate and pinch the hitch. Lay your gear out and clip it together on the ground to check the fit before you commit it to your climbing system.',
            2 => 'Check the strength markings on the spine and confirm the gate type before you assign it to a life-support role. You can compare shapes and gate styles in our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/">auto-locking carabiners</a>.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Keep it working',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Load carabiners along the spine and avoid three-way loading or loading across the gate. Keep them out of fuel and bar oil, which collect grit and slow the gate.',
            1 => 'Treat it as life-support gear: follow the manufacturer\'s instructions, check it before every climb, and retire it after a hard fall, a drop onto a hard surface, or any damage you can\'t explain. Use it within an ANSI Z133 program and only after proper training.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does it lock automatically?',
          'a' => 'It is listed with our auto-locking carabiners. Check the gate on the exact version you buy, since some DMM models come in more than one gate style.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is it rated for arborist climbing?',
          'a' => 'Check the markings on the spine and DMM\'s instructions against your employer\'s requirements under ANSI Z133.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/dmm/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'carabiner+chains',
        'creator' => 'brenkee',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/55128416@N05',
        'license' => 'CC0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/55128416@N05/12835828245',
      ),
    ),
    '/auto-locking-carabiners/isc-offset-d-supersafe-carabiner/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/auto-locking-carabiners/isc-offset-d-supersafe-carabiner/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/',
      'name' => 'ISC Offset D Supersafe Carabiner',
      'brand' => 'isc',
      'brand_name' => 'ISC',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 28,
      'keyword' => 'isc offset d supersafe carabiner',
      'title' => 'ISC Offset D Supersafe Carabiner',
      'h1' => 'ISC Offset D Supersafe Carabiner',
      'meta' => 'The ISC Offset D Supersafe carabiner is an offset D auto-locker for tree climbing systems. How the shape helps, where to clip it, and what to check before use.',
      'tagline' => 'An offset D auto-locker from ISC for strong, tidy connections on your saddle.',
      'summary' => 'The ISC Offset D Supersafe carabiner combines an offset D shape with ISC\'s auto-locking Supersafe gate. The offset D keeps the load near the spine while opening wide enough to clip rope eyes, rings and devices.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Offset D shape keeps loads near the spine',
        1 => 'Supersafe auto-locking gate',
        2 => 'Wide gate opening for eyes and rings',
        3 => 'Made by ISC, a UK rigging and climbing maker',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Shape',
          1 => 'Offset D',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate',
          1 => 'Supersafe auto-locking',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'ISC',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why choose an offset D',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'An offset D puts the load close to the spine, which is the strongest part of a carabiner, while the wider gate end makes clipping a thick eye splice or a pulley side plate easier. That makes it a good all-rounder for hitch climbing systems, lanyard ends and connecting to a bridge.',
            1 => 'Look at how the gate sits on your saddle when you build your system. An offset D clipped to a bridge or lanyard ring should hang with the gate facing out and away from bark, so it does not get pushed open against a stem. Set it up on the ground, hang in it, and check the orientation before you leave the ground.',
            2 => 'The Supersafe gate is designed so it cannot be opened by a single accidental motion. See other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/">auto-locking carabiners</a> if you prefer an oval or HMS shape.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Daily checks',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Make sure the gate closes fully and the lock engages without help. Look for cracks, deep rope grooves and corrosion, especially if you work near salt air or treated wood.',
            1 => 'Treat it as life-support gear: follow the manufacturer\'s instructions, check it before every climb, and retire it after a hard fall, a drop onto a hard surface, or any damage you can\'t explain. Use it within an ANSI Z133 program and only after proper training.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the Supersafe gate triple action?',
          'a' => 'It is ISC\'s auto-locking gate design that needs more than one deliberate motion to open. Check ISC\'s instructions for the exact opening sequence.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Steel or aluminum?',
          'a' => 'ISC makes offset D carabiners in more than one material. Check the listing and the spine markings for the version you buy.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/isc/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => '3D-printed Carabiner Clip',
        'creator' => 'Creative Tools',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/33907867@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/33907867@N02/15004955370',
      ),
    ),
    '/auto-locking-carabiners/kong-ovalone-aluminum-auto-locking-carabiner/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/auto-locking-carabiners/kong-ovalone-aluminum-auto-locking-carabiner/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/',
      'name' => 'KONG Ovalone Aluminum Auto-Locking Carabiner',
      'brand' => 'kong',
      'brand_name' => 'KONG',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 24,
      'keyword' => 'kong ovalone aluminum auto-locking carabiner',
      'title' => 'KONG Ovalone Aluminum Auto-Locking Carabiner',
      'h1' => 'KONG Ovalone Aluminum Auto-Locking Carabiner',
      'meta' => 'The KONG Ovalone aluminum auto-locking carabiner is a symmetric oval for pulleys, devices and racking. Where an oval shines on a climbing rig and what to check.',
      'tagline' => 'A symmetric aluminum oval that keeps pulleys and devices centered.',
      'summary' => 'The KONG Ovalone aluminum auto-locking carabiner is a classic symmetric oval. Its even shape lets pulleys, rope grabs and other hardware sit centered, which is why ovals stay popular for rescue kits, rigging setups and climbing systems.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Symmetric oval shape centers hardware',
        1 => 'Auto-locking gate',
        2 => 'Lighter aluminum version of a classic design',
        3 => 'Works well with pulleys and devices',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Shape',
          1 => 'Oval',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Aluminum',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Gate',
          1 => 'Auto-locking',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where an oval earns its spot',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'An oval loads evenly on both sides, so a pulley or device does not shift toward the gate. That makes the Ovalone a natural match for haul systems, mechanical advantage setups, rope grabs and racking several pieces of hardware together.',
            1 => 'On the ground, an oval is also handy for racking. Clip pulleys, rope grabs and spare connectors to one carabiner on a gear loop and they stay organized and easy to grab when the climber calls for something. Keep life-support connectors on a separate, clearly marked rack so they do not get mixed with rigging hardware.',
            2 => 'Ovals are not as strong as D shapes of similar weight, so check the spine markings and match the carabiner to the job. Compare it with our other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/">auto-locking carabiners</a>.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Keep the gate clean and lubricate it sparingly with a product the manufacturer approves if it starts to drag. Look for wear grooves where rope runs and replace the carabiner if the gate fails to close by itself.',
            1 => 'Treat it as life-support gear: follow the manufacturer\'s instructions, check it before every climb, and retire it after a hard fall, a drop onto a hard surface, or any damage you can\'t explain. Use it within an ANSI Z133 program and only after proper training.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is an oval good for my climbing hitch?',
          'a' => 'It can work, but many climbers prefer an HMS or offset D for hitches. Ovals shine with pulleys and devices.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is there a steel version?',
          'a' => 'KONG also makes the Ovalone in steel. Choose steel for heavy wear and aluminum to save weight.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/kong/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Carabiner',
        'creator' => 'mikemol',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/28208534@N07',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/28208534@N07/8350737864',
      ),
