        array (
          0 => 'Gate',
          1 => 'Orca auto-locking',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Rock Exotica',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why climbers like the rockO',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The rockO has a big basket and a generous gate opening, so it clips large eye splices, rings and device attachment points without fuss. Arborists use it as a master carabiner on an anchor, to attach SRS devices, or to connect a lanyard to a saddle.',
            1 => 'When choosing between carabiners, think about how often you clip and unclip it. A large, smooth-opening carabiner like the rockO makes repeated clipping easier on cold days and with gloves on.',
            2 => 'The Orca gate locks automatically and is designed to open smoothly with gloves on. Compare it with our other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/">auto-locking carabiners</a>.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check that the gate snaps shut and locks every time. Look for wear grooves, cracks, burrs or corrosion and keep it free of sap and grit.',
            1 => 'Treat it as life-support gear: follow the manufacturer\'s instructions, check it before every climb, and retire it after a hard fall, a drop onto a hard surface, or any damage you can\'t explain. Use it within an ANSI Z133 program and only after proper training.',
            2 => 'Keep your rockO carabiners clean and dry, and store them away from chemicals and corrosive materials. Mark them if you use them for different roles so they stay in the right place on your saddle.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is it good for SRS devices?',
          'a' => 'Many climbers use it to attach SRS devices. Confirm compatibility in the device maker\'s instructions.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What makes the Orca lock different?',
          'a' => 'It is Rock Exotica\'s auto-locking gate style. See Rock Exotica\'s instructions for how to open and close it.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/rock-exotica/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => '3D-printed Carabiner Clip',
        'creator' => 'Creative Tools',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/33907867@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/33907867@N02/15004955370',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/rock-o-wire-eye-carabiner/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/rock-o-wire-eye-carabiner/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/',
      'name' => 'Rock Exotica rockO Wire Eye Carabiner',
      'brand' => 'rock-exotica',
      'brand_name' => 'Rock Exotica',
      'kind' => 'carabiner',
      'price' => 40,
      'keyword' => 'rocko wire eye carabiner',
      'title' => 'Rock Exotica rockO Wire Eye Carabiner',
      'h1' => 'Rock Exotica rockO Wire Eye Carabiner',
      'meta' => 'The Rock Exotica rockO Wire Eye carabiner adds a captive eye to keep a sling, eye or device in place. Where it helps on a climbing system and what to check.',
      'tagline' => 'A rockO carabiner with a captive eye that keeps a connection in position.',
      'summary' => 'The Rock Exotica rockO Wire Eye carabiner is a rockO auto-locker with a captive wire eye that holds an attached eye, sling or device in one spot. That helps keep the connection oriented so it does not rotate onto the gate.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Captive eye holds connections in place',
        1 => 'Large rockO aluminum body',
        2 => 'Auto-locking gate',
        3 => 'Helps prevent cross-loading',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Auto-locking carabiner with captive eye',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Aluminum',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Rock Exotica',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a captive eye helps',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A sling, rope eye or device can migrate around a carabiner as you move, ending up on the gate or across the minor axis. The captive eye on this rockO keeps one connection fixed at the spine end, so the carabiner stays oriented when you climb, rig a bridge or set a lanyard.',
            1 => 'A captive eye is especially useful on lanyard ends and device attachments, where the connection is clipped and unclipped often. Keeping it fixed means one less thing to check each time you reposition.',
            2 => 'Check Rock Exotica\'s instructions for what can be captured and how. Compare it with our other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/carabiners-and-hardware/auto-locking-carabiners/">auto-locking carabiners</a>.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the wire eye for kinks, fraying or a loose fit, and confirm the gate locks fully. Keep it clean of sap and grit.',
            1 => 'Treat it as life-support gear: follow the manufacturer\'s instructions, check it before every climb, and retire it after a hard fall, a drop onto a hard surface, or any damage you can\'t explain. Use it within an ANSI Z133 program and only after proper training.',
            2 => 'Keep track of when it went into service and check it as part of your regular gear inspection. If the eye has been bent or damaged, retire the carabiner rather than trying to repair it.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What goes in the captive eye?',
          'a' => 'Follow Rock Exotica\'s instructions. It is meant to hold one connection in place at the spine end.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I remove the wire eye?',
          'a' => 'Only if the manufacturer\'s instructions allow it. Do not modify life-support hardware.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/rock-exotica/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/carabiner-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Carabiner',
        'creator' => 'mikemol',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/28208534@N07',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/28208534@N07/8350737864',
      ),
