      'h1' => 'Tachyon Ash Replacement Lanyard',
      'meta' => 'The Tachyon Ash replacement lanyard is Teufelberger tachyon rope for replacing a worn positioning lanyard. How to match your adjuster and when to retire it.',
      'tagline' => 'Teufelberger tachyon rope in Ash, made up as a replacement lanyard.',
      'summary' => 'The Tachyon Ash replacement lanyard is a positioning lanyard made from Teufelberger tachyon rope in the Ash colorway. Use it to replace a worn lanyard while keeping your adjuster and connectors if they still pass inspection.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Teufelberger tachyon rope',
        1 => 'Ash colorway',
        2 => 'Holds friction hitches and runs well through hardware',
        3 => 'Replace the rope, keep good hardware',
        4 => 'Check length and terminations before ordering',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope',
          1 => 'Teufelberger tachyon',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Color',
          1 => 'Ash',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Replacement positioning lanyard',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Teufelberger',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fresh rope for your lanyard',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The lanyard rope is the part that wears out first. Every flip around the stem grinds bark into the cover, and every adjustment works the fibers. When it gets fuzzy, glazed or stiff, it is time for new rope.',
            1 => 'Tachyon is a popular choice because it has a firm, round feel, holds a hitch well and runs cleanly through mechanical adjusters. The Ash color is a quieter option than the brighter tachyon colors. Compare our other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/fliplines-and-lanyards/rope-lanyards/">rope lanyards</a> if you want a different rope.',
            2 => 'When you swap in a new lanyard, check that the adjuster grips and releases the new rope as smoothly as it did the old one. Fresh rope can feel firmer at first and loosen up after a few climbs. Test it close to the ground before you rely on it for spar work.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Matching and inspecting',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Confirm your adjuster is approved for tachyon\'s diameter and construction, and that the terminations suit the connectors you will use. Set it up only as the manufacturers instruct.',
            1 => 'Inspect it before every climb for cuts, glazing, flat spots and splice damage. Lanyards are life-support gear: they must meet the applicable standard, be used within ANSI Z133 and be used by trained climbers. Keep the saw away from it and retire it after any cut.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the Ash version different from other tachyon colors?',
          'a' => 'The color is different. Check the listing for diameter and termination details.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need a new adjuster too?',
          'a' => 'Not if your current adjuster passes inspection and is approved for tachyon.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
