          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Storing more than one throw line',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why throw line needs its own storage',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Climbers who set lines often end up carrying two throw lines: one in use and one spare for when the first hangs up, or a second line for setting a redirect or isolating a limb. Nesting bags let you keep both lines separate without carrying two full-size bags. Pull out the one you need and the other stays packed.',
            1 => 'Thin throw line tangles faster than anything else in the truck. A loose pile turns into a bird\'s nest the moment you try to throw from it, and untangling it eats time at the start of every job. Keeping the line in a container that opens wide and lets it flake out cleanly is the easiest fix. Compare options in our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/">gear storage</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Throwing from it and caring for it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Feed the line in loosely rather than wrapping it, and keep the end tied off inside so it cannot be pulled away. Set the bag on the ground a step back from where you throw so the line peels off the top without catching your feet or brush.',
            1 => 'Keep throw line dry and out of the sun between jobs, and away from fuel and bar oil. Before setting a climbing line, check the throw line and weight for cuts or damage, and keep the area under the throw clear of people.',
            2 => 'Using two different line colors helps when both lines are in the tree at once, since you can tell at a glance which one to pull.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why carry two throw lines?',
          'a' => 'A spare saves time when the first line hangs up, and a second line helps when isolating a crotch or setting a redirect.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do the nested bags tangle the lines?',
          'a' => 'No. Each line stays in its own bag, which is the point of the design.',
        ),
      ),
