        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '10 mm',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Friction hitch on climbing line',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Matching hitch cord to rope',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A friction hitch only works when the hitch cord and climbing line suit each other. The cord should usually be a bit smaller than or close to the rope\'s diameter, and the cover materials need to grip without melting or glazing. A 10mm hitch cord pairs with a range of common arborist climbing lines, but the right hitch, wraps, and braids depend on your rope.',
            1 => 'Set it up low and test it on the ground. If it slips, add a wrap or change the hitch; if it binds, back off. Compare lengths and other cords in our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/hitch-cord-and-split-tails/eye-and-eye-prusiks/">eye and eye prusiks</a>.',
            2 => 'Eye-to-eye cords connect to your bridge or climbing system with a carabiner through both eyes, so they are quick to swap when worn. Length matters too: too long and the hitch tends poorly, too short and the hitch will not dress properly.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Wear, heat, and retirement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Hitch cord takes the most heat and abrasion of anything in your system. Check it before each climb for glazing, stiff spots, cover damage, and wear at the eyes. Pull it from service if it has been shock loaded, contaminated with chemicals, or if you find any damage.',
            1 => 'Use it within the maker\'s instructions and ANSI Z133 practice, and get trained on friction hitches before you rely on one. Many climbers carry a spare in their saddle bag.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What climbing rope works with a 10mm eye-to-eye prusik?',
          'a' => 'Many common arborist climbing lines in the 11 to 13mm range work, but always test the hitch on your own rope and follow the maker\'s guidance.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I replace hitch cord?',
          'a' => 'When inspection shows glazing, cover damage, stiffness, or wear at the eyes. Busy climbers replace it often.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'coiling',
        'creator' => 'michael pollak',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06/6796140986',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/hitch-cord-and-split-tails/prusik-loops/teufelberger-8mm-ocean-polyester-thimble-loop/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/hitch-cord-and-split-tails/prusik-loops/teufelberger-8mm-ocean-polyester-thimble-loop/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/hitch-cord-and-split-tails/',
      'name' => 'Teufelberger 8mm Ocean Polyester Thimble Loop',
      'brand' => 'teufelberger',
      'brand_name' => 'Teufelberger',
      'kind' => 'rope',
      'price' => 30,
      'keyword' => 'ocean polyester thimble loop',
      'title' => 'Teufelberger 8mm Ocean Polyester Thimble Loop',
      'h1' => 'Teufelberger 8mm Ocean Polyester Thimble Loop',
      'meta' => 'The Teufelberger Ocean Polyester thimble loop is an 8mm polyester loop with a thimble eye for prusik and attachment work. How to use it and how to inspect it.',
      'tagline' => 'An 8mm polyester loop with a thimble eye for prusik and hitch setups.',
      'summary' => 'The Teufelberger Ocean Polyester thimble loop is an 8mm polyester prusik loop with a thimble that protects the eye where it meets hardware. Climbers and riggers use loops like this for friction hitches, short tethers, and attachment points.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '8 mm polyester cord',
        1 => 'Thimble protects the eye from wear',
        2 => 'Useful for prusiks and short attachments',
        3 => 'Low stretch polyester',
        4 => 'Compact and easy to carry',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Prusik loop with thimble',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '8 mm',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Polyester',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where a thimble loop fits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A thimble keeps the eye of a loop open and gives a carabiner a smooth, hard surface to bear on. That cuts wear at the point where soft cord meets metal, especially on a loop that gets clipped and unclipped all day. An 8mm polyester loop is a handy size for prusiks on rigging lines, a short attachment for a pulley, or a backup hitch.',
            1 => 'Polyester stretches little and stands up to abrasion, which suits these jobs. Compare other cords and loops in our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/hitch-cord-and-split-tails/">hitch cord and split tails</a>.',
            2 => 'Rigging crews use short loops like this to attach a pulley or block to a sling, or to tie a prusik onto a lowering line for a mechanical advantage setup. Having a few spares in the rigging bag saves time on the job.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use within limits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'An 8mm loop is not a substitute for a climbing hitch cord unless the maker approves it for that use with your rope. Check the rated strength, test any hitch on the ground first, and never exceed what the loop is rated for.',
            1 => 'Inspect the cord, the thimble, and the splice or stitching before each use. Retire it after a shock load or if you find cuts, glazing, or a deformed thimble. Follow the maker\'s instructions and ANSI Z133 practice.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use an 8mm thimble loop as my climbing friction hitch?',
          'a' => 'Only if the maker approves it for that use with your rope. Most climbers use a dedicated hitch cord closer to their rope diameter.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What does the thimble do?',
          'a' => 'It keeps the eye open and protects the cord from wear where it contacts a carabiner or pulley.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/teufelberger/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Walt coiling the rope at the summit',
        'creator' => 'Tristan Higbee',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62264264@N03',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62264264@N03/8305035257',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/other-gear/rock-exotica-enforcer-with-case/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/other-gear/rock-exotica-enforcer-with-case/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/other-gear/',
      'name' => 'Rock Exotica Enforcer with Case',
      'brand' => 'rock-exotica',
      'brand_name' => 'Rock Exotica',
      'kind' => 'rigging-device',
      'price' => 1000,
      'keyword' => 'rock exotica enforcer',
      'title' => 'Rock Exotica Enforcer Load Cell with Case',
      'h1' => 'Rock Exotica Enforcer with Case',
      'meta' => 'The Rock Exotica Enforcer is a load cell that measures forces in rope systems. How tree crews use it to check rigging loads and train climbers.',
      'tagline' => 'A portable load cell for measuring real forces in rigging and climbing systems.',
      'summary' => 'The Rock Exotica Enforcer is a portable load cell that clips inline in a rope system and records the forces it sees. Arborists use it to measure rigging loads, compare techniques, and teach crews what a drop actually puts on the gear.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Measures force inline in a rope system',
        1 => 'Records peak loads',
        2 => 'Useful for rigging training and testing',
        3 => 'Carry case protects the unit',
        4 => 'Compact enough for field use',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Load cell (force meter)',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Rock Exotica',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Includes',
          1 => 'Carry case',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why measure forces',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Most rigging decisions are made by feel: how big a piece, how much run, how hard to let it run. A load cell replaces guesswork with a number. Put the Enforcer inline between the rigging point and the block or lowering device, take a cut, and you see the peak force the system saw. Do the same piece with more friction or a shorter drop and the difference is obvious.',
            1 => 'That makes it a strong training tool. New climbers learn quickly why a small piece dropped a few feet loads a rigging point more than they expected. See more specialty gear in our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/other-gear/">other gear</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Using it safely',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A load cell is an instrument, not a rigging connector to leave in the system. Use it within its rated range and the maker\'s instructions, check the batteries and calibration as directed, and keep it in the case between jobs.',
            1 => 'Testing rigging loads means setting up controlled drops, so plan them carefully. Keep everyone out of the drop zone, use rated hardware throughout, and follow ANSI Z133 practice. Treat the numbers as a guide to better rigging, not permission to push gear closer to its limits.',
            2 => 'Record what you test. A short log of piece size, drop, friction setup, and peak load gives your crew a reference that is far more useful than memory.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the Rock Exotica Enforcer used for in tree work?',
          'a' => 'Measuring rigging and climbing loads for training, testing techniques, and understanding how hard a drop loads the gear.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I leave it rigged in the system all day?',
          'a' => 'It is meant for measurement and training. Follow Rock Exotica\'s instructions on how it may be used and loaded.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/rock-exotica/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Pulley Block Equipment Rigging Edited 2020',
        'creator' => 'chimpwithcan',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188454520@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188454520@N02/49920521488',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/other-gear/teufelberger-rope-tarp/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/other-gear/teufelberger-rope-tarp/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/other-gear/',
      'name' => 'Teufelberger Rope Tarp',
      'brand' => 'teufelberger',
      'brand_name' => 'Teufelberger',
      'kind' => 'case-bag',
      'price' => 45,
      'keyword' => 'teufelberger rope tarp',
      'title' => 'Teufelberger Rope Tarp for Clean Climbing Rope',
      'h1' => 'Teufelberger Rope Tarp',
      'meta' => 'The Teufelberger rope tarp gives your climbing line a clean place to lie at the base of the tree. Why a rope tarp extends rope life and how climbers use it.',
      'tagline' => 'A ground tarp that keeps your climbing rope out of the dirt at the base of the tree.',
      'summary' => 'The Teufelberger rope tarp is a ground sheet for your climbing line, laid at the base of the tree so the rope stays out of the dirt, sawdust, and mud while you climb. Many styles also wrap up to carry the rope.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Keeps rope off the ground',
        1 => 'Cuts grit damage to the braid',
        2 => 'Easy to spot the rope pile',
        3 => 'Folds up with the rope inside',
        4 => 'Quick to lay out and pack',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Rope tarp',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Teufelberger',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Ground protection for climbing rope',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why lay rope on a tarp',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Grit that gets into a climbing rope stays there, grinding the fibers each time the line runs through your hitch, pulley, or friction saver. A tarp at the base of the tree keeps the tail of the rope out of the dirt, sawdust, and wet grass for the whole climb. It also marks where the rope is, which helps the ground crew keep wood and the chipper away from it.',
            1 => 'At the end of the day the rope gets flaked back onto the tarp and wrapped up for the ride home. Browse other specialty items in our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/other-gear/">other gear</a> section.',
            2 => 'Tarps are also handy on hard surfaces. Driveways and patios are rough on rope, and the tarp gives the line a smooth surface instead of concrete. On wet days it also keeps the rope out of puddles and mud.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care tips',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Shake the tarp out before folding so sawdust and leaves do not end up inside the bundle. Dry it and the rope before storing, and keep both away from fuel, bar oil, and battery acid.',
            1 => 'A tarp protects the rope from dirt but does not replace inspection. Run the rope through your hands before every climb and retire it according to Teufelberger\'s guidance and ANSI Z133 practice.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a rope tarp better than a rope bag?',
          'a' => 'Both keep rope clean. A tarp gives a flat, visible spot at the tree; a bag is faster to carry and deploy. Many climbers use both.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a rope tarp for rigging lines?',
          'a' => 'Yes, though rigging lines are often long and heavy, so a large tarp or bag works best.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/teufelberger/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'REL-NL-WK7-2-duffel-bag-10454',
        'creator' => 'Trendhim',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/148219711@N08',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/148219711@N08/50326073447',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/rings/smc-radius-rings/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/rings/smc-radius-rings/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/rings/',
      'name' => 'SMC Radius Rings',
      'brand' => 'smc',
      'brand_name' => 'SMC',
      'kind' => 'rigging-device',
      'price' => 25,
      'keyword' => 'smc radius rings',
      'title' => 'SMC Radius Rings for Climbing and Friction Savers',
      'h1' => 'SMC Radius Rings',
      'meta' => 'SMC Radius rings are smooth, rounded rings for building friction savers, adjustable bridges, and anchor setups. Where climbers use them and how to inspect them.',
      'tagline' => 'Smooth, rounded rings for friction savers, bridges, and anchor builds.',
      'summary' => 'SMC Radius rings are rounded metal rings used to build friction savers, adjustable bridges, and anchors in tree climbing systems. A ring gives rope a smooth surface to run over and a simple connection with no moving parts.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Smooth radius for rope to run over',
        1 => 'No moving parts or gates',
        2 => 'Useful for friction savers and bridges',
        3 => 'Simple to inspect',
        4 => 'From a long-time rescue hardware maker',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Climbing ring',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'SMC',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Friction savers, bridges, anchors',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where rings fit in a climbing kit',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Rings show up all over a modern climbing system. A pair on a length of rope makes a ring friction saver. A ring on a rope bridge lets your climbing system slide side to side as you work. A ring at an anchor gives you a smooth redirect without a carabiner gate in the way. Because there is no gate to open and nothing to rotate, rings are simple and easy to check.',
            1 => 'Pick the size and strength that suit the job and the other hardware they meet. See all sizes in our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/rings/">rings</a> section.',
            2 => 'Ring size changes how rope behaves. A larger radius is gentler on the line and lets friction hitches and hardware pass more easily, while a smaller ring is lighter. Match ring size to the rope and the other hardware in the system.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and limits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check rings before each use for grooves worn by rope, burrs, cracks, and deformation. A groove that catches your fingernail is a reason to retire the ring, because a sharp edge will cut rope fast.',
            1 => 'Use rings only for the loads and configurations the maker rates them for, and keep them from being side-loaded against a stem. Life-support use must follow the maker\'s instructions and ANSI Z133 practice.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What are SMC Radius rings used for in tree climbing?',
          'a' => 'Building ring friction savers, as the sliding point on a rope bridge, and for anchors or redirects.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'When should I retire a climbing ring?',
          'a' => 'When you find grooves, burrs, cracks, or deformation, or after a serious shock load.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/smc/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-5.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-5-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Nautical #5',
        'creator' => 'Graham B Finney',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188487900@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188487900@N02/51798984894',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/swivels/dmm-nexus-swivel/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/swivels/dmm-nexus-swivel/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/swivels/',
      'name' => 'DMM Nexus Swivel',
      'brand' => 'dmm',
      'brand_name' => 'DMM',
      'kind' => 'rigging-device',
      'price' => 95,
      'keyword' => 'dmm nexus swivel',
      'title' => 'DMM Nexus Swivel for Climbing and Rigging',
      'h1' => 'DMM Nexus Swivel',
      'meta' => 'The DMM Nexus swivel lets connected gear spin freely so ropes and lanyards do not twist. How arborists use it in climbing and rigging, and what to inspect.',
      'tagline' => 'A DMM swivel with multiple attachment points to stop twists in your system.',
      'summary' => 'The DMM Nexus swivel is a bearing swivel that lets the parts of a climbing or rigging system rotate freely, so ropes and lanyards do not twist up. Its larger attachment eye can take more than one connector, which makes it useful as a small hub.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Free-spinning bearing swivel',
        1 => 'Stops rope and lanyard twist',
        2 => 'Larger eye takes multiple connectors',
        3 => 'Useful in climbing and rigging',
        4 => 'Machined DMM hardware',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Swivel',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'DMM',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Climbing and rigging systems',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why add a swivel',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Every time a climber spins on a single line or a rigged piece rotates on the way down, twist goes into the rope. Over a day it builds up into kinks and hockles that make devices and hitches run badly. A swivel between the rope and your saddle, or between the rigging line and the load, lets that rotation happen at the bearing instead.',
            1 => 'The Nexus adds a larger attachment eye, so you can clip a second connector for a positioning lanyard, a pulley, or a tool line. Compare other models in our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/swivels/">swivels</a>.',
            2 => 'In rigging, a swivel between the lowering line and a heavy piece lets the wood rotate as it comes down without spinning the rope into kinks. That keeps the line running clean through the block and the lowering device.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and use',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check that the swivel spins smoothly before each use, with no grinding, play, or flat spots. Look for cracks, sharp edges, and wear where connectors bear on the eyes. Retire it after a shock load or any sign of damage.',
            1 => 'Load it only in the directions and configurations DMM approves, and make sure every connector on it is oriented correctly and closed. Life-support use must follow the maker\'s instructions and ANSI Z133 practice.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Where should a swivel go in a climbing system?',
          'a' => 'Common spots are between the bridge and the climbing device, or at the end of a rope in an SRS setup. Follow the maker\'s instructions for your system.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use the DMM Nexus swivel for rigging?',
          'a' => 'Swivels are used in rigging to stop line twist. Check DMM\'s rated strength against your expected loads.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/dmm/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-6.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-6-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Arborist Cutting',
        'creator' => 'matt.boman',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/10384097@N08',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/10384097@N08/6109956022',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/climbing-gear/swivels/petzl-swivel-open/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/swivels/petzl-swivel-open/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/swivels/',
      'name' => 'Petzl Swivel Open',
      'brand' => 'petzl',
      'brand_name' => 'Petzl',
      'kind' => 'rigging-device',
      'price' => 85,
      'keyword' => 'petzl swivel open',
      'title' => 'Petzl Swivel Open: Openable Swivel for Climbers',
      'h1' => 'Petzl Swivel Open',
      'meta' => 'The Petzl Swivel Open is a swivel with an openable side, so it goes straight onto a saddle bridge or fixed loop. How climbers use it and what to check.',
      'tagline' => 'An openable Petzl swivel that installs directly on a bridge or fixed loop.',
      'summary' => 'The Petzl Swivel Open is a bearing swivel with one side that opens, so you can put it on a saddle bridge or closed loop without taking anything apart. It stops your climbing system from twisting while you work and spin in the tree.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Openable side for direct installation',
        1 => 'Bearing swivel stops rope twist',
        2 => 'Fits on bridges and fixed loops',
        3 => 'Compact Petzl design',
        4 => 'Popular for SRS setups',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Openable swivel',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Petzl',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Saddle bridges and SRS setups',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What the openable side solves',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Most swivels have two closed eyes, so you need connectors on both sides. The Swivel Open lets you open one side and put it straight onto a rope bridge or another closed loop. That removes a carabiner from the stack, shortens the system, and keeps the swivel\'s orientation consistent.',
            1 => 'Climbers often use it on the bridge with the climbing device below it, so the device can rotate as you move around the tree without twisting the rope. See other options in our <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/swivels/">swivels</a>.',
            2 => 'In single-rope setups, the swivel also keeps the device oriented to the rope as you work out a limb and come back, so the line does not twist up between the anchor and your saddle. Less twist means a smoother descent and fewer kinks at the end of the day.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and use',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Before each climb, check that the swivel turns freely, the openable side closes and locks fully, and there are no cracks, sharp edges, or worn spots. Retire it after a shock load or any sign of damage.',
            1 => 'Make sure the opening side is closed and secured every time you load it. Use it only in the configurations Petzl approves, and follow the maker\'s instructions and ANSI Z133 practice for life-support gear.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why use the Petzl Swivel Open instead of a regular swivel?',
          'a' => 'It opens on one side, so you can install it directly on a bridge or loop without an extra carabiner.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the Petzl Swivel Open life-support rated?',
          'a' => 'It is built for work-at-height systems. Check the markings and Petzl\'s instructions for its rated strength and approved uses.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Pruning in RVA (Carytown, Virginia)',
        'creator' => 'Gamma Man',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06/48982894227',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/helmets/husqvarna-functional-helmet-with-wheel-ratchet-adjustment/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/helmets/husqvarna-functional-helmet-with-wheel-ratchet-adjustment/',
      'category' => '/climbing/helmets/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna Functional Helmet with Wheel Ratchet Adjustment',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'helmet',
      'price' => 70,
      'keyword' => 'husqvarna functional helmet',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna Functional Helmet with Wheel Ratchet',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna Functional Helmet with Wheel Ratchet Adjustment',
      'meta' => 'The Husqvarna Functional helmet pairs a hard hat, mesh visor, and ear muffs with a wheel ratchet. Who it suits and how it differs from a climbing helmet.',
      'tagline' => 'A forestry helmet system with visor, ear muffs, and quick wheel ratchet fit.',
      'summary' => 'The Husqvarna Functional helmet is a forestry helmet system with a hard shell, mesh face visor, and ear muffs, fitted with a wheel ratchet for quick sizing. It suits saw work on the ground: felling, bucking, and limbing.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Hard hat, visor, and ear muffs in one',
        1 => 'Wheel ratchet for quick fit',
        2 => 'Mesh visor keeps chips off your face',
        3 => 'Built for ground saw work',
        4 => 'Affordable all-in-one setup',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Forestry helmet system',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Husqvarna',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Adjustment',
          1 => 'Wheel ratchet',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Includes',
          1 => 'Mesh visor and ear muffs',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Built for ground saw work',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Running a saw on the ground means three hazards at once: falling wood, flying chips, and noise. A forestry helmet system like the Husqvarna Functional handles all three with one piece of kit. The visor flips down for cutting and up for talking, and the ear muffs swing out of the way when the saw is off.',
            1 => 'The wheel ratchet lets you tighten or loosen the fit with gloves on, which helps when switching between a beanie in winter and a bare head in summer. Compare other helmets in our <a href="/climbing/helmets/">helmets</a> section.',
            2 => 'It is a good fit for ground workers on a tree crew and for homeowners who run a saw occasionally. Everything is in one package, so there is no guessing about which visor or ear muffs fit the shell.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Ground helmet vs climbing helmet',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'This is a forestry helmet, not a climbing helmet. Climbing helmets have a chin strap system designed to keep the helmet on in a fall and are tested for different impacts. If you climb or work from a lift, use a helmet rated for that work, as ANSI Z133 and your employer require.',
            1 => 'Check the helmet\'s markings for the standards it meets, and replace the shell after a heavy impact or at the age the maker recommends. A mesh visor protects from chips but is not safety eyewear, so wear rated glasses too.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I climb in the Husqvarna Functional helmet?',
          'a' => 'It is designed for ground forestry work. Climbers should use a helmet made and rated for climbing, with a proper chin strap.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I still need safety glasses with the mesh visor?',
          'a' => 'Yes. A mesh visor stops chips but not fine dust or small fragments, so wear rated eye protection underneath.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'felling',
        1 => 'ground',
        2 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/helmet-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/helmet-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => '3rd Regiment, Basic Camp, FHCC CST 2021',
        'creator' => 'U.S. Army ROTC',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/136737541@N05',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/136737541@N05/51323491277',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/helmets/petzl-vertex-helmet/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/helmets/petzl-vertex-helmet/',
      'category' => '/climbing/helmets/',
      'name' => 'Petzl Vertex Helmet',
      'brand' => 'petzl',
      'brand_name' => 'Petzl',
      'kind' => 'helmet',
      'price' => 125,
      'keyword' => 'petzl vertex helmet',
      'title' => 'Petzl Vertex Helmet for Arborists and Climbers',
      'h1' => 'Petzl Vertex Helmet',
      'meta' => 'The Petzl Vertex helmet is a comfortable work-at-height helmet with a secure chin strap and accessory slots. Why arborists wear it, fit tips, and add-ons.',
      'tagline' => 'A comfortable work-at-height helmet that climbers wear all day.',
      'summary' => 'The Petzl Vertex helmet is a work-at-height helmet built for climbing and rope access, with a six-point textile suspension, an adjustable chin strap, and slots for a visor, ear muffs, and headlamp. It is one of the most common climbing helmets on tree crews.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Work-at-height design with chin strap',
        1 => 'Six-point textile suspension',
        2 => 'Slots for visor, ear muffs, and headlamp',
        3 => 'Comfortable for long climbs',
        4 => 'Several colors available',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Work-at-height helmet',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Petzl',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Suspension',
          1 => 'Six-point textile',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Retention',
          1 => 'Adjustable chin strap',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why climbers choose the Vertex',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A climbing helmet gets worn all day, so comfort decides whether it stays on your head. The Vertex sits low and stable, with a suspension that spreads the weight and a chin strap that keeps it on when you lean, swing, or look up into the canopy. Fit adjusts quickly, which helps when you add or take off a beanie as the day warms up.',
            1 => 'Accessory slots let you build it into a full setup: a mesh or clear visor, ear muffs, and a headlamp for early starts or storm work. See other models in our <a href="/climbing/helmets/">helmets</a> section.',
            2 => 'Bright colors help the ground crew track the climber in the canopy, which matters when wood is coming down and everyone needs to know where the climber is.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit, standards, and replacement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Adjust the headband so the helmet sits level and does not rock, then snug the chin strap. Check the label for the standards your version meets, and make sure it fits what ANSI Z133 and your employer require for climbing and aerial work.',
            1 => 'Inspect the shell, suspension, and strap before each use. Replace the helmet after a significant impact even if you see no damage, and follow Petzl\'s guidance on service life. Keep it out of the sun on the dash and away from solvents.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the Petzl Vertex helmet good for tree climbing?',
          'a' => 'Yes. It is one of the most widely used helmets among climbing arborists. Check that your version meets the standards your work requires.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I add ear muffs and a visor?',
          'a' => 'Yes. The Vertex has slots for compatible visors, ear muffs, and headlamps.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'bucket',
        2 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/helmet-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/helmet-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'TVCF004',
        'creator' => 'Symic',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00/33549425170',
