      'title' => 'Weaver L-Shaped Climber Pads for Tree Spurs',
      'h1' => 'Weaver L-Shaped Climber Pads',
      'meta' => 'Weaver L-shaped climber pads spread spur pressure across the calf for comfort on removals. How they fit, when to replace worn pads, and what to check each day.',
      'tagline' => 'L-shaped calf pads that make tree spurs more comfortable on long climbs.',
      'summary' => 'Weaver L-shaped climber pads replace worn or uncomfortable pads on tree spurs, wrapping the calf in an L-shape to spread pressure from the shank. A good set of pads is often the cheapest way to make your spurs feel better on long removals.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'L-shape wraps the calf to spread pressure',
        1 => 'Replaces worn or flattened spur pads',
        2 => 'Makes long spar climbs more comfortable',
        3 => 'Check compatibility with your spurs',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Climber pads',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Shape',
          1 => 'L-shaped',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Tree spurs',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why pads matter',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'When you stand in your spurs, the shank pushes against your calf with every step. Thin or flattened pads let that pressure land in one spot, and after a few hours it hurts. L-shaped pads wrap around the calf so the load is spread across a wider area, which makes a long removal noticeably easier.',
            1 => 'They are a straightforward upgrade for older spurs or a replacement when the original pads have packed down. Browse other options in our <a href="/climbing/spurs/climber-pads/">climber pads</a> section.',
            2 => 'Pads are also a wear item. Sap, sweat, and rain break down the padding and covers over time, and a pad that has gone hard or flat no longer protects your leg. If your spurs fit well but hurt, the pads are the first thing to look at before you blame the shanks or straps.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fitting and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check that the pads suit your spur model and strap layout before ordering. Fit them so the shank sits centered on the pad and the upper strap holds everything snug without cutting into the leg.',
            1 => 'Clean off sap and mud after use and let the pads dry away from direct heat. Inspect the pads, straps, and buckles each time you inspect the gaffs. Spur work should always be done with a flipline or lanyard, under ANSI Z133, by a trained climber.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will Weaver L-pads fit other brands of spurs?',
          'a' => 'Many spurs share a similar layout, but confirm fit with your spur model before relying on them.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How long do climber pads last?',
          'a' => 'It depends on use. Replace them when the padding flattens, the cover tears, or the attachment points wear.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/weaver/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/spur-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/spur-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Fall Protection Training, 2013',
        'creator' => 'Western Area Power Admin',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05/8813825794',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/buckingham-dowel-and-screw-style-replacement-gaff-screws/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/buckingham-dowel-and-screw-style-replacement-gaff-screws/',
      'category' => '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/',
      'name' => 'Buckingham Dowel and Screw Style Replacement Gaff Screws',
      'brand' => 'buckingham',
      'brand_name' => 'Buckingham',
      'kind' => 'spur',
      'price' => 9,
      'keyword' => 'buckingham replacement gaff screws',
      'title' => 'Buckingham Replacement Gaff Screws',
      'h1' => 'Buckingham Replacement Gaff Screws, Dowel and Screw Style',
      'meta' => 'Buckingham replacement gaff screws for dowel and screw style spur gaffs. Why a loose gaff is dangerous, how to check the screw, and when to replace it.',
      'tagline' => 'Replacement screws that hold Buckingham dowel and screw style gaffs on the shank.',
      'summary' => 'These Buckingham replacement gaff screws hold dowel and screw style gaffs on Buckingham climbing spurs. A worn or stripped screw lets a gaff loosen, so having the correct replacement on hand is part of keeping spurs safe.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Replacement screw for dowel and screw style gaffs',
        1 => 'Keeps gaffs tight on the shank',
        2 => 'Cheap insurance against a loose gaff',
        3 => 'Use the correct Buckingham part',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Buckingham dowel and screw style gaffs',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Replacement gaff screw',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Climbing spur maintenance',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why the screw matters',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On a dowel and screw style spur, the gaff is located by a dowel and held tight by a screw. If that screw strips, stretches, or backs out, the gaff can move under load. A gaff that rocks or shifts on the shank is a serious problem and the spurs should not be climbed on until it is fixed.',
            1 => 'Keep a couple of Buckingham gaff screws with your spare gaffs so a worn one can be replaced at the shop rather than ignored. More parts are in our <a href="/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/">replacement gaffs and parts</a> section.',
            2 => 'Gaff screws also get replaced whenever you change gaffs. Reusing a worn screw on a fresh gaff is false economy. Check the threads in the shank too; damaged threads in the spur itself mean the spur needs attention from someone qualified before it goes back in service.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Installation and checks',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Use only the correct Buckingham part for your spurs and follow Buckingham\'s instructions for installation, including tightening and any thread-locking guidance. Do not substitute a hardware-store screw.',
            1 => 'Before every climb, check that each gaff is tight with no play, the screw head is undamaged, and the gaff itself is sharp, uncracked, and within its wear limit. Spur work belongs to trained climbers working under ANSI Z133, always with a flipline or lanyard in place.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a regular screw instead?',
          'a' => 'No. Use the correct Buckingham part. The wrong screw may not hold the gaff securely.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I know if a gaff screw needs replacing?',
          'a' => 'Replace it if the threads are damaged, the head is chewed up, or the gaff will not stay tight.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/buckingham/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/spur-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/spur-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Fall Protection Training, 2013',
        'creator' => 'Western Area Power Admin',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05/8813827776',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/buckingham-magnetic-gaff-cover/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/buckingham-magnetic-gaff-cover/',
      'category' => '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/',
      'name' => 'Buckingham Magnetic Gaff Cover',
      'brand' => 'buckingham',
      'brand_name' => 'Buckingham',
      'kind' => 'spur',
      'price' => 24,
      'keyword' => 'buckingham magnetic gaff cover',
      'title' => 'Buckingham Magnetic Gaff Cover for Climbing Spurs',
      'h1' => 'Buckingham Magnetic Gaff Cover',
      'meta' => 'The Buckingham magnetic gaff cover protects spur gaffs and the people around them. Why gaff guards matter, how a magnetic cover works, and daily care tips.',
      'tagline' => 'A magnetic cover that guards spur gaffs between climbs.',
      'summary' => 'The Buckingham magnetic gaff cover slips over a spur gaff and holds in place with a magnet, protecting the point and anyone handling the spurs. It goes on and off quickly, which makes it more likely to actually get used between climbs.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Holds on the gaff with a magnet',
        1 => 'Quick on and off between climbs',
        2 => 'Protects the sharpened point',
        3 => 'Protects hands, bags, and truck seats',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Gaff cover',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Retention',
          1 => 'Magnetic',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Maker',
          1 => 'Buckingham',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why cover your gaffs',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A sharp gaff is what keeps you in the tree, and it is also what cuts hands, tears gear bags, and punctures truck seats. Every time the spurs come off your legs, the gaffs should be covered. A dull or chipped gaff from rattling around the truck bed is a gaff that can cut out.',
            1 => 'A magnetic cover is quick to put on, so it gets used. Find other guards and spur parts in our <a href="/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/">replacement gaffs and parts</a> section.',
            2 => 'Covers also protect the gaffs from each other. Spurs packed in a bag together will knock their points against the opposite shank every time the truck hits a pothole. A quick magnetic cover on each gaff stops that and saves a lot of time at the sharpening stone. Keep a spare cover in the truck, since they tend to get left on stumps.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check that the cover suits your gaff style and length. Make sure the steel gaff seats fully in the cover before you pack the spurs away. Magnetic retention works on steel; confirm it holds well on your gaffs.',
            1 => 'Keep the covers clean of sap and grit, which can dull the point. Replace a cover that is cracked or no longer holds. Inspect gaffs for sharpness, cracks, and tightness before every climb, and do spur work only with training and under ANSI Z133.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does the magnetic gaff cover fit every spur?',
          'a' => 'Check it against your gaff style and length. Gaff shapes vary between makers and models.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Should I climb with gaff covers on?',
          'a' => 'No. Remove the covers before climbing and put them back on as soon as the spurs come off.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/buckingham/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/spur-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/spur-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Fall protection field trials',
        'creator' => 'Western Area Power Admin',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05/15137306027',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/buckingham-replacement-gaffs-dowel-style/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/buckingham-replacement-gaffs-dowel-style/',
      'category' => '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/',
      'name' => 'Buckingham Replacement Gaffs (Dowel Style)',
      'brand' => 'buckingham',
      'brand_name' => 'Buckingham',
      'kind' => 'spur',
      'price' => 59,
      'keyword' => 'buckingham replacement gaffs',
      'title' => 'Buckingham Replacement Gaffs, Dowel Style',
      'h1' => 'Buckingham Replacement Gaffs (Dowel Style)',
      'meta' => 'Buckingham replacement gaffs in dowel style for Buckingham climbing spurs. When to replace a gaff, sharpening basics, and what to check before every climb.',
      'tagline' => 'Dowel style replacement gaffs to keep Buckingham spurs biting.',
      'summary' => 'These Buckingham replacement gaffs fit Buckingham spurs that use the dowel style attachment. Gaffs wear down with sharpening and use, and swapping them is how you keep a good set of spurs biting cleanly.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Dowel style attachment for Buckingham spurs',
        1 => 'Restores bite on worn spurs',
        2 => 'Tree gaff profile for bark',
        3 => 'Replace when worn past the limit',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Buckingham spurs with dowel style gaffs',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Replacement tree gaffs',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Attachment',
          1 => 'Dowel style',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'When to replace gaffs',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Each time a gaff is sharpened, a little metal comes off. Eventually it is too short to reach through bark into solid wood, and that is when cut-outs happen. Replace gaffs when they are below the maker\'s minimum length, cracked, bent, or will not hold a proper edge and shape.',
            1 => 'Check your spurs are dowel style before ordering; Buckingham has used more than one attachment design. Screws, guards, and straps are in our <a href="/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/">replacement gaffs and parts</a> section.',
            2 => 'Look at both gaffs side by side when you inspect. If one is noticeably shorter or a different shape, the spurs will bite unevenly and feel unsteady. Replacing both at the same time keeps them matched and is the usual practice for working climbers.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Installing and maintaining',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Install gaffs following Buckingham\'s instructions using the correct screws, and make sure they seat fully with no play. Sharpen only the way the maker recommends, keeping the original profile. Grinding the wrong faces or overheating the steel changes how the gaff bites.',
            1 => 'Inspect gaffs before every climb and keep them covered when off your legs. Spur work should only be done by trained climbers on removals, with a flipline in place, under ANSI Z133.',
            2 => 'Keep the old gaffs out of the parts bin so nobody reinstalls them by mistake.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Are these gaffs sold as a pair?',
          'a' => 'Pack quantity varies by listing. Check the product details when you buy.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How should I sharpen tree gaffs?',
          'a' => 'Follow Buckingham\'s sharpening guidance and keep the factory profile. Do not file the inside face to a needle point.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/buckingham/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/spur-5.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/spur-5-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'TVCF003',
        'creator' => 'Symic',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00/33549423240',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/weaver-bungee-gaff-guards/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/weaver-bungee-gaff-guards/',
      'category' => '/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/',
      'name' => 'Weaver Bungee Gaff Guards',
      'brand' => 'weaver',
      'brand_name' => 'Weaver',
      'kind' => 'spur',
      'price' => 15,
      'keyword' => 'weaver bungee gaff guards',
      'title' => 'Weaver Bungee Gaff Guards for Climbing Spurs',
      'h1' => 'Weaver Bungee Gaff Guards',
      'meta' => 'Weaver bungee gaff guards cover sharp spur gaffs between climbs and hold with an elastic cord. Why gaff guards matter, fitting tips, and when to replace them.',
      'tagline' => 'Gaff guards with bungee retention to protect spurs between climbs.',
      'summary' => 'Weaver bungee gaff guards cover your spur gaffs and hold in place with an elastic bungee cord around the shank. They keep the points sharp and keep everyone around the spurs from getting cut.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Bungee cord holds the guard on the shank',
        1 => 'Protects gaff points in the truck',
        2 => 'Protects hands, bags, and seats',
        3 => 'Inexpensive spur care item',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Gaff guards',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Retention',
          1 => 'Bungee cord',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Maker',
          1 => 'Weaver',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Keep gaffs sharp and people safe',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A gaff knocks its point off fast if it rattles around a truck box with saws and wedges. A dull gaff skates on bark and leads to cut-outs. Guards protect the point between climbs and keep the gaff from slicing a gear bag or a hand reaching into one.',
            1 => 'The bungee keeps the guard on without fiddly buckles. Other guards and spur parts are in our <a href="/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/">replacement gaffs and parts</a> section.',
            2 => 'Guards also stop gaffs from wearing against each other and against the shanks when spurs are packed together in a bag. Every chip off the point is metal you will have to file away to get the shape back, which shortens the life of the gaff.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Seat the gaff fully in the guard and stretch the bungee around the shank so it cannot slip off. Check that the guard suits your gaff length and style.',
            1 => 'Replace the guard when the bungee loses its stretch or the guard is cut through. Remove guards before climbing, inspect gaffs before every climb, and follow ANSI Z133 for spur work.',
            2 => 'Make putting guards on part of the routine: spurs off, guards on, then they go into the bag. A spare pair in the truck covers the ones that inevitably go missing at the bottom of a tree.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Bungee or velcro gaff guards?',
          'a' => 'Bungee guards are fast to put on; velcro guards wrap snug and adjust. Both protect the point.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do these fit pole gaffs?',
          'a' => 'Check the guard size against your gaffs. Tree and pole gaffs differ in length and shape.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/weaver/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/spur-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/spur-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Wood pole ascent',
        'creator' => 'Western Area Power Admin',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/86561000@N05/16380468763',
