      'keyword' => 'weaver brahma webb lower climber straps',
      'title' => 'Weaver Brahma Webb Lower Climber Straps',
      'h1' => 'Weaver Brahma Webb Lower Climber Straps',
      'meta' => 'Weaver Brahma Webb lower climber straps hold spur stirrups snug to the boot with low-upkeep webbing. Fitting tips, care, and when to replace lower spur straps.',
      'tagline' => 'Webbing lower straps for spurs that handle wet days without much care.',
      'summary' => 'Weaver Brahma Webb lower climber straps are webbing replacement ankle straps for climbing spurs. Webbing shrugs off rain and mud better than leather and needs less upkeep, while still holding the stirrup snug to your boot.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Webbing lower (ankle) straps',
        1 => 'Handles wet conditions with little upkeep',
        2 => 'Holds the stirrup snug under the arch',
        3 => 'Replacement for worn lower straps',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Lower climber straps',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Webbing',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Line',
          1 => 'Weaver Brahma Webb',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why webbing lower straps',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Lower straps live in the wettest, dirtiest spot on your spurs: down at the boot, in mud, wet grass, and sawdust. Webbing does not need conditioning, dries quickly, and does not crack the way neglected leather can. For crews working in wet climates or climbers who do not want to baby their gear, webbing is the practical choice.',
            1 => 'Confirm the strap suits your spur layout before ordering. Upper straps, gaffs, and guards are in our <a href="/climbing/spurs/replacement-gaffs-and-parts/">replacement gaffs and parts</a> section.',
            2 => 'Webbing straps are also a good match for climbers who work storm cleanups and wet-season removals, when gear rarely gets a chance to dry out between jobs. Replace both lower straps at once so they cinch the same on each leg, and keep a spare set in the truck for when one gets cut by a gaff or a chain.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Route the strap across the boot as your spur maker intends and cinch it so the stirrup cannot shift. Wear boots with a solid heel and shank for spur work.',
            1 => 'Inspect the webbing and buckles before every climb. Replace straps that are cut, frayed, melted, or have damaged hardware. Spur work belongs on removals, done by trained climbers with a flipline in place, following ANSI Z133.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will webbing straps stretch when wet?',
          'a' => 'Webbing holds up well in wet conditions. Re-check tightness during the day either way.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I clean webbing spur straps?',
          'a' => 'Rinse off mud and sap with water and mild soap and let them air dry.',
        ),
      ),
