      'title' => 'Petzl Airline Throwline for Arborists',
      'h1' => 'Petzl Airline Throwline',
      'meta' => 'Petzl Airline is a thin, low-friction throwline for setting climbing and rigging lines. How it handles in the canopy, storage, and why it is never for climbing.',
      'tagline' => 'A thin, slick throwline from Petzl for setting climbing lines.',
      'summary' => 'Petzl Airline is a thin, slick throwline made for arborists to set climbing and rigging lines in the canopy. Its smooth finish lets it slide over bark and through unions with little friction, and it pairs with Petzl\'s Jet throw weights or any common throw bag.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Thin, low-friction throwline',
        1 => 'Slides easily over bark and unions',
        2 => 'Works with Petzl Jet and other throw weights',
        3 => 'For setting lines only, not for climbing',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Throwline',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Setting climbing and rigging lines',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Maker',
          1 => 'Petzl',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How Airline handles',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A good throwline gets out of the way: it pays out without tangles, slides over bark so you can walk the line where you want it, and pulls a climbing line into place without sawing at the tree. Airline is thin and slick, which keeps friction down and lets a moderate throw bag gain height.',
            1 => 'Pair it with a throw weight that suits the canopy and store it in a cube. Other lines and kits are in our <a href="/climbing/throw-weight-and-line/throw-line/">throw line</a> section.',
            2 => 'Once the line is over a limb, the slick finish also helps when you need to isolate a union. You can flip the line along a branch, pull the bag up to a lower crotch, or walk it around to a better tie-in point without fighting friction. That saves throws, and saves your arm on a long day of setting lines for climbs and rigging.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the drop zone before every throw and keep well clear of power lines, following ANSI Z133 approach distances. Watch the bag on the way down.',
            1 => 'Stuff the line loosely into its storage bag after each use. Inspect it for flat spots, fused areas, and abrasion, and replace it once it starts to break. Throwline is never for climbing or for holding a load.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use Airline with a non-Petzl throw bag?',
          'a' => 'Yes. It works with common throw weights; tie it on with a secure knot.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why does a thin throwline help?',
          'a' => 'Less diameter means less friction over bark and through unions, so the bag gets more height and the line is easier to pull through.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/throw-line-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/throw-line-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'TVCF002',
        'creator' => 'Symic',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00/33804504181',
      ),
    ),
    '/climbing/throw-weight-and-line/throw-line/weaver-12oz-throw-weight-and-line-kit/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/throw-weight-and-line/throw-line/weaver-12oz-throw-weight-and-line-kit/',
      'category' => '/climbing/throw-weight-and-line/throw-line/',
      'name' => 'Weaver 12 oz Throw Weight and Line Kit',
      'brand' => 'weaver',
      'brand_name' => 'Weaver',
      'kind' => 'throw-line',
      'price' => 35,
      'keyword' => 'weaver 12 oz throw weight',
      'title' => 'Weaver 12 oz Throw Weight and Line Kit',
      'h1' => 'Weaver 12 oz Throw Weight and Line Kit',
      'meta' => 'The Weaver 12 oz throw weight and line kit sets climbing lines and rigging points. Why 12 oz is a good all-round bag, throwing tips, and caring for the line.',
      'tagline' => 'A 12 oz throw bag and line, a good all-round choice for most trees.',
      'summary' => 'The Weaver 12 oz throw weight and line kit gives you a mid-weight throw bag and throw line for setting climbing lines and rigging points from the ground. A 12 oz bag is a good all-round weight: light enough to throw high, heavy enough to come back down through most canopies.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '12 oz throw weight',
        1 => 'Throw line included',
        2 => 'All-round weight for most trees',
        3 => 'Sets climbing and rigging lines from the ground',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Throw weight',
          1 => '12 oz',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Throw weight and line kit',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Maker',
          1 => 'Weaver',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why 12 oz is a good starting weight',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'If you only carry one throw bag, a mid-weight is the sensible pick. It flies high enough for most residential and park trees, and it has enough mass to pull line back down through moderate foliage and over a rough union. Lighter bags go higher but hang up easier; heavier ones punch through but take more effort.',
            1 => 'Climbers who work big, dense trees often add a heavier bag later. See other bags and lines in our <a href="/climbing/throw-weight-and-line/throw-line/">throw line</a> section.',
            2 => 'For a first kit, a mid-weight bag is also forgiving while you build your technique. It does not punish a sloppy throw as much as a heavy bag, and it is easier to retrieve when the bag lands in a crotch you did not intend. Practice in an open area before throwing over a work tree.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Throwing and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the drop zone, call your throw, and keep well away from power lines according to ANSI Z133. Throw with a smooth underhand swing and watch the bag all the way down.',
            1 => 'Store the line in a bag or cube so it pays out cleanly. Inspect it for nicks and fused spots and replace it when worn. Throw line is only for pulling up a climbing or rigging line, never for climbing or rigging loads.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is 12 oz good for a first throw bag?',
          'a' => 'Yes. It is a balanced weight that suits most trees and is easy to learn on.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I keep throw line from tangling?',
          'a' => 'Stuff it loosely into a cube or bag rather than coiling it, and let it pay out from the bag when you throw.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'beginner',
      'brand_path' => '/weaver/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/throw-line-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/throw-line-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'coiling',
        'creator' => 'michael pollak',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06/6796140986',
      ),
    ),
    '/clothing/arborist-boots/orange-laces-for-scafell-lite-boots/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/clothing/arborist-boots/orange-laces-for-scafell-lite-boots/',
      'category' => '/clothing/arborist-boots/',
      'name' => 'Orange Laces for Scafell Lite Boots',
      'brand' => 'arbortec',
      'brand_name' => 'Arbortec',
      'kind' => 'boot',
      'price' => 12,
      'keyword' => 'laces for scafell lite boots',
      'title' => 'Orange Replacement Laces for Scafell Lite Boots',
      'h1' => 'Orange Laces for Scafell Lite Boots',
      'meta' => 'Orange replacement laces for Scafell Lite boots from Arbortec. Why to replace worn laces before they snap, lacing tips for a snug fit, and boot care basics.',
      'tagline' => 'Orange replacement laces for Arbortec Scafell Lite chainsaw boots.',
      'summary' => 'These orange laces for Scafell Lite boots replace worn laces on Arbortec\'s Scafell Lite chainsaw boots. Laces wear through on hooks and eyelets long before a good boot is finished, and a fresh pair keeps the boot snug on the foot where it belongs.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Replacement laces for Scafell Lite boots',
        1 => 'High-visibility orange',
        2 => 'Keep a spare pair in the truck',
        3 => 'Cheap fix that keeps boots fitting right',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Arbortec Scafell Lite boots',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Color',
          1 => 'Orange',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Replacement boot laces',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why replace laces early',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Boot laces wear at the hooks and eyelets, and in a chainsaw boot they also pick up sawdust, mud, and bar oil that grind at the fibers. A lace that snaps halfway through a climb or while you are bucking leaves the boot loose on your foot, which is exactly when ankles roll and spurs shift.',
            1 => 'Swapping laces when they start to fuzz is cheap and quick. Keep a spare pair in the truck. More footwear is in our <a href="/clothing/arborist-boots/">arborist boots</a> section.',
            2 => 'Orange is also easy to spot against mud.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Lacing and boot care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Lace the boot firmly through the instep so your heel stays seated, and tuck or trim the ends so they cannot catch on brush, spur straps, or a chain. A well-laced boot is part of how a chainsaw boot and spurs work as designed.',
            1 => 'Clean mud and sawdust off your boots at the end of the day and let them dry away from direct heat. Check the uppers, the cut-protection areas, and the soles for damage, and follow the boot maker\'s guidance on when to replace them.',
            2 => 'Re-check your laces at lunch. Lacing loosens as boots flex and warm up, and a quick re-tie keeps your heel locked in for the afternoon climb.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will these laces fit other boots?',
          'a' => 'They are sold for Scafell Lite boots. They may fit similar boots, but check the length against your current laces.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why orange laces?',
          'a' => 'They match the Scafell Lite\'s look and are easy to spot, which helps when you are checking that your boots are laced and tucked.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'apparel',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/arbortec/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/boot-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/boot-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Old Boot, New Boot',
        'creator' => 'General Wesc',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/35518575@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/35518575@N00/5198346357',
      ),
    ),
    '/clothing/sweatshirts-and-jackets/arbortec-black-large-breathedry-waterproof-smock/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/clothing/sweatshirts-and-jackets/arbortec-black-large-breathedry-waterproof-smock/',
      'category' => '/clothing/sweatshirts-and-jackets/',
      'name' => 'Arbortec BreatheDry Waterproof Smock (Black, Large)',
      'brand' => 'arbortec',
      'brand_name' => 'Arbortec',
      'kind' => 'apparel',
      'price' => 109,
      'keyword' => 'arbortec breathedry waterproof smock',
      'title' => 'Arbortec BreatheDry Waterproof Smock, Black',
      'h1' => 'Arbortec BreatheDry Waterproof Smock (Black, Large)',
      'meta' => 'The Arbortec BreatheDry waterproof smock is a breathable pullover for wet days in the tree. Why a smock suits climbers, how to fit it, and how to care for it.',
      'tagline' => 'A waterproof, breathable pullover smock for climbing and groundwork in the rain.',
      'summary' => 'The Arbortec BreatheDry waterproof smock is a breathable pullover jacket for tree work in wet weather, shown here in black, size large. A smock has no full-length front zip, so there is less to snag and nothing to fail when you are climbing, rigging, or dragging brush in the rain.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Waterproof, breathable BreatheDry fabric',
        1 => 'Pullover smock design with no full front zip',
        2 => 'Less to snag on branches and gear',
        3 => 'Black, size large',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Waterproof smock',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fabric',
          1 => 'Arbortec BreatheDry',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Color',
          1 => 'Black',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Size',
          1 => 'Large',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why climbers like a smock',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A full-zip jacket under a saddle is awkward: the zip bunches under the waist belt, the hem catches on hardware, and the front can work open. A smock pulls over your head, sits cleanly under the saddle, and has less to snag on branches. Breathable fabric matters too, because a fully sealed jacket soaks you in sweat by the second climb.',
            1 => 'It works just as well on the ground for chipping, dragging brush, and running ropes in a downpour. See more jackets and layers in our <a href="/clothing/sweatshirts-and-jackets/">sweatshirts and jackets</a> section.',
            2 => 'Black hides sap and bar oil better than lighter colors, but it is not high-visibility. On road jobs or busy sites, add the high-visibility garment the job requires.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the size against your usual layers; you want room for a fleece underneath and enough arm length to reach overhead without the hem lifting. Make sure the smock does not cover or interfere with your saddle\'s attachment points.',
            1 => 'Wash it following the care label, using a cleaner suited to waterproof fabrics rather than standard detergent, and reproof it when water stops beading on the surface. Keep it away from bar oil and fuel where you can, as they can affect the coating.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I wear the smock over a climbing saddle?',
          'a' => 'Most climbers wear it under the saddle. Make sure it does not cover or interfere with your attachment points.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I keep it waterproof?',
          'a' => 'Wash with a technical fabric cleaner and reproof it when water stops beading, following the care label.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'apparel',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/arbortec/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/apparel-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/apparel-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Great flannel shirts for women are hard to come by. But flannel is our signature, inspired by top-notch menswear shirts - especially designed and tailored for you. Take up to 20% off this weekend on all flannels, including the Park City flannel pictured h',
        'creator' => 'Tradlands',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/87743767@N07',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/87743767@N07/24785884538',
      ),
    ),
    '/clothing/sweatshirts-and-jackets/arborwear-blacksafety-yellow-tech-double-thick-pullover-sweatshirt/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/clothing/sweatshirts-and-jackets/arborwear-blacksafety-yellow-tech-double-thick-pullover-sweatshirt/',
      'category' => '/clothing/sweatshirts-and-jackets/',
      'name' => 'Arborwear Tech Double Thick Pullover Sweatshirt (Black/Safety Yellow)',
      'brand' => 'arborwear',
      'brand_name' => 'Arborwear',
      'kind' => 'apparel',
      'price' => 85,
      'keyword' => 'arborwear tech double thick pullover sweatshirt',
      'title' => 'Arborwear Tech Double Thick Pullover Sweatshirt',
      'h1' => 'Arborwear Tech Double Thick Pullover Sweatshirt',
      'meta' => 'The Arborwear Tech Double Thick pullover sweatshirt in black and safety yellow. A warm, high-visibility layer for cold mornings on the ground and in the tree.',
      'tagline' => 'A heavy, two-tone pullover for cold days on the crew, in black and safety yellow.',
      'summary' => 'The Arborwear Tech Double Thick pullover sweatshirt is a heavyweight work sweatshirt built for tree crews, here in black with safety yellow. It is the layer you reach for on cold mornings, warm enough to work in and bright enough to be seen on the ground.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Heavy double-thick construction for warmth',
        1 => 'Black and safety yellow color scheme',
        2 => 'Pullover design with nothing to snag',
        3 => 'Built for work, not the couch',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Pullover sweatshirt',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Color',
          1 => 'Black/safety yellow',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Maker',
          1 => 'Arborwear',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'A working layer for cold days',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Fall cleanups and winter removals start cold. A heavy pullover keeps your core warm while you set up, run the chipper, or wait for the crane, and it holds up to brush, bark, and the rub of a saddle better than a light hoodie. The safety yellow panels add visibility for ground workers near traffic and equipment.',
            1 => 'Layer it under a shell when it rains and peel it off once you are working hard. More layers are in our <a href="/clothing/sweatshirts-and-jackets/">sweatshirts and jackets</a> section.',
            2 => 'A pullover also has no zipper to snag on bark or gear, and no open front to flap around while you work. It stays put under a saddle or a chainsaw chap harness and does not bunch the way a hoodie can.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit and safety notes',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pick a size that lets you reach overhead and lean back in a saddle without the hem riding up. Loose cuffs and drawstrings can catch in a chipper or a rope system, so keep them tucked and never wear loose clothing near chippers or stump grinders.',
            1 => 'A bright sweatshirt helps, but it is not a substitute for ANSI-rated high-visibility apparel where a job, road work zone, or employer requires it. Wash it per the care label to keep the colors bright.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is this sweatshirt a hi-vis garment?',
          'a' => 'It adds visibility, but do not treat it as rated high-visibility apparel unless the label states a certification.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I climb in it?',
          'a' => 'Yes, as a warm layer. Keep it from interfering with your saddle and keep cuffs and hems tidy.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'apparel',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/arborwear/',
      'photo' => 
