      'title' => 'Greenteeth Stump Grinder Bolt Replacement',
      'h1' => 'Greenteeth Stump Grinder Bolt',
      'meta' => 'A replacement Greenteeth stump grinder bolt for holding pockets and teeth on the cutter wheel. Why worn bolts are a hazard and how to check them every day.',
      'tagline' => 'A replacement bolt for Greenteeth pockets and teeth on a stump grinder wheel.',
      'summary' => 'This Greenteeth stump grinder bolt secures the teeth and pockets on a Greenteeth cutter wheel. Bolts take a beating from rocks and vibration, and a worn or stretched one can let a tooth come loose.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Replacement Greenteeth bolt',
        1 => 'Secures teeth and pockets to the wheel',
        2 => 'Replace worn or damaged bolts promptly',
        3 => 'Good to stock as spares',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Green Manufacturing',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Part type',
          1 => 'Stump grinder bolt',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'System',
          1 => 'Greenteeth (confirm series)',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why bolts matter',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The wheel on a stump grinder spins fast and hits wood, dirt, and stones. A tooth thrown from the wheel is dangerous. Bolts with worn heads, damaged threads, or signs of stretching should be replaced, not reused.',
            1 => 'Match bolts to your Greenteeth series. More teeth and parts are in our <a href="/jobsite/stump-grinding/">stump grinding</a> section.',
            2 => 'Bolts also wear at the head from contact with dirt and debris. A rounded or worn head can make removal difficult and is a sign the bolt has reached the end of its life.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Daily checks',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'With the machine off and the wheel stopped, inspect bolts and nuts and torque them to Green Manufacturing\'s spec. Check again after hitting a rock.',
            1 => 'Keep the work area clear, use the grinder\'s guards and a debris shield, and wear eye, face, and hearing protection.',
            2 => 'Replace bolts when you replace teeth or pockets, or sooner if they show wear. Keep spares in the truck so you don\'t have to stop the job. Use the right socket so you don\'t round off the heads. Clean the threads before reassembly and use new nuts where the manufacturer calls for them. If a bolt is hard to remove, don\'t force it with heat near the wheel; follow the maker\'s advice.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I reuse old bolts?',
          'a' => 'Replace any bolt that is worn, bent, or damaged. Follow Green Manufacturing\'s guidance on replacement.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How tight should they be?',
          'a' => 'Use the torque spec from Green Manufacturing for your system.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/green-manufacturing/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Route Clearance',
        'creator' => 'Georgia National Guard',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40994485@N04',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/40994485@N04/33581236171',
      ),
    ),
    '/jobsite/stump-grinding/stein-modular-guard-system-replacement-panel/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/jobsite/stump-grinding/stein-modular-guard-system-replacement-panel/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/stump-grinding/',
      'name' => 'Stein Modular Guard System Replacement Panel',
      'brand' => 'stein',
      'brand_name' => 'Stein',
      'kind' => 'jobsite',
      'price' => 150,
      'keyword' => 'stein modular guard system',
      'title' => 'Stein Modular Guard System Replacement Panel',
      'h1' => 'Stein Modular Guard System Replacement Panel',
      'meta' => 'A replacement panel for the Stein modular guard system, a portable debris screen for stump grinding. Replace damaged panels to keep chips and stones contained.',
      'tagline' => 'A replacement screen panel for Stein\'s portable stump grinding guard.',
      'summary' => 'This replacement panel fits the Stein modular guard system, a portable screen set up around a stump grinding job to help contain flying chips and stones. Replace a damaged panel to keep the barrier complete.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Replacement panel for the Stein modular guard',
        1 => 'Helps contain debris from stump grinding',
        2 => 'Swap out a damaged panel without replacing the set',
        3 => 'Portable, modular setup',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Stein',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Guard / debris screen panel',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'System',
          1 => 'Stein modular guard system',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why use a debris guard',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Stump grinders throw chips, dirt, and stones a long way. Near houses, cars, windows, and sidewalks, a portable guard set up around the stump adds a barrier on top of the machine\'s own guards.',
            1 => 'A modular system lets you shape the screen to the job and replace one panel when it gets damaged. See more in our <a href="/jobsite/stump-grinding/">stump grinding</a> section.',
            2 => 'Guards are especially useful on tight residential lots, near glass, and along driveways and roads. A full screen reduces the chance of a thrown stone hitting a window or a car.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Setup and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Set panels on the side where people, property, or traffic are at risk, and keep the screen stable. Inspect panels for tears, holes, or bent frames, and replace any that are damaged.',
            1 => 'A guard does not replace an exclusion zone. Keep bystanders clear, and wear eye, face, and hearing protection when grinding.',
            2 => 'Store panels flat or stacked so the frames don\'t get bent in the truck. A complete, undamaged guard is much more effective than one with a missing panel. Check panel connections and stakes before each job, and replace any panel with a torn screen. Panels are lighter and easier to handle than a full sheet of plywood, and they set up quickly on uneven ground.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I buy one panel instead of a full set?',
          'a' => 'Yes. This listing is a single replacement panel for an existing Stein system.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does a guard mean bystanders can stand close?',
          'a' => 'No. Keep a clear zone around the grinder at all times.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/stein/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/jobsite-8.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/jobsite-8-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Chipper',
        'creator' => 'photofarmer',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62528187@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62528187@N00/6941971832',
      ),
    ),
    '/light-duty-slings/weaver-loop-runners/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/light-duty-slings/weaver-loop-runners/',
      'category' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/light-duty-slings/',
      'name' => 'Weaver Loop Runners',
      'brand' => 'weaver',
      'brand_name' => 'Weaver',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'price' => 19,
      'keyword' => 'weaver loop runners',
      'title' => 'Weaver Loop Runners: Light-Duty Webbing Slings',
      'h1' => 'Weaver Loop Runners',
      'meta' => 'Weaver loop runners are sewn webbing loops for light rigging, gear anchors, and redirects. How to hitch them, where they fit, and how to inspect the webbing.',
      'tagline' => 'Sewn webbing loops for light rigging points, redirects, and gear anchors.',
      'summary' => 'Weaver loop runners are sewn, endless webbing slings for light-duty work: hanging a small block, setting a redirect, or girth-hitching an anchor for gear. They pack small, cost little, and cover a lot of the odd jobs a crew runs into on a pruning or light removal day.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Sewn endless loop of flat webbing',
        1 => 'Girth, choke, or basket around a limb',
        2 => 'Light and compact on the saddle or in the bag',
        3 => 'Handy for redirects and small rigging points',
        4 => 'Inexpensive enough to keep several lengths',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Sewn webbing loop sling',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Weaver',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Duty',
          1 => 'Light-duty rigging and anchors',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rating',
          1 => 'Marked on the sling; varies by length and width',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where loop runners earn their place',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A loop runner is the sling you grab when the job is small. Girth-hitch one around a limb to hang a light pulley for a lowering line on pruning cuts, or to set a quick redirect that keeps your rope out of a crotch. On the ground it works as a temporary anchor around a stem or a truck hitch point for a tag line.',
            1 => 'Because they are flat webbing, runners sit close to the bark and are easy to untie and pull down. Keep a couple of lengths in the rigging bag so you are not tying up a long rope sling for a two-minute task. Browse the rest of our <a href="/rigging/rigging-slings/">rigging slings</a> when the load gets heavier.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Limits and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Light-duty means light-duty. Webbing runners are not a substitute for a rated rope sling or round sling under a shock-loaded rigging block, and a choked or girth-hitched sling holds less than one pulled straight. Read the marking and size the sling for the real load.',
            1 => 'Before each use, check for cuts, abrasion, glazing from heat, chemical stains, and broken stitching at the sewn joint. Keep runners away from the saw and sharp edges. If a runner is ever used as part of a climbing system, life-support use must follow ANSI Z133, the manufacturer\'s instructions, and your training.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a Weaver loop runner for rigging logs?',
          'a' => 'Only small, controlled pieces within its marked rating. For blocking down wood or any shock load, use a rope or round sling sized for the job.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How should I attach a loop runner to a limb?',
          'a' => 'A girth hitch or a basket around the limb is common. Remember that a girth or choke hitch reduces the sling\'s capacity compared with a straight pull.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/weaver/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'D75_8819-1',
        'creator' => 'Mark F. Levisay',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/31395633@N08',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/31395633@N08/52706266887',
      ),
    ),
    '/micro-pulleys/cmi-rna-pulley/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/micro-pulleys/cmi-rna-pulley/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/micro-pulleys/',
      'name' => 'CMI RNA Pulley',
      'brand' => 'cmi',
      'brand_name' => 'CMI',
      'kind' => 'pulley',
      'price' => 49,
      'keyword' => 'cmi rna pulley',
      'title' => 'CMI RNA Pulley: Micro Pulley for Climbing Systems',
      'h1' => 'CMI RNA Pulley',
      'meta' => 'The CMI RNA pulley is a compact micro pulley for hitch tending and small haul systems. Where it fits in a climbing setup, what to check, and how to care for it.',
      'tagline' => 'A compact CMI micro pulley for hitch tending and small rope systems.',
      'summary' => 'The CMI RNA pulley is a small, light micro pulley built for rope access style systems, and tree climbers use it as a hitch tender and in small mechanical advantage setups. It clips onto a carabiner below your friction hitch and keeps slack moving through as you climb.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Compact micro pulley from CMI',
        1 => 'Tends a friction hitch so slack feeds smoothly',
        2 => 'Useful for small haul and redirect setups',
        3 => 'Light enough to live on the saddle',
        4 => 'Made by an established US rope-hardware maker',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Micro pulley',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'CMI',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Typical uses',
          1 => 'Hitch tending, small haul systems, redirects',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rating and rope range',
          1 => 'Marked on the side plate',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How climbers use a micro pulley',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On a moving rope system the most common job for a micro pulley is tending the hitch. The pulley rides on the bridge carabiner below a Distel or VT, and as you pull slack up through the system the sheave pushes the hitch up the rope. It saves you reaching down to advance the knot on every pull and makes footlocking or body-thrusting smoother.',
            1 => 'The same pulley earns its keep off the saddle too: a small redirect, a pulley on a tag line, or the moving pulley in a light-duty haul. See our other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/micro-pulleys/">micro pulleys</a> to compare size and weight.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit, inspection, and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the side-plate marking for the rope diameter range and rated strength, and make sure your hitch cord and climbing line fall inside it. A rope that is too big for the sheave rubs the side plates and loses efficiency.',
            1 => 'Before each climb, spin the sheave and listen for grinding, look for grooves worn into the sheave or plates, and check that the side plates close fully on the axle. Keep it clean of sap and grit. Any pulley in your life-support system has to be inspected before use, and life-support use must follow ANSI Z133, the manufacturer\'s instructions, and your training.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a micro pulley required for hitch climbing?',
          'a' => 'No, but most climbers use one. It tends the hitch so you do not have to push it up the rope by hand, which makes advancing faster and smoother.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will the CMI RNA pulley work with my hitch cord?',
          'a' => 'Check the rope range on the side plate against your hitch cord and climbing line diameter before you put it into service.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/cmi/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Block and tackle',
        'creator' => 'quinet',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/91994044@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/91994044@N00/14200854012',
      ),
    ),
    '/other-gear/notch-rc-grapple/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/other-gear/notch-rc-grapple/',
      'category' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/other-gear/',
      'name' => 'Notch RC Grapple',
      'brand' => 'notch-equipment',
      'brand_name' => 'Notch',
      'kind' => 'throw-line',
      'price' => 39,
      'keyword' => 'notch rc grapple',
      'title' => 'Notch RC Grapple: Line Retrieval Hook for Arborists',
      'h1' => 'Notch RC Grapple',
      'meta' => 'The Notch RC Grapple is a small grapple for snagging and retrieving lines, lanyards, and hung gear in the canopy. How crews use it and what to watch for.',
      'tagline' => 'A small grapple for snagging stuck lines and hung gear out of the canopy.',
      'summary' => 'The Notch RC Grapple is a compact retrieval grapple that tree crews use to snag a stuck throw line, a dropped lanyard tail, or a rope that will not come back down. Tie it to a light line, toss or lower it, and hook what you need instead of sending a climber up for it.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Compact grapple for retrieving lines and gear',
        1 => 'Ties to throw line or light cord',
        2 => 'Saves a climb to fetch a hung line',
        3 => 'Small enough to keep in the throw-line bag',
        4 => 'From Notch, a long-time arborist gear brand',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Retrieval grapple',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Notch',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Snagging and retrieving lines and gear',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Not for',
          1 => 'Life support or anchoring a climber',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What a grapple is for',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Every crew has watched a throw line wrap itself around a stub, or a rope tail drape over a limb just out of reach. A small grapple tied to a length of throw line lets you go after it from the ground or from your position in the tree. Swing or toss the grapple past the stuck line, let it settle, and pull back slowly so the tines catch.',
            1 => 'It also helps when you are retrieving a false crotch setup or pulling a tag line back across a gap. Keep it in the throw-line bag with your other <a href="/climbing/climbing-gear/other-gear/">climbing extras</a> so it is there when you need it.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use it carefully',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A grapple is a retrieval tool only. Never use it as an anchor, never clip it into a climbing or rigging system, and never put a person\'s weight on it. Make sure the drop zone is clear before you throw, because a grapple that pulls free comes back down hard.',
            1 => 'Pull steadily rather than yanking, so you do not snap a limb or set the hooks into something you cannot release. Check the tines and the tie-in point for bending or cracks, and replace the cord you tie it to when it gets worn.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I anchor a rope with the Notch RC Grapple?',
          'a' => 'No. It is a retrieval tool for snagging lines and gear and must never be used for climbing, rigging, or any load-bearing anchor.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What line should I tie the grapple to?',
          'a' => 'A spare throw line or light cord works well. Use something you can afford to cut away if the grapple gets stuck.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/notch-equipment/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/throw-line-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/throw-line-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Surgeon',
        'creator' => 'Tim Sheerman-Chase',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/68932647@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/68932647@N00/7738607258',
      ),
    ),
    '/parts-and-accessories/husqvarna-545-ii-550xp-ii-saw-tune-up-kit/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/parts-and-accessories/husqvarna-545-ii-550xp-ii-saw-tune-up-kit/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna 545 Mark II / 550 XP Mark II Tune-Up Kit',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 34,
      'keyword' => '550 xp mark ii tune-up kit',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna 550 XP Mark II Tune-Up Kit (Also 545 II)',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna 545 Mark II / 550 XP Mark II Tune-Up Kit',
      'meta' => 'A Husqvarna 550 XP Mark II tune-up kit that also fits the 545 Mark II. What a tune-up covers, when to do it, and how it keeps a ground saw cutting hard.',
      'tagline' => 'Service parts for a routine tune-up on the 545 Mark II and 550 XP Mark II saws.',
      'summary' => 'A Husqvarna 550 XP Mark II tune-up kit puts the common wear parts for a routine service in one package, and it fits the 545 Mark II as well. It is the easy way to bring a hard-working ground saw back to clean starts and full power after a season of bucking and limbing.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Matched to the 545 Mark II and 550 XP Mark II',
        1 => 'Groups routine wear parts in one order',
        2 => 'Restores easy starting and clean running',
        3 => 'Good annual or seasonal service item',
        4 => 'Keeps a spare on the truck for the busy months',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Husqvarna 545 Mark II, 550 XP Mark II',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Tune-up / service kit',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Contents',
          1 => 'Check the listing for the exact parts included',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why tune up a ground saw',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The 545 Mark II and 550 XP Mark II are workhorse 50cc-class saws for limbing, bucking, and small felling. They run on AutoTune, which adjusts the mixture on its own, but it can only work with what it is given. A choked air filter, a tired spark plug, or a dirty fuel filter will still cost you power and make the saw hard to start.',
            1 => 'Replacing those wear parts together is quick and cheap compared with the downtime of a saw that will not start at the chipper. Many crews do it at the start of the season and again whenever a saw has been run hard in dusty wood. Find more service parts in our <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/">power equipment parts</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Doing the service',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Work on a cool saw with the switch off and the chain brake on. Clean around the covers before you open them so grit does not fall into the intake. Fit new parts as the operator\'s manual describes, gap and torque the plug to spec, and use fresh, correctly mixed fuel afterward.',
            1 => 'Compare the kit contents with your saw\'s parts list before you start, and if the saw still runs poorly after a tune-up, have a dealer check it rather than chasing settings yourself.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does this kit fit the 545 Mark II and the 550 XP Mark II?',
          'a' => 'Yes, it is listed for both. Always confirm the parts against your saw\'s manual or model plate before installing.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I tune up my 550 XP Mark II?',
          'a' => 'Follow the manual\'s maintenance schedule. Most working crews clean the air filter often and replace the wear parts at least once a season.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'maintenance',
        1 => 'felling',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Skoda Fabia Inlet Manifold',
        'creator' => 'L.C.Nøttaasen',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/35166455@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/35166455@N00/4717400798',
      ),
    ),
    '/parts/husqvarna-belt-for-k770-cut-off-saw/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/parts/husqvarna-belt-for-k770-cut-off-saw/',
      'category' => '/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/',
      'name' => 'Husqvarna Drive Belt for K770 Cut-Off Saw',
      'brand' => 'husqvarna',
      'brand_name' => 'Husqvarna',
      'kind' => 'parts',
      'price' => 29,
      'keyword' => 'husqvarna k770 belt',
      'title' => 'Husqvarna K770 Belt: Replacement Cut-Off Saw Drive Belt',
      'h1' => 'Husqvarna K770 Belt for Cut-Off Saw',
      'meta' => 'Replacement Husqvarna K770 belt for the K770 power cutter. Signs a drive belt is worn, how to fit and tension it, and why to keep a spare in the truck box.',
      'tagline' => 'A replacement drive belt to keep a Husqvarna K770 power cutter on the job.',
      'summary' => 'The Husqvarna K770 belt is the replacement drive belt for the K770 cut-off saw, the power cutter many crews carry for cutting cable, bolts, and hardware on removals and storm work. A worn belt slips and stalls the blade, so a spare in the truck box keeps the saw working.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Replacement drive belt for the K770',
        1 => 'Restores full blade speed under load',
        2 => 'Small, cheap spare that prevents downtime',
        3 => 'Swap in the field with basic tools',
        4 => 'Genuine fit for the cutting arm',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Husqvarna K770 cut-off saw',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Drive belt',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Husqvarna',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'When the belt needs replacing',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A power cutter\'s belt does a lot of work. Signs it is due: the blade slows or stops in the cut while the engine keeps revving, you smell hot rubber, or you can see cracks, glazing, fraying, or missing ribs on the belt. Running a slipping belt only builds heat and wears the pulleys.',
            1 => 'On tree jobs the K770 tends to come out for cutting old cable-and-bracing hardware, embedded bolts, or chain, so it may sit for weeks between uses. Check the belt before you need it, not when you are standing at the stump. More replacement parts are in our <a href="/jobsite/power-equipment/parts/">power equipment parts</a> section.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fitting and tension',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'With the engine off and the blade removed or guarded, remove the belt guard, release the tension, and slip the old belt off. Fit the new belt, set tension as the operator\'s manual describes, and refit the guard before you start the saw.',
            1 => 'Run the saw briefly, then recheck tension, because a new belt seats in. Wear eye, hearing, and respiratory protection when cutting, and keep the blade guard set to throw sparks and debris away from you and the crew.',
            2 => 'Store the spare belt flat in its packaging, out of sun and away from fuel, so it is in good shape when you finally need it.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will this belt fit other Husqvarna cut-off saws?',
          'a' => 'It is listed for the K770. For other models, check the parts list for your saw before ordering.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why does my K770 blade stop while the engine still runs?',
          'a' => 'A slipping or loose belt is the most common cause. Check tension and belt condition first.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'maintenance',
        1 => 'ground',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/husqvarna/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/parts-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/parts-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Sharpening Chainsaw Blades',
        'creator' => 'NCDOTcommunications',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03/36914543386',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/9-16-ship-auger-bit/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/9-16-ship-auger-bit/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-accessories/',
      'name' => '9/16" Ship Auger Bit',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'power-tool',
      'price' => 39,
      'keyword' => 'ship auger bit',
      'title' => '9/16 Ship Auger Bit for Tree Cabling and Bracing',
      'h1' => '9/16" Ship Auger Bit',
      'meta' => 'A 9/16 ship auger bit for drilling clean through-holes for cabling and bracing hardware. Why a ship auger suits green wood, sizing tips, and drilling advice.',
      'tagline' => 'A long ship auger bit for drilling clean holes for tree cabling hardware.',
      'summary' => 'A 9/16 ship auger bit is the drill bit arborists reach for when installing cabling and bracing hardware, because its single spur and open flute clear green, wet wood without binding. The 9/16 inch size is a common match for 1/2 inch threaded rod and eye bolts.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '9/16 inch diameter for 1/2 inch rod hardware',
        1 => 'Single-spur ship auger pattern',
        2 => 'Open flute clears wet chips',
        3 => 'Long reach for through-bolting',
        4 => 'Fits a heavy-duty drill',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '9/16 in',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Ship auger bit',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Common pairing',
          1 => '1/2 in threaded rod and eye bolts',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a ship auger for tree work',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Green wood is wet, stringy, and heavy, and standard twist bits pack up and bind. A ship auger has a single cutting spur and a wide, open flute that lifts chips out of a deep hole, so you can drill through a stem for a through-bolt or a dynamic anchor without constantly backing out.',
            1 => 'Sizing the bit just over the rod diameter, such as 9/16 inch for 1/2 inch rod, gives a snug fit that still lets the hardware go in without forcing it. Check the hardware maker\'s instructions for the hole size they specify. Our <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/">cabling and bracing</a> section has the rod, eyes, and cable to go with it.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Drilling tips',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Use a powerful drill with a side handle and a slow speed, and keep the bit straight so the hole lines up from one side of the stem to the other. Back out every so often to clear chips. Drilling aloft means managing torque on a lanyard, so set your position well and keep both hands on the drill.',
            1 => 'Keep the cutting spur sharp and dry the bit after use so it does not rust. Cabling and bracing should be planned and installed by trained arborists following ANSI A300 and Z133 practice.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What size hardware does a 9/16 bit match?',
          'a' => 'It is commonly used for 1/2 inch threaded rod and eye bolts. Always confirm the hole size the hardware maker specifies.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Why not use a spade bit or twist bit?',
          'a' => 'In wet, green wood they tend to clog and bind. A ship auger clears chips better on deep through-holes.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/power-tool-5.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/power-tool-5-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Leaf Blowers 06',
        'creator' => 'hectorir',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/30806435@N04',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/30806435@N04/4117325805',
      ),
    ),
    '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/rig-guy-wire-stops/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/rig-guy-wire-stops/',
      'category' => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/',
      'name' => 'Rigguy Wire Stops',
      'brand' => 'rigguy',
      'brand_name' => 'Rigguy',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'price' => 14,
      'keyword' => 'rigguy wire stops',
      'title' => 'Rigguy Wire Stops for Tree Cabling and Guying',
      'h1' => 'Rigguy Wire Stops',
      'meta' => 'Rigguy wire stops terminate wire rope or strand without bulky clamps, for tree cabling and guying. Sizing, installation basics, and inspection for arborists.',
      'tagline' => 'Compact wire terminations for tree cabling and guying work.',
      'summary' => 'Rigguy wire stops are compact terminations that hold the end of wire rope or strand, used by arborists for cabling, guying, and tree support systems. They replace stacks of wire rope clips on some installs, which makes for a neater, faster job in the canopy.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Terminates wire without clamp stacks',
        1 => 'Clean, compact look in the tree',
        2 => 'Quick to install aloft',
        3 => 'Sized to match a specific wire diameter',
        4 => 'Suits cabling and guying jobs',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Wire termination / stop',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Rigguy',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sizing',
          1 => 'Match the stop to your wire diameter',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where wire stops fit',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Steel cabling systems and tree guying both need a reliable way to end the wire. Traditional methods use thimbles and wire rope clips or preformed dead-ends. A wire stop is a compact alternative on some systems: the wire passes through the anchor hole or hardware and the stop holds it on the far side.',
            1 => 'The result is a tidy install with fewer parts hanging in the canopy, and less time aloft with a wrench. Look through our <a href="/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/cabling-hardware/">cabling hardware</a> for compatible wire and anchors.',
            2 => 'Fewer parts also means fewer places for a mistake. With clips, every saddle has to be on the live side and every nut torqued; with a stop sized correctly, the termination is a single, consistent piece that is easy to check from a distance on later inspections.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Sizing and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A wire stop only works on the wire size it is made for, so match it exactly to your cable or strand diameter and follow the maker\'s instructions for installation, hole size, and load use. Do not mix brands or improvise with a stop that is close to the right size.',
            1 => 'Cabling systems should be designed and installed to ANSI A300 by trained arborists and inspected on a regular schedule. At each check, look for wire slipping, corrosion, broken strands, and bark growing over the hardware. Wire stops are tree-support hardware, not climbing or rigging gear.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use Rigguy wire stops for lifting or rigging?',
          'a' => 'No. Use them for the cabling or guying applications the maker lists, not for rigging, lifting, or life support.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I pick the right size?',
          'a' => 'Match the stop to the exact diameter of your wire or strand and follow the maker\'s sizing chart.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'plant-health',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/rigguy/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/cable-hardware-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Eye Bolt - Basil Watson\'s Sopwith-Type Biplane, Melbourne, Victoria, 1916',
        'creator' => 'Photographer: David Thompson',
        'creator_url' => '',
        'license' => 'CC BY 4.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://collections.museumsvictoria.com.au/items/393624',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging-pulleys/smc-apex-1-1-pulley/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging-pulleys/smc-apex-1-1-pulley/',
      'category' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/',
      'name' => 'SMC Apex 1.1 Pulley',
      'brand' => 'smc',
      'brand_name' => 'SMC',
      'kind' => 'pulley',
      'price' => 79,
      'keyword' => 'smc apex 1.1 pulley',
      'title' => 'SMC Apex 1.1 Pulley for Arborist Rigging Systems',
      'h1' => 'SMC Apex 1.1 Pulley',
      'meta' => 'The SMC Apex 1.1 pulley is a compact, rescue-grade pulley for redirects, haul systems, and light rigging. How tree crews use it and how to inspect it.',
      'tagline' => 'A compact rescue-grade pulley for redirects, haul systems, and light rigging.',
      'summary' => 'The SMC Apex 1.1 pulley is a compact pulley from a well-known rescue hardware maker, and tree crews use it for redirects, mechanical advantage systems, and light rigging. It is small enough to carry on the saddle but built for real rope work.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Compact pulley from SMC',
        1 => 'Good for redirects and haul systems',
        2 => 'Fits on a standard locking carabiner',
        3 => 'Rescue-grade construction',
        4 => 'Small enough to carry aloft',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Single pulley',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'SMC',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Size',
          1 => '1.1',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope range and rating',
          1 => 'Marked on the side plate',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Using a compact pulley on tree jobs',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A small, strong pulley is one of the most useful pieces in the rigging bag. Use it as a redirect to change the direction of a pull, as the moving pulley in a 3:1 for tensioning a line, or to take friction out of a tag line that has to run around a stem.',
            1 => 'For climbers it can work in a pulley-saver setup or as part of a haul system to lift a saw or a heavier tool. Compare sizes and styles in our <a href="/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/">rigging pulleys</a> before you choose.',
            2 => 'Compared with running rope over a carabiner, a pulley cuts friction and rope wear considerably, which you notice on every pull of a haul system and in the life of your tag lines.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Limits and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the markings for the rope diameter range and rated strength, and keep loads well inside them. A compact pulley is not a rigging block for blocking down big wood; shock loads belong on a block built for them.',
            1 => 'Before each use, spin the sheave, open and close the side plates, and look for cracks, sharp edges, grooves, or a wobbling axle. Clean off sap and grit. If the pulley ever goes into a life-support system, life-support use must follow ANSI Z133, the manufacturer\'s instructions, and your training.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use the SMC Apex pulley for blocking down wood?',
          'a' => 'Not for heavy or shock-loaded rigging. Use a rigging block rated for the job and keep compact pulleys for redirects and haul systems.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What rope size does it take?',
          'a' => 'Read the rope range marked on the side plate and match it to your line before use.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/smc/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'block and tackle',
        'creator' => 'Joelk75',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/75001512@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/75001512@N00/4143247025',
      ),
