        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why choose an aluminum block',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Every pound counts when you are carrying gear aloft and setting rigging points all day. An aluminum block gives you a proper rigging block, with a large sheave and a strong frame, at a weight that makes it easier to send up and reposition.',
            1 => 'The 3/4 inch size covers the rope most crews use for daily rigging. For the biggest wood, a 1 inch block may be the better tool. See all our <a href="/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-blocks/">rigging blocks</a> to compare.',
            2 => 'A lighter block also makes it easier to reposition the rigging point as the removal comes down. Rather than leaving a heavy block where it is and rigging from a poor angle, the climber can move it to a better spot quickly.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Rating and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Use the marked rating and remember the rigging point load can be close to twice the load on the line. Pair the block with slings, rope, and a lowering device in the same range, and keep the line running straight into the sheave.',
            1 => 'Aluminum shows wear faster than steel, so inspect the sheave groove for grooving, the side plates for cracks, burrs, or bending, and the axle for play before each job. Retire the block after a severe shock load. Follow ANSI Z133 for all rigging.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is an aluminum block strong enough for removals?',
          'a' => 'Within its marked rating, yes. Choose the size and rating for the loads you expect to generate.',
