          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Plenty of rigging is not heavy rigging: lowering limbs over a roof on a pruning job, taking small tops in pieces, or controlling brush over a fence. A compact rigging pulley handles these jobs with less weight than a full-size block, so you are more likely to rig the piece than to drop it and hope.',
            1 => 'Step up to a medium or large block when the wood gets bigger. Compare sizes in our <a href="/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-blocks/">rigging blocks</a>.',
            2 => 'A compact block is light enough to send up with the climber at the start of the job and leave on the saddle until needed. That makes it easier to rig a limb over a target rather than take the chance of a free drop.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Ratings and checks',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the marked rope range and rating, and remember the rigging point sees close to double the line load. Keep shock loads modest by limiting drop distance and letting the ground worker run the friction smoothly.',
            1 => 'Before each use, spin the sheave, check the side plates and frame for cracks or bends, and look for grooves in the sheave. Clean off sap and grit. Rigging should follow ANSI Z133 and the maker\'s instructions.',
            2 => 'Store it clean and dry, and retire it if it has been shock loaded beyond its rating or dropped onto a hard surface from height.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can the ISC compact pulley replace my main rigging block?',
          'a' => 'For light work, yes. For blocking down big wood, use a larger block rated for the loads.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does the color mean anything?',
          'a' => 'The grey frame and grey wheel identify this version. Check the marking for its rope range and rating.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/isc/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Block and tackle',
        'creator' => 'quinet',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/91994044@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/91994044@N00/14200854012',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-blocks/isc-medium-rigging-pulley-for-20mm-rope-300kn-grey-with-green-wheel/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-blocks/isc-medium-rigging-pulley-for-20mm-rope-300kn-grey-with-green-wheel/',
      'category' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-blocks/',
      'name' => 'ISC Medium Rigging Pulley for 20mm Rope (Grey with Green Wheel)',
      'brand' => 'isc',
      'brand_name' => 'ISC',
      'kind' => 'pulley',
      'price' => 285,
      'keyword' => 'isc medium rigging pulley',
      'title' => 'ISC Medium Rigging Pulley for 20mm Rope',
      'h1' => 'ISC Medium Rigging Pulley for 20mm Rope',
      'meta' => 'The ISC medium rigging pulley takes rope up to 20mm for heavier removal rigging. How it compares with the compact pulley, how to hang it, and how to inspect it.',
      'tagline' => 'A mid-size ISC rigging block for heavier removals with rope up to 20mm.',
      'summary' => 'The ISC medium rigging pulley is a mid-size rigging block made for rope up to 20mm, which covers the heavier lines crews use on removals. This version has a grey frame and a green sheave, and it fills the gap between compact pulleys and the largest blocks.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Takes rope up to 20mm',
        1 => 'Mid-size block for removal rigging',
        2 => 'Grey frame with green sheave',
        3 => 'Strong frame for heavier work',
        4 => 'Hangs from a rigging sling',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Medium rigging pulley',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'ISC',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope size',
          1 => 'Up to 20 mm',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Color',
          1 => 'Grey with green wheel',
        ),
        4 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rating',
          1 => 'Marked on the pulley',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'A block for heavier removals',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'When you are rigging tops and leaders on a removal, you need a block that can take a heavier line and the shock that comes with blocking wood. The medium ISC rigging pulley takes rope up to 20mm, so it works with the 5/8 and 3/4 inch class lines many crews run for removals.',
            1 => 'It is heavier than a compact pulley, but far more appropriate for big pieces. Compare it with other <a href="/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-blocks/">rigging blocks</a> to find the right size for your work.',
            2 => 'Because the frame is built for a heavier line, it also tolerates the wear of daily removal work better than smaller pulleys. Pair it with a rigging sling and lowering device rated for the same rope and load range.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Rating and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Read the marked rating on the pulley before use and plan for the rigging point to see close to double the line load. Match the sling, rope, and lowering device to the same working range.',
            1 => 'Inspect the sheave, side plates, and axle before every job, looking for cracks, bends, grooves, and play. Retire the pulley after a severe shock load or any damage you cannot rule out. Rigging should follow ANSI Z133 and the maker\'s instructions.',
            2 => 'Store it clean and dry and keep a simple record of the jobs it has done.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What rope sizes can I use in the ISC medium pulley?',
          'a' => 'Rope up to 20mm. Check the marking and the maker\'s documentation for the full range.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Medium or compact ISC pulley?',
          'a' => 'Compact suits pruning and light work. Medium suits removals and heavier lines up to 20mm.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/isc/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'block and tackle',
        'creator' => 'Joelk75',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/75001512@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/75001512@N00/4143247025',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-blocks/smc-apex-direct-1-5/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-blocks/smc-apex-direct-1-5/',
      'category' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-blocks/',
      'name' => 'SMC Apex Direct 1.5 Pulley',
      'brand' => 'smc',
      'brand_name' => 'SMC',
      'kind' => 'pulley',
      'price' => 109,
      'keyword' => 'smc apex direct 1.5',
      'title' => 'SMC Apex Direct 1.5 Pulley for Rigging and Haul Systems',
      'h1' => 'SMC Apex Direct 1.5 Pulley',
      'meta' => 'The SMC Apex Direct 1.5 is a larger rescue-grade pulley for haul systems, redirects, and light rigging. How tree crews use it, sizing notes, and inspection.',
      'tagline' => 'A larger SMC rescue-grade pulley for haul systems and light rigging.',
      'summary' => 'The SMC Apex Direct 1.5 is a rescue-grade pulley in a larger size than the 1.1 version, giving a bigger sheave for haul systems, redirects, and light rigging. Tree crews use it where a micro pulley is too small but a full rigging block is more than the job needs.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Larger size in the SMC Apex Direct line',
        1 => 'Bigger sheave for smoother running',
        2 => 'Haul systems, redirects, light rigging',
        3 => 'Rescue-grade construction',
        4 => 'Clips to a locking carabiner',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Single pulley',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'SMC',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Size',
          1 => '1.5',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope range and rating',
          1 => 'Marked on the side plate',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a larger sheave helps',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A bigger sheave bends the rope less and generally runs more efficiently, which you feel when you are pulling through a 3:1 to tension a line, hauling a heavy tool to the climber, or redirecting a lowering line around a stem.',
            1 => 'The 1.5 size sits between compact pulleys and true rigging blocks. Compare it with the 1.1 and other options in our <a href="/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-blocks/">rigging blocks</a> to match the pulley to your system.',
            2 => 'It also suits tag lines that have to run around a stem or over a fence. The larger sheave reduces wear on the rope and makes the ground worker\'s job easier.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use within the rating',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Read the side-plate markings for the rope range and strength and keep loads well inside them. Do not use it in place of a rigging block for blocking down heavy wood.',
            1 => 'Before each use, spin the sheave, check the side plates and axle for cracks, burrs, or play, and clean off sap and grit. If it goes into a climbing or rescue system, life-support use must follow ANSI Z133, the manufacturer\'s instructions, and your training.',
            2 => 'Store it dry and away from chemicals, and retire it after a drop from height onto a hard surface unless the maker says otherwise.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Should I choose the 1.1 or the 1.5 size?',
          'a' => 'The 1.1 is lighter and more compact. The 1.5 gives a larger sheave for smoother running in haul systems.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can it replace a rigging block?',
          'a' => 'Not for heavy or shock-loaded rigging. Use a rated rigging block for blocking down wood.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/smc/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Carabiners and Pulley',
        'creator' => 'psd',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/45581782@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/45581782@N00/2411001794',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-plates/rock-exotica-tririg/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-plates/rock-exotica-tririg/',
      'category' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/',
      'name' => 'Rock Exotica TriRig',
      'brand' => 'rock-exotica',
      'brand_name' => 'Rock Exotica',
      'kind' => 'rigging-device',
      'price' => 75,
      'keyword' => 'rock exotica tririg',
      'title' => 'Rock Exotica TriRig: Rigging Plate for Rope Systems',
      'h1' => 'Rock Exotica TriRig',
      'meta' => 'The Rock Exotica TriRig is a compact rigging plate for organizing anchors, pulleys, and carabiners in rope systems. How crews use it and how to inspect it.',
      'tagline' => 'A compact rigging plate for organizing anchors and connectors in rope systems.',
      'summary' => 'The Rock Exotica TriRig is a compact rigging plate that gives a rope system several connection points in one piece of hardware. Tree crews use a plate like this to organize an anchor, keep carabiners from cross-loading, and build tidy haul or lowering systems.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Multiple connection points in one plate',
        1 => 'Keeps carabiners organized and loaded correctly',
        2 => 'Useful for anchors and haul systems',
        3 => 'Compact and light',
        4 => 'Made by Rock Exotica',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Rigging plate',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Rock Exotica',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rating',
          1 => 'Marked on the plate',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What a rigging plate does',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'When several pieces meet at one anchor, carabiners stacked on carabiners can twist, cross-load, and turn a simple setup into a tangle. A rigging plate gives each piece its own hole: the anchor sling on one side, the pulley or lowering device on another, a backup or tag line on a third.',
            1 => 'On tree jobs that might mean a ground anchor for a 3:1, a rescue or haul setup, or a neat way to split a load between two slings. Browse more <a href="/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/">blocks and rigging hardware</a> to build the rest of the system.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Load the plate in line with the direction the maker shows, use correctly sized carabiners, and keep everything inside the marked rating. Spread angles between anchor legs increase the force on each leg, so keep them narrow.',
            1 => 'Check for cracks, burrs, deformation, and worn edges in the holes before each use. Retire the plate if it has been dropped from height onto a hard surface or shock loaded. If it is part of a life-support system, life-support use must follow ANSI Z133, the manufacturer\'s instructions, and your training.',
            2 => 'Store it with your other rigging hardware, away from chemicals, and keep it clean so you can see any damage at a glance.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need a rigging plate for tree work?',
          'a' => 'Not always, but it makes multi-piece anchors and haul systems cleaner and helps prevent cross-loaded carabiners.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use the TriRig in a climbing system?',
          'a' => 'Check the maker\'s rating and instructions. Use life-support gear only as the manufacturer and ANSI Z133 allow.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/rock-exotica/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rigging-device-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Tree Pruning in RVA (Carytown, Virginia)',
        'creator' => 'Gamma Man',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/51625243@N06/48982704071',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/petzl-spin-l2-pulley/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/petzl-spin-l2-pulley/',
      'category' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/',
      'name' => 'Petzl SPIN L2 Pulley',
      'brand' => 'petzl',
      'brand_name' => 'Petzl',
      'kind' => 'pulley',
      'price' => 115,
      'keyword' => 'petzl spin l2',
      'title' => 'Petzl SPIN L2 Double Pulley with Swivel',
      'h1' => 'Petzl SPIN L2 Pulley',
      'meta' => 'The Petzl SPIN L2 is a double pulley with a built-in swivel for haul systems and rigging. How tree crews use it, why the swivel helps, and care tips.',
      'tagline' => 'A swiveling double pulley for haul systems, redirects, and rope work.',
      'summary' => 'The Petzl SPIN L2 is a double pulley with a built-in swivel and sealed ball-bearing sheaves, made for haul systems and rope work where efficiency matters. Tree crews use it to build mechanical advantage for tensioning lines, pulling tops, and lifting gear.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Double sheave pulley',
        1 => 'Built-in swivel keeps ropes from twisting',
        2 => 'Sealed ball bearings',
        3 => 'Opens to load the rope with the pulley clipped in',
        4 => 'Good base for haul and tensioning systems',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Double pulley with swivel',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Petzl',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sheaves',
          1 => '2, sealed ball bearings',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope range and rating',
          1 => 'Marked on the pulley',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a double pulley with a swivel',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A double pulley lets you build higher ratios, such as a 4:1 or 5:1, with fewer parts. The integrated swivel lets the pulley turn to line up with the load, so the rope strands do not twist around each other as you haul. That matters when you are pulling a lean out of a tree or tensioning a speed line across a yard.',
            1 => 'Petzl\'s side plates open so you can load the rope without unclipping the pulley from its anchor. See our other <a href="/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/">rigging pulleys</a> for single options to pair it with.',
            2 => 'Sealed ball bearings keep friction low, so more of your pull goes into the load. That matters on long hauls or when you are building a high ratio system.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the marked rope range and rating and stay well within them. Mechanical advantage multiplies your pull, which makes it easy to overload a sling, an anchor, or a tree, so plan the forces before you start.',
            1 => 'Before each use, spin both sheaves, test the swivel, and check the side plates for cracks or deformation. Rinse off grit and sap and let it dry. If it becomes part of a rescue or life-support system, life-support use must follow ANSI Z133, the manufacturer\'s instructions, and your training.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between the SPIN L1 and SPIN L2?',
          'a' => 'The SPIN L1 is a single pulley, and the SPIN L2 is a double. Both have a swivel and ball-bearing sheaves.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use the SPIN L2 for blocking down wood?',
          'a' => 'No. It is a haul and tensioning pulley. Use a rated rigging block for shock-loaded rigging.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Pulley Block Equipment Rigging Edited 2020',
        'creator' => 'chimpwithcan',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188454520@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/188454520@N02/49920521488',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/progress-capture-pulley-40kn-double-bushing/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/progress-capture-pulley-40kn-double-bushing/',
      'category' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/',
      'name' => 'Progress Capture Pulley 40kN, Double Bushing',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'pulley',
      'price' => 69,
      'keyword' => 'progress capture pulley',
      'title' => 'Progress Capture Pulley 40kN with Double Bushing',
      'h1' => 'Progress Capture Pulley 40kN, Double Bushing',
      'meta' => 'A 40kN progress capture pulley with double bushings, made to work with a prusik in haul systems. How progress capture works and how to inspect it before use.',
      'tagline' => 'A pulley shaped to tend a prusik, so a haul system holds every pull.',
      'summary' => 'A progress capture pulley is a pulley with square-edged side plates that let a prusik or friction hitch sit against it without being pulled into the sheave. This one carries a 40kN rating and uses double bushings, and crews use it to build haul systems that hold their gain between pulls.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '40kN rated, per the product name',
        1 => 'Double bushing sheave',
        2 => 'Side plates shaped to tend a prusik',
        3 => 'Builds haul systems that hold their gain',
        4 => 'Simple and durable',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Progress capture pulley',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rating',
          1 => '40 kN',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sheave',
          1 => 'Bushing',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope range',
          1 => 'Marked on the pulley',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How progress capture works',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In a haul system you pull, then you have to reset. Without something to hold the rope, you lose what you just gained. A progress capture pulley works with a prusik on the line: as you pull, rope runs through the pulley and the prusik slides against the side plates. When you let go, the prusik grabs and the load stays put.',
            1 => 'On tree jobs that might mean pulling a top over in a felling setup, tensioning a speed line, or lifting a heavy piece off a structure. Find more options in our <a href="/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/">rigging pulleys</a>.',
            2 => 'The square-edged side plates are what make it work. A rounded pulley can drag the prusik into the sheave, where it fails to grab, while a progress capture pulley stops the hitch at the side plates every time.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Bushings, rating, and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Bushings are simple and durable and handle high static loads, though they spin with more friction than ball bearings. Keep loads within the rating and remember mechanical advantage makes it easy to overload anchors and trees.',
            1 => 'Check the sheave, side plates, and axle for wear, cracks, and play before each use, and inspect the prusik for glazing. Rescue and life-support use must follow the manufacturer\'s instructions, ANSI Z133, and proper training.',
            2 => 'Store it clean and dry, and keep it with the prusiks you use with it so the system is complete when you need it.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between bushings and ball bearings?',
          'a' => 'Bushings are durable and simple but have more friction. Ball bearings spin more freely and suit long hauls.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use a standard pulley with a prusik?',
          'a' => 'Only if it is designed to tend a prusik. Rounded side plates can pull the hitch into the sheave.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Block and Tackle',
        'creator' => 'www.mgaylard.co.uk and thanks for looking',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/16564965@N04',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/16564965@N04/52721499095',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/rock-exotica-omni-block-1-1/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/rock-exotica-omni-block-1-1/',
      'category' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/',
      'name' => 'Rock Exotica Omni-Block 1.1',
      'brand' => 'rock-exotica',
      'brand_name' => 'Rock Exotica',
      'kind' => 'pulley',
      'price' => 99,
      'keyword' => 'rock exotica omni-block',
      'title' => 'Rock Exotica Omni-Block 1.1 Swivel Pulley',
      'h1' => 'Rock Exotica Omni-Block 1.1',
      'meta' => 'The Rock Exotica Omni-Block 1.1 is a swivel pulley that opens with the carabiner still in place. How tree crews use it for redirects, haul systems, and climbs.',
      'tagline' => 'A swiveling Rock Exotica pulley that loads rope without unclipping.',
      'summary' => 'The Rock Exotica Omni-Block 1.1 is a compact swivel pulley whose side plates open while it stays clipped to the anchor, so you can load the rope without dropping anything. Tree climbers and rigging crews use it for redirects, haul systems, and climbing system setups.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Built-in swivel',
        1 => 'Side plates open while still clipped',
        2 => 'Compact 1.1 size',
        3 => 'Redirects, haul systems, and climbing setups',
        4 => 'Made by Rock Exotica',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Swivel pulley',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Rock Exotica',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Size',
          1 => '1.1',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope range and rating',
          1 => 'Marked on the pulley',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why crews like the Omni-Block',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Being able to open a pulley without unclipping it is a real advantage aloft. You can drop the rope in mid-system, keep the pulley attached to the anchor, and avoid fumbling hardware over a drop zone. The swivel lets the pulley line up with the load so ropes do not twist.',
            1 => 'Climbers use it in SRT base anchors, redirects, and haul systems, and rigging crews use it for tag lines and light tensioning. Compare it with other <a href="/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/">rigging pulleys</a>.',
            2 => 'The rounded frame and smooth sheave keep friction low, so it works well in haul systems and anywhere a rope needs to change direction without much wear.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Limits and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Read the marked rope range and rating and keep loads inside them. It is not a rigging block for blocking down wood.',
            1 => 'Before each use, check that the side plates close and lock fully, the swivel turns freely, and the sheave spins smoothly with no grooves or play. Rinse off grit and sap. Life-support use must follow the maker\'s instructions, ANSI Z133, and your training.',
            2 => 'Store it clean and dry, away from chemicals, and retire it after a drop from height onto a hard surface unless the maker says otherwise.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use the Omni-Block in my climbing system?',
          'a' => 'Many climbers do. Confirm it meets your system\'s requirements and use it per the maker\'s instructions and ANSI Z133.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What does the 1.1 mean?',
          'a' => 'It is the size designation in the Omni-Block range. Larger sizes are made for bigger rope and loads.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/rock-exotica/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Block and tackle',
        'creator' => 'quinet',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/91994044@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/91994044@N00/14200854012',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/spin-l1d/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/spin-l1d/',
      'category' => '/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/',
      'name' => 'Petzl SPIN L1D Pulley',
      'brand' => 'petzl',
      'brand_name' => 'Petzl',
      'kind' => 'pulley',
      'price' => 89,
      'keyword' => 'petzl spin l1d',
      'title' => 'Petzl SPIN L1D Swivel Pulley with Becket',
      'h1' => 'Petzl SPIN L1D Pulley',
      'meta' => 'The Petzl SPIN L1D is a swivel pulley with a becket for building haul systems and tensioning lines. How tree crews use it, what the becket adds, and checks.',
      'tagline' => 'A swivel pulley with a becket for building compact haul systems.',
      'summary' => 'The Petzl SPIN L1D is a single swivel pulley with a becket, an extra attachment point below the sheave, and sealed ball-bearing efficiency. Tree crews use it to build compact haul systems, tension speed lines, and redirect ropes with low friction.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Single pulley with built-in swivel',
        1 => 'Becket attachment point for haul systems',
        2 => 'Sealed ball bearing sheave',
        3 => 'Side plates open while clipped',
        4 => 'Pairs with the SPIN L2',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Single swivel pulley with becket',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Petzl',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sheave',
          1 => 'Sealed ball bearing',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope range and rating',
          1 => 'Marked on the pulley',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What the becket adds',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A becket is an extra attachment point on the pulley. It lets you tie or clip the end of the rope directly to the pulley, which is how you build a compact 3:1 or 5:1 with fewer carabiners. The swivel keeps strands from twisting as you haul.',
            1 => 'On tree jobs that might be a quick system to tension a speed line, pull a lean out of a tree, or lift a stuck saw. Pair it with the SPIN L2 and other <a href="/rigging/blocks-and-other-hardware/rigging-pulleys/">rigging pulleys</a> for higher ratios.',
            2 => 'Because the side plates open while the pulley stays clipped, you can load the rope in mid-system without dropping anything over the work zone.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Stay inside the marked rope range and rating and plan forces before you pull, because mechanical advantage makes overloading easy.',
            1 => 'Before each use, spin the sheave, check the swivel and becket for wear or deformation, and confirm the side plates close fully. Rinse and dry it after dirty jobs. Life-support use must follow the maker\'s instructions, ANSI Z133, and your training.',
            2 => 'Retire it after a drop from height onto a hard surface unless Petzl\'s guidance says otherwise, and keep a record of its use.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'SPIN L1 or SPIN L1D?',
          'a' => 'The L1D adds a becket, an extra attachment point, which is handy for building haul systems.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is it suitable for blocking down wood?',
          'a' => 'No. Use a rated rigging block for shock-loaded rigging.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'block and tackle',
        'creator' => 'Joelk75',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/75001512@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/75001512@N00/4143247025',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/lowering-devices/isc-rigging-rope-wrench-70kg/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/lowering-devices/isc-rigging-rope-wrench-70kg/',
      'category' => '/rigging/lowering-devices/',
      'name' => 'ISC Rigging Rope Wrench (70kg)',
      'brand' => 'isc',
      'brand_name' => 'ISC',
      'kind' => 'friction-device',
      'price' => 149,
      'keyword' => 'isc rigging rope wrench',
      'title' => 'ISC Rigging Rope Wrench for Light Lowering',
      'h1' => 'ISC Rigging Rope Wrench (70kg)',
      'meta' => 'The ISC rigging rope wrench adds friction for lowering light loads up to 70kg. Where it fits on pruning jobs, how it differs from a porta-wrap, and checks.',
      'tagline' => 'A friction device for lowering light limbs and brush in the canopy.',
      'summary' => 'The ISC rigging rope wrench is a friction device for lowering light loads, listed for up to 70kg, that crews use when a porta-wrap at the base is more than the job needs. It helps a climber or ground worker control small limbs and brush on pruning jobs.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Light-load lowering device',
        1 => 'Listed for loads up to 70kg',
        2 => 'Good for pruning and small limbs',
        3 => 'Compact and light',
        4 => 'From ISC, maker of the original Rope Wrench',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Rigging friction device',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'ISC',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Load',
          1 => 'Up to 70 kg, per product name',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope range',
          1 => 'See maker\'s instructions',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Light lowering in the canopy',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Plenty of pruning cuts are too heavy to drop over a roof or a garden but nowhere near the size that needs a full rigging block and porta-wrap. A light-duty rope wrench adds just enough friction to let a limb down slowly and in control.',
            1 => 'It works with smaller rope and lighter loads than heavy lowering devices, so it is quick to set and easy to carry. See our other <a href="/rigging/lowering-devices/">lowering devices</a> when the wood gets bigger.',
            2 => 'Unlike a porta-wrap at the base of the tree, a light device like this can be set close to the work, which suits single-person pruning setups where the climber controls the descent of small limbs.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Know the limits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The 70kg figure is a hard limit, not a guide. Shock loading a light device by letting pieces free-fall onto it multiplies the force, so keep pieces small and catch them with little slack.',
            1 => 'Follow ISC\'s instructions for rope size, setup, and inspection. Check for wear grooves, cracks, and burrs before each use. Do not use it as a climbing device unless the maker says it is designed for that. Rigging should follow ANSI Z133 and be done by trained crew.',
            2 => 'Store it clean and dry, and keep it with the rope it was set up for so the system stays matched.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is the ISC rigging rope wrench the same as the climbing Rope Wrench?',
          'a' => 'No. This version is for rigging light loads. Use climbing devices only as their maker intends.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What happens if I exceed 70kg?',
          'a' => 'You risk losing control of the load and damaging the device. Use a heavier lowering device for larger pieces.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
        1 => 'pruning',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/isc/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/friction-device-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/friction-device-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => '100127-F-9898L-026',
        'creator' => 'expertinfantry',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/58297778@N04',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/58297778@N04/5444263877',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/cmi-ropejack-bag/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/cmi-ropejack-bag/',
      'category' => '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/',
      'name' => 'CMI RopeJack Bag',
      'brand' => 'cmi',
      'brand_name' => 'CMI',
      'kind' => 'case-bag',
      'price' => 45,
      'keyword' => 'cmi ropejack bag',
      'title' => 'CMI RopeJack Bag for Storing Rigging Gear',
      'h1' => 'CMI RopeJack Bag',
      'meta' => 'The CMI RopeJack bag keeps a RopeJack or mechanical advantage kit together and protected in the truck. What it holds, why a dedicated bag helps, and care tips.',
      'tagline' => 'A dedicated bag to keep a RopeJack or haul kit together and protected.',
      'summary' => 'The CMI RopeJack bag is a dedicated carry bag for CMI\'s RopeJack and its accessories, so a mechanical advantage kit stays together and protected in the truck box. When the tree needs a pull, the whole kit comes out in one grab.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Dedicated bag for the CMI RopeJack',
        1 => 'Keeps the haul kit together',
        2 => 'Protects hardware from grit and weather',
        3 => 'Easy to grab from the truck',
        4 => 'Made by CMI',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Gear bag',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'CMI',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'CMI RopeJack and accessories',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why a dedicated bag',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A mechanical advantage kit is only useful if all the parts are there when you need them. Keeping the RopeJack, its carabiners, and slings in their own bag means the kit comes out complete, every time, rather than pieces drifting into other bags on the truck.',
            1 => 'A bag also keeps grit and moisture off the hardware. Browse our <a href="/rigging/mechanical-advantage/">mechanical advantage</a> section for gear to round out the kit.',
            2 => 'When the pull needs to happen fast, such as a tree that sits back on the saw, nobody wants to search the truck for a missing carabiner. A dedicated bag keeps the kit ready.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Packing and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pack the hardware so it does not rattle or rub against sharp edges, and keep the bag away from fuel, bar oil, and chemicals. Let wet gear dry before you pack it away.',
            1 => 'Use the time when you pack the bag to inspect the kit: check slings for cuts and the hardware for cracks or burrs. The bag itself is storage only and is not a load-bearing item.',
            2 => 'Check the bag itself for torn seams, worn bottoms, and broken zippers or closures, and replace it before it starts losing gear. A clear label on the outside helps the crew grab the right bag on a busy job.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does the bag come with a RopeJack?',
          'a' => 'No. This is the bag only. Check the listing for what is included.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I store other gear in it?',
          'a' => 'Yes, it works for any small rigging or haul kit that fits.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/cmi/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/case-bag-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'army duffel bag',
        'creator' => 'Prince Roy',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/17352537@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/17352537@N00/20210201772',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/petzl-jag-traxion-double-pulley/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/petzl-jag-traxion-double-pulley/',
      'category' => '/rigging/mechanical-advantage/',
      'name' => 'Petzl JAG TRAXION Double Pulley',
      'brand' => 'petzl',
      'brand_name' => 'Petzl',
      'kind' => 'pulley',
      'price' => 125,
      'keyword' => 'petzl jag traxion',
      'title' => 'Petzl JAG TRAXION Progress Capture Double Pulley',
      'h1' => 'Petzl JAG TRAXION Double Pulley',
      'meta' => 'The Petzl JAG TRAXION is a double pulley with built-in progress capture for compact haul systems. How tree crews use it, setting up a 4:1, and inspection.',
      'tagline' => 'A double pulley with built-in progress capture for compact haul systems.',
      'summary' => 'The Petzl JAG TRAXION is a double pulley with built-in progress capture, so a haul system holds the load each time you stop pulling. It is the heart of Petzl\'s JAG system kits, and tree crews use it to tension lines and pull tops with a compact, efficient 4:1.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Double pulley with integrated cam',
        1 => 'Holds the load between pulls',
        2 => 'Core of a compact 4:1 haul system',
        3 => 'Efficient ball-bearing sheaves',
        4 => 'Petzl JAG system component',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Type',
          1 => 'Double pulley with progress capture',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Petzl',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sheaves',
          1 => '2, ball bearing',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Rope range and rating',
          1 => 'Marked on the pulley',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How tree crews use it',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pair the JAG TRAXION with a second double pulley and you have a compact 4:1 that grabs and holds every pull. That makes it handy for tensioning a speed line, pulling a lean out of a tree before the cut, or lifting a stuck piece off a structure. Because it holds on its own, you can reset without needing a second person on a prusik.',
            1 => 'See our other <a href="/rigging/mechanical-advantage/">mechanical advantage</a> gear for kits and accessories.',
            2 => 'Efficient ball-bearing sheaves mean less of your pull is lost to friction, which matters when you need to move something heavy with a small crew.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Use and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Mechanical advantage makes it easy to exceed what an anchor, sling, or tree can handle, so plan the forces before you pull and stay well inside the marked rating.',
            1 => 'Before each use, check the cam for free movement and clean teeth, spin the sheaves, and look for cracks, wear, or deformation. Rinse off grit and sap. Rescue and life-support use must follow the maker\'s instructions, ANSI Z133, and proper training.',
            2 => 'Store it dry and away from chemicals, and retire it after a drop from height or any damage you cannot rule out.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use the JAG TRAXION alone?',
          'a' => 'It works best as part of a system with a second pulley. Petzl sells complete JAG system kits.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does the cam damage rope?',
          'a' => 'Used correctly, no. Avoid shock loading it and keep the cam clean.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/petzl/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/pulley-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/pulley-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Carabiners and Pulley',
        'creator' => 'psd',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/45581782@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/45581782@N00/2411001794',
      ),
