      'title' => 'Samson 3/4 Pro-Master Rigging Rope: Buying Guide',
      'h1' => 'Samson 3/4 in Pro-Master Rigging Rope',
      'meta' => 'Samson 3/4 in Pro-Master rigging rope is a heavy rigging line for lowering big wood with blocks and lowering devices. Use, inspection and safety notes.',
      'tagline' => 'A 3/4 in rigging line for crews lowering heavy wood.',
      'summary' => 'Samson 3/4 in Pro-Master rigging rope is a heavy rigging line for crews lowering big leads and wood with blocks, lowering devices and port-a-wraps. The thick diameter gives it more capacity and more to grip on the bollard than smaller lines.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '3/4 in rigging line from Samson',
        1 => 'For heavy lowering with blocks and devices',
        2 => 'Thick diameter for bollard grip',
        3 => 'Stands up to repeated rigging cycles',
        4 => 'Sold in hanks, check length at checkout',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Brand',
          1 => 'Samson',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '3/4 in',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Rigging and lowering',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sold',
          1 => 'Hanks; length varies by listing',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Where a 3/4 in rigging line fits',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Most removal crews keep a 1/2 in or 5/8 in line for everyday pieces and a 3/4 in line for the heavy stuff: big tops, wide leads and trunk sections. The thicker rope gives more capacity and more surface on the lowering device, which helps the groundie control a heavy run.',
            1 => 'Size the whole system together. Your block, sling, lowering device and anchor all need to handle the forces you are generating, not just the rope. Shock loads from letting wood free fall add up fast.',
            2 => 'A 3/4 in line is heavy and stiff compared with smaller rigging ropes, so it takes more effort to throw and to drag through the canopy. Many crews only pull it out when the job calls for it and leave it rigged through a block on the tree for the big pieces. Store it coiled in a dry bag or bin, out of the sun and away from fuel, oil and saw chain, and log how many heavy loads it has seen.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Check the rope after every job for cuts, glazing, crushed sections and heat damage from the bollard. Keep it out of the chipper path and away from saw chain.',
            1 => 'Rigging should be planned by trained, qualified people following ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s working load guidance. Retire the line once it shows damage. See more <a href="/rope/rigging-rope/">rigging rope</a>.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'When do I need 3/4 in rigging rope instead of 1/2 in?',
          'a' => 'When the wood you are lowering and the forces involved exceed what your smaller line and hardware are rated for. Work it out with your crew lead before the cut.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I splice Samson Pro-Master?',
          'a' => 'Use a splice that Samson approves for that construction and have it done by someone trained in it.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'rigging',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/samson-rope/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'coiling',
        'creator' => 'michael pollak',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06/6796140986',
      ),
    ),
    '/rope/tech-cordage/teufelberger-8mm-ocean-polyester-hitch-cord-by-the-foot/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rope/tech-cordage/teufelberger-8mm-ocean-polyester-hitch-cord-by-the-foot/',
      'category' => '/rope/tech-cordage/',
      'name' => 'Teufelberger 8mm Ocean Polyester Hitch Cord (By the Foot)',
      'brand' => 'teufelberger',
      'brand_name' => 'Teufelberger',
      'kind' => 'rope',
      'price' => 1,
      'keyword' => 'ocean polyester hitch cord',
      'title' => 'Teufelberger Ocean Polyester Hitch Cord, 8mm',
      'h1' => 'Teufelberger 8mm Ocean Polyester Hitch Cord (By the Foot)',
      'meta' => 'Ocean polyester hitch cord in 8mm, sold by the foot from Teufelberger, for tree climbers tying their own friction hitches and eye-to-eyes. Sizing and care tips.',
      'tagline' => '8mm polyester hitch cord, cut to the length you need.',
      'summary' => 'Ocean polyester hitch cord in 8mm is a slim, grippy cord for tying friction hitches on arborist climbing lines. Buy it by the foot to make up a hitch, a short prusik or a spare to keep in the bag.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '8mm polyester hitch cord',
        1 => 'Sold by the foot',
        2 => 'Slim diameter for a firm grab',
        3 => 'Good for tuning your own hitch length',
        4 => 'Inspect often, replace when glazed',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '8 mm',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Polyester',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Sold',
          1 => 'By the foot',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing hitch cord',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'An 8mm cord grabs harder than thicker cords on the same climbing line, so it suits climbers on thinner ropes or anyone who likes a positive bite. On thick lines it can grab too hard and get grabby on descent, so test it before you commit.',
            1 => 'Buying by the foot lets you cut exactly what your hitch needs. Measure a hitch you already like and add enough for terminations.',
            2 => 'Ocean polyester cord is a long-standing choice for footlock prusiks and hitch cords because it is simple, consistent and affordable. Buying it by the foot also makes it easy to keep a spare length in the bag so a worn hitch can be swapped on the job rather than ending the climb. Expect to replace hitch cord more often than any other soft part of your system, especially if you descend fast.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Terminations, wear and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Eyes on a hitch cord carry your full weight. Have them sewn or spliced by a qualified provider, or use a knot taught and tested in your training. Do not improvise terminations at height.',
            1 => 'Life-support gear must meet ANSI Z133 requirements and the manufacturer\'s instructions, be inspected before every climb, and be used only by trained climbers. Hitch cord is a consumable. Replace it when the cover glazes, fuzzes heavily or stiffens from heat. See more <a href="/rope/tech-cordage/">tech cordage</a>.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How much 8mm Ocean cord do I need for a Distel?',
          'a' => 'Measure a hitch you like and add enough for your terminations. Many climbers start around 30 inches and adjust.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is 8mm too thin for my rope?',
          'a' => 'It works best on thinner climbing lines. On larger ropes it may grab too hard, so test low to the ground.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/teufelberger/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Walt coiling the rope at the summit',
        'creator' => 'Tristan Higbee',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62264264@N03',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/62264264@N03/8305035257',
      ),
