        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why crews wear them',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Tinted safety glasses let you look up into a sunlit canopy without squinting, which helps you spot deadwood, cracks and the next tie-in point. They also keep chips and sawdust out of your eyes while you buck and chip.',
            1 => 'The Ammo has the look of a sport sunglass, which is part of the appeal: crew members are more likely to keep on a pair they like wearing.',
            2 => 'ERB is a long-running safety brand, and the O.N.E. line is its sport-style range. Keep a few pairs in the truck so nobody heads to the chipper without eye protection.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit, care and replacement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Good safety glasses sit close to the face without pressing on your temples under a helmet strap or earmuff cushion. Try them with your helmet and hearing protection on, because that is how you will wear them all day.',
            1 => 'Keep a spare pair in the truck so a scratched or lost pair never means working without eye protection. Rinse grit off with water before wiping the lenses, store them in a case or soft bag, and replace them once scratches start to blur your view. Look for the ANSI Z87.1 marking on the frame and lens when you need rated safety eyewear. Browse more <a href="/safety/eye-protection/">eye protection</a>.',
