          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Chainsaws, chippers, and stump grinders are loud enough to damage hearing over a normal workday, and the muffs that get left in the truck do nothing. Helmet-mounted earmuffs like the X4P3E solve that by staying attached to the helmet. Flip them down to buck a log, swing them out to talk with the groundie, and they are still there for the next cut.',
            1 => 'The P3E arms clip into the side accessory slots found on many hard hats and work helmets. Check that your helmet has compatible slots before you buy, and fit the arms so the cups seal fully around the ears without pressing on the helmet shell. Browse the rest of our <a href="/safety/hearing-protection/">hearing protection</a> for headband and plug options.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit, care, and replacement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'An earmuff only works if the cushion seals. Hair, glasses arms, and hat brims under the cup all break that seal, so keep the area clear. Wipe sweat and sawdust off the cushions at the end of the day, and replace the cushions and foam inserts when they go hard, flat, or cracked. The Peltor HYX4 hygiene kit is the matching replacement set for X4 cups.',
            1 => 'Pick hearing protection with a noise reduction rating suited to the equipment you run, follow 3M\'s fitting instructions, and pair it with eye and face protection as ANSI Z133 calls for on saw work.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will the X4P3E fit my climbing helmet?',
          'a' => 'It fits helmets with the standard side accessory slots the P3E attachment is made for. Many climbing-style arborist helmets use their own muff mounts, so confirm compatibility with the helmet maker first.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What replacement pads does it use?',
          'a' => 'The Peltor HYX4 hygiene kit, which includes new ear cushions and foam inserts for X4 series earmuffs.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/3m/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/eye-ear-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/eye-ear-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => '000ElmTreeVitality',
        'creator' => 'Symic',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/73344134@N00/33607662022',
      ),
    ),
    '/safety/hearing-protection/peltor-earmuff-hygiene-kit-hyx4/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/safety/hearing-protection/peltor-earmuff-hygiene-kit-hyx4/',
      'category' => '/safety/hearing-protection/',
      'name' => '3M Peltor HYX4 Earmuff Hygiene Kit',
      'brand' => '3m',
      'brand_name' => '3M',
      'kind' => 'eye-ear',
      'price' => 16,
      'keyword' => 'peltor hyx4 hygiene kit',
      'title' => 'Peltor HYX4 Hygiene Kit for X4 Earmuffs',
      'h1' => '3M Peltor HYX4 Hygiene Kit for X4 Earmuffs',
      'meta' => 'The Peltor HYX4 hygiene kit replaces worn ear cushions and foam inserts on X4 series earmuffs, so the seal holds and saw noise stays out. When to swap them.',
      'tagline' => 'Fresh cushions and foam inserts to restore the seal on Peltor X4 earmuffs.',
      'summary' => 'The Peltor HYX4 hygiene kit is the replacement cushion and foam insert set for 3M Peltor X4 series earmuffs, including helmet-mounted versions like the X4P3E. Swapping them brings back the seal that sweat, sawdust, and sun slowly take away.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Replacement ear cushions for Peltor X4 cups',
        1 => 'Includes foam inserts for inside the cups',
        2 => 'Snaps in without tools',
        3 => 'Restores the seal that worn pads lose',
        4 => 'Fits headband and helmet-mounted X4 models',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Fits',
          1 => 'Peltor X4 series earmuffs',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Contents',
          1 => 'Ear cushions and foam inserts',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Install',
          1 => 'Snap-in, no tools',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why the pads matter',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'An earmuff blocks noise with two things: the cup and the seal around your ear. On a tree crew the seal takes the beating. Sweat, bar oil, sawdust, and heat in the truck cab harden the cushions until they no longer conform to the side of your head, and noise leaks straight past them. New cushions are the cheapest way to get back the protection the muff was rated for.',
            1 => 'Swap the cushions and foam when they feel stiff, look flattened or cracked, or no longer spring back when pressed. Many crews keep a spare kit in the truck and change pads on a schedule rather than waiting for them to split.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Changing them out',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Pull the old cushion off the cup, remove the old foam, set the new foam in place, and press the new cushion on until it seats all the way around. Check that it is fully seated, since a gap at one edge defeats the point. Clean the cups with mild soap and water while they are open, and let them dry before reassembly.',
            1 => 'The kit fits X4 series muffs, including the helmet-mounted X4P3E. See our <a href="/safety/hearing-protection/">hearing protection</a> section for the earmuffs themselves.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I replace earmuff cushions?',
          'a' => 'Replace them whenever they harden, crack, or stop sealing. With daily saw use that is often several times a year. Follow 3M\'s guidance for your model.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will the HYX4 fit other Peltor earmuffs?',
          'a' => 'It is made for the X4 series. Other Peltor lines use their own kits, so match the kit number to your earmuff model.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'safety',
        1 => 'maintenance',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => '/3m/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/eye-ear-3.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/eye-ear-3-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Sharpening Chainsaw Blades',
        'creator' => 'NCDOTcommunications',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39320593@N03/36914543386',
      ),
    ),
    '/safety/traffic-control/jbc-36-road-closed-sign/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/safety/traffic-control/jbc-36-road-closed-sign/',
      'category' => '/safety/traffic-control/',
      'name' => 'JBC 36 in. Road Closed Sign',
      'brand' => 'jbc',
      'brand_name' => 'JBC',
      'kind' => 'traffic',
      'price' => 95,
      'keyword' => 'road closed sign',
      'title' => 'JBC 36 in. Road Closed Sign for Tree Work Zones',
      'h1' => 'JBC 36 in. Road Closed Sign',
      'meta' => 'A 36 inch Road Closed sign for tree crews that shut a street during a removal or crane pick. Where to place it, how to stand it up, and what rules apply.',
      'tagline' => 'A 36 inch Road Closed sign for full street closures on removals and crane jobs.',
      'summary' => 'A 36 inch road closed sign tells drivers well ahead of the work that the street is shut, not just narrowed. Tree crews put it up for removals, crane picks, and chipper setups that take the full roadway.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '36 in. sign face sized for roadside work zones',
        1 => 'Road Closed legend for full closures',
        2 => 'Pairs with a sign stand and cones',
        3 => 'Easy to load and set up from the truck',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Size',
          1 => '36 in.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Legend',
          1 => 'ROAD CLOSED',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Full road closures',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'When a tree job closes the road',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Some jobs cannot be done with a lane shift. A crane parked across the street, a big spar coming down over the road, or a chip truck and loader filling both lanes all mean the road is shut while you work. A Road Closed sign at each end of the work area, backed up with cones or barricades, tells drivers to turn around before they reach the hazard. It is also the sign that keeps a driver from following a GPS route straight into your landing zone.',
            1 => 'Set the sign where drivers can see it with time to react and still have a place to turn. Keep it upright, clean, and out of the drop zone, and pick it up as soon as the road reopens so drivers keep trusting your signs.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Permits and the rulebook',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Closing a public road usually takes a permit and sometimes a police detail. Sign placement and spacing should follow the MUTCD and your state and local rules, and ANSI Z133 expects a work zone plan whenever traffic is affected. Flaggers need training. Two signs and a stack of cones in the truck mean you are ready when a job turns out to need a closure.',
            1 => 'A sign stand keeps a 36 inch sign steady in wind. See our <a href="/safety/traffic-control/">traffic control</a> gear for stands, cones, and other work zone signs.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need a permit to close a road for tree work?',
          'a' => 'Usually, yes. Check with the city, county, or state agency that owns the road before you plan a closure.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is one sign enough?',
          'a' => 'No. A closure normally needs signs and channelizing devices at each approach, laid out to MUTCD and local requirements.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/jbc/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/traffic-4.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/traffic-4-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Traffic cone',
        'creator' => 'grassrootsgroundswell',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/88920309@N05',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/88920309@N05/11371145396',
      ),
    ),
    '/safety/traffic-control/jbc-36-work-area-ahead-sign/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/safety/traffic-control/jbc-36-work-area-ahead-sign/',
      'category' => '/safety/traffic-control/',
      'name' => 'JBC 36 in. Work Area Ahead Sign',
      'brand' => 'jbc',
      'brand_name' => 'JBC',
      'kind' => 'traffic',
      'price' => 95,
      'keyword' => 'work area ahead sign',
      'title' => 'JBC 36 in. Work Area Ahead Sign for Tree Crews',
      'h1' => 'JBC 36 in. Work Area Ahead Sign',
      'meta' => 'A 36 inch Work Area Ahead sign gives drivers advance warning of a tree crew on the roadside. Where to place it, what it pairs with, and the rules to follow.',
      'tagline' => 'A 36 inch advance warning sign for roadside tree and utility work.',
      'summary' => 'A work area ahead sign is the first thing a driver sees before your trucks, chipper, and ground crew. This 36 inch version is sized for roadside jobs where the crew is working on or near the travel lane.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '36 in. advance warning sign',
        1 => 'Work Area Ahead legend',
        2 => 'Sized for roadside work zones',
        3 => 'Works with a sign stand, cones, and flaggers',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Size',
          1 => '36 in.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Legend',
          1 => 'WORK AREA AHEAD',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Advance warning',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Give drivers time to react',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Most tree crews spend a lot of time on the shoulder: trimming over a road, dragging brush to the chipper, or parking a bucket truck in a lane. Drivers do not expect people and equipment there. An advance warning sign set well before the work zone buys them the seconds they need to slow down and move over. On fast roads, the sign often needs to go farther out than seems natural, so drivers coming around a bend still have room to brake.',
            1 => 'Place the sign so it is visible from a distance, not hidden behind a parked truck or a curve. On a two-way road, cover both approaches. Take it down when the crew leaves, because a warning sign left out with no work behind it teaches drivers to ignore the next one.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Part of a work zone setup',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The sign is only the start of the layout. Cones or drums, a lane taper where needed, and trained flaggers finish the job. Follow the MUTCD and local rules for spacing, and plan traffic control as part of the job briefing as ANSI Z133 expects. Keep the sign face clean, since a coat of road grime and sawdust cuts its visibility, especially at dawn and dusk.',
            1 => 'Pair it with a sign stand that keeps it upright in wind. Our <a href="/safety/traffic-control/">traffic control</a> section has stands, cones, and other signs.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How far ahead should the sign go?',
          'a' => 'It depends on road speed and conditions. Use the MUTCD and your local agency\'s spacing tables for the road you are working on.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use it for a full road closure?',
          'a' => 'Use it as advance warning, but a full closure also needs Road Closed signs and barricades at the closure itself.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/jbc/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/traffic-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/traffic-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Traffic Cone',
        'creator' => 'pdugmore2001',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/78528924@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/78528924@N00/6173960958',
      ),
    ),
    '/safety/traffic-control/plasticade-tree-work-ahead-sign/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/safety/traffic-control/plasticade-tree-work-ahead-sign/',
      'category' => '/safety/traffic-control/',
      'name' => 'Plasticade Tree Work Ahead Sign',
      'brand' => 'plasticade',
      'brand_name' => 'Plasticade',
      'kind' => 'traffic',
      'price' => 89,
      'keyword' => 'tree work ahead sign',
      'title' => 'Plasticade Tree Work Ahead Sign for Roadside Jobs',
      'h1' => 'Plasticade Tree Work Ahead Sign',
      'meta' => 'A Plasticade Tree Work Ahead sign tells drivers exactly why the road narrows: a tree crew is on site. Placement tips and how it fits into a work zone setup.',
      'tagline' => 'A plastic Tree Work Ahead warning sign for roadside crews.',
      'summary' => 'A tree work ahead sign does more than a generic warning: it tells drivers there may be falling limbs, brush on the shoulder, and people near the lane. This Plasticade sign is a lightweight plastic option crews can set out and pick up quickly.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => 'Tree Work Ahead legend tells drivers what to expect',
        1 => 'Plastic construction is light to carry',
        2 => 'Quick to set out and pick up',
        3 => 'Use with cones, stands, and flaggers',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Legend',
          1 => 'TREE WORK AHEAD',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Material',
          1 => 'Plastic',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Advance warning for tree work',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'A sign that names the hazard',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Drivers have learned to tune out generic orange signs. A sign that says tree work gets more attention, because it tells them what is coming: trucks, a chipper, limbs coming down, and people on foot near the lane. That extra bit of attention is what you want while a groundie is feeding brush ten feet from traffic. It also helps the crew: a driver who knows tree work is ahead is less likely to be surprised by a groundie stepping out from behind the chipper.',
            1 => 'Set the sign on each approach, far enough out that drivers can slow down before they reach the first truck. Keep it clean and upright, and pull it in when the job wraps up.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Setting up the work zone',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Treat the sign as one piece of a layout that also includes cones, a taper if a lane is closed, and trained flaggers when traffic has to stop. Follow the MUTCD and your state rules for spacing, and plan traffic control during the job briefing as ANSI Z133 expects. Plastic signs are light, so a sturdy base or ballast keeps them from tipping over in gusts from passing trucks.',
            1 => 'Check the sign before buying if a road authority requires a specific sheeting or size on the roads you work. Browse our <a href="/safety/traffic-control/">traffic control</a> gear for stands and other signs.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a Tree Work Ahead sign required?',
          'a' => 'Requirements vary by road agency. Many crews use it alongside the standard advance warning signs the MUTCD and local rules call for.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does it need a stand?',
          'a' => 'Use a stand or base suited to the sign so it stays upright in wind and passing traffic.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'ground',
        1 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'all',
      'brand_path' => NULL,
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/traffic-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/traffic-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Traffic cones',
        'creator' => 'oatsy40',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/68089229@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/68089229@N06/22654103994',
      ),
    ),
    '/shop-all/10-5mm-replacement-rope-bridge-with-sewn-eyes/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/shop-all/10-5mm-replacement-rope-bridge-with-sewn-eyes/',
      'category' => '/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/saddle-parts-and-shoulder-harnesses/',
      'name' => '10.5mm Replacement Rope Bridge with Sewn Eyes',
      'brand' => 'gap',
      'brand_name' => 'Gap Arborist Supply',
      'kind' => 'saddle',
      'price' => 45,
      'keyword' => 'replacement rope bridge',
      'title' => '10.5mm Replacement Rope Bridge with Sewn Eyes',
      'h1' => '10.5mm Replacement Rope Bridge with Sewn Eyes',
      'meta' => 'A 10.5mm replacement rope bridge with sewn eyes for arborist saddles. How to size it, when to retire the old bridge, and what to check before you climb.',
      'tagline' => 'A sewn-eye rope bridge to replace a worn bridge on your climbing saddle.',
      'summary' => 'A replacement rope bridge is the cheapest way to keep a good saddle in service. This 10.5mm bridge has sewn eyes at each end and is meant to swap in when the bridge on your saddle is worn, glazed, or due for retirement.',
      'highlights' => 
      array (
        0 => '10.5mm rope bridge',
        1 => 'Sewn eyes at both ends',
        2 => 'Replaces a worn saddle bridge',
        3 => 'Check length and attachment against your saddle',
      ),
      'specs' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Diameter',
          1 => '10.5 mm',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Terminations',
          1 => 'Sewn eyes',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          0 => 'Use',
          1 => 'Saddle bridge replacement',
        ),
      ),
      'body' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why the bridge wears first',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On a bridge saddle, the bridge carries your weight and takes the friction of the ring or carabiner sliding back and forth all day. It is usually the first part of the saddle to show wear: fuzzing, glazing, a flat spot where the ring rides, or stiffness from sap and dirt. Replacing it on schedule is cheaper than replacing the saddle and safer than nursing an old bridge along. Keep a log of when the bridge went into service so you know when it is due.',
            1 => 'Most saddle makers set a retirement interval for the bridge and expect it to be inspected before every climb. Look for cuts, core damage, heat glazing, contamination, and any damage at the sewn terminations.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fitting a new bridge',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Bridges are not universal. Match the length, diameter, and end terminations to what your saddle maker specifies, and install it exactly as their instructions show, including any bridge rings or attachment points. If your saddle calls for a different diameter or length, use that one.',
            1 => 'Have a competent person check the setup before you weight it. Life-support gear must meet the applicable standard, be used per ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions, and be inspected each day. More <a href="/climbing/saddles-and-harnesses/saddle-parts-and-shoulder-harnesses/">saddle parts</a> are in this section.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Will this bridge fit my saddle?',
          'a' => 'Only if your saddle maker approves a 10.5mm sewn-eye bridge of this length. Check the saddle manual before you order.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How often should I replace a saddle bridge?',
          'a' => 'Follow the saddle maker\'s retirement guidance, and retire it immediately if inspection shows cuts, glazing, core damage, or damaged stitching.',
        ),
      ),
      'use' => 
      array (
        0 => 'climbing',
        1 => 'safety',
      ),
      'level' => 'pro',
      'brand_path' => '/gap-arborist-supply/',
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/saddle-2.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/saddle-2-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Saving Tyr 6',
        'creator' => 'ToobyDoo',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/7977078@N02',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/7977078@N02/2876232015',
      ),
