    '/climbing/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/',
      'name' => 'Climbing',
      'h1' => 'Climbing Tree Gear for Arborists',
      'keyword' => 'tree gear',
      'title' => 'Climbing Tree Gear: Saddles, Spurs, Rope Tools & More',
      'meta' => 'Climbing tree gear for working arborists: saddles, spurs, carabiners, friction devices, lanyards, helmets and throw line, explained by tree people.',
      'kind' => 'saddle',
      'intro' => 'This is the hub for all of our climbing tree gear: saddles, spurs, hardware, friction devices, lanyards, helmets and throw line. Whether you are building a first kit or replacing worn tree gear, the sections below get you to the right shelf fast.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'How the climbing department is organized',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Climbing gear breaks down by the job each piece does in your system. Saddles and harnesses are what you sit in. Rope tools, friction devices and hitch cord control how you move. Carabiners, rings, swivels and pulleys connect it all together. Lanyards and fliplines keep you positioned while you cut, and spurs get you up spars and removals.',
            1 => 'Around that core sit the support items: helmets, throw weights and line for setting your tie-in, and gear bags for keeping it clean. Each subcategory has its own buying guide, so you can drill down to exactly what you need.',
            2 => 'If you are not sure where something lives, start with the climbing gear section. It holds most of the hardware and devices, from ascenders to carabiners, and each listing explains what the item does in a working system.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Building a first climbing kit',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A working kit starts with a saddle that fits, a climbing rope rated for arborist use, a friction hitch or mechanical device, connectors that meet ANSI Z133, a flipline or adjustable lanyard, a helmet and a throw line setup. Buy the saddle first and fit it in person if you can, because comfort decides how long you will stay productive in the tree.',
            1 => 'If you are just starting, a moving rope system (MRS) with a hitch and micro pulley is simple to learn and inspect. Many climbers later add a stationary rope system (SRS) for long ascents and big canopies. Either way, get trained before you leave the ground.',
            2 => 'Do not overlook the small items that make a kit usable on day one: a micro pulley to tend your hitch, a friction saver to protect your rope and the tree, a rope bag to keep the line clean, and a couple of gear carabiners for tools. A complete tree climbing kit is often the easiest way to get all of that matched and compatible in one go.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'New, used and wholesale gear',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'We get asked about used tree climbing gear a lot. Hardware such as carabiners and pulleys can be inspected, but you rarely know how textile gear like rope, saddles and lanyards has been stored, loaded or exposed to chemicals. For life-support items, buy new from a source you trust and keep a record of when each item went into service.',
            1 => 'Crews outfitting several climbers can save by standardizing on one or two connector models and one rope diameter. That keeps spares interchangeable and makes daily inspections faster.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Safety and inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Every piece of life-support tree gear should meet the applicable standard, be used per the manufacturer\'s instructions, and be inspected before each climb. Retire anything that has been shock loaded, cut, glazed, cracked or that you simply cannot vouch for. When in doubt, take it out of service.',
            1 => 'Treat inspection as part of the climb, not a separate chore. Run your rope through your hands as you flake it out, check every connector gate as you clip it to your saddle, and look at stitching on your bridge and lanyard before you leave the ground. Keep a simple log for each climber\'s life-support gear with dates in service and any notes from inspections. When a crew shares gear, the log is how everyone knows what has happened to it.',
            2 => 'Finally, pair good gear with good habits: a work plan, a clear drop zone, a trained ground person and an aerial rescue plan for every climb.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What tree gear do I need to start climbing?',
          'a' => 'At minimum: a fitted arborist saddle, an arborist climbing rope, a friction hitch or mechanical friction device, Z133-compliant locking connectors, a lanyard or flipline, a helmet and a throw line kit, plus proper training.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is it safe to buy used climbing gear?',
          'a' => 'Used metal hardware can sometimes be inspected and trusted, but used rope, saddles and lanyards are hard to evaluate. For life-support textiles, buying new is the safer choice.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between MRS and SRS?',
          'a' => 'In a moving rope system the rope runs over the anchor and moves as you climb. In a stationary rope system the rope is fixed and you move along it with a device. Both are common in tree work.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need spurs to climb trees?',
          'a' => 'Only for removals and some spar work. Spurs wound living trees, so they are not used for pruning trees that are staying, except in a rescue.',
        ),
      ),
