    '/climbing/climbing-gear/ascent-and-descent/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/ascent-and-descent/',
      'name' => 'Ascent & Descent',
      'h1' => 'Tree Climbing Ascenders and Descenders',
      'keyword' => 'tree climbing ascenders and descenders',
      'title' => 'Tree Climbing Ascenders and Descenders',
      'meta' => 'Tree climbing ascenders and descenders: hand ascenders, foot ascenders, rope grabs, belay and mechanical friction devices for MRS and SRS work.',
      'kind' => 'ascender',
      'intro' => 'Tree climbing ascenders and descenders move you up the rope and bring you back down under control. This section covers hand and foot ascenders, rope grabs, figure 8s, belay devices and the mechanical friction devices that run MRS and SRS systems.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Choosing gear for the way you climb',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'For SRS ascents, a hand ascender paired with a foot ascender or a chest-mounted device lets you walk up the rope efficiently. Descending and working the canopy is handled by your friction device, a rope wrench and hitch, or an all-in-one mechanical device. For MRS, ascent is usually done with a hitch and micro pulley, sometimes with a foot ascender added to save energy. Whatever you choose, confirm each device is rated for your rope diameter, use it per the manufacturer\'s instructions, get trained on it, and inspect it before every climb.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Descent and control',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Coming down matters as much as going up. Mechanical friction devices and figure 8 descenders give smooth, controlled descents and can be set to add friction for heavier loads. Rope grabs help with positioning and lanyard adjustment, and a belay device can serve the ground crew during a rescue or when lowering a climber, where approved. Practice changeovers from ascent to descent low to the ground until they are second nature.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is a belay device the same as a climbing friction device?',
          'a' => 'Not always. Some belay devices are approved for rope access or tree work, others are not. Check the manufacturer\'s instructions for the uses each device is approved for.',
        ),
      ),
      'parent' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/',
