    '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/friction-savers/',
      'name' => 'Friction Savers',
      'h1' => 'Friction Savers & Canopy Anchors',
      'keyword' => 'friction saver',
      'title' => 'Friction Savers for Tree Climbing Arborists',
      'meta' => 'Friction savers for tree climbing: adjustable friction savers, ring-and-ring slings, anchor slings and the ART Ropeguide to protect rope, tree and climber.',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'intro' => 'A friction saver sits between your rope and the tie-in limb, protecting the bark and your rope while making MRS climbing smoother. We carry adjustable friction savers, anchor slings and ring-style designs.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why use one',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Running a rope directly over bark wears the rope, heats it, and damages the cambium. An arborist friction saver gives the rope a smooth metal or ring path, cuts friction so advancing your hitch is easier, and keeps the tie-in clean. Adjustable friction savers fit a range of limb diameters, and cambium savers with rings can often be retrieved from the ground. Size the sling to the limb, rate it for life support, and inspect before every climb.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Types of friction savers',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Ring-and-ring friction savers use two rings on a sling; they are light, cheap and simple. Adjustable friction savers let you set the length to fit different limb sizes. Anchor slings with steel or aluminum rings can be girth-hitched or wrapped around a stem. Some designs, like the ART Ropeguide, add a pulley for smoother rope travel. Retrievable designs can be set and retrieved from the ground with the right technique. Inspect slings and rings for wear before every climb.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need a friction saver for SRS?',
          'a' => 'Not always, because SRS anchors are often set differently, but many climbers still use an anchor sling or friction saver to protect the tree and rope.',
        ),
      ),
      'parent' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/',
