    '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/',
      'name' => 'Gear Storage',
      'h1' => 'Climbing Rope Storage, Rope Bags & Gear Bags',
      'keyword' => 'climbing rope storage',
      'title' => 'Climbing Rope Storage, Gear Bags & Organizers',
      'meta' => 'Climbing rope storage and gear organizers for arborists: rope bags, haul bags and duffels that keep rope clean, dry and ready for the next job.',
      'kind' => 'case-bag',
      'intro' => 'Good climbing rope storage keeps your life-support gear clean, dry and out of the truck bed. Choose rope bags for individual lines and larger gear bags for saddles, hardware and the rest of your climbing kit.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Why storage matters',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Rope and textiles fail from what happens between climbs as much as from climbing. Sunlight, bar oil, fuel, battery acid and grit all shorten their life. A rope left loose in a truck bed picks up sawdust and grit that work into the core and wear it from the inside. Proper climbing equipment storage is one of the cheapest ways to make gear last longer.',
            1 => 'Bags also speed up the job. When each rope lives in its own bag and the saddle and hardware ride in a dedicated gear bag, setup at the tree is quick and you are less likely to leave something behind.',
            2 => 'Storage is also part of an inspection routine. If every rope has its own bag and every bag has a tag with the rope\'s length and date in service, it is easy to see what is due for a closer look.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Rope bags and gear bags',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A rope bag is sized for one or two climbing lines and usually has a wide mouth and a ground tarp or base so you can feed rope straight out of it. A gear bag or haul bag is larger and tougher, built to carry saddles, helmets, connectors and devices. Some climbers use a tarp-style bag that opens flat to keep rope off the ground at the base of the tree.',
            1 => 'Think about the size of your kit, whether you hike into jobs, and whether you want backpack straps or a simple duffel. Bright colors are easier to spot in the brush pile.',
            2 => 'For crews, consistent storage across the truck pays off. Keep the same bag style for each climber\'s rope and the same bag for rigging lines, labeled clearly, so anyone on the crew can find the right line in a hurry.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Organizing your climbing kit',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A simple climbing gear organizer system helps: one bag per rope, labeled by length, and a separate bag for hardware. Keep a small pouch for spares like hitch cord and a backup carabiner. Store everything dry and out of direct sun, and never in the same compartment as fuel or bar oil.',
            1 => 'Many climbers also use a rope tarp at the base of the tree. It keeps rope and gear off dirt, sap and rainwater, makes it easier to see what is laid out, and folds up quickly at the end of the job.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspect when you pack',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Packing up is a good time for a quick look at your gear. Flake rope into the bag and run it through your hands to feel for flat spots or damage. Note anything suspect and remove it from service until it can be inspected properly.',
            1 => 'If you find contamination such as fuel or oil on a life-support textile, retire it. These chemicals can weaken fibers in ways you cannot see.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How should I store climbing rope?',
          'a' => 'Clean, dry, out of direct sunlight and away from fuel, oil and chemicals. A rope bag keeps it protected and ready to deploy.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the difference between a rope bag and a gear bag?',
          'a' => 'A rope bag is sized to hold and deploy a climbing line. A gear bag or haul bag is larger and carries saddles, helmets and hardware.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I wash rope bags?',
          'a' => 'Most can be rinsed or hand washed. Follow the maker\'s care instructions and let the bag dry fully before storing rope in it.',
        ),
      ),
      'parent' => '/climbing/climbing-gear/',
      'children' => 
      array (
        0 => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/gear-bags/',
        1 => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/rope-bags/',
      ),
      'products' => 
      array (
        0 => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/gear-bags/buckingham-haul-bag/',
        1 => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/gear-bags/edelrid-kurt-haulbag/',
        2 => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/gear-bags/petzl-duffel-85l-large-gear-bag/',
        3 => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/rope-bags/courant-cross-rope-bag-23-36-l/',
        4 => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/rope-bags/treehog-40l-rope-bag/',
        5 => '/rope-bags/courant-cargo-bag-50l-blue/',
        6 => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/gear-bags/arbortec-python-50l-backpack-gear-bag/',
        7 => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/gear-bags/petzl-duffel-65l-transport-bag/',
        8 => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/gear-bags/weaver-cavern-gear-bag/',
        9 => '/climbing/climbing-gear/gear-storage/rope-bags/edelrid-caddy-ii/',
