    '/climbing/tree-climbing-kits/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/climbing/tree-climbing-kits/',
      'name' => 'Climbing Kits',
      'h1' => 'Tree Climbing Gear Kits',
      'keyword' => 'tree climbing gear kits',
      'title' => 'Tree Climbing Gear Kits for Arborists',
      'meta' => 'Tree climbing gear kits for arborists: rope climbing kits that bundle the core gear for a working system, with advice on fit, training and upgrades.',
      'kind' => 'rope',
      'intro' => 'Tree climbing gear kits bundle the core arborist gear for a working climbing system, saving you the job of matching every rope, connector and device yourself. Start with a kit, then personalize it as you learn what you like.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What a climbing kit includes',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A typical arborist rope climbing kit includes a climbing rope, a hitch cord or friction device, a micro pulley, connectors, and often a friction saver and throw line. Some kits add a saddle, lanyard and helmet. Check exactly what is in a kit before buying so you know what you still need to add.',
            1 => 'The value of a kit is compatibility. The rope diameter, hitch cord, pulley and connectors are chosen to work together, which matters a lot when you are new and do not yet know which combinations behave well.',
            2 => 'Rope length is another thing to check. Climbing rope comes in lengths that suit different tree heights, and a kit rope should be long enough for the trees you work in with some extra for tying off.',
            3 => 'Check whether connectors in the kit are auto-locking and rated for climbing, and that the rope and hitch cord come with terminations finished by a qualified maker.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Kit vs building your own',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Experienced climbers often build their own setup piece by piece. For a newer climber or a crew outfitting a new hire, a kit gets reliable arborist tree climbing gear into service quickly. As you climb more, you will find preferences, maybe a different hitch, a mechanical device or a lighter rope, and you can swap parts as long as each new component is compatible.',
            1 => 'Kits are also a good way to stock a backup set on the truck, so a damaged rope or device does not end the day.',
            2 => 'Some climbers buy a kit as a starting point and keep the parts that work for them, then replace the rest over time. That is a perfectly good way to learn what you like without buying every option on its own.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit the saddle separately',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'If a kit does not include a saddle, choose one separately based on fit. If it does include one, check sizing carefully. A poorly fitting saddle is the one thing in a kit that is hard to live with, so make sure you can adjust or exchange it.',
            1 => 'Sizing for helmets and lanyards matters too. A helmet should fit snugly with the chinstrap adjusted, and a lanyard should be long enough to wrap the stems you climb.',
            2 => 'Hang in the saddle with the rest of the kit attached to check that everything sits where you expect it.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Training and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A kit is only as safe as the person climbing on it. Get hands-on training from a qualified instructor, read every component\'s instructions, and inspect your gear before every climb. All life-support components should meet ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s ratings for your use.',
            1 => 'Keep the instruction sheets that come with each component in your gear bag or a binder on the truck. They contain the approved configurations and inspection criteria for each device.',
            2 => 'Plan regular practice of aerial rescue with the kit, too. Knowing your own system well is part of being able to help a climber in trouble.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Are tree climbing kits good for beginners?',
          'a' => 'Yes. A kit gives you a matched, compatible system, which is ideal when you are learning, as long as you also get proper training.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do climbing kits include a saddle?',
          'a' => 'Some do and some do not. Check the contents list before you buy.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I upgrade parts in a kit later?',
          'a' => 'Yes, as long as each new part is compatible with your rope and the rest of the system.',
        ),
      ),
      'parent' => '/climbing/',
      'children' => 
      array (
      ),
      'products' => 
      array (
        0 => '/climbing/tree-climbing-kits/arborist-rope-climbing-kits/',
      ),
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/rope-0.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/rope-0-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'coiling',
        'creator' => 'michael pollak',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/22294215@N06/6796140986',
      ),
    ),
    '/clothing/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/clothing/',
      'name' => 'Clothing',
      'h1' => 'Arborist Clothing & Apparel',
      'keyword' => 'arborist clothing',
      'title' => 'Arborist Clothing: Pants, Boots, Hats & Hoodies',
      'meta' => 'Arborist clothing and apparel for tree work: climbing pants, chainsaw pants, arborist boots, hats, hoodies and shirts built for climbing and cutting.',
      'kind' => 'apparel',
      'intro' => 'Arborist clothing has to stretch for climbing, shed sawdust and sap, and stand up to brush and bark every day. Shop climbing pants, chainsaw pants, arborist boots, hats, hoodies and shirts built for tree work.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Dress for the job',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Tree work covers a lot of ground. A climber needs pants that move with the leg and resist abrasion, plus boots that work with spurs or on rope. Ground crew need cut protection when running a saw, and everyone needs visibility near traffic and layers for cold mornings. Good arborist apparel is built around these jobs instead of looking like it.',
            1 => 'Cotton work clothes still have a place, but technical fabrics with stretch panels and reinforced knees last longer and are more comfortable in the tree. Brands like Arborwear, Arbortec and Clogger build clothing specifically for tree crews.',
            2 => 'Gloves are part of the outfit too. Climbers often prefer thin, grippy gloves for rope work, while ground crew want heavier gloves for brush hauling and chipper feeding.',
            3 => 'Arborist shirts work best with a fitted cut that does not snag on limbs or rope, and long sleeves give protection from sun, sap and scratches. Forestry clothing for logging and land clearing tends to be heavier, built for brush and cold, while climbing apparel leans lighter and stretchier.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Cut protection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Chainsaw pants and chaps contain fibers designed to clog the saw\'s sprocket when cut, stopping the chain before it reaches your leg. In the US, look for labels showing compliance with ASTM F1897 for leg protection, and for chainsaw boots, ASTM F1818. Many climbers wear chainsaw-protective pants aloft as well as on the ground.',
            1 => 'No cut protection is cut proof. It works alongside training, good technique and a well-maintained saw.',
            2 => 'Chainsaw-protective clothing should be replaced after it has been cut into the protective layer. Patching protective pants or chaps is generally not recommended unless the manufacturer allows it. Wash them according to the label, since harsh detergents and high heat can reduce effectiveness.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Boots, hats and layers',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Arborist boots range from lightweight climbing boots to full chainsaw-protective boots. Hats and caps keep sun and sawdust off and add warmth under a helmet in winter, while hoodies and sweatshirts layer well for cold starts. Choose layers you can shed as the day warms up, and keep loose cords and drawstrings away from the saw.',
            1 => 'Weather sets the layering plan. In summer, look for lightweight, breathable fabrics that dry fast and protect from sun. In winter, a base layer, an insulating hoodie and a windproof jacket give you a range to work with. Rain gear should be tough enough to survive brush.',
            2 => 'Logging apparel such as heavy canvas can work for ground crews on cold days, but it is often too stiff for climbing.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Visibility and safety',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'High-visibility colors help the ground crew and drivers see you. When working near traffic, follow the applicable traffic control rules for your site. Always pair clothing with the rest of your protective equipment: helmet, eye and hearing protection and gloves.',
            1 => 'When choosing clothing for a crew, consider buying in a common color so crew members are easy to spot and recognize on the site.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What should arborists wear?',
          'a' => 'Stretchy, durable pants or chainsaw-protective pants, supportive boots, layers for the weather, high visibility when needed, plus a helmet, eye and ear protection and gloves.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do climbers need chainsaw pants?',
          'a' => 'Many climbers wear them whenever they run a saw. Requirements vary by employer and task, so follow your company\'s safety policy and ANSI Z133.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the best arborist clothing brand?',
          'a' => 'Arborwear, Arbortec and Clogger are all popular with tree crews. The best choice depends on fit and the work you do.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is cotton okay for tree work?',
          'a' => 'It works, but technical fabrics with stretch and reinforcement usually last longer and move better when climbing.',
        ),
      ),
      'parent' => NULL,
      'children' => 
      array (
        0 => '/clothing/arborist-boots/',
        1 => '/clothing/hats-and-caps/',
        2 => '/clothing/sweatshirts-and-jackets/',
