    '/clothing/arborist-boots/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/clothing/arborist-boots/',
      'name' => 'Arborist Boots',
      'h1' => 'Tree Climbing Boots & Arborist Boots',
      'keyword' => 'tree climbing boots',
      'title' => 'Tree Climbing Boots & Arborist Chainsaw Boots',
      'meta' => 'Tree climbing boots and arborist chainsaw boots from Arbortec and Arbpro. Supportive boots for spurs, rope work and ground saw work, with fit tips.',
      'kind' => 'boot',
      'intro' => 'Tree climbing boots have to support your arches on spurs, grip on rope and bark, and protect your feet around saws. Compare arborist boots from Arbortec and Arbpro, including chainsaw-protective models.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'What makes a good climbing boot',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The best tree climbing boots combine a stiff shank, firm ankle support and a sole that grips. A stiff shank keeps the spur stirrup from crushing your arch and spreads load across the foot. Ankle support keeps you stable when you stand sideways on a stem. A sole with a defined heel helps foot ascenders and footlocking, and an aggressive tread grips wet bark and slopes.',
            1 => 'Weight matters too. A lighter boot is easier on your legs over a long day of climbing. That is why many arborist climbing boots use lightweight materials while keeping the support where it counts.',
            2 => 'Arborist boots often have extra features like reinforced toe caps that resist rope and bark wear, and hooks or loops that keep laces from snagging on spurs. Some arbpro boots use a clip and step system that makes lacing faster and keeps it secure.',
            3 => 'Tree boots for climbing on rope only can be lighter and more flexible, but if you ever wear spurs, the stiff shank becomes essential. Many climbers own two pairs: a lighter boot for rope work and a supportive, stiff boot for spur work and removals. Matching boots to the day\'s work keeps your feet comfortable and stable.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Chainsaw protection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Chainsaw boots include cut-protective layers in the upper that clog a running chain. In the US, chainsaw-protective footwear is covered by ASTM F1818; in Europe, by the EN ISO 17249 standard with protection classes. Check the label for what each boot is certified to. For ground crew bucking and felling, and climbers who run a saw a lot, cut-protective boots add a layer of protection on top of training and good technique.',
            1 => 'Steel or composite toe caps are common in arborist boots too. Check whether the toe and cut protection suit your work and any requirements from your employer.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Models, fit and break-in',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'The Arbortec Scafell Lite is a lightweight chainsaw boot built for climbers who want protection without heavy weight. Arbpro models like the Andrew Cervino and the Clip N Step focus on support and fit for climbing and ground work. Consider your mix of work: mostly climbing, mostly ground saw work, or a mix. Waterproof membranes help in wet climates but can be warmer in summer.',
            1 => 'Fit tree boots in the afternoon when your feet are slightly swollen, with the socks you work in. Your heel should not lift, toes should have room, and the boot should feel secure laced. Break new boots in on the ground before a long climb on spurs. Arborist boots with lace-lock or quick-lace systems save time and stay tight.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Care and replacement',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Clean off mud and sap, dry boots away from direct heat, and treat leather regularly. Replace boots when the shank softens, the sole wears smooth or any cut protection is damaged, because a cut-protective boot that has stopped a chain should be retired.',
            1 => 'Replace laces as soon as they fray. A lace that snaps while you are on spurs is more than an inconvenience.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What are the best tree climbing boots?',
          'a' => 'Boots with a stiff shank, strong ankle support, a grippy sole and a defined heel, sized well for your feet. Chainsaw protection is a plus for climbers who run saws.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Do I need chainsaw boots as a climber?',
          'a' => 'Many climbers choose them. Requirements vary by employer and task, so follow your company policy and ANSI Z133.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use regular work boots with spurs?',
          'a' => 'Boots without a stiff shank let the spur stirrup press into your arch. Climbing-specific boots are far more comfortable and stable.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How long do arborist boots last?',
          'a' => 'It depends on use, but replace them when the shank softens, soles wear out or cut protection is damaged.',
        ),
      ),
      'parent' => '/clothing/',
      'children' => 
      array (
      ),
      'products' => 
      array (
        0 => '/clothing/arborist-boots/arbortec-scafell-lite-chainsaw-boots-lime-green/',
        1 => '/clothing/arborist-boots/arbpro-andrew-cervino-boots/',
        2 => '/clothing/arborist-boots/arbpro-clip-n-step-boots/',
        3 => '/arborist-boots/haix-protector-prime-orange-chainsaw-boots/',
        4 => '/clothing/arborist-boots/arbortec-kayo-chainsaw-climbing-boot/',
        5 => '/clothing/arborist-boots/arbpro-evo-2-boots/',
        6 => '/clothing/arborist-boots/wesco-highliner-10-boots-brown/',
        7 => '/clothing/arborist-boots/wesco-highliner-black-10-boots/',
        8 => '/clothing/arborist-boots/arbpro-laces-for-arbpro-clip-n-step-and-evo-boots/',
        9 => '/arborist-boots/haix-protector-ultra-chainsaw-boots/',
        10 => '/clothing/arborist-boots/orange-laces-for-scafell-lite-boots/',
      ),
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/boot-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/boot-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Old Boot, New Boot',
        'creator' => 'General Wesc',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/35518575@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/35518575@N00/5198346357',
      ),
    ),
    '/clothing/hats-and-caps/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/clothing/hats-and-caps/',
      'name' => 'Hats & Caps',
      'h1' => 'Tree Hats, Caps & Neck Warmers',
      'keyword' => 'tree hats',
      'title' => 'Tree Hats, Caps & Neck Warmers for Arborists',
      'meta' => 'Tree hats and caps for arborists: stocking caps, neck warmers and work hats from Arborwear and Husqvarna that fit under a helmet or wear on breaks.',
      'kind' => 'apparel',
      'intro' => 'Tree hats and caps keep sun, sawdust and cold off your head on and off the job. We carry Arborwear stocking caps and neck warmers plus Husqvarna hats.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Hats for the job',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'On the job, most of your time is under a helmet, so a hat needs to fit underneath it without changing how the helmet sits. A thin stocking cap or beanie adds warmth on cold mornings. A neck warmer keeps wind and sawdust out of your collar and can be pulled up over your face in bitter weather.',
            1 => 'Off the helmet, a work hat with a brim keeps the sun off during brush hauling or chipper work. Many arborists also wear a tree logo hat as everyday crew gear.',
            2 => 'Arborwear stocking caps and neck warmers are built for tree work with fabrics that hold up to bark and sawdust. Husqvarna hats are a good everyday option for the crew.',
            3 => 'Look for flat seams and low-profile designs that will not create pressure points under the helmet suspension.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Fit under a helmet',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Before you wear any hat under a helmet, adjust the helmet so it still fits snug and the chinstrap sits correctly. A bulky hat can loosen the fit and reduce protection. Choose thin, close-fitting materials and readjust the helmet when you take the hat off later in the day.',
            1 => 'Some climbing helmets have winter liners designed for them. These are a good alternative to a separate hat, because they are designed not to affect the helmet\'s fit.',
            2 => 'Take your helmet to try-ons where you can, or test any new hat at home under the helmet before wearing it on the job. If the helmet will not adjust snug over the hat, the hat is too thick for climbing.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Layers and care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'In winter, layering a thin cap with a neck warmer works better than one thick hat. Wool and synthetic blends stay warm when damp from sweat. Wash hats regularly, since sawdust and sweat break down fabric over time.',
            1 => 'Keep loose cords and pom-poms away from saws and rope. Anything that can catch is a hazard in the tree.',
            2 => 'Sun protection is easy to overlook in summer. A brimmed hat during ground work, sunscreen and a neck covering help prevent sunburn on long days in the open.',
            3 => 'A spare dry cap in the truck is a small thing that makes a big difference on wet winter days.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Crew gear',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Many tree companies outfit their crew with matching hats and caps. A consistent look helps customers recognize your crew on site and adds a professional touch. Choose durable styles that hold up to daily wear, and remember to keep logos and colors visible but not distracting near traffic.',
            1 => 'Off the job, a tree hat is also a simple way to show what you do. The same rules apply: pick something comfortable and tough enough to survive the truck cab.',
            2 => 'Btr tree hats and tree logo hats are popular searches for crew wear; whichever brand you choose, pick quality fabric and stitching that last.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I wear a hat under my climbing helmet?',
          'a' => 'Yes, if it is thin and the helmet still fits snugly with the chinstrap correctly adjusted.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What hat is best for cold weather tree work?',
          'a' => 'A thin wool or synthetic stocking cap under your helmet plus a neck warmer works well for most climbers.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
