    '/plant-care/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/plant-care/',
      'name' => 'Plant Care',
      'h1' => 'Tree Care Supplies for Plant Health Work',
      'keyword' => 'tree care supplies',
      'title' => 'Tree Care Supplies: Cabling, Bracing & Planting',
      'meta' => 'Tree care supplies for plant health work: tree cabling and bracing hardware, planting and staking supplies and wound treatment for the arborist\'s service truck.',
      'kind' => 'cable-hardware',
      'intro' => 'Tree care supplies cover the work that keeps trees standing and healthy: cabling and bracing systems, planting and staking hardware, and wound treatment. This department is for arborists who offer more than removals.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Cabling and bracing',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Supplemental support systems reduce the risk of failure in weak unions and heavy limbs. Steel cable systems use EHS or common grade cable with eye bolts or lags, while bracing rods add rigid support across a split or weak crotch. Installation should follow ANSI A300 support system standards and be done by trained arborists.',
            1 => 'Plan for inspection. A support system is not install-and-forget; it should be checked on a schedule and adjusted or replaced as the tree grows.',
            2 => 'Common cabling hardware includes eye bolts, eye lag screws and J-lags. Eye bolts through the limb give the strongest anchor; lag hardware threads into the wood and suits lighter systems. The right choice depends on limb size, wood condition and the loads the system is expected to handle.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Planting and staking',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Newly planted trees sometimes need temporary staking to stay upright until roots establish. Wide, soft tree straps spread load on the bark, and earth anchors hold guys in place without driving stakes through the root ball. Remove staking once the tree can stand on its own, usually within the first year or so.',
            1 => 'Plant at the right depth with the root flare visible, mulch wide and shallow, and keep mulch off the trunk.',
            2 => 'Good planting supplies make staking easy to do right. Tree support webbing comes on a roll so you can cut what you need, and duck bill style earth anchors drive in with a rod and set by pulling up. Wire rope clamps finish guy lines to anchors when installed as the clamp maker specifies. Check staked trees during the season to make sure straps are not rubbing or cutting into the bark.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Wound care',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Most pruning cuts heal best when made correctly and left alone. Some arborists use wound treatment on specific species or in specific situations, such as reducing pest attraction. Follow local guidance and the product label.',
            1 => 'Wound treatment is not a substitute for good pruning. Make cuts just outside the branch collar, avoid flush cuts and leave the tree\'s natural defenses intact. If a wound needs attention for disease or pest reasons, follow local extension or certified arborist guidance.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Who this is for',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Plant health care is a skilled service. If you are a homeowner, we suggest hiring an ISA-certified arborist to assess and install support systems. If you are a pro, use the supplies here alongside current standards and your own training.',
            1 => 'Our goal with this department is to keep the common consumables in one place so a plant health crew can restock quickly. Pair these supplies with good record keeping: note what was installed or applied, where and when, so follow-up visits are efficient and the customer understands the value of ongoing care.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What standard covers tree cabling and bracing?',
          'a' => 'In the US, ANSI A300 includes a part on supplemental support systems. Follow it along with your company\'s procedures.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How long should a newly planted tree stay staked?',
          'a' => 'Only as long as needed, often one growing season. Long-term staking can weaken the trunk.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Is pruning sealer necessary?',
          'a' => 'Usually not. A correct cut is the main thing; some situations call for treatment, so follow local guidance.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can a homeowner install a tree cabling kit?',
          'a' => 'We recommend hiring a qualified arborist. Cabling involves working at height and judging tree structure.',
        ),
      ),
      'parent' => NULL,
      'children' => 
      array (
        0 => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/',
        1 => '/plant-care/planting/',
      ),
      'products' => 
      array (
        0 => '/plant-care/cabling-and-bracing/bracing/bracing-rods/',
