    '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/',
      'name' => 'Crane Slings',
      'h1' => 'Crane Slings for Tree Removal',
      'keyword' => 'crane slings',
      'title' => 'Crane Slings & Tree Slings for Crane Removals',
      'meta' => 'Crane slings and tree slings for crane-assisted removals: endless round slings and premium rope crane slings with tags, plus tips on hitches and inspection.',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'intro' => 'Crane slings attach a tree section to the crane hook, so they carry the whole pick. For crane-assisted tree removal, we stock endless round slings and premium rope tree slings built for heavy, controlled lifts.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Round slings and rope slings',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Endless polyester round slings have a load-bearing core inside a protective cover. They are soft on bark, grip well in a choker hitch and are color-coded by rated capacity on many products. Rope crane slings with spliced eyes are durable and easy to set on rough stems.',
            1 => 'Whichever you choose, the sling\'s tag shows its rated capacity in vertical, choker and basket hitches. A sling without a readable tag should not be used for crane work.',
            2 => 'A tree sling for crane work should be long enough to wrap the stem with room to spare and to reach the hook comfortably. Many crews carry several lengths and capacities so they can match the sling to each pick. Round slings fold small for storage, which helps on trucks that are already full.',
            3 => 'Round slings and rope slings each have a learning curve. Round slings can roll on smooth stems, so set them with a choke that bites, and rope slings can be stiffer to set on small stems. Try both on lower-stakes picks to learn how they behave before a big job.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Hitches and capacity',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A choker hitch reduces a sling\'s rated capacity compared to a vertical lift, and a basket hitch can increase it, depending on angles. Sling angles matter: the flatter the angle, the more load on each leg. Work with the crane operator and the lift plan so the pick stays inside the rated capacity of every component.',
            1 => 'Crane straps and crane slings should be rated with margin above the expected weight of the pick. Tree sections are hard to estimate, and green wood is heavier than people think. Work from weight charts for the species and diameter where possible, and use the crane\'s load indicator to confirm before cutting free.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Protecting crane slings',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Saw chain, sharp stubs and rough bark can cut a sling. Set slings away from the cut, use sleeves or protection where they pass over edges, and cut well clear of the sling. Store slings clean, dry and out of sunlight.',
            1 => 'Never let a sling get pinched in the kerf or cut by the climber\'s saw. Place slings with enough distance above the cut so the saw stays well clear. Some crews use protective sleeves on slings where they cross rough bark or edges.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection and standards',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Inspect crane slings before each shift for cuts, burns, broken stitches, exposed core and chemical damage, and remove damaged slings from service. Crane-assisted tree work should follow ANSI Z133, the crane manufacturer\'s instructions and applicable crane rules, and involve qualified riggers and operators.',
            1 => 'Crane slinging is a specialized skill. Climbers and ground crew working with cranes should be trained in crane signals, rigging and the specific hazards of crane-assisted removals. Keep a clear exclusion zone under the load and follow the lift plan.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is the best sling for crane tree removal?',
          'a' => 'Many crews use endless round slings or rope crane slings rated well above the expected pick. The right choice depends on the lift plan.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Does a choker hitch reduce sling capacity?',
          'a' => 'Yes. Check the sling tag for its choker rating and plan around that number.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I use an arborist sling for a crane pick?',
          'a' => 'Only if it is rated and tagged for that load and hitch. Most crane work calls for slings made and tagged for lifting.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'When should I retire a crane sling?',
          'a' => 'Retire it if it is cut, burned, has exposed core yarns, broken stitching, a missing tag or any damage the manufacturer lists.',
        ),
      ),
      'parent' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/',
      'children' => 
      array (
      ),
      'products' => 
      array (
        0 => '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/5-8-x-20-premium-crane-sling/',
        1 => '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/endless-loop-round-slings/',
        2 => '/shop-all/endless-loop-polyester-round-sling-7-x-20-blue/',
        3 => '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/2-eye-to-eye-webbing-sling/',
        4 => '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/5-8-x-20-standard-crane-sling/',
        5 => '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/5-8-x-30-premium-crane-sling/',
        6 => '/shop-all/endless-loop-round-slings-1/',
        7 => '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/5-8-x-30-standard-crane-sling/',
        8 => '/rigging/rigging-slings/crane-slings/4-eye-to-eye-webbing-sling/',
        9 => '/shop-all/4-endless-loop-polyester-round-sling/',
      ),
      'photo' => 
      array (
        'src' => '/assets/photos/sling-1.webp',
        'sm' => '/assets/photos/sling-1-sm.webp',
        'title' => 'Climbing paraphernalia',
        'creator' => 'Ruth and Dave',
        'creator_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/95142644@N00',
        'license' => 'CC BY 2.0',
        'license_url' => 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/',
        'source_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/95142644@N00/3584852771',
      ),
    ),
    '/rigging/rigging-slings/heavy-duty-slings/' => 
    array (
      'path' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/heavy-duty-slings/',
      'name' => 'Heavy-Duty Slings',
      'h1' => 'Heavy Duty Slings for Tree Rigging',
      'keyword' => 'heavy duty sling',
      'title' => 'Heavy Duty Sling Options: Dead-Eye & Bull Rope Slings',
      'meta' => 'Heavy duty sling options for big rigging: dead-eye slings, bull rope rigging slings and double-head slings built to hang blocks and choke heavy wood.',
      'kind' => 'sling',
      'intro' => 'A heavy duty sling handles the large, dynamic loads of removals where big wood is being lowered. These dead-eye, bull rope and double-head rigging slings anchor blocks and choke heavy pieces.',
      'guide' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Dead-eye slings',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'A dead-eye sling has one spliced eye and a tail. Pass the tail around the stem and through the eye to choke it, or tie a knot to the rope. They grip stems well and pass easily through crotches. A larger diameter dead-eye spreads load on bark and handles heavier pieces.',
            1 => 'Dead-eye slings come in several diameters and lengths. A thicker sling holds blocks for larger removals and is less likely to cut into soft bark. A thinner, shorter one is quicker to set on small limbs and pieces being lowered. Many arborists keep a range so they can pick the right one at the tie-in.',
          ),
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Bull rope and double-head slings',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Bull rope rigging slings are made from heavy rigging rope and are built to hang blocks for big removals. Double-head slings have an eye at each end, so you can basket or choke them around a stem and clip both eyes to the block.',
            1 => 'The sling holding the block often sees the highest load in the system, so it should be at least as strong as the block and rope. Wrap it on solid wood below the block\'s attachment point.',
            2 => 'Some crews prefer a sling with a protective sleeve where it wraps the trunk to reduce wear. Others use a cambium saver or ring setup to protect the bark. Choose what works for your trees and your rigging style, but keep the rating of the sling in mind first.',
          ),
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Matching sling to job',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Choose diameter and length based on the stems you work and the loads you plan to lower. Too short and you cannot wrap the stem; too long and the block hangs low and swings. Many crews carry a few sizes.',
            1 => 'Trad slings and loop runners also have their place for lighter rigging and redirects. For heavy-duty rigging and big removals, stronger rope slings with spliced eyes and a large diameter are a safer bet. Choose length so the block hangs close to the stem and the rope runs cleanly.',
            2 => 'Double-head rigging slings are popular for hanging blocks on large stems because the two eyes clip together on the block, spreading load evenly. Long double-head slings, such as 25 feet or so, can wrap big trunks with room to spare, which is useful on large removals and crane-assisted jobs.',
          ),
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'h' => 'Inspection',
          'p' => 
          array (
            0 => 'Look for cuts, glazing, abrasion and damaged splices before each use. Replace slings that have been shock loaded heavily or show damage, and keep them away from saw chains and sharp edges. Follow ANSI Z133 and the manufacturer\'s instructions.',
            1 => 'Keep a record of when each heavy sling went into service and any severe shock loads it has taken. That history helps decide when to retire it. Damaged splices, a hardened or glazed section, or core damage are clear signs to take a sling out of service.',
          ),
        ),
      ),
      'faq' => 
      array (
        0 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'What is a dead-eye sling used for?',
          'a' => 'It chokes stems to hang blocks or attach pieces being lowered, with one eye and a free tail.',
        ),
        1 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How strong should a block sling be?',
          'a' => 'At least as strong as the block and rope it supports, with margin for dynamic loads.',
        ),
        2 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'Can I splice my own heavy duty sling?',
          'a' => 'Only if you are trained in the splice for that rope and test your work. Otherwise, buy professionally made slings.',
        ),
        3 => 
        array (
          'q' => 'How do I protect heavy slings from wear?',
          'a' => 'Keep them clean, out of the saw\'s path and off sharp edges, and rotate how they are set so wear is spread.',
        ),
      ),
      'parent' => '/rigging/rigging-slings/',
      'children' => 
      array (
      ),
      'products' => 
      array (
        0 => '/heavy-duty-slings/9-16-dead-eye-rigging-sling/',
        1 => '/rigging/rigging-slings/heavy-duty-slings/1-2-bull-rope-rigging-sling/',
        2 => '/rigging/rigging-slings/ring-slings/double-head-rigging-sling-3-4-x-25-long/',
